window.DOMINANCE_TOPICS = [{"slug":"cv-introduction","title":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"1-6","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system","title":"1. Why do we need a circulatory system?"},{"id":"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways","title":"2. The two major circulatory pathways"},{"id":"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers","title":"3. The heart and its four chambers"},{"id":"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right","title":"4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?"},{"id":"5-systole-and-diastole","title":"5. Systole and diastole"},{"id":"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system","title":"6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\">1. Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> makes this continuous transport possible.</p>\n<p>It consists of three major components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> Pumps blood through the circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> Provide pathways through which blood travels.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">2. The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood circulates through two connected pathways:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong> Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>These two circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>, meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">3. The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling</strong>, while the <strong>ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong> Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>How does blood flow through the heart?</h3>\n<p>Follow the pathway:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p>The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards.</p>\n<p>Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood.</p>\n<p>This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart</strong>, regardless of oxygen content.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures.</p>\n<p>To understand why, consider <strong>vascular resistance</strong>, which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied.</p>\n<p>A similar principle applies to blood vessels.</p>\n<p>The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network.</p>\n<p>The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>What does this mean for the ventricles?</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>left ventricle</strong> must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right ventricle</strong> pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall.</p>\n<p>The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing <strong>pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs)</strong> and interfering with gas exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute.</p>\n<p><em>The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\">5. Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p>The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation.</p>\n<p><strong>Systole</strong> refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while <strong>diastole</strong> refers to its period of relaxation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Importantly, <strong>atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole</strong>. The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract.</p>\n<p><em>The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\">6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence.</p>\n<p>Two important components make this possible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac muscle (myocardium):</strong> Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Specialised cardiac conducting system:</strong> Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence.</p>\n<p><em>The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The two circulations are arranged <strong>in series</strong>: blood ejected by the right ventricle passes through the lungs and then returns to the left ventricle. If one side persistently pumped more than the other, blood would accumulate in the circulation receiving the greater output, raising its pressures. Approximately equal long-term outputs therefore prevent progressive congestion.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The left ventricle must generate higher pressure</strong> because systemic vascular resistance is much greater than pulmonary vascular resistance. More myocardial muscle permits greater pressure generation. Both ventricles normally pump approximately the same volume per minute; wall thickness differs primarily because of the pressure load, not because the left ventricle must pump more blood.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. A vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>A vein.</strong> Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart; veins carry blood towards it, regardless of oxygen content. Pulmonary veins are the relevant example: they return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Early ventricular contraction raises intraventricular pressure while both the AV and semilunar valves are closed. This is <em>isovolumetric contraction</em>. Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the pulmonary artery or aorta and opens the corresponding outflow valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also contracting? Explain the timing.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Normally, no.</strong> Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole. The AV nodal conduction delay helps ensure atrial contraction contributes its final portion of ventricular filling before ventricular systole starts. Most filling has already occurred passively.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Systemic and pulmonary circulations operate in series:</strong> long-term right and left ventricular outputs must be approximately equal.</li><li><strong>Arteries and veins:</strong> defined by direction of blood movement relative to the heart, not oxygenation.</li><li><strong>Left versus right ventricle:</strong> similar flow, different pressure loads; the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall.</li><li><strong>Systole and diastole:</strong> contraction and relaxation are coordinated but differ between atria and ventricles.</li><li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> normally completes ventricular filling near the end of ventricular diastole.</li><li><strong>Electrical activation precedes mechanical contraction:</strong> the conducting system coordinates timing, while myocardium supplies force.</li></ol>\n<hr />\n<p><em>Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters.</em></p>\n","plain":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed. The circulatory system makes this continuous transport possible. It consists of three major components: Heart: Pumps blood through the circulation. Blood vessels: Provide pathways through which blood travels. Blood: Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away. 2. The two major circulatory pathways Blood circulates through two connected pathways: Pulmonary circulation: Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart. Systemic circulation: Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart. Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation. Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge. OpenStax College, original illustration , CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified. These two circuits are arranged in series , meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway. Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. 3. The heart and its four chambers The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps. Right atrium: Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. Right ventricle: Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. Left atrium: Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. Left ventricle: Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body. The atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling , while the ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries . Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart. Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge. Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., original illustration , CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified. How does blood flow through the heart? Follow the pathway: Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues. The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards. Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood. This is because arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart , regardless of oxygen content. 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures. To understand why, consider vascular resistance , which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow. Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied. A similar principle applies to blood vessels. The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow. Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network. The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures. What does this mean for the ventricles? The left ventricle must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker. The right ventricle pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall. The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs) and interfering with gas exchange. Key distinction: The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute. The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics. 5. Systole and diastole The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation. Systole refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while diastole refers to its period of relaxation. Atrial systole: The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling. Atrial diastole: The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles. Ventricular systole: The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle). Ventricular diastole: The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction. Importantly, atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole . The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract. The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter. 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence. Two important components make this possible: Cardiac muscle (myocardium): Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection. Specialised cardiac conducting system: Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat. Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence. The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters. Understanding Check 1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute? Reveal explanation The two circulations are arranged in series : blood ejected by the right ventricle passes through the lungs and then returns to the left ventricle. If one side persistently pumped more than the other, blood would accumulate in the circulation receiving the greater output, raising its pressures. Approximately equal long-term outputs therefore prevent progressive congestion. 2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle? Reveal explanation The left ventricle must generate higher pressure because systemic vascular resistance is much greater than pulmonary vascular resistance. More myocardial muscle permits greater pressure generation. Both ventricles normally pump approximately the same volume per minute; wall thickness differs primarily because of the pressure load, not because the left ventricle must pump more blood. 3. A vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain. Reveal explanation A vein. Arteries are defined by carrying blood away from the heart; veins carry blood towards it, regardless of oxygen content. Pulmonary veins are the relevant example: they return oxygenated blood from the lungs to the left atrium. 4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected? Reveal explanation No. Early ventricular contraction raises intraventricular pressure while both the AV and semilunar valves are closed. This is isovolumetric contraction . Ejection starts only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the pulmonary artery or aorta and opens the corresponding outflow valve. 5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also contracting? Explain the timing. Reveal explanation Normally, no. Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole. The AV nodal conduction delay helps ensure atrial contraction contributes its final portion of ventricular filling before ventricular systole starts. Most filling has already occurred passively. Chapter Essentials Systemic and pulmonary circulations operate in series: long-term right and left ventricular outputs must be approximately equal. Arteries and veins: defined by direction of blood movement relative to the heart, not oxygenation. Left versus right ventricle: similar flow, different pressure loads; the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall. Systole and diastole: contraction and relaxation are coordinated but differ between atria and ventricles. Atrial systole: normally completes ventricular filling near the end of ventricular diastole. Electrical activation precedes mechanical contraction: the conducting system coordinates timing, while myocardium supplies force. Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters."},{"slug":"cardiac-muscle","title":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"4 and 18-20; added foundations","coverage":"supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different","title":"1. What makes cardiac muscle different?"},{"id":"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together","title":"2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together"},{"id":"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential","title":"3. The cardiac muscle action potential"},{"id":"4-excitationcontraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force","title":"4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force"},{"id":"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax","title":"5. How does cardiac muscle relax?"},{"id":"6-why-cant-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus","title":"6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?"},{"id":"7-how-does-sympathetic-stimulation-affect-cardiac-muscle","title":"7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle?"},{"id":"8-connecting-cardiac-muscle-physiology-to-the-pumping-heart","title":"8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-muscle-physiology\">Cardiac Muscle Physiology</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf — slides 4, 18 and 20. <strong>Essential supplementary physiology:</strong> cellular structure, action potentials, calcium handling and sympathetic effects.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different\">1. What makes cardiac muscle different?</h2>\n<p>The heart must contract repeatedly throughout life, generating enough force to circulate blood while allowing sufficient time for its chambers to refill between beats. Its cells must respond to electrical stimulation, communicate with neighbouring cells and relax effectively after each contraction.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle is <strong>striated and involuntary</strong>, with short, branched cells that typically contain one centrally located nucleus. Like skeletal muscle, its contractile proteins are organised into sarcomeres containing actin and myosin.</p>\n<p>However, cardiac muscle has several distinctive features:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Intercalated discs</strong> connect neighbouring cardiomyocytes mechanically and electrically.</li><li><strong>Prolonged action potentials and refractory periods</strong> prevent normal tetanic contraction.</li><li><strong>Calcium-induced calcium release</strong> links electrical excitation to contraction.</li><li><strong>Numerous mitochondria</strong> support its continuous demand for ATP.</li></ul>\n<p>Cardiac muscle exhibits excitability, conductivity and contractility. Automaticity—the ability to generate impulses spontaneously—is particularly important in specialised pacemaker cells rather than every working ventricular myocyte.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-intercalated-discs-how-separate-cells-function-together\">2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together</h2>\n<p>A single cardiac muscle cell cannot pump blood effectively in isolation. To generate coordinated force, neighbouring cells must remain firmly connected and transmit electrical signals to one another.</p>\n<p>These connections occur at <strong>intercalated discs</strong>, which contain three important junctional structures.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fascia adherens</strong></td><td>Anchor actin filaments and transmit contractile force between cells.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Desmosomes</strong></td><td>Hold neighbouring cells together, resisting separation during repeated contractions.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Gap junctions</strong></td><td>Permit ionic current to pass between cells, allowing electrical excitation to spread.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>When one cardiomyocyte becomes electrically excited, local current passes through gap junctions to neighbouring cells, bringing their membranes towards threshold and propagating the action potential. Fascia adherens and desmosomes maintain mechanical continuity as those cells contract.</p>\n<p>Thus, cardiac muscle behaves as a <strong><span data-term=\"functional-syncytium\">functional syncytium</span></strong>: many separate cells act together as a coordinated unit. The atria and ventricles form two electrically distinct functional syncytia. Their usual connection is through the specialised atrioventricular conducting pathway, allowing atrial contraction to precede ventricular contraction.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7e/1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" alt=\"Branched cardiac muscle fibres and an enlarged intercalated disc showing desmosomes and gap junctions.\" width=\"1018\" height=\"476\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs.</strong> Desmosomes and gap junctions are labelled; fascia adherens are discussed in the text but not separately labelled. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1020_Cardiac_Muscle.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cardiac-muscle-action-potential\">3. The cardiac muscle action potential</h2>\n<p>For a ventricular cardiomyocyte to contract, its membrane must first undergo an <strong>action potential</strong>—a temporary change in membrane potential resulting from ionic movements.</p>\n<p>Unlike the brief action potential of skeletal muscle, a ventricular action potential lasts approximately <strong>200–300 milliseconds</strong> and has a prolonged plateau. Its resting membrane potential is approximately <strong>−90 mV</strong>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Main electrical event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Phase 0 — Rapid depolarisation</strong></td><td>Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing rapid Na⁺ influx.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 1 — Initial repolarisation</strong></td><td>Fast Na⁺ channels inactivate, while transient outward K⁺ current produces a brief fall in membrane potential.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 2 — Plateau</strong></td><td>Inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels partly balances outward K⁺ current.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 3 — Repolarisation</strong></td><td>Ca²⁺ entry declines, and outward K⁺ currents restore the negative membrane potential.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Phase 4 — Resting phase</strong></td><td>A relatively stable membrane potential is maintained largely by resting K⁺ conductance.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-the-plateau-occur\">Why does the plateau occur?</h3>\n<p>During phase 2, Ca²⁺ entering the cell tends to maintain depolarisation while K⁺ leaving the cell tends to repolarise it. Because these currents partly balance one another, membrane potential changes relatively slowly. The resulting plateau prolongs the action potential and refractory period, while the Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels also helps trigger contraction.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/26/2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" alt=\"Ventricular action potential and refractory period with a comparison of skeletal and cardiac muscle electrical and mechanical activity.\" width=\"1837\" height=\"2019\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction.</strong> Compare the long cardiac refractory period with the mechanical twitch; the graph does not separately number all five phases. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2026_Action_Potential_Heart_Contraction.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> This is a ventricular <em>contractile-cell</em> action potential. Sinoatrial nodal pacemaker cells have no stable resting membrane potential, and their phase 0 upstroke relies primarily on Ca²⁺ entry rather than fast Na⁺ entry. Their behaviour belongs in the Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity chapter.</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=v7Q9BrNfIpQ\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"4-excitationcontraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force</h2>\n<p>An action potential must initiate changes inside the cardiomyocyte that activate the contractile proteins <strong>(actin and myosin)</strong>. This is <strong><span data-term=\"ecc\">excitation–contraction coupling (ECC)</span></strong>, and its essential link is <strong>Ca²⁺</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The action potential reaches the T-tubules.</strong> Electrical excitation spreads along the sarcolemma and into T-tubules, carrying depolarisation into the cell.</li><li><strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels open.</strong> Depolarisation permits a small influx of extracellular Ca²⁺ through L-type channels.</li><li><strong>Ca²⁺ triggers further release.</strong> The incoming Ca²⁺ activates <strong>RyR2 (Ryanodine Receptor Type 2)</strong> channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), releasing much more stored Ca²⁺. This is <strong><span data-term=\"cicr\">CICR (Calcium-Induced Calcium Release)</span></strong>. Entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger; the SR supplies much of the Ca²⁺ that activates contraction.</li><li><strong>Ca²⁺ binds troponin C.</strong> Tropomyosin moves away from the myosin-binding sites on actin.</li><li><strong>Actin–myosin cross-bridge cycling produces force.</strong> Myosin interacts with actin using energy from ATP hydrolysis, shortening sarcomeres and generating contraction.</li></ol>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs02-figure-03\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/10/Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" alt=\"Diagram showing cardiac T-tubule L-type calcium channels, ryanodine receptor, sodium–calcium exchanger, sarcoplasmic reticulum, SERCA and phospholamban.\" width=\"756\" height=\"567\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling.</strong> L-type channels, RyR, NCX, SERCA and phospholamban are shown; troponin C and actin–myosin interactions are explained in the adjacent text. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac_calcium_cycling_and_excitation-contraction_coupling.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PeaBrainC</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"why-is-extracellular-ca-especially-important-in-cardiac-muscle\">Why is extracellular Ca²⁺ especially important in cardiac muscle?</h3>\n<p>In skeletal muscle, an action potential activates voltage-sensitive proteins in the T-tubule membrane. These proteins are physically connected to Ca²⁺-release channels on the SR, so activation directly opens those channels and releases stored Ca²⁺. In other words, skeletal muscle does not first require Ca²⁺ to enter from outside to trigger SR release.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle works differently: extracellular Ca²⁺ must enter through L-type channels first, and that incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 channels to release more Ca²⁺ from the SR. <strong>Skeletal muscle uses direct mechanical coupling; cardiac muscle uses CICR.</strong> Reducing Ca²⁺ entry into cardiac muscle therefore generally weakens contraction, even if the SR still contains stored Ca²⁺.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-cardiac-muscle-relax\">5. How does cardiac muscle relax?</h2>\n<p>The myocardium must relax after each contraction so its chambers can refill. Cytosolic Ca²⁺ must fall: Ca²⁺ dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin again limits myosin-binding sites on actin, and interactions between the contractile proteins <strong>(actin and myosin)</strong> decrease.</p>\n<p>Two major mechanisms remove cytosolic Ca²⁺:</p>\n<ul><li><strong><span data-term=\"serca\">SERCA2a (Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase, isoform 2a)</span>:</strong> Uses ATP to pump Ca²⁺ back into the SR for storage and reuse.</li><li><strong><span data-term=\"ncx\">NCX (Na⁺–Ca²⁺ Exchanger)</span>:</strong> Normally uses the Na⁺ gradient to extrude Ca²⁺ from the cell.</li></ul>\n<p>Other pathways, including the plasma membrane Ca²⁺ ATPase, make smaller contributions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-impaired-serca2a-activity-matter\">Why does impaired SERCA2a activity matter?</h3>\n<p><strong>Slower relaxation:</strong> When Ca²⁺ is returned to the SR more slowly, cytosolic Ca²⁺ remains elevated longer, delaying relaxation and potentially impairing ventricular filling.</p>\n<p><strong>Potentially weaker later contractions:</strong> Less Ca²⁺ may be stored in the SR for the next heartbeat. Consequently, less Ca²⁺ may be released to activate actin–myosin interactions on subsequent beats.</p>\n<p>Effective pumping therefore depends on <strong>both strong contraction and efficient relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-cant-cardiac-muscle-undergo-normal-tetanus\">6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus?</h2>\n<p>Skeletal muscle can undergo sustained tetanic contraction because its electrical refractory period is much shorter than its mechanical twitch. Repeated stimuli can arrive while force is still being generated.</p>\n<p>Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action potential and <strong>long refractory period</strong> that overlaps most of its contraction. It cannot normally be re-excited rapidly enough for skeletal-type fused tetanus.</p>\n<p>This protects the heart's rhythmic pumping: if the ventricles stayed contracted continuously, they could not relax, refill and eject blood in successive beats. See Figure 02 for the electrical and mechanical timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-sympathetic-stimulation-affect-cardiac-muscle\">7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle?</h2>\n<p>During exercise or stress, the heart may need to beat faster, contract more strongly and relax more quickly.</p>\n<p>Noradrenaline and adrenaline stimulate <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> on cardiomyocytes:</p>\n<p><strong>Noradrenaline/adrenaline → β₁ receptor → Gs protein → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) → activation of PKA (Protein Kinase A).</strong></p>\n<p>PKA phosphorylates proteins involved in Ca²⁺ handling, leading to two particularly important effects:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Stronger contraction:</strong> Enhanced L-type Ca²⁺ channel activity increases trigger Ca²⁺ entry, promoting greater SR Ca²⁺ release and stronger actin–myosin interaction.</li><li><strong>Faster relaxation:</strong> PKA phosphorylation of <strong><span data-term=\"phospholamban\">phospholamban</span></strong> reduces its usual inhibition of SERCA2a, allowing Ca²⁺ to return to the SR more rapidly.</li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"inotropy-lusitropy-and-chronotropy\">Inotropy, lusitropy and chronotropy</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it describes</th><th>Positive effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">Inotropy</span></strong></td><td>Myocardial contractile strength</td><td>Stronger contraction</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">Lusitropy</span></strong></td><td>Rate of myocardial relaxation</td><td>Faster relaxation</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span></strong></td><td>Heart rate</td><td>Faster heart rate</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Increased Ca²⁺ availability enhances inotropy, while faster SR Ca²⁺ reuptake improves lusitropy. Sympathetic stimulation of pacemaker cells also increases chronotropy. Together these help the heart meet increased circulatory demands.</p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> Increased inotropy is improved intrinsic contractile ability under comparable loading conditions—not simply stronger contraction because the ventricle contains more blood. This becomes important when studying the Frank–Starling mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-connecting-cardiac-muscle-physiology-to-the-pumping-heart\">8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart</h2>\n<h3 id=\"why-doesnt-ventricular-contraction-immediately-mean-ejection\">Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately mean ejection?</h3>\n<p>Electrical activation initiates contraction, but ventricular pressure must first exceed pressure in the corresponding outflow artery before blood can be ejected. The lecturer notes that right ventricular ejection can begin before left ventricular ejection because pressure in the pulmonary artery is considerably lower than in the aorta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, <strong>the onset of contraction and the onset of ejection are not necessarily simultaneous</strong>. The detailed pressure changes belong in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-happens-to-diastole-when-heart-rate-increases\">What happens to diastole when heart rate increases?</h3>\n<p>As heart rate rises, each cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Both systole and diastole can shorten, but <strong>diastole usually shortens proportionately more</strong>. This matters because ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion occur during diastole.</p>\n<p>Moderate increases in heart rate are accommodated by adjustments such as greater contractility and faster relaxation. Excessive tachycardia, however, can reduce filling and myocardial perfusion, so beating more often does not always increase the volume pumped per minute.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A cardiomyocyte contracts normally, but neighbouring cells fail to activate together. Which intercalated-disc component is most likely affected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Gap junctions.</strong> They form low-resistance pathways for ionic current to pass directly between neighbouring cardiomyocytes. If this electrical coupling is disrupted, an action potential may not spread reliably, so contractions lose coordination. <strong>Fascia adherens and desmosomes</strong> instead mainly maintain mechanical attachment and transmission of force.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Which ion produces the rapid ventricular phase 0 upstroke, and how is this different from the phase 2 plateau?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Phase 0:</strong> fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing a large inward Na⁺ current and rapid depolarisation. <strong>Phase 2:</strong> a smaller, sustained inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels is roughly balanced by outward K⁺ current, maintaining the plateau. The plateau prolongs electrical activity and helps support Ca²⁺-dependent contraction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. What happens if RyR2 channels fail to open despite normal Ca²⁺ entry through L-type channels?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Contraction becomes much weaker.</strong> Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels is primarily the trigger for opening ryanodine receptor type 2 (RyR2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Without that amplified Ca²⁺ release, the cytosolic Ca²⁺ rise is too small to activate the normal number of actin–myosin interactions.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Explain how impaired SERCA2a can delay relaxation and weaken later contractions.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Relaxation is delayed</strong> because SERCA2a normally pumps cytosolic Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). If its activity falls, Ca²⁺ remains elevated in the cytosol longer, delaying detachment of Ca²⁺ from troponin. Reduced SR refilling can also leave less Ca²⁺ available for release on later beats, potentially reducing force.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why can skeletal muscle undergo tetanus while cardiac muscle normally cannot?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Skeletal muscle can summate contractions</strong> because its refractory period ends well before a twitch finishes; repeated stimuli can then produce fused tetanus. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action-potential plateau and refractory period that overlaps most of contraction, preventing a new propagated action potential soon enough for normal tetanic summation. This preserves time for relaxation and filling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. How can sympathetic stimulation increase contractile strength and speed of relaxation at the same time?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>β₁-receptor activation → cAMP → protein kinase A (PKA).</strong> PKA promotes L-type Ca²⁺ entry and larger SR Ca²⁺ release, producing a stronger contraction (<em>positive inotropy</em>). PKA also phosphorylates phospholamban, relieving its inhibition of SERCA2a so Ca²⁺ returns to the SR faster (<em>positive lusitropy</em>). The same signal therefore supports both stronger contraction and faster relaxation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why can extreme tachycardia lower cardiac output despite a faster heart rate?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac output equals <strong>heart rate × stroke volume (CO = HR × SV)</strong>. Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole substantially, leaving less time for ventricular filling. This can lower end-diastolic volume and stroke volume. If stroke volume decreases proportionally more than heart rate increases, cardiac output falls despite the faster rhythm.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Intercalated discs:</strong> Fascia adherens and desmosomes provide mechanical coupling; gap junctions enable electrical communication.</li><li><strong>Functional syncytium:</strong> Separate cardiomyocytes act as coordinated electrical units; the atria and ventricles form distinct functional syncytia.</li><li><strong>Ventricular action potential:</strong> Phase 0—Na⁺ influx; phase 2—Ca²⁺ influx balanced by K⁺ efflux; phase 3—mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation.</li><li><strong>Excitation–contraction coupling:</strong> L-type Ca²⁺ entry triggers RyR2-mediated SR release; Ca²⁺ binds troponin C to permit actin–myosin interaction.</li><li><strong>Relaxation:</strong> SERCA2a returns Ca²⁺ to the SR; NCX removes Ca²⁺ from the cell.</li><li><strong>Refractoriness:</strong> The prolonged refractory period prevents normal tetanic contraction and preserves rhythmic pumping.</li><li><strong>Sympathetic effects:</strong> β₁–cAMP–PKA signalling increases contractile strength and speeds relaxation.</li><li><strong>Key terms:</strong> Inotropy is contractile strength; lusitropy is relaxation rate; chronotropy is heart rate.</li><li><strong>Fast heart rates:</strong> Diastole shortens disproportionately, and excessive tachycardia may compromise ventricular filling and coronary perfusion.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf — slides 4, 18 and 20. Essential supplementary physiology: cellular structure, action potentials, calcium handling and sympathetic effects. 1. What makes cardiac muscle different? The heart must contract repeatedly throughout life, generating enough force to circulate blood while allowing sufficient time for its chambers to refill between beats. Its cells must respond to electrical stimulation, communicate with neighbouring cells and relax effectively after each contraction. Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary , with short, branched cells that typically contain one centrally located nucleus. Like skeletal muscle, its contractile proteins are organised into sarcomeres containing actin and myosin. However, cardiac muscle has several distinctive features: Intercalated discs connect neighbouring cardiomyocytes mechanically and electrically. Prolonged action potentials and refractory periods prevent normal tetanic contraction. Calcium-induced calcium release links electrical excitation to contraction. Numerous mitochondria support its continuous demand for ATP. Cardiac muscle exhibits excitability, conductivity and contractility. Automaticity—the ability to generate impulses spontaneously—is particularly important in specialised pacemaker cells rather than every working ventricular myocyte. 2. Intercalated discs: how separate cells function together A single cardiac muscle cell cannot pump blood effectively in isolation. To generate coordinated force, neighbouring cells must remain firmly connected and transmit electrical signals to one another. These connections occur at intercalated discs , which contain three important junctional structures. Structure Role Fascia adherens Anchor actin filaments and transmit contractile force between cells. Desmosomes Hold neighbouring cells together, resisting separation during repeated contractions. Gap junctions Permit ionic current to pass between cells, allowing electrical excitation to spread. When one cardiomyocyte becomes electrically excited, local current passes through gap junctions to neighbouring cells, bringing their membranes towards threshold and propagating the action potential. Fascia adherens and desmosomes maintain mechanical continuity as those cells contract. Thus, cardiac muscle behaves as a functional syncytium : many separate cells act together as a coordinated unit. The atria and ventricles form two electrically distinct functional syncytia. Their usual connection is through the specialised atrioventricular conducting pathway, allowing atrial contraction to precede ventricular contraction. Figure 01. Cardiac muscle and intercalated discs. Desmosomes and gap junctions are labelled; fascia adherens are discussed in the text but not separately labelled. Image: OpenStax · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 3. The cardiac muscle action potential For a ventricular cardiomyocyte to contract, its membrane must first undergo an action potential —a temporary change in membrane potential resulting from ionic movements. Unlike the brief action potential of skeletal muscle, a ventricular action potential lasts approximately 200–300 milliseconds and has a prolonged plateau. Its resting membrane potential is approximately −90 mV . Phase Main electrical event Phase 0 — Rapid depolarisation Fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing rapid Na⁺ influx. Phase 1 — Initial repolarisation Fast Na⁺ channels inactivate, while transient outward K⁺ current produces a brief fall in membrane potential. Phase 2 — Plateau Inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels partly balances outward K⁺ current. Phase 3 — Repolarisation Ca²⁺ entry declines, and outward K⁺ currents restore the negative membrane potential. Phase 4 — Resting phase A relatively stable membrane potential is maintained largely by resting K⁺ conductance. Why does the plateau occur? During phase 2, Ca²⁺ entering the cell tends to maintain depolarisation while K⁺ leaving the cell tends to repolarise it. Because these currents partly balance one another, membrane potential changes relatively slowly. The resulting plateau prolongs the action potential and refractory period, while the Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels also helps trigger contraction. Figure 02. Electrical activity, refractoriness and contraction. Compare the long cardiac refractory period with the mechanical twitch; the graph does not separately number all five phases. Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. Important distinction: This is a ventricular contractile-cell action potential. Sinoatrial nodal pacemaker cells have no stable resting membrane potential, and their phase 0 upstroke relies primarily on Ca²⁺ entry rather than fast Na⁺ entry. Their behaviour belongs in the Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity chapter. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Action Potential — Alila Medical Media Revisit the ventricular action potential: identify the fast Na⁺ upstroke, Ca²⁺-dependent plateau and prolonged refractory period. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. 4. Excitation–contraction coupling: from electrical signal to force An action potential must initiate changes inside the cardiomyocyte that activate the contractile proteins (actin and myosin) . This is excitation–contraction coupling (ECC) , and its essential link is Ca²⁺ . The action potential reaches the T-tubules. Electrical excitation spreads along the sarcolemma and into T-tubules, carrying depolarisation into the cell. L-type Ca²⁺ channels open. Depolarisation permits a small influx of extracellular Ca²⁺ through L-type channels. Ca²⁺ triggers further release. The incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 (Ryanodine Receptor Type 2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR), releasing much more stored Ca²⁺. This is CICR (Calcium-Induced Calcium Release) . Entering Ca²⁺ is the trigger; the SR supplies much of the Ca²⁺ that activates contraction. Ca²⁺ binds troponin C. Tropomyosin moves away from the myosin-binding sites on actin. Actin–myosin cross-bridge cycling produces force. Myosin interacts with actin using energy from ATP hydrolysis, shortening sarcomeres and generating contraction. Figure 03. Cardiac calcium cycling. L-type channels, RyR, NCX, SERCA and phospholamban are shown; troponin C and actin–myosin interactions are explained in the adjacent text. Image: PeaBrainC · CC BY-SA 4.0 · unmodified. Why is extracellular Ca²⁺ especially important in cardiac muscle? In skeletal muscle, an action potential activates voltage-sensitive proteins in the T-tubule membrane. These proteins are physically connected to Ca²⁺-release channels on the SR, so activation directly opens those channels and releases stored Ca²⁺. In other words, skeletal muscle does not first require Ca²⁺ to enter from outside to trigger SR release. Cardiac muscle works differently: extracellular Ca²⁺ must enter through L-type channels first, and that incoming Ca²⁺ activates RyR2 channels to release more Ca²⁺ from the SR. Skeletal muscle uses direct mechanical coupling; cardiac muscle uses CICR. Reducing Ca²⁺ entry into cardiac muscle therefore generally weakens contraction, even if the SR still contains stored Ca²⁺. 5. How does cardiac muscle relax? The myocardium must relax after each contraction so its chambers can refill. Cytosolic Ca²⁺ must fall: Ca²⁺ dissociates from troponin C, tropomyosin again limits myosin-binding sites on actin, and interactions between the contractile proteins (actin and myosin) decrease. Two major mechanisms remove cytosolic Ca²⁺: SERCA2a (Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase, isoform 2a) : Uses ATP to pump Ca²⁺ back into the SR for storage and reuse. NCX (Na⁺–Ca²⁺ Exchanger) : Normally uses the Na⁺ gradient to extrude Ca²⁺ from the cell. Other pathways, including the plasma membrane Ca²⁺ ATPase, make smaller contributions. Why does impaired SERCA2a activity matter? Slower relaxation: When Ca²⁺ is returned to the SR more slowly, cytosolic Ca²⁺ remains elevated longer, delaying relaxation and potentially impairing ventricular filling. Potentially weaker later contractions: Less Ca²⁺ may be stored in the SR for the next heartbeat. Consequently, less Ca²⁺ may be released to activate actin–myosin interactions on subsequent beats. Effective pumping therefore depends on both strong contraction and efficient relaxation . 6. Why can't cardiac muscle undergo normal tetanus? Skeletal muscle can undergo sustained tetanic contraction because its electrical refractory period is much shorter than its mechanical twitch. Repeated stimuli can arrive while force is still being generated. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action potential and long refractory period that overlaps most of its contraction. It cannot normally be re-excited rapidly enough for skeletal-type fused tetanus. This protects the heart's rhythmic pumping: if the ventricles stayed contracted continuously, they could not relax, refill and eject blood in successive beats. See Figure 02 for the electrical and mechanical timing. 7. How does sympathetic stimulation affect cardiac muscle? During exercise or stress, the heart may need to beat faster, contract more strongly and relax more quickly. Noradrenaline and adrenaline stimulate β₁-adrenergic receptors on cardiomyocytes: Noradrenaline/adrenaline → β₁ receptor → Gs protein → adenylyl cyclase → increased cAMP (cyclic adenosine monophosphate) → activation of PKA (Protein Kinase A). PKA phosphorylates proteins involved in Ca²⁺ handling, leading to two particularly important effects: Stronger contraction: Enhanced L-type Ca²⁺ channel activity increases trigger Ca²⁺ entry, promoting greater SR Ca²⁺ release and stronger actin–myosin interaction. Faster relaxation: PKA phosphorylation of phospholamban reduces its usual inhibition of SERCA2a, allowing Ca²⁺ to return to the SR more rapidly. Inotropy, lusitropy and chronotropy Term What it describes Positive effect Inotropy Myocardial contractile strength Stronger contraction Lusitropy Rate of myocardial relaxation Faster relaxation Chronotropy Heart rate Faster heart rate Increased Ca²⁺ availability enhances inotropy, while faster SR Ca²⁺ reuptake improves lusitropy. Sympathetic stimulation of pacemaker cells also increases chronotropy. Together these help the heart meet increased circulatory demands. Important distinction: Increased inotropy is improved intrinsic contractile ability under comparable loading conditions—not simply stronger contraction because the ventricle contains more blood. This becomes important when studying the Frank–Starling mechanism. 8. Connecting cardiac muscle physiology to the pumping heart Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately mean ejection? Electrical activation initiates contraction, but ventricular pressure must first exceed pressure in the corresponding outflow artery before blood can be ejected. The lecturer notes that right ventricular ejection can begin before left ventricular ejection because pressure in the pulmonary artery is considerably lower than in the aorta. Therefore, the onset of contraction and the onset of ejection are not necessarily simultaneous . The detailed pressure changes belong in the Cardiac Cycle chapter. What happens to diastole when heart rate increases? As heart rate rises, each cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Both systole and diastole can shorten, but diastole usually shortens proportionately more . This matters because ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion occur during diastole. Moderate increases in heart rate are accommodated by adjustments such as greater contractility and faster relaxation. Excessive tachycardia, however, can reduce filling and myocardial perfusion, so beating more often does not always increase the volume pumped per minute. Understanding Check 1. A cardiomyocyte contracts normally, but neighbouring cells fail to activate together. Which intercalated-disc component is most likely affected? Reveal explanation Gap junctions. They form low-resistance pathways for ionic current to pass directly between neighbouring cardiomyocytes. If this electrical coupling is disrupted, an action potential may not spread reliably, so contractions lose coordination. Fascia adherens and desmosomes instead mainly maintain mechanical attachment and transmission of force. 2. Which ion produces the rapid ventricular phase 0 upstroke, and how is this different from the phase 2 plateau? Reveal explanation Phase 0: fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open, producing a large inward Na⁺ current and rapid depolarisation. Phase 2: a smaller, sustained inward Ca²⁺ current through L-type channels is roughly balanced by outward K⁺ current, maintaining the plateau. The plateau prolongs electrical activity and helps support Ca²⁺-dependent contraction. 3. What happens if RyR2 channels fail to open despite normal Ca²⁺ entry through L-type channels? Reveal explanation Contraction becomes much weaker. Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels is primarily the trigger for opening ryanodine receptor type 2 (RyR2) channels on the sarcoplasmic reticulum. Without that amplified Ca²⁺ release, the cytosolic Ca²⁺ rise is too small to activate the normal number of actin–myosin interactions. 4. Explain how impaired SERCA2a can delay relaxation and weaken later contractions. Reveal explanation Relaxation is delayed because SERCA2a normally pumps cytosolic Ca²⁺ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR). If its activity falls, Ca²⁺ remains elevated in the cytosol longer, delaying detachment of Ca²⁺ from troponin. Reduced SR refilling can also leave less Ca²⁺ available for release on later beats, potentially reducing force. 5. Why can skeletal muscle undergo tetanus while cardiac muscle normally cannot? Reveal explanation Skeletal muscle can summate contractions because its refractory period ends well before a twitch finishes; repeated stimuli can then produce fused tetanus. Cardiac muscle has a prolonged action-potential plateau and refractory period that overlaps most of contraction, preventing a new propagated action potential soon enough for normal tetanic summation. This preserves time for relaxation and filling. 6. How can sympathetic stimulation increase contractile strength and speed of relaxation at the same time? Reveal explanation β₁-receptor activation → cAMP → protein kinase A (PKA). PKA promotes L-type Ca²⁺ entry and larger SR Ca²⁺ release, producing a stronger contraction ( positive inotropy ). PKA also phosphorylates phospholamban, relieving its inhibition of SERCA2a so Ca²⁺ returns to the SR faster ( positive lusitropy ). The same signal therefore supports both stronger contraction and faster relaxation. 7. Why can extreme tachycardia lower cardiac output despite a faster heart rate? Reveal explanation Cardiac output equals heart rate × stroke volume (CO = HR × SV) . Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole substantially, leaving less time for ventricular filling. This can lower end-diastolic volume and stroke volume. If stroke volume decreases proportionally more than heart rate increases, cardiac output falls despite the faster rhythm. Chapter Essentials Intercalated discs: Fascia adherens and desmosomes provide mechanical coupling; gap junctions enable electrical communication. Functional syncytium: Separate cardiomyocytes act as coordinated electrical units; the atria and ventricles form distinct functional syncytia. Ventricular action potential: Phase 0—Na⁺ influx; phase 2—Ca²⁺ influx balanced by K⁺ efflux; phase 3—mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation. Excitation–contraction coupling: L-type Ca²⁺ entry triggers RyR2-mediated SR release; Ca²⁺ binds troponin C to permit actin–myosin interaction. Relaxation: SERCA2a returns Ca²⁺ to the SR; NCX removes Ca²⁺ from the cell. Refractoriness: The prolonged refractory period prevents normal tetanic contraction and preserves rhythmic pumping. Sympathetic effects: β₁–cAMP–PKA signalling increases contractile strength and speeds relaxation. Key terms: Inotropy is contractile strength; lusitropy is relaxation rate; chronotropy is heart rate. Fast heart rates: Diastole shortens disproportionately, and excessive tachycardia may compromise ventricular filling and coronary perfusion."},{"slug":"cardiac-conduction","title":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"7-10","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system","title":"1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?"},{"id":"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction","title":"2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker","title":"3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?"},{"id":"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesnt-each-set-its-own-rhythm","title":"4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?"},{"id":"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction","title":"5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?"},{"id":"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid","title":"6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?"},{"id":"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity","title":"7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?"},{"id":"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances","title":"8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-conduction-and-automaticity\">Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 7–10. <strong>Supplementary foundations:</strong> pacemaker action potentials, autonomic modulation and clinical consequences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-need-a-conducting-system\">1. Why does the heart need a conducting system?</h2>\n<p>The heart has four chambers, but efficient pumping requires more than all four chambers contracting: <strong>they must contract in the correct sequence</strong>.</p>\n<p>The atria must be electrically activated before the ventricles so atrial contraction can complete the final portion of ventricular filling. The ventricles must then be activated in a coordinated manner to generate pressure for blood ejection.</p>\n<p>The <strong>specialised cardiac conducting system</strong> generates and distributes electrical impulses through the myocardium. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a new motor nerve impulse to initiate every beat. Specialised cardiac cells can spontaneously generate electrical impulses—<strong><span data-term=\"automaticity\">automaticity</span></strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-pathway-of-cardiac-electrical-conduction\">2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction</h2>\n<p><strong>SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium.</strong></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Location or function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sinoatrial (SA) node</strong></td><td>Near the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium; normally initiates each heartbeat.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Atrial conducting pathways</strong></td><td>Distribute excitation through both atria and towards the AV node.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Atrioventricular (AV) node</strong></td><td>Lower right atrial septal region, near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid leaflet; slows conduction before ventricular activation.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bundle of His</strong></td><td>Conducts impulses from the AV node into the interventricular septum.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Right and left bundle branches</strong></td><td>Carry excitation towards their respective ventricles.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Purkinje fibres</strong></td><td>Rapidly distribute excitation through the ventricular myocardium.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-01\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/ff/2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Sectioned heart showing the SA node, atrial pathways including Bachmann's bundle, AV node, His bundle, bundle branches and Purkinje fibres.\" width=\"2021\" height=\"1354\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system.</strong> Follow the impulse from the right atrial SA node into the His–Purkinje network. The three internodal pathways shown are traditional teaching descriptions, not necessarily three separately insulated bundles. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018_Conduction_System_of_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">OpenStax College</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"what-about-the-internodal-pathways\">What about the internodal pathways?</h3>\n<p>The lecture names three traditional internodal pathways: <strong>anterior</strong> (associated with Bachmann's bundle), <strong>middle</strong> (Wenckebach) and <strong>posterior</strong> (Thorel). These names help follow the lecturer's slides. However, the atria do not necessarily contain three separate, insulated conducting bundles corresponding exactly to these pathways; excitation also spreads through ordinary atrial myocardium. <strong>Bachmann's bundle</strong> is particularly important for spreading excitation from the right to the left atrium, allowing the atria to be activated close together in time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-sa-node-the-normal-pacemaker\">3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker?</h2>\n<p>The SA node normally sets the rhythm because its cells spontaneously reach threshold faster than other potential pacemaker regions.</p>\n<p>An ordinary ventricular contractile cell has a relatively stable resting membrane potential and usually requires an incoming impulse for a new action potential. <strong>SA nodal cells lack a stable resting membrane potential</strong>: after repolarisation, the membrane gradually becomes less negative until it reaches threshold. This gradual rise is <strong><span data-term=\"pacemaker-potential\">spontaneous diastolic depolarisation (the pacemaker potential)</span></strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"how-does-the-sa-nodal-action-potential-occur\">How does the SA nodal action potential occur?</h3>\n<p><strong>Phase 4 — Spontaneous diastolic depolarisation:</strong> After repolarisation, the negative SA nodal membrane potential activates <strong>HCN (hyperpolarisation-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated) channels</strong>. These carry the <strong><span data-term=\"funny-current\">funny current (I_f)</span></strong>. Although hyperpolarisation <em>opens</em> the channels, the current they produce at typical SA nodal potentials is net inward, bringing positive charge into the cell and gradually moving membrane potential towards threshold. A reduction in outward K⁺ current and inward Ca²⁺ currents also contribute.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 0 — Depolarisation:</strong> On reaching threshold, mainly <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> open. This differs from working ventricular cardiomyocytes, whose rapid phase 0 depends primarily on fast Na⁺ channels.</p>\n<p><strong>Phase 3 — Repolarisation:</strong> Ca²⁺ channels inactivate and outward K⁺ currents restore a negative membrane potential. The next pacemaker cycle can then begin.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs03-figure-02\">\n<a href=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://www.mdpi.com/cells/cells-13-00004/article_deploy/html/images/cells-13-00004-g001.png\" alt=\"Side-by-side SA nodal pacemaker and ventricular cardiomyocyte action potentials: spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation and calcium upstroke in nodal cells contrasted with sodium upstroke and plateau in ventricular cells, above an ECG.\" width=\"1500\" height=\"1125\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials.</strong> Compare the spontaneously rising phase 4 of the SA node with the stable resting potential, fast phase 0 and phase 2 plateau of the ventricular cardiomyocyte. The lower ECG trace is supplementary and will be explained later. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Image: <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.3390/cells13010004\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Jonker, Barnett, Boink &amp; Christoffels, <em>Cells</em> (2024), Figure 1</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 4.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"4-if-other-conducting-cells-can-fire-spontaneously-why-doesnt-each-set-its-own-rhythm\">4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm?</h2>\n<p>The SA node is not the only region with automaticity. Other specialised conducting cells can also generate impulses, but their inherent rates are generally slower.</p>\n<p>The SA node normally reaches threshold <strong>before</strong> these slower pacemaker regions. When an impulse reaches them, it activates and resets their electrical activity, preventing them from independently determining the rhythm. <strong>The SA node dominates because it normally fires faster, not because the other cells have lost automaticity.</strong></p>\n<p>A related mechanism is <strong><span data-term=\"overdrive-suppression\">overdrive suppression</span></strong>, in which sustained faster activation suppresses subsidiary automaticity, sometimes delaying an escape rhythm after faster pacing stops. Resetting by incoming impulses and overdrive suppression are related but not identical.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-if-the-sa-node-fails\">What if the SA node fails?</h3>\n<p>A slower subsidiary pacemaker may take over:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pacemaker region</th><th>Typical rate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SA node</td><td>Normal adult resting sinus rate: 60–100 beats/min</td></tr><tr><td>AV junction</td><td>Junctional escape rhythm: approximately 40–60 beats/min</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pacemaker</td><td>Ventricular escape rhythm: approximately 20–40 beats/min</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These ranges are guides, not fixed values. A subsidiary pacemaker can preserve ventricular activity if the normal pacemaker or its conduction fails, but slower rate and altered coordination may reduce pumping efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-the-av-node-delay-electrical-conduction\">5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction?</h2>\n<p>The AV node conducts relatively slowly, partly because nodal action potential upstrokes are Ca²⁺-dependent and cell-to-cell conduction is less rapid than in the His–Purkinje system.</p>\n<p>Why is the delay useful? <strong>The atria must complete their contraction before the ventricles contract.</strong> After atrial activation, atrial contraction pushes the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The AV node briefly delays the impulse so this contribution to ventricular filling can occur before ventricular systole begins. Without an appropriate delay, excessive overlap of atrial and ventricular contractions could reduce the effectiveness of atrial contribution.</p>\n<p>Importantly, <strong>most ventricular filling occurs passively</strong> before atrial systole. Atrial contraction normally contributes the remaining portion; it does not provide most of the blood entering the ventricles.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-doesnt-the-impulse-simply-spread-from-atria-to-ventricles\">Why doesn't the impulse simply spread from atria to ventricles?</h3>\n<p>The cardiac fibrous skeleton electrically insulates most atrial myocardium from ventricular myocardium. Under normal conditions, impulses cross via the specialised AV conducting pathway, preserving the atrial-to-ventricular activation sequence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-ventricular-conduction-so-rapid\">6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid?</h2>\n<p>After the AV node, the impulse enters the <strong>His–Purkinje system</strong>, where rapid Na⁺-dependent depolarisation and effective cell-to-cell coupling permit fast conduction. The Purkinje network distributes excitation widely through the ventricles so large regions of myocardium become activated within a short period. This lets the ventricles develop pressure and eject blood efficiently.</p>\n<p>Activation normally begins in regions of the interventricular septum, then spreads through the Purkinje network to the ventricular endocardium and through the working myocardium. The precise sequence is more complex than a single wave travelling upwards from the apex; its ECG implications are discussed later.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-should-conduction-be-slow-in-one-region-and-fast-in-another\">Why should conduction be slow in one region and fast in another?</h3>\n<p><strong>The AV node conducts slowly</strong>, briefly delaying ventricular activation so the atria can complete their contraction and deliver the final portion of blood into the ventricles.</p>\n<p><strong>The His–Purkinje network then conducts rapidly</strong>, distributing excitation throughout both ventricles within a short time so that the ventricular myocardium generates pressure and ejects blood effectively.</p>\n<p>In short, **slow AV nodal conduction provides correct <em>timing between chambers</em>, while rapid His–Purkinje conduction provides coordinated <em>activation within the ventricles</em>.**</p>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"ScZVGHDisa0\" data-media-title=\"Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ScZVGHDisa0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-the-autonomic-nervous-system-influence-pacemaker-activity\">7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity?</h2>\n<p>The SA node is automatic, but its firing rate is modifiable. The key relationship is simple: <strong>the faster phase 4 reaches threshold, the sooner the next action potential occurs</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"sympathetic-stimulation\">Sympathetic stimulation</h3>\n<p>Noradrenaline activates <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong>, increasing cAMP-mediated signalling in pacemaker cells. This enhances pacemaker currents and generally steepens phase 4 depolarisation. The SA node reaches threshold sooner and fires more frequently, increasing heart rate. Sympathetic stimulation also increases AV nodal conduction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"parasympathetic-stimulation\">Parasympathetic stimulation</h3>\n<p>Vagal acetylcholine activates <strong>M₂ muscarinic receptors</strong>, reducing cAMP-dependent inward currents and increasing K⁺ conductance through acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. This can make the membrane more negative and slow phase 4 depolarisation. Threshold is reached later, so heart rate falls. AV nodal conduction also slows.</p>\n<ul><li><strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">Chronotropy</span>:</strong> Effect on heart rate.</li><li><strong><span data-term=\"dromotropy\">Dromotropy</span>:</strong> Effect on conduction velocity, particularly AV nodal conduction.</li></ul>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation usually produces positive chronotropy and dromotropy; vagal stimulation produces negative chronotropy and dromotropy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-significance-of-conduction-disturbances\">8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances</h2>\n<p>Electrical dysfunction may occur because the normal pacemaker fails, an impulse is delayed or transmission is interrupted.</p>\n<p><strong>SA nodal dysfunction:</strong> The normal pacemaker fires too slowly or pauses, potentially allowing a subsidiary pacemaker to take over.</p>\n<p><strong>AV conduction block:</strong> Impulses may reach the ventricles more slowly, some may fail to conduct, or atrial-to-ventricular conduction may be completely interrupted. In complete AV block, atria and ventricles may operate under separate pacemakers, with a slower escape rhythm maintaining ventricular activity.</p>\n<p>These disturbances affect effective pumping because both heart rate and the coordination of contraction matter. Their ECG manifestations belong in the ECG chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does the SA node dominate even though other conducting cells can generate impulses?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The SA node normally reaches threshold first.</strong> Its spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation generally produces a faster intrinsic rhythm than that of subsidiary pacemakers. Each SA-initiated impulse arrives before slower cells discharge independently and resets their electrical activity. This is why the SA node usually controls the rhythm, although other pacemakers can take over if it fails.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does hyperpolarisation activate the funny current even though it helps depolarise the cell?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>HCN channels are activated by sufficiently negative membrane potentials</strong>—hence the name <em>hyperpolarisation-activated</em>. At typical SA nodal voltages, the mixed Na⁺/K⁺ current passing through them is <strong>net inward</strong>, bringing positive charge into the cell. The membrane therefore gradually becomes less negative and moves towards threshold. Channel opening and the direction of the resulting current are different concepts.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why is the SA nodal phase 0 different from that of a ventricular contractile cell?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SA nodal phase 0 is mainly mediated by L-type Ca²⁺ influx</strong>; working ventricular muscle has a rapid phase 0 dominated by fast voltage-gated Na⁺ influx. The cells express different functional channel patterns and resting membrane properties. Consequently, the SA nodal upstroke is slower than the ventricular contractile-cell upstroke.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why is AV nodal conduction slow but His–Purkinje conduction fast?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The AV node provides timing:</strong> its slow conduction briefly separates atrial activation from ventricular activation, allowing atrial contraction to complete its contribution to filling. <strong>The His–Purkinje network provides coordinated distribution:</strong> it rapidly excites widely separated ventricular regions so pressure develops efficiently. Delayed chamber-to-chamber conduction and rapid conduction within the ventricles serve different purposes.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. How does sympathetic stimulation increase heart rate?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Noradrenaline activates <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> in pacemaker cells, increasing <strong>cAMP</strong>. This enhances depolarising pacemaker currents (including HCN-mediated I<sub>f</sub> and Ca²⁺ currents), so phase 4 generally rises towards threshold more rapidly. The SA node reaches threshold sooner, fires more often, and <strong>heart rate increases</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why can vagal stimulation slow an automatically firing SA node?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Automaticity means spontaneous discharge, not a fixed rate.</strong> Vagal acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors, reducing cAMP-mediated depolarising currents and opening acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. The membrane can become more negative and phase 4 rises more slowly, so reaching threshold takes longer and the SA node fires less often.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. If atrial impulses cannot reach the ventricles, must the ventricles stop immediately?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Not necessarily.</strong> In complete AV block, impulses from the atria do not conduct to the ventricles, but a subsidiary junctional or ventricular pacemaker may generate an <em>escape rhythm</em>. This maintains some ventricular contractions independently of the atria; it is often slower and may reduce cardiac output. An escape rhythm is a potential protective response, not a guarantee of adequate circulation.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Normal pathway:</strong> SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium.</li><li><strong>SA node:</strong> Normally sets heart rate by reaching threshold sooner than other pacemaker regions.</li><li><strong>Automaticity:</strong> Specialised cardiac cells generate impulses without requiring external nerve stimulation.</li><li><strong>SA nodal action potential:</strong> Phase 4 spontaneous depolarisation; phase 0 mainly Ca²⁺ influx; phase 3 K⁺-mediated repolarisation.</li><li><strong>Funny current:</strong> Hyperpolarisation activates HCN channels, producing a net inward current that contributes to the pacemaker potential.</li><li><strong>AV delay:</strong> Allows atrial contraction to complete its contribution to ventricular filling before ventricular systole.</li><li><strong>His–Purkinje network:</strong> Rapid impulse distribution allows coordinated ventricular activation.</li><li><strong>Subsidiary pacemakers:</strong> Can generate escape rhythms if normal pacemaker activity or conduction fails.</li><li><strong>Autonomic effects:</strong> Sympathetic β₁ stimulation accelerates SA firing and AV conduction; parasympathetic M₂ stimulation slows both.</li><li><strong>Chronotropy versus dromotropy:</strong> Chronotropy affects heart rate; dromotropy affects conduction velocity.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 7–10. Supplementary foundations: pacemaker action potentials, autonomic modulation and clinical consequences. 1. Why does the heart need a conducting system? The heart has four chambers, but efficient pumping requires more than all four chambers contracting: they must contract in the correct sequence . The atria must be electrically activated before the ventricles so atrial contraction can complete the final portion of ventricular filling. The ventricles must then be activated in a coordinated manner to generate pressure for blood ejection. The specialised cardiac conducting system generates and distributes electrical impulses through the myocardium. Unlike skeletal muscle, the heart does not need a new motor nerve impulse to initiate every beat. Specialised cardiac cells can spontaneously generate electrical impulses— automaticity . 2. The pathway of cardiac electrical conduction SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → bundle of His → right and left bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium. Structure Location or function Sinoatrial (SA) node Near the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium; normally initiates each heartbeat. Atrial conducting pathways Distribute excitation through both atria and towards the AV node. Atrioventricular (AV) node Lower right atrial septal region, near the coronary sinus opening and septal tricuspid leaflet; slows conduction before ventricular activation. Bundle of His Conducts impulses from the AV node into the interventricular septum. Right and left bundle branches Carry excitation towards their respective ventricles. Purkinje fibres Rapidly distribute excitation through the ventricular myocardium. Figure 01. Specialised cardiac conducting system. Follow the impulse from the right atrial SA node into the His–Purkinje network. The three internodal pathways shown are traditional teaching descriptions, not necessarily three separately insulated bundles. Image: OpenStax College · CC BY 3.0 · unmodified. What about the internodal pathways? The lecture names three traditional internodal pathways: anterior (associated with Bachmann's bundle), middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel). These names help follow the lecturer's slides. However, the atria do not necessarily contain three separate, insulated conducting bundles corresponding exactly to these pathways; excitation also spreads through ordinary atrial myocardium. Bachmann's bundle is particularly important for spreading excitation from the right to the left atrium, allowing the atria to be activated close together in time. 3. Why is the SA node the normal pacemaker? The SA node normally sets the rhythm because its cells spontaneously reach threshold faster than other potential pacemaker regions. An ordinary ventricular contractile cell has a relatively stable resting membrane potential and usually requires an incoming impulse for a new action potential. SA nodal cells lack a stable resting membrane potential : after repolarisation, the membrane gradually becomes less negative until it reaches threshold. This gradual rise is spontaneous diastolic depolarisation (the pacemaker potential) . How does the SA nodal action potential occur? Phase 4 — Spontaneous diastolic depolarisation: After repolarisation, the negative SA nodal membrane potential activates HCN (hyperpolarisation-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated) channels . These carry the funny current (I_f) . Although hyperpolarisation opens the channels, the current they produce at typical SA nodal potentials is net inward, bringing positive charge into the cell and gradually moving membrane potential towards threshold. A reduction in outward K⁺ current and inward Ca²⁺ currents also contribute. Phase 0 — Depolarisation: On reaching threshold, mainly L-type Ca²⁺ channels open. This differs from working ventricular cardiomyocytes, whose rapid phase 0 depends primarily on fast Na⁺ channels. Phase 3 — Repolarisation: Ca²⁺ channels inactivate and outward K⁺ currents restore a negative membrane potential. The next pacemaker cycle can then begin. Figure 02. Pacemaker versus ventricular action potentials. Compare the spontaneously rising phase 4 of the SA node with the stable resting potential, fast phase 0 and phase 2 plateau of the ventricular cardiomyocyte. The lower ECG trace is supplementary and will be explained later. Image: Jonker, Barnett, Boink Christoffels, Cells (2024), Figure 1 · CC BY 4.0 · unmodified. 4. If other conducting cells can fire spontaneously, why doesn't each set its own rhythm? The SA node is not the only region with automaticity. Other specialised conducting cells can also generate impulses, but their inherent rates are generally slower. The SA node normally reaches threshold before these slower pacemaker regions. When an impulse reaches them, it activates and resets their electrical activity, preventing them from independently determining the rhythm. The SA node dominates because it normally fires faster, not because the other cells have lost automaticity. A related mechanism is overdrive suppression , in which sustained faster activation suppresses subsidiary automaticity, sometimes delaying an escape rhythm after faster pacing stops. Resetting by incoming impulses and overdrive suppression are related but not identical. What if the SA node fails? A slower subsidiary pacemaker may take over: Pacemaker region Typical rate SA node Normal adult resting sinus rate: 60–100 beats/min AV junction Junctional escape rhythm: approximately 40–60 beats/min Ventricular pacemaker Ventricular escape rhythm: approximately 20–40 beats/min These ranges are guides, not fixed values. A subsidiary pacemaker can preserve ventricular activity if the normal pacemaker or its conduction fails, but slower rate and altered coordination may reduce pumping efficiency. 5. Why does the AV node delay electrical conduction? The AV node conducts relatively slowly, partly because nodal action potential upstrokes are Ca²⁺-dependent and cell-to-cell conduction is less rapid than in the His–Purkinje system. Why is the delay useful? The atria must complete their contraction before the ventricles contract. After atrial activation, atrial contraction pushes the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The AV node briefly delays the impulse so this contribution to ventricular filling can occur before ventricular systole begins. Without an appropriate delay, excessive overlap of atrial and ventricular contractions could reduce the effectiveness of atrial contribution. Importantly, most ventricular filling occurs passively before atrial systole. Atrial contraction normally contributes the remaining portion; it does not provide most of the blood entering the ventricles. Why doesn't the impulse simply spread from atria to ventricles? The cardiac fibrous skeleton electrically insulates most atrial myocardium from ventricular myocardium. Under normal conditions, impulses cross via the specialised AV conducting pathway, preserving the atrial-to-ventricular activation sequence. 6. Why is ventricular conduction so rapid? After the AV node, the impulse enters the His–Purkinje system , where rapid Na⁺-dependent depolarisation and effective cell-to-cell coupling permit fast conduction. The Purkinje network distributes excitation widely through the ventricles so large regions of myocardium become activated within a short period. This lets the ventricles develop pressure and eject blood efficiently. Activation normally begins in regions of the interventricular septum, then spreads through the Purkinje network to the ventricular endocardium and through the working myocardium. The precise sequence is more complex than a single wave travelling upwards from the apex; its ECG implications are discussed later. Why should conduction be slow in one region and fast in another? The AV node conducts slowly , briefly delaying ventricular activation so the atria can complete their contraction and deliver the final portion of blood into the ventricles. The His–Purkinje network then conducts rapidly , distributing excitation throughout both ventricles within a short time so that the ventricular myocardium generates pressure and ejects blood effectively. In short, **slow AV nodal conduction provides correct timing between chambers , while rapid His–Purkinje conduction provides coordinated activation within the ventricles .** OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO Cardiac Conduction and ECG — Alila Medical Media Trace SA node → AV node → His–Purkinje fibres, and notice why atrial activation precedes ventricular activation. ECG details are supplementary. The chapter text above remains the complete explanation. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not downloaded or rehosted. 7. How does the autonomic nervous system influence pacemaker activity? The SA node is automatic, but its firing rate is modifiable. The key relationship is simple: the faster phase 4 reaches threshold, the sooner the next action potential occurs . Sympathetic stimulation Noradrenaline activates β₁-adrenergic receptors , increasing cAMP-mediated signalling in pacemaker cells. This enhances pacemaker currents and generally steepens phase 4 depolarisation. The SA node reaches threshold sooner and fires more frequently, increasing heart rate. Sympathetic stimulation also increases AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic stimulation Vagal acetylcholine activates M₂ muscarinic receptors , reducing cAMP-dependent inward currents and increasing K⁺ conductance through acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. This can make the membrane more negative and slow phase 4 depolarisation. Threshold is reached later, so heart rate falls. AV nodal conduction also slows. Chronotropy : Effect on heart rate. Dromotropy : Effect on conduction velocity, particularly AV nodal conduction. Sympathetic stimulation usually produces positive chronotropy and dromotropy; vagal stimulation produces negative chronotropy and dromotropy. 8. Clinical significance of conduction disturbances Electrical dysfunction may occur because the normal pacemaker fails, an impulse is delayed or transmission is interrupted. SA nodal dysfunction: The normal pacemaker fires too slowly or pauses, potentially allowing a subsidiary pacemaker to take over. AV conduction block: Impulses may reach the ventricles more slowly, some may fail to conduct, or atrial-to-ventricular conduction may be completely interrupted. In complete AV block, atria and ventricles may operate under separate pacemakers, with a slower escape rhythm maintaining ventricular activity. These disturbances affect effective pumping because both heart rate and the coordination of contraction matter. Their ECG manifestations belong in the ECG chapter. Understanding Check 1. Why does the SA node dominate even though other conducting cells can generate impulses? Reveal explanation The SA node normally reaches threshold first. Its spontaneous phase 4 depolarisation generally produces a faster intrinsic rhythm than that of subsidiary pacemakers. Each SA-initiated impulse arrives before slower cells discharge independently and resets their electrical activity. This is why the SA node usually controls the rhythm, although other pacemakers can take over if it fails. 2. Why does hyperpolarisation activate the funny current even though it helps depolarise the cell? Reveal explanation HCN channels are activated by sufficiently negative membrane potentials —hence the name hyperpolarisation-activated . At typical SA nodal voltages, the mixed Na⁺/K⁺ current passing through them is net inward , bringing positive charge into the cell. The membrane therefore gradually becomes less negative and moves towards threshold. Channel opening and the direction of the resulting current are different concepts. 3. Why is the SA nodal phase 0 different from that of a ventricular contractile cell? Reveal explanation SA nodal phase 0 is mainly mediated by L-type Ca²⁺ influx ; working ventricular muscle has a rapid phase 0 dominated by fast voltage-gated Na⁺ influx. The cells express different functional channel patterns and resting membrane properties. Consequently, the SA nodal upstroke is slower than the ventricular contractile-cell upstroke. 4. Why is AV nodal conduction slow but His–Purkinje conduction fast? Reveal explanation The AV node provides timing: its slow conduction briefly separates atrial activation from ventricular activation, allowing atrial contraction to complete its contribution to filling. The His–Purkinje network provides coordinated distribution: it rapidly excites widely separated ventricular regions so pressure develops efficiently. Delayed chamber-to-chamber conduction and rapid conduction within the ventricles serve different purposes. 5. How does sympathetic stimulation increase heart rate? Reveal explanation Noradrenaline activates β₁-adrenergic receptors in pacemaker cells, increasing cAMP . This enhances depolarising pacemaker currents (including HCN-mediated I f and Ca²⁺ currents), so phase 4 generally rises towards threshold more rapidly. The SA node reaches threshold sooner, fires more often, and heart rate increases . 6. Why can vagal stimulation slow an automatically firing SA node? Reveal explanation Automaticity means spontaneous discharge, not a fixed rate. Vagal acetylcholine acts on M₂ receptors, reducing cAMP-mediated depolarising currents and opening acetylcholine-sensitive K⁺ channels. The membrane can become more negative and phase 4 rises more slowly, so reaching threshold takes longer and the SA node fires less often. 7. If atrial impulses cannot reach the ventricles, must the ventricles stop immediately? Reveal explanation Not necessarily. In complete AV block, impulses from the atria do not conduct to the ventricles, but a subsidiary junctional or ventricular pacemaker may generate an escape rhythm . This maintains some ventricular contractions independently of the atria; it is often slower and may reduce cardiac output. An escape rhythm is a potential protective response, not a guarantee of adequate circulation. Chapter Essentials Normal pathway: SA node → atrial myocardium → AV node → His bundle → bundle branches → Purkinje fibres → ventricular myocardium. SA node: Normally sets heart rate by reaching threshold sooner than other pacemaker regions. Automaticity: Specialised cardiac cells generate impulses without requiring external nerve stimulation. SA nodal action potential: Phase 4 spontaneous depolarisation; phase 0 mainly Ca²⁺ influx; phase 3 K⁺-mediated repolarisation. Funny current: Hyperpolarisation activates HCN channels, producing a net inward current that contributes to the pacemaker potential. AV delay: Allows atrial contraction to complete its contribution to ventricular filling before ventricular systole. His–Purkinje network: Rapid impulse distribution allows coordinated ventricular activation. Subsidiary pacemakers: Can generate escape rhythms if normal pacemaker activity or conduction fails. Autonomic effects: Sympathetic β₁ stimulation accelerates SA firing and AV conduction; parasympathetic M₂ stimulation slows both. Chronotropy versus dromotropy: Chronotropy affects heart rate; dromotropy affects conduction velocity."},{"slug":"cardiac-cycle","title":"The Cardiac Cycle","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"11-21","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-actually-drives-the-cardiac-cycle","title":"1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle?"},{"id":"2-follow-one-complete-heartbeat","title":"2. Follow one complete heartbeat"},{"id":"3-the-cycle-on-one-compact-map","title":"3. The cycle on one compact map"},{"id":"4-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump","title":"4. How much blood does the ventricle pump?"},{"id":"5-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-without-memorising-the-curves","title":"5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves"},{"id":"6-what-about-the-right-heart-and-faster-heart-rates","title":"6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"the-cardiac-cycle\">The Cardiac Cycle</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 11–21. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> pressure-gradient reasoning, Wiggers interpretation and standard ejection-phase terminology.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-actually-drives-the-cardiac-cycle\">1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>cardiac cycle</strong> is one complete sequence of ventricular filling, contraction, ejection and relaxation. To understand it, follow what happens to <strong>pressure</strong>, the <strong>valves</strong> and the <strong>amount of blood in the ventricle</strong>.</p>\n<p>Start with the left heart: <strong>left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta</strong>. Blood flows through an open valve when pressure upstream is higher than pressure downstream. Valves open and close <em>passively</em> as these pressures change; they do not receive individual nerve impulses telling them when to move.</p>\n<div class=\"pressure-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Left-heart pressure rules\"><div><strong>Mitral valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LA</sub> &gt; P<sub>LV</sub></span><small>Blood enters the ventricle</small></div><div><strong>Aortic valve opens</strong><span>P<sub>LV</sub> &gt; P<sub>aorta</sub></span><small>Blood leaves the ventricle</small></div></div>\n<p>Here <strong>P</strong> means pressure, <strong>LA</strong> is left atrium and <strong>LV</strong> is left ventricle. When either pressure difference reverses, the corresponding valve closes. The two valves respond to <em>different</em> pressure differences, which is why <strong>both may be closed at once</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-follow-one-complete-heartbeat\">2. Follow one complete heartbeat</h2>\n<p>We begin with the ventricle relaxed and about to receive blood. Each phase ends only when a change in electrical activity or pressure creates the conditions for the next event.</p>\n<h3 id=\"stage-1-why-does-blood-enter-the-ventricle\">Stage 1 — Why does blood enter the ventricle?</h3>\n<p>During ventricular <strong>diastole</strong>, the myocardium relaxes and ventricular pressure falls. Once P<sub>LV</sub> falls below P<sub>LA</sub>, the <strong>mitral valve opens</strong>. Pulmonary venous blood has continued entering the left atrium while the mitral valve was closed, so blood is available to flow into the ventricle.</p>\n<p>At first, filling is <strong>rapid</strong>: blood moves down the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient, and the relaxed, compliant ventricle can accept an increasing volume without much rise in pressure. As filling progresses, the pressure difference narrows and the flow rate slows. This later, slower period is <strong><span data-term=\"diastasis\">diastasis</span></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>During filling:</strong> mitral valve open; aortic valve closed; ventricular <strong>volume rises</strong>, while pressure remains relatively low.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Atrial depolarisation occurs and the atria contract, adding the final portion of ventricular filling.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-2-why-contract-the-atria-if-filling-is-already-occurring\">Stage 2 — Why contract the atria if filling is already occurring?</h3>\n<p>Most filling normally occurs <strong>passively</strong> before atrial contraction. Near the end of diastole, atrial systole pushes an additional volume into the ventricle—the <strong><span data-term=\"atrial-kick\">atrial kick</span></strong>. It matters especially when a ventricle is stiff or relaxes poorly.</p>\n<p>The lecturer illustrates approximately <strong>70% passive filling and 30% atrial contribution</strong>. These are teaching estimates, not constant percentages: atrial contraction often contributes roughly <strong>10–30%</strong> at rest, depending on age, rate and ventricular properties.</p>\n<p>The <strong>P wave</strong> is atrial depolarisation; atrial contraction follows and produces the <strong>a wave</strong> in the atrial pressure tracing. After filling ends, the ventricle contains its <strong><span data-term=\"edv\">end-diastolic volume (EDV)</span></strong>—approximately 130 mL in the lecturer's example.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Ventricular depolarisation (QRS complex) initiates contraction and raises ventricular pressure.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-3-why-doesnt-ventricular-contraction-immediately-eject-blood\">Stage 3 — Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately eject blood?</h3>\n<p>As ventricular contraction starts, P<sub>LV</sub> increases. Once it exceeds P<sub>LA</sub>, the <strong>mitral valve closes</strong>, contributing to the first heart sound (<strong>S1</strong>). Yet P<sub>LV</sub> is still <em>below aortic pressure</em>, so the <strong>aortic valve stays closed</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Both valves are closed.</strong> The ventricle develops force, but blood cannot enter or escape. Thus <strong>pressure rises rapidly while ventricular volume remains at EDV</strong>. This is <strong><span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span></strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↑</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = EDV</span></div>\n<p>Contraction does <em>not</em> necessarily mean ejection: the ventricle must first overcome arterial pressure. With aortic diastolic pressure around <strong>80 mmHg</strong>, for example, left ventricular pressure must rise above that level to open the aortic valve. The lecturer uses approximately <strong>10 mmHg</strong> for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure as a right-heart comparison; these are illustrative values.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What ends this phase?</strong> P<sub>LV</sub> exceeds P<sub>aorta</sub> → the aortic valve opens.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-4-how-does-blood-begin-leaving-the-ventricle\">Stage 4 — How does blood begin leaving the ventricle?</h3>\n<p>Once P<sub>LV</sub> exceeds aortic pressure, the <strong>aortic valve opens</strong>. Blood now enters the aorta, so <strong>ventricular volume decreases</strong>.</p>\n<p>Initially, a high rate of outflow produces <strong>rapid ejection</strong>. Later, outflow decreases as ventricular force and the pressure conditions change; this is <strong>reduced ejection</strong>. Ventricular pressure may continue <em>rising at first</em> after the valve opens, even though ventricular volume is already falling; the two measurements need not move in the same direction.</p>\n<p>The lecturer describes ejection as initially slow then rapid; the conventional physiological subdivision is <strong>rapid ejection followed by reduced ejection</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>During ejection:</strong> mitral valve closed; aortic valve open; blood leaves and ventricular volume falls. The blood remaining at the end is <strong><span data-term=\"esv\">end-systolic volume (ESV)</span></strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> Ventricular force declines; ventricular pressure begins to fall.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-5-why-does-the-aortic-valve-close\">Stage 5 — Why does the aortic valve close?</h3>\n<p>As the ventricle begins to relax, its pressure falls. Blood may still be flowing forward late in ejection, but eventually <strong>aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure</strong>. The pressure gradient reverses and the <strong>aortic valve closes</strong>, contributing to <strong>S2</strong> (the second heart sound).</p>\n<p>The lecturer calls the brief period of falling ventricular pressure before semilunar valve closure <strong>protodiastole</strong>. It precedes the following isovolumetric relaxation phase.</p>\n<div class=\"transition-line\"><strong>What happens next?</strong> The aortic valve has closed, but the mitral valve is not yet open.</div>\n<h3 id=\"stage-6-why-doesnt-filling-restart-immediately\">Stage 6 — Why doesn't filling restart immediately?</h3>\n<p>Although the aortic valve is now closed, P<sub>LV</sub> is still above P<sub>LA</sub>, keeping the <strong>mitral valve closed</strong>. The ventricle relaxes further, so pressure drops quickly, but neither valve is open and <strong>volume remains at ESV</strong>. This is <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"mechanism-strip\"><span><strong>Mitral</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Aortic</strong> closed</span><span><strong>Pressure</strong> ↓</span><span><strong>Volume</strong> = ESV</span></div>\n<p>When P<sub>LV</sub> finally falls below P<sub>LA</sub>, the mitral valve <strong>reopens</strong>. Blood enters and a <strong>new cardiac cycle begins</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"compare-the-two-isovolumetric-phases\">Compare the two isovolumetric phases</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Isovolumetric contraction</th><th>Isovolumetric relaxation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Muscle</td><td>Developing force</td><td>Relaxing</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral and aortic valves</td><td>Both closed</td><td>Both closed</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure</td><td>Increasing ↑</td><td>Decreasing ↓</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular volume</td><td>Constant at <strong>EDV</strong></td><td>Constant at <strong>ESV</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Ends when</td><td>Aortic valve opens</td><td>Mitral valve opens</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why is volume constant in both?</strong> Neither valve is open. <strong>Why does pressure change in opposite directions?</strong> The myocardium is generating force in one phase and relaxing in the other.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-cycle-on-one-compact-map\">3. The cycle on one compact map</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>What changes?</th><th>Immediate consequence</th><th>Next phase</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve opens</td><td>Filling</td></tr><tr><td>Atria depolarise</td><td>Atrial contraction adds final blood volume</td><td>Atrial systole</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve closes (S1)</td><td>Isovolumetric contraction</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure</td><td>Aortic valve opens</td><td>Ejection</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure</td><td>Aortic valve closes (S2)</td><td>Isovolumetric relaxation</td></tr><tr><td>Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure</td><td>Mitral valve reopens</td><td>Next filling phase</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>This map is for <strong>revision after</strong> the explanation: its purpose is to reconstruct each transition, not to replace the reasoning behind it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">4. How much blood does the ventricle pump?</h2>\n<p>A ventricle does not empty completely during systole. <strong>EDV</strong> is the blood it contains immediately before contraction; <strong>ESV</strong> is what remains after ejection. Their difference is the <strong>stroke volume (SV)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Stroke volume</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mi>SV</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>EDV</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ESV</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = <strong>80 mL</strong></span></div>\n<p>The <strong><span data-term=\"ejection-fraction\">ejection fraction (EF)</span></strong> is the <em>fraction of EDV</em> ejected, expressed as a percentage.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ejection fraction</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mi>EF</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>SV</mi><mi>EDV</mi></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 = <strong>61.5%</strong></span></div>\n<p>The lecturer uses EDV ≈130 mL, SV ≈70–90 mL and EF ≈65% as illustrative values. <strong>EF is not the fraction of all blood in the body ejected per beat</strong>—it is the fraction of end-diastolic blood within that ventricle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-reading-the-wiggers-diagram-without-memorising-the-curves\">5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves</h2>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"wiggers\">Wiggers diagram</span></strong> aligns the ECG, atrial pressure, ventricular pressure, aortic pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds along one time axis.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs04-wiggers\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f4/Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" alt=\"Wiggers diagram showing ECG, atrial, ventricular and aortic pressure traces, ventricular volume and heart sounds across the cardiac cycle.\" width=\"1098\" height=\"841\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. The Wiggers diagram.</strong> First follow ventricular pressure, ventricular volume and valve transitions; then connect them to the ECG and heart sounds. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">DanielChangMD (revised from DestinyQx); SVG redrawn by xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wiggers_Diagram.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 2.5</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"read-it-in-four-passes\">Read it in four passes</h3>\n<ol><li><strong>Find QRS and mitral closure.</strong> QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>.</li><li><strong>Find aortic opening.</strong> Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now <strong>falls</strong> as ejection begins.</li><li><strong>Find aortic closure.</strong> Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and <strong>S2</strong>. A small <strong>dicrotic notch</strong> appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation.</li><li><strong>Find mitral reopening.</strong> Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve <strong>rises</strong> as filling resumes.</li></ol>\n<p>The <strong>P wave</strong> represents atrial depolarisation, followed by atrial contraction near the end of diastole. The <strong>T wave</strong> represents ventricular repolarisation during late systole; it does not mean all contraction stops instantly.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-are-there-a-c-and-v-waves-in-atrial-pressure\">Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure?</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Cause</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>a wave</strong></td><td>Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>c wave</strong></td><td>Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>v wave</strong></td><td>Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <strong>v wave</strong> does <em>not</em> require atrial contraction. Blood continues returning to the atrium even while the mitral valve remains closed. S1 accompanies AV valve closure; S2 accompanies semilunar valve closure. Heart-sound details follow in Chapter 05.</p>\n<h3 id=\"watch-the-mechanism\">Watch the mechanism</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource animation-resource\" data-animation-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/56/Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" data-media-alt=\"Animated heart with valve motion and cardiac cycle pressure curves.\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL</span><h3>Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces</h3><p>Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-animation\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Play animation</button><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cardiac-Cycle-Animated.gif\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. Loaded only when selected.</p></div>\n<div class=\"media-resource video-resource\" data-youtube-id=\"IS9TD9fHFv0\" data-media-title=\"The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO</span><h3>The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media</h3><p>Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-video\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Watch video</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IS9TD9fHFv0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open on YouTube</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">© Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-about-the-right-heart-and-faster-heart-rates\">6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>right heart follows the same valve-pressure logic</strong> at lower pressures: tricuspid opening depends on right atrial versus right ventricular pressure; pulmonary valve opening depends on right ventricular versus pulmonary arterial pressure.</p>\n<p>Because pulmonary arterial pressure is much lower than aortic pressure, <strong>right ventricular ejection may begin slightly earlier</strong> even though both ventricles are activated close together. Differences in valve closure timing during respiration are developed in Chapter 05.</p>\n<p>When heart rate increases, the cardiac cycle becomes shorter. <strong>Diastole shortens proportionately more than systole.</strong> This reduces the time available for ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion. At moderately faster rates, increased contractility and quicker relaxation help preserve pumping; with <strong>excessive tachycardia</strong>, EDV and stroke volume may fall. A higher heart rate therefore does <strong>not necessarily</strong> increase cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Left ventricular pressure exceeds left atrial pressure but remains below aortic pressure. Which valves are open, and what happens to ventricular volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed.</strong> Ventricular pressure has reversed the mitral gradient, closing that valve, but has not yet exceeded aortic pressure to open the aortic valve. The ventricle is in <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>: pressure increases while blood volume remains at approximately EDV.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. During early ventricular ejection, ventricular volume decreases while ventricular pressure may still rise. How can both occur simultaneously?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Volume and pressure measure different things.</strong> Once left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and blood leaves, so volume falls. Meanwhile, myocardial contraction may continue generating greater force early in ejection, allowing ventricular pressure to continue rising despite the decreasing blood volume. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Ventricular repolarisation has begun, but the aortic valve remains open and blood is still being ejected. Is this physiologically possible? Explain.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Yes.</strong> Ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) occurs during late systole, but electrical recovery does not instantly end mechanical force or forward blood flow. The aortic valve can remain open while ventricular pressure and flow conditions still favour ejection. It closes when aortic pressure exceeds left ventricular pressure and the pressure gradient reverses.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why does the mitral valve remain closed just after the aortic valve closes?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Left ventricular pressure is still above left atrial pressure.</strong> The aortic valve closes when aortic pressure becomes greater than ventricular pressure. That event does not automatically open the mitral valve: ventricular pressure must fall further, below atrial pressure. While both are closed, the ventricle undergoes <em>isovolumetric relaxation</em>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises during ventricular systole. Which wave explains this, and why?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The v wave.</strong> Pulmonary venous blood continues entering the left atrium while the mitral valve is closed during ventricular systole. Blood accumulates, increasing atrial pressure without requiring atrial contraction. When the mitral valve reopens, the accumulated blood can flow into the ventricle.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Ventricular pressure falls rapidly while volume remains fixed at its minimum value. Identify the phase and valve positions.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation.</strong> The aortic valve has closed after ventricular pressure fell below aortic pressure; the mitral valve has not yet opened because ventricular pressure remains above atrial pressure. With both valves closed, ventricular pressure can decrease while volume stays at <strong>ESV</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. End-diastolic volume is 140 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. Calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SV = EDV − ESV = 140 − 60 = 80 mL.</strong> <strong>EF = (SV ÷ EDV) × 100 = (80 ÷ 140) × 100 ≈ 57.1%.</strong> Stroke volume is the actual volume ejected; ejection fraction expresses it as a percentage of the blood present at end-diastole.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Why may right ventricular ejection begin before left ventricular ejection?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The right ventricle ejects against a much lower arterial pressure.</strong> Pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure is normally far below aortic diastolic pressure. Even with closely timed ventricular activation, right ventricular pressure may surpass the pulmonary arterial pressure threshold first, opening the pulmonary valve before the aortic valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might extreme tachycardia reduce stroke volume even though ventricular contractions occur more frequently?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Diastole shortens disproportionately as heart rate rises.</strong> At very high rates, the ventricles may not have enough time to fill, reducing EDV and the blood available for each ejection. Therefore <strong>stroke volume may decrease</strong>; whether overall cardiac output increases or falls depends on the product CO = HR × SV.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Pressure gradients drive valve movements:</strong> the mitral valve depends on LA–LV pressure; the aortic valve depends on LV–aortic pressure.</li><li><strong>Filling:</strong> Mitral open, aortic closed; ventricular volume rises, largely without atrial contraction.</li><li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> Adds the final volume and creates the <strong>a wave</strong> of atrial pressure.</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> Both valves closed; pressure rises at constant <strong>EDV</strong>; mitral closure contributes to <strong>S1</strong>.</li><li><strong>Ejection:</strong> Aortic valve opens when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure; ventricular volume falls.</li><li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> Both valves closed; pressure falls at constant <strong>ESV</strong>; aortic closure contributes to <strong>S2</strong>.</li><li><strong>Stroke volume:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV.</li><li><strong>Ejection fraction:</strong> EF = SV/EDV × 100%.</li><li><strong>Wiggers diagram:</strong> Match valve transitions to simultaneous pressure, volume, ECG and heart-sound traces.</li><li><strong>Tachycardia:</strong> Diastole shortens disproportionately; extreme rates can impair filling and coronary perfusion.</li></ol>","plain":"The Cardiac Cycle Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 11–21. Supplementary clarification: pressure-gradient reasoning, Wiggers interpretation and standard ejection-phase terminology. 1. What actually drives the cardiac cycle? The cardiac cycle is one complete sequence of ventricular filling, contraction, ejection and relaxation. To understand it, follow what happens to pressure , the valves and the amount of blood in the ventricle . Start with the left heart: left atrium → mitral valve → left ventricle → aortic valve → aorta . Blood flows through an open valve when pressure upstream is higher than pressure downstream. Valves open and close passively as these pressures change; they do not receive individual nerve impulses telling them when to move. Mitral valve opens P LA P LV Blood enters the ventricle Aortic valve opens P LV P aorta Blood leaves the ventricle Here P means pressure, LA is left atrium and LV is left ventricle. When either pressure difference reverses, the corresponding valve closes. The two valves respond to different pressure differences, which is why both may be closed at once . 2. Follow one complete heartbeat We begin with the ventricle relaxed and about to receive blood. Each phase ends only when a change in electrical activity or pressure creates the conditions for the next event. Stage 1 — Why does blood enter the ventricle? During ventricular diastole , the myocardium relaxes and ventricular pressure falls. Once P LV falls below P LA , the mitral valve opens . Pulmonary venous blood has continued entering the left atrium while the mitral valve was closed, so blood is available to flow into the ventricle. At first, filling is rapid : blood moves down the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient, and the relaxed, compliant ventricle can accept an increasing volume without much rise in pressure. As filling progresses, the pressure difference narrows and the flow rate slows. This later, slower period is diastasis . During filling: mitral valve open; aortic valve closed; ventricular volume rises , while pressure remains relatively low. What happens next? Atrial depolarisation occurs and the atria contract, adding the final portion of ventricular filling. Stage 2 — Why contract the atria if filling is already occurring? Most filling normally occurs passively before atrial contraction. Near the end of diastole, atrial systole pushes an additional volume into the ventricle—the atrial kick . It matters especially when a ventricle is stiff or relaxes poorly. The lecturer illustrates approximately 70% passive filling and 30% atrial contribution . These are teaching estimates, not constant percentages: atrial contraction often contributes roughly 10–30% at rest, depending on age, rate and ventricular properties. The P wave is atrial depolarisation; atrial contraction follows and produces the a wave in the atrial pressure tracing. After filling ends, the ventricle contains its end-diastolic volume (EDV) —approximately 130 mL in the lecturer's example. What happens next? Ventricular depolarisation (QRS complex) initiates contraction and raises ventricular pressure. Stage 3 — Why doesn't ventricular contraction immediately eject blood? As ventricular contraction starts, P LV increases. Once it exceeds P LA , the mitral valve closes , contributing to the first heart sound ( S1 ). Yet P LV is still below aortic pressure , so the aortic valve stays closed . Both valves are closed. The ventricle develops force, but blood cannot enter or escape. Thus pressure rises rapidly while ventricular volume remains at EDV . This is isovolumetric contraction . Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↑ Volume = EDV Contraction does not necessarily mean ejection: the ventricle must first overcome arterial pressure. With aortic diastolic pressure around 80 mmHg , for example, left ventricular pressure must rise above that level to open the aortic valve. The lecturer uses approximately 10 mmHg for pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure as a right-heart comparison; these are illustrative values. What ends this phase? P LV exceeds P aorta → the aortic valve opens. Stage 4 — How does blood begin leaving the ventricle? Once P LV exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens . Blood now enters the aorta, so ventricular volume decreases . Initially, a high rate of outflow produces rapid ejection . Later, outflow decreases as ventricular force and the pressure conditions change; this is reduced ejection . Ventricular pressure may continue rising at first after the valve opens, even though ventricular volume is already falling; the two measurements need not move in the same direction. The lecturer describes ejection as initially slow then rapid; the conventional physiological subdivision is rapid ejection followed by reduced ejection . During ejection: mitral valve closed; aortic valve open; blood leaves and ventricular volume falls. The blood remaining at the end is end-systolic volume (ESV) . What happens next? Ventricular force declines; ventricular pressure begins to fall. Stage 5 — Why does the aortic valve close? As the ventricle begins to relax, its pressure falls. Blood may still be flowing forward late in ejection, but eventually aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure . The pressure gradient reverses and the aortic valve closes , contributing to S2 (the second heart sound). The lecturer calls the brief period of falling ventricular pressure before semilunar valve closure protodiastole . It precedes the following isovolumetric relaxation phase. What happens next? The aortic valve has closed, but the mitral valve is not yet open. Stage 6 — Why doesn't filling restart immediately? Although the aortic valve is now closed, P LV is still above P LA , keeping the mitral valve closed . The ventricle relaxes further, so pressure drops quickly, but neither valve is open and volume remains at ESV . This is isovolumetric relaxation . Mitral closed Aortic closed Pressure ↓ Volume = ESV When P LV finally falls below P LA , the mitral valve reopens . Blood enters and a new cardiac cycle begins . Compare the two isovolumetric phases Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Muscle Developing force Relaxing Mitral and aortic valves Both closed Both closed Ventricular pressure Increasing ↑ Decreasing ↓ Ventricular volume Constant at EDV Constant at ESV Ends when Aortic valve opens Mitral valve opens Why is volume constant in both? Neither valve is open. Why does pressure change in opposite directions? The myocardium is generating force in one phase and relaxing in the other. 3. The cycle on one compact map What changes? Immediate consequence Next phase Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve opens Filling Atria depolarise Atrial contraction adds final blood volume Atrial systole Ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure Mitral valve closes (S1) Isovolumetric contraction Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure Aortic valve opens Ejection Aortic pressure exceeds ventricular pressure Aortic valve closes (S2) Isovolumetric relaxation Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure Mitral valve reopens Next filling phase This map is for revision after the explanation: its purpose is to reconstruct each transition, not to replace the reasoning behind it. 4. How much blood does the ventricle pump? A ventricle does not empty completely during systole. EDV is the blood it contains immediately before contraction; ESV is what remains after ejection. Their difference is the stroke volume (SV) . Stroke volume SV = EDV − ESV Example: 130 mL − 50 mL = 80 mL The ejection fraction (EF) is the fraction of EDV ejected, expressed as a percentage. Ejection fraction EF = SV EDV × 100 % Example: (80 ÷ 130) × 100 = 61.5% The lecturer uses EDV ≈130 mL, SV ≈70–90 mL and EF ≈65% as illustrative values. EF is not the fraction of all blood in the body ejected per beat —it is the fraction of end-diastolic blood within that ventricle. 5. Reading the Wiggers diagram without memorising the curves A Wiggers diagram aligns the ECG, atrial pressure, ventricular pressure, aortic pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds along one time axis. Figure 01. The Wiggers diagram. First follow ventricular pressure, ventricular volume and valve transitions; then connect them to the ECG and heart sounds. Tap to enlarge. DanielChangMD (revised from DestinyQx); SVG redrawn by xavax · Source · CC BY-SA 2.5 · unmodified. Read it in four passes Find QRS and mitral closure. QRS represents ventricular depolarisation. Pressure then rises, the mitral valve closes and volume stays at EDV: isovolumetric contraction . Find aortic opening. Left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure. The volume curve now falls as ejection begins. Find aortic closure. Pressure falls below aortic pressure, producing valve closure and S2 . A small dicrotic notch appears on the aortic pressure trace around this event. Ventricular volume then stays at ESV during isovolumetric relaxation. Find mitral reopening. Ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; the mitral valve opens, and the volume curve rises as filling resumes. The P wave represents atrial depolarisation, followed by atrial contraction near the end of diastole. The T wave represents ventricular repolarisation during late systole; it does not mean all contraction stops instantly. Why are there a, c and v waves in atrial pressure? Wave Cause a wave Atrial contraction increases atrial pressure. c wave Early ventricular contraction pushes the closed AV valve towards the atrium. v wave Venous blood continues entering the atrium while the AV valve is closed. The v wave does not require atrial contraction. Blood continues returning to the atrium even while the mitral valve remains closed. S1 accompanies AV valve closure; S2 accompanies semilunar valve closure. Heart-sound details follow in Chapter 05. Watch the mechanism SHORT ANIMATION · OPTIONAL Cardiac cycle and Wiggers traces Observe when each valve closes, when volume stops changing and when ejection starts. Play animation DrJanaOfficial; diagram derived from Xavax · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 4.0 . Loaded only when selected. OPTIONAL NARRATED VIDEO The Cardiac Cycle — Alila Medical Media Identify the four valve transitions and compare the two isovolumetric phases. Watch video Open on YouTube © Alila Medical Media. Official YouTube embed; not rehosted. 6. What about the right heart and faster heart rates? The right heart follows the same valve-pressure logic at lower pressures: tricuspid opening depends on right atrial versus right ventricular pressure; pulmonary valve opening depends on right ventricular versus pulmonary arterial pressure. Because pulmonary arterial pressure is much lower than aortic pressure, right ventricular ejection may begin slightly earlier even though both ventricles are activated close together. Differences in valve closure timing during respiration are developed in Chapter 05. When heart rate increases, the cardiac cycle becomes shorter. Diastole shortens proportionately more than systole. This reduces the time available for ventricular filling and much of left ventricular coronary perfusion. At moderately faster rates, increased contractility and quicker relaxation help preserve pumping; with excessive tachycardia , EDV and stroke volume may fall. A higher heart rate therefore does not necessarily increase cardiac output. Understanding Check 1. Left ventricular pressure exceeds left atrial pressure but remains below aortic pressure. Which valves are open, and what happens to ventricular volume? Reveal explanation Both the mitral and aortic valves are closed. Ventricular pressure has reversed the mitral gradient, closing that valve, but has not yet exceeded aortic pressure to open the aortic valve. The ventricle is in isovolumetric contraction : pressure increases while blood volume remains at approximately EDV. 2. During early ventricular ejection, ventricular volume decreases while ventricular pressure may still rise. How can both occur simultaneously? Reveal explanation Volume and pressure measure different things. Once left ventricular pressure exceeds aortic pressure, the aortic valve opens and blood leaves, so volume falls. Meanwhile, myocardial contraction may continue generating greater force early in ejection, allowing ventricular pressure to continue rising despite the decreasing blood volume. Ejection does not require pressure to be falling. 3. Ventricular repolarisation has begun, but the aortic valve remains open and blood is still being ejected. Is this physiologically possible? Explain. Reveal explanation Yes. Ventricular repolarisation (the T wave) occurs during late systole, but electrical recovery does not instantly end mechanical force or forward blood flow. The aortic valve can remain open while ventricular pressure and flow conditions still favour ejection. It closes when aortic pressure exceeds left ventricular pressure and the pressure gradient reverses. 4. Why does the mitral valve remain closed just after the aortic valve closes? Reveal explanation Left ventricular pressure is still above left atrial pressure. The aortic valve closes when aortic pressure becomes greater than ventricular pressure. That event does not automatically open the mitral valve: ventricular pressure must fall further, below atrial pressure. While both are closed, the ventricle undergoes isovolumetric relaxation . 5. The atrium is not contracting, yet its pressure rises during ventricular systole. Which wave explains this, and why? Reveal explanation The v wave. Pulmonary venous blood continues entering the left atrium while the mitral valve is closed during ventricular systole. Blood accumulates, increasing atrial pressure without requiring atrial contraction. When the mitral valve reopens, the accumulated blood can flow into the ventricle. 6. Ventricular pressure falls rapidly while volume remains fixed at its minimum value. Identify the phase and valve positions. Reveal explanation Isovolumetric relaxation. The aortic valve has closed after ventricular pressure fell below aortic pressure; the mitral valve has not yet opened because ventricular pressure remains above atrial pressure. With both valves closed, ventricular pressure can decrease while volume stays at ESV . 7. End-diastolic volume is 140 mL and end-systolic volume is 60 mL. Calculate stroke volume and ejection fraction. Reveal explanation SV = EDV − ESV = 140 − 60 = 80 mL. EF = (SV ÷ EDV) × 100 = (80 ÷ 140) × 100 ≈ 57.1%. Stroke volume is the actual volume ejected; ejection fraction expresses it as a percentage of the blood present at end-diastole. 8. Why may right ventricular ejection begin before left ventricular ejection? Reveal explanation The right ventricle ejects against a much lower arterial pressure. Pulmonary arterial diastolic pressure is normally far below aortic diastolic pressure. Even with closely timed ventricular activation, right ventricular pressure may surpass the pulmonary arterial pressure threshold first, opening the pulmonary valve before the aortic valve. 9. Why might extreme tachycardia reduce stroke volume even though ventricular contractions occur more frequently? Reveal explanation Diastole shortens disproportionately as heart rate rises. At very high rates, the ventricles may not have enough time to fill, reducing EDV and the blood available for each ejection. Therefore stroke volume may decrease ; whether overall cardiac output increases or falls depends on the product CO = HR × SV. Chapter Essentials Pressure gradients drive valve movements: the mitral valve depends on LA–LV pressure; the aortic valve depends on LV–aortic pressure. Filling: Mitral open, aortic closed; ventricular volume rises, largely without atrial contraction. Atrial systole: Adds the final volume and creates the a wave of atrial pressure. Isovolumetric contraction: Both valves closed; pressure rises at constant EDV ; mitral closure contributes to S1 . Ejection: Aortic valve opens when LV pressure exceeds aortic pressure; ventricular volume falls. Isovolumetric relaxation: Both valves closed; pressure falls at constant ESV ; aortic closure contributes to S2 . Stroke volume: SV = EDV − ESV. Ejection fraction: EF = SV/EDV × 100%. Wiggers diagram: Match valve transitions to simultaneous pressure, volume, ECG and heart-sound traces. Tachycardia: Diastole shortens disproportionately; extreme rates can impair filling and coronary perfusion."},{"slug":"heart-sounds","title":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"18-19 and 27-34","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds","title":"1. Why does the heart produce sounds?"},{"id":"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2","title":"2. Why does S1 occur before S2?"},{"id":"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration","title":"3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?"},{"id":"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4","title":"4. What causes S3 and S4?"},{"id":"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-intensity","title":"5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity?"},{"id":"6-what-makes-a-murmur","title":"6. What makes a murmur?"},{"id":"7-predict-a-murmurs-timing-without-memorising-a-list","title":"7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list"},{"id":"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve","title":"8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?"},{"id":"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere","title":"9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"heart-sounds-and-murmurs\">Heart Sounds and Murmurs</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 27–34, with related concepts from slides 18–19. <strong>Supplementary explanation:</strong> mechanisms of S3 and S4, auscultation landmarks, and applying valve physiology to murmur timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-heart-produce-sounds\">1. Why does the heart produce sounds?</h2>\n<p>During the cardiac cycle, pressure changes make heart valves close, and blood accelerates or decelerates inside the heart. Sudden changes in blood motion set the valve apparatus, surrounding tissues and blood into <strong>vibration</strong>. Some of these vibrations travel through the chest wall and can be heard with a stethoscope.</p>\n<p>Heart sounds are therefore <strong>not simply leaflets hitting one another</strong>. The two principal sounds normally heard are <strong>S1</strong> and <strong>S2</strong>.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Sound</th><th>Main valve event</th><th>When it occurs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>S1 — “lub”</strong></td><td>Mitral and tricuspid closure</td><td>Beginning of ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>S2 — “dub”</strong></td><td>Aortic and pulmonary closure</td><td>Beginning of ventricular diastole</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"heart-sound-line\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Heart sound timeline\"><span><strong>S1</strong><small>AV valves close</small></span><span class=\"timeline-phase\"><strong>Systole</strong><small>Ventricular pressure and ejection</small></span><span><strong>S2</strong><small>Semilunar valves close</small></span><span class=\"timeline-phase\"><strong>Diastole</strong><small>Relaxation and filling</small></span></div>\n<h3 id=\"listen-to-normal-s1-and-s2\">Listen to normal S1 and S2</h3>\n<div class=\"media-resource audio-resource\" data-audio-src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/72/HROgg.ogg\" data-audio-mp3=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/72/HROgg.ogg/HROgg.ogg.mp3\" data-media-title=\"Normal heart sounds at approximately 70 beats per minute\"><span class=\"media-eyebrow\">REAL HEART-SOUND RECORDING · 19 SECONDS</span><h3>Normal S1–S2 sequence</h3><p>Listen for two sounds per beat. The shorter S1-to-S2 interval is systole; the usually longer S2-to-next-S1 interval is diastole at a normal resting rate.</p><button type=\"button\" class=\"media-play\" data-media-action=\"play-audio\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Listen</button> <a class=\"media-outlink\" href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HROgg.ogg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source / alternative playback</a><div class=\"media-stage\"></div><p class=\"media-credit\">James Heilman, MD · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC0 public-domain dedication</a>. Audio is loaded only when selected; the explanation above works without it.</p></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-does-s1-occur-before-s2\">2. Why does S1 occur before S2?</h2>\n<h3 id=\"s1-the-start-of-ventricular-systole\">S1: the start of ventricular systole</h3>\n<p>Ventricular depolarisation (QRS) is followed by myocardial contraction, raising ventricular pressure. Once ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure, the <strong>AV valves close</strong>. Their closure and the associated sudden changes in blood motion produce <strong>S1</strong>, around the onset of <span data-term=\"isovolumetric\">isovolumetric contraction</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s2-the-end-of-ventricular-ejection\">S2: the end of ventricular ejection</h3>\n<p>Later, ventricular force and pressure decline. When aortic and pulmonary arterial pressures exceed the corresponding ventricular pressures, the <strong>semilunar valves close</strong>. This produces <strong>S2</strong>, around the beginning of isovolumetric relaxation.</p>\n<p>The important pressure logic is: <strong>S1 follows reversal of the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient; S2 follows reversal of the ventricular-to-arterial pressure gradient.</strong> Closure generates audible vibrations; a valve does not make a sound simply because it is open.</p>\n<p>The lecturer gives illustrative durations of approximately <strong>0.15 s for S1</strong> and <strong>0.12 s for S2</strong>. Sound duration and intensity can vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-does-s2-sometimes-split-with-inspiration\">3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration?</h2>\n<p>S2 has two components: <strong>A2</strong> (aortic valve closure) and <strong>P2</strong> (pulmonary valve closure). Usually <strong>A2 precedes P2</strong> by a short interval.</p>\n<p>During inspiration, more negative intrathoracic pressure promotes <strong>systemic venous return to the right heart</strong>. Right ventricular filling rises, and right ventricular ejection may last slightly longer. This delays <strong>P2 relative to A2</strong>, making the two components more clearly separated. Respiratory changes in pulmonary venous return and left ventricular timing can contribute as well.</p>\n<div class=\"sound-split\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Aortic and pulmonary valve closure timing\"><div><strong>Expiration</strong><span>A2 <b>·</b> P2</span><small>Short separation; may sound single</small></div><div><strong>Inspiration</strong><span>A2 <b>····</b> P2</span><small>Longer separation: physiological splitting</small></div></div>\n<p><strong>The important point is widening of the A2–P2 interval during inspiration</strong>, not that the two valves necessarily close at precisely the same time during expiration. More complex patterns of splitting are advanced clinical material beyond this lecturer's primary scope.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-causes-s3-and-s4\">4. What causes S3 and S4?</h2>\n<p>Both are additional low-frequency sounds in <strong>diastole</strong>, but they correspond to <em>different phases of filling</em>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s3-early-diastole-rapid-ventricular-filling\">S3 — Early diastole: rapid ventricular filling</h3>\n<p><strong>S3 occurs shortly after S2</strong>, when blood enters the ventricle rapidly. Rapid filling and sudden deceleration of blood can set ventricular structures vibrating. S3 can be physiological in children and younger adults, and during pregnancy. In an older or symptomatic adult, it can indicate volume overload or ventricular dysfunction, including heart failure.</p>\n<h3 id=\"s4-late-diastole-atrial-contraction-against-resistance\">S4 — Late diastole: atrial contraction against resistance</h3>\n<p><strong>S4 occurs shortly before S1</strong>, during atrial contraction. If the ventricle is relatively stiff and noncompliant, the additional blood delivered by atrial systole encounters greater resistance to filling, generating low-frequency vibrations.</p>\n<p>S4 is usually abnormal in adults. A typical atrial S4 is <strong>absent in atrial fibrillation</strong> because there is no effective coordinated atrial contraction to produce it.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th><strong>S3</strong></th><th><strong>S4</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Early diastole, just after S2</td><td>Late diastole, just before S1</td></tr><tr><td>Trigger</td><td>Rapid ventricular filling</td><td>Atrial contraction</td></tr><tr><td>Interpretation</td><td>Sometimes normal in younger people; may indicate overload/dysfunction in older adults</td><td>Often suggests a stiff ventricle in adults</td></tr><tr><td>Needs atrial contraction?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Audio interpretation:</strong> A new low-pitched sound just after S2 suggests <strong>S3</strong>; one just before S1 suggests <strong>S4</strong>. Our normal recording above demonstrates S1/S2, <strong>not</strong> a verified S3 or S4 example. Separate recordings should be added only after checking their identity and rights.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-do-heart-sounds-change-in-intensity\">5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity?</h2>\n<p>Sound intensity depends on valve motion, pressure changes, the rate at which blood flow is stopped, and how well the vibrations travel to the stethoscope.</p>\n<p>For example, <strong>A2 may be accentuated in systemic hypertension</strong>, and <strong>P2 in pulmonary hypertension</strong>, because higher arterial pressure can produce more energetic closing events. However, severe valve immobility may make a closure sound softer despite disease.</p>\n<p>Thus, <strong>a loud or soft sound alone cannot reliably establish disease severity</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-makes-a-murmur\">6. What makes a murmur?</h2>\n<p>A <strong>heart murmur</strong> is a relatively sustained sound produced when blood flow and cardiac structures generate audible vibrations, often in association with disturbed or turbulent flow.</p>\n<p>Examples of conditions that change the flow include:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Stenosis:</strong> A valve narrows, so blood has difficulty passing <em>forward</em> through it.</li><li><strong>Regurgitation (incompetence):</strong> A valve fails to close properly and allows blood to leak <em>backward</em>.</li><li><strong>High-flow states:</strong> For example, anaemia can increase flow or reduce blood viscosity enough to produce a murmur despite structurally normal valves.</li></ul>\n<p>To predict when a murmur occurs, first identify <strong>the direction of abnormal flow</strong> and <strong>whether that flow happens during ventricular systole or diastole</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-predict-a-murmurs-timing-without-memorising-a-list\">7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list</h2>\n<p>Remember the normal valve pattern:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>During</th><th>AV valves (mitral/tricuspid)</th><th>Semilunar valves (aortic/pulmonary)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Ventricular systole</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>closed</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>open</strong> for ejection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ventricular diastole</strong></td><td>Should be <strong>open</strong> for filling</td><td>Should be <strong>closed</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"systolic-murmurs\">Systolic murmurs</h3>\n<p><strong>Aortic or pulmonary stenosis:</strong> These valves should be open during ventricular ejection. A narrowed opening obstructs <strong>forward systolic flow</strong>, so turbulent high-velocity flow can produce a systolic ejection murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> These AV valves should be closed during ventricular contraction. If they leak, blood moves <strong>backward from ventricle to atrium during systole</strong>, creating a systolic regurgitant murmur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"diastolic-murmurs\">Diastolic murmurs</h3>\n<p><strong>Mitral or tricuspid stenosis:</strong> AV valves should open during ventricular filling. A narrowed opening obstructs <strong>forward flow from atrium to ventricle during diastole</strong>, causing a diastolic murmur.</p>\n<p><strong>Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation:</strong> Semilunar valves should remain closed during diastole. If they leak, arterial pressure drives <strong>backward flow into the ventricle during diastole</strong>, causing a diastolic murmur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"murmur-timing-at-a-glance\">Murmur timing at a glance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Valve group</th><th>Stenosis</th><th>Regurgitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aortic / pulmonary</strong></td><td><strong>Systolic</strong> (forward outflow obstruction)</td><td><strong>Diastolic</strong> (backflow into ventricle)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral / tricuspid</strong></td><td><strong>Diastolic</strong> (forward filling obstruction)</td><td><strong>Systolic</strong> (backflow into atrium)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>To solve a new question, ask <strong>which valve should be open or shut during the abnormal flow?</strong> This explains the timing. Murmur loudness alone is not a dependable measure of lesion severity; location, character, radiation and other signs also matter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-where-do-we-listen-to-each-heart-valve\">8. Where do we listen to each heart valve?</h2>\n<p>The best listening points on the chest are <strong>not necessarily directly over the anatomical valve openings</strong>. Vibrations are transmitted through blood and cardiac tissues.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Auscultation area</th><th>Surface landmark</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Aortic</strong></td><td>Second <strong>right</strong> intercostal space, near sternum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pulmonary</strong></td><td>Second <strong>left</strong> intercostal space, near sternum</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tricuspid</strong></td><td>Lower <strong>left</strong> sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mitral</strong></td><td><strong>Apex</strong>, commonly fifth left intercostal space near midclavicular line</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration teaching-illustration-portrait\" id=\"cvs05-auscultation\">\n<a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/44/Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" alt=\"Anterior chest surface showing colour-coded cardiac auscultation points: navy aortic, yellow pulmonary, red tricuspid, green mitral, light blue Erb's point.\" width=\"436\" height=\"600\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Cardiac auscultation areas.</strong> Navy = aortic; yellow = pulmonary; red = tricuspid; green = mitral; light blue = Erb's point (supplementary). <span class=\"figure-credit\">Vinne2, after Ickle and Gray's Anatomy · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heart_sounds_auscultation_areas.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>A stethoscope's <strong>diaphragm</strong> transmits many higher-frequency sounds well (e.g. S1, S2 and many murmurs). Its <strong>bell</strong>, applied lightly, can help detect low-frequency S3, S4 and some diastolic murmurs. This is a supplementary clinical technique.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-why-can-bruits-or-murmurs-arise-elsewhere\">9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere?</h2>\n<p>A <strong><span data-term=\"bruit\">bruit</span></strong> is an abnormal vascular sound heard over a vessel when flow generates audible vibrations.</p>\n<p>The lecture also names conditions that can produce abnormal cardiovascular sounds:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)</strong></td><td>Aorta-to-pulmonary artery flow may continue during both systole and diastole, producing a <em>continuous</em> murmur.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ventricular septal defect (VSD)</strong></td><td>Abnormal flow between ventricles commonly produces a systolic murmur.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Arteriovenous fistula</strong></td><td>High-velocity artery-to-vein flow can create a bruit.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Anaemia</strong></td><td>Reduced viscosity and increased flow can cause a functional murmur without valve stenosis.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vascular aneurysm</strong></td><td>Altered vessel geometry may disturb flow; not every aneurysm produces a bruit.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Hypervascular goitre</strong></td><td>Increased thyroid blood flow can cause a bruit over the gland.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The common mechanism is <strong>flow-induced vibration</strong>, but its timing depends on the relevant pressure gradient and anatomy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A sound is heard at the beginning of ventricular systole. Which valves are mainly responsible, and what pressure change closes them?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S1, mainly from mitral and tricuspid valve closure.</strong> Early ventricular contraction raises ventricular pressure above atrial pressure, reversing the gradient across the AV valves. Their closure abruptly alters blood motion and generates vibrations heard as S1.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does physiological splitting of S2 increase with inspiration?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. Greater right ventricular filling can prolong ejection and <strong>delay pulmonary valve closure (P2)</strong> relative to aortic closure (A2). The A2–P2 interval widens. Expiration usually narrows the separation; the valves are not required to close at exactly the same instant.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. An extra sound occurs shortly after S2 during rapid ventricular filling. Identify it and explain when it may be physiological.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S3.</strong> Rapid early-diastolic filling and deceleration produce vibrations in ventricular structures. An S3 may be normal in children or young adults, but in an older symptomatic patient it can suggest ventricular overload or dysfunction. Timing and clinical context are essential.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. A low-frequency sound just before S1 disappears when a patient develops atrial fibrillation. Explain the likely sound and why it disappears.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>S4.</strong> It occurs in late diastole when effective atrial contraction forces blood into a relatively stiff ventricle, producing vibrations. In atrial fibrillation, coordinated atrial contraction is lost; without the atrial kick, the usual mechanism producing an atrial S4 is absent.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why can mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur but mitral regurgitation cause a systolic murmur?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Stenosis obstructs normal forward filling:</strong> the mitral valve should be open in diastole for left atrium-to-ventricle flow, so narrowing generates a diastolic murmur. <strong>Regurgitation permits backward flow:</strong> the valve should be closed during ventricular systole, but leakage sends blood into the left atrium and produces a systolic murmur.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. Name the lesion and predict the murmur's timing.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Aortic regurgitation; diastolic murmur.</strong> During ventricular relaxation, aortic pressure is greater than left ventricular pressure and the aortic valve should be closed. If it is incompetent, blood leaks backward into the ventricle during diastole and generates an audible murmur.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why might an anaemic patient have a systolic flow murmur even when all four valves are structurally normal?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Anaemia reduces blood viscosity and often increases cardiac output. Faster flow through otherwise normal valves may generate audible flow-related vibrations, often as an ejection-type systolic murmur. A murmur therefore does not automatically mean a damaged or narrowed valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. A stethoscope is placed over the second right intercostal space. Which valve area is being examined, and why is this not necessarily the valve's anatomical position?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The aortic auscultation area.</strong> Valve vibrations travel with blood and through surrounding tissues, so the point at which they are best transmitted to the chest wall is not necessarily directly over the anatomical aortic valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Listen to the normal heart recording. Which interval corresponds to ventricular systole, and why?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The S1-to-S2 interval is ventricular systole.</strong> S1 follows AV valve closure as ventricular pressure rises; S2 follows semilunar valve closure when ventricular ejection ends. The S2-to-next-S1 interval is ventricular diastole. The recording illustrates normal S1 and S2, not a proven S3 or S4.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. A continuous murmur is heard in a patient with a patent ductus arteriosus. Why does the abnormal flow persist across both parts of the cardiac cycle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A patent ductus joins the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Because aortic pressure is typically greater than pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole, blood may continue to cross the connection throughout the cycle. Thus the sound can be <strong>continuous</strong>, unlike a murmur confined to one phase.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>S1:</strong> Mostly due to mitral and tricuspid closure at the start of ventricular systole.</li><li><strong>S2:</strong> Mostly due to aortic and pulmonary closure at the start of ventricular diastole.</li><li><strong>S2 splitting:</strong> Inspiration normally widens the A2–P2 interval, largely through relative delay of P2.</li><li><strong>S3:</strong> Early-diastolic rapid-filling sound; may be physiological in younger people.</li><li><strong>S4:</strong> Late-diastolic sound involving atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle; typically absent in atrial fibrillation.</li><li><strong>Murmurs:</strong> Audible flow-induced vibrations; assess timing, direction and affected valve.</li><li><strong>Semilunar stenosis / AV regurgitation:</strong> Usually <strong>systolic</strong> murmurs.</li><li><strong>AV stenosis / semilunar regurgitation:</strong> Usually <strong>diastolic</strong> murmurs.</li><li><strong>Auscultation:</strong> Aortic—second right; pulmonary—second left; tricuspid—lower left sternal border; mitral—apex.</li><li><strong>Auscultation areas are listening points</strong>, not the exact anatomical valve locations.</li><li><strong>Bruits and nonvalvular murmurs:</strong> Can accompany abnormal vascular connections, turbulent flow or high-flow states.</li><li><strong>Continuous PDA murmur:</strong> Flow can persist because the aorta-to-pulmonary artery pressure difference remains present through much of the cardiac cycle.</li></ol>","plain":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 27–34, with related concepts from slides 18–19. Supplementary explanation: mechanisms of S3 and S4, auscultation landmarks, and applying valve physiology to murmur timing. 1. Why does the heart produce sounds? During the cardiac cycle, pressure changes make heart valves close, and blood accelerates or decelerates inside the heart. Sudden changes in blood motion set the valve apparatus, surrounding tissues and blood into vibration . Some of these vibrations travel through the chest wall and can be heard with a stethoscope. Heart sounds are therefore not simply leaflets hitting one another . The two principal sounds normally heard are S1 and S2 . Sound Main valve event When it occurs S1 — “lub” Mitral and tricuspid closure Beginning of ventricular systole S2 — “dub” Aortic and pulmonary closure Beginning of ventricular diastole S1 AV valves close Systole Ventricular pressure and ejection S2 Semilunar valves close Diastole Relaxation and filling Listen to normal S1 and S2 REAL HEART-SOUND RECORDING · 19 SECONDS Normal S1–S2 sequence Listen for two sounds per beat. The shorter S1-to-S2 interval is systole; the usually longer S2-to-next-S1 interval is diastole at a normal resting rate. Listen Source / alternative playback James Heilman, MD · CC0 public-domain dedication . Audio is loaded only when selected; the explanation above works without it. 2. Why does S1 occur before S2? S1: the start of ventricular systole Ventricular depolarisation (QRS) is followed by myocardial contraction, raising ventricular pressure. Once ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure, the AV valves close . Their closure and the associated sudden changes in blood motion produce S1 , around the onset of isovolumetric contraction . S2: the end of ventricular ejection Later, ventricular force and pressure decline. When aortic and pulmonary arterial pressures exceed the corresponding ventricular pressures, the semilunar valves close . This produces S2 , around the beginning of isovolumetric relaxation. The important pressure logic is: S1 follows reversal of the atrial-to-ventricular pressure gradient; S2 follows reversal of the ventricular-to-arterial pressure gradient. Closure generates audible vibrations; a valve does not make a sound simply because it is open. The lecturer gives illustrative durations of approximately 0.15 s for S1 and 0.12 s for S2 . Sound duration and intensity can vary. 3. Why does S2 sometimes split with inspiration? S2 has two components: A2 (aortic valve closure) and P2 (pulmonary valve closure). Usually A2 precedes P2 by a short interval. During inspiration, more negative intrathoracic pressure promotes systemic venous return to the right heart . Right ventricular filling rises, and right ventricular ejection may last slightly longer. This delays P2 relative to A2 , making the two components more clearly separated. Respiratory changes in pulmonary venous return and left ventricular timing can contribute as well. Expiration A2 · P2 Short separation; may sound single Inspiration A2 ···· P2 Longer separation: physiological splitting The important point is widening of the A2–P2 interval during inspiration , not that the two valves necessarily close at precisely the same time during expiration. More complex patterns of splitting are advanced clinical material beyond this lecturer's primary scope. 4. What causes S3 and S4? Both are additional low-frequency sounds in diastole , but they correspond to different phases of filling . S3 — Early diastole: rapid ventricular filling S3 occurs shortly after S2 , when blood enters the ventricle rapidly. Rapid filling and sudden deceleration of blood can set ventricular structures vibrating. S3 can be physiological in children and younger adults, and during pregnancy. In an older or symptomatic adult, it can indicate volume overload or ventricular dysfunction, including heart failure. S4 — Late diastole: atrial contraction against resistance S4 occurs shortly before S1 , during atrial contraction. If the ventricle is relatively stiff and noncompliant, the additional blood delivered by atrial systole encounters greater resistance to filling, generating low-frequency vibrations. S4 is usually abnormal in adults. A typical atrial S4 is absent in atrial fibrillation because there is no effective coordinated atrial contraction to produce it. Feature S3 S4 Timing Early diastole, just after S2 Late diastole, just before S1 Trigger Rapid ventricular filling Atrial contraction Interpretation Sometimes normal in younger people; may indicate overload/dysfunction in older adults Often suggests a stiff ventricle in adults Needs atrial contraction? No Yes Audio interpretation: A new low-pitched sound just after S2 suggests S3 ; one just before S1 suggests S4 . Our normal recording above demonstrates S1/S2, not a verified S3 or S4 example. Separate recordings should be added only after checking their identity and rights. 5. Why do heart sounds change in intensity? Sound intensity depends on valve motion, pressure changes, the rate at which blood flow is stopped, and how well the vibrations travel to the stethoscope. For example, A2 may be accentuated in systemic hypertension , and P2 in pulmonary hypertension , because higher arterial pressure can produce more energetic closing events. However, severe valve immobility may make a closure sound softer despite disease. Thus, a loud or soft sound alone cannot reliably establish disease severity . 6. What makes a murmur? A heart murmur is a relatively sustained sound produced when blood flow and cardiac structures generate audible vibrations, often in association with disturbed or turbulent flow. Examples of conditions that change the flow include: Stenosis: A valve narrows, so blood has difficulty passing forward through it. Regurgitation (incompetence): A valve fails to close properly and allows blood to leak backward . High-flow states: For example, anaemia can increase flow or reduce blood viscosity enough to produce a murmur despite structurally normal valves. To predict when a murmur occurs, first identify the direction of abnormal flow and whether that flow happens during ventricular systole or diastole . 7. Predict a murmur's timing without memorising a list Remember the normal valve pattern: During AV valves (mitral/tricuspid) Semilunar valves (aortic/pulmonary) Ventricular systole Should be closed Should be open for ejection Ventricular diastole Should be open for filling Should be closed Systolic murmurs Aortic or pulmonary stenosis: These valves should be open during ventricular ejection. A narrowed opening obstructs forward systolic flow , so turbulent high-velocity flow can produce a systolic ejection murmur. Mitral or tricuspid regurgitation: These AV valves should be closed during ventricular contraction. If they leak, blood moves backward from ventricle to atrium during systole , creating a systolic regurgitant murmur. Diastolic murmurs Mitral or tricuspid stenosis: AV valves should open during ventricular filling. A narrowed opening obstructs forward flow from atrium to ventricle during diastole , causing a diastolic murmur. Aortic or pulmonary regurgitation: Semilunar valves should remain closed during diastole. If they leak, arterial pressure drives backward flow into the ventricle during diastole , causing a diastolic murmur. Murmur timing at a glance Valve group Stenosis Regurgitation Aortic / pulmonary Systolic (forward outflow obstruction) Diastolic (backflow into ventricle) Mitral / tricuspid Diastolic (forward filling obstruction) Systolic (backflow into atrium) To solve a new question, ask which valve should be open or shut during the abnormal flow? This explains the timing. Murmur loudness alone is not a dependable measure of lesion severity; location, character, radiation and other signs also matter. 8. Where do we listen to each heart valve? The best listening points on the chest are not necessarily directly over the anatomical valve openings . Vibrations are transmitted through blood and cardiac tissues. Auscultation area Surface landmark Aortic Second right intercostal space, near sternum Pulmonary Second left intercostal space, near sternum Tricuspid Lower left sternal border Mitral Apex , commonly fifth left intercostal space near midclavicular line Figure 01. Cardiac auscultation areas. Navy = aortic; yellow = pulmonary; red = tricuspid; green = mitral; light blue = Erb's point (supplementary). Vinne2, after Ickle and Gray's Anatomy · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. A stethoscope's diaphragm transmits many higher-frequency sounds well (e.g. S1, S2 and many murmurs). Its bell , applied lightly, can help detect low-frequency S3, S4 and some diastolic murmurs. This is a supplementary clinical technique. 9. Why can bruits or murmurs arise elsewhere? A bruit is an abnormal vascular sound heard over a vessel when flow generates audible vibrations. The lecture also names conditions that can produce abnormal cardiovascular sounds: Condition Mechanism Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) Aorta-to-pulmonary artery flow may continue during both systole and diastole, producing a continuous murmur. Ventricular septal defect (VSD) Abnormal flow between ventricles commonly produces a systolic murmur. Arteriovenous fistula High-velocity artery-to-vein flow can create a bruit. Anaemia Reduced viscosity and increased flow can cause a functional murmur without valve stenosis. Vascular aneurysm Altered vessel geometry may disturb flow; not every aneurysm produces a bruit. Hypervascular goitre Increased thyroid blood flow can cause a bruit over the gland. The common mechanism is flow-induced vibration , but its timing depends on the relevant pressure gradient and anatomy. Understanding Check 1. A sound is heard at the beginning of ventricular systole. Which valves are mainly responsible, and what pressure change closes them? Reveal explanation S1, mainly from mitral and tricuspid valve closure. Early ventricular contraction raises ventricular pressure above atrial pressure, reversing the gradient across the AV valves. Their closure abruptly alters blood motion and generates vibrations heard as S1. 2. Why does physiological splitting of S2 increase with inspiration? Reveal explanation Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart. Greater right ventricular filling can prolong ejection and delay pulmonary valve closure (P2) relative to aortic closure (A2). The A2–P2 interval widens. Expiration usually narrows the separation; the valves are not required to close at exactly the same instant. 3. An extra sound occurs shortly after S2 during rapid ventricular filling. Identify it and explain when it may be physiological. Reveal explanation S3. Rapid early-diastolic filling and deceleration produce vibrations in ventricular structures. An S3 may be normal in children or young adults, but in an older symptomatic patient it can suggest ventricular overload or dysfunction. Timing and clinical context are essential. 4. A low-frequency sound just before S1 disappears when a patient develops atrial fibrillation. Explain the likely sound and why it disappears. Reveal explanation S4. It occurs in late diastole when effective atrial contraction forces blood into a relatively stiff ventricle, producing vibrations. In atrial fibrillation, coordinated atrial contraction is lost; without the atrial kick, the usual mechanism producing an atrial S4 is absent. 5. Why can mitral stenosis cause a diastolic murmur but mitral regurgitation cause a systolic murmur? Reveal explanation Stenosis obstructs normal forward filling: the mitral valve should be open in diastole for left atrium-to-ventricle flow, so narrowing generates a diastolic murmur. Regurgitation permits backward flow: the valve should be closed during ventricular systole, but leakage sends blood into the left atrium and produces a systolic murmur. 6. A valve leaks blood from the aorta back into the left ventricle. Name the lesion and predict the murmur's timing. Reveal explanation Aortic regurgitation; diastolic murmur. During ventricular relaxation, aortic pressure is greater than left ventricular pressure and the aortic valve should be closed. If it is incompetent, blood leaks backward into the ventricle during diastole and generates an audible murmur. 7. Why might an anaemic patient have a systolic flow murmur even when all four valves are structurally normal? Reveal explanation Anaemia reduces blood viscosity and often increases cardiac output. Faster flow through otherwise normal valves may generate audible flow-related vibrations, often as an ejection-type systolic murmur. A murmur therefore does not automatically mean a damaged or narrowed valve. 8. A stethoscope is placed over the second right intercostal space. Which valve area is being examined, and why is this not necessarily the valve's anatomical position? Reveal explanation The aortic auscultation area. Valve vibrations travel with blood and through surrounding tissues, so the point at which they are best transmitted to the chest wall is not necessarily directly over the anatomical aortic valve. 9. Listen to the normal heart recording. Which interval corresponds to ventricular systole, and why? Reveal explanation The S1-to-S2 interval is ventricular systole. S1 follows AV valve closure as ventricular pressure rises; S2 follows semilunar valve closure when ventricular ejection ends. The S2-to-next-S1 interval is ventricular diastole. The recording illustrates normal S1 and S2, not a proven S3 or S4. 10. A continuous murmur is heard in a patient with a patent ductus arteriosus. Why does the abnormal flow persist across both parts of the cardiac cycle? Reveal explanation A patent ductus joins the aorta to the pulmonary artery. Because aortic pressure is typically greater than pulmonary arterial pressure during both systole and diastole, blood may continue to cross the connection throughout the cycle. Thus the sound can be continuous , unlike a murmur confined to one phase. Chapter Essentials S1: Mostly due to mitral and tricuspid closure at the start of ventricular systole. S2: Mostly due to aortic and pulmonary closure at the start of ventricular diastole. S2 splitting: Inspiration normally widens the A2–P2 interval, largely through relative delay of P2. S3: Early-diastolic rapid-filling sound; may be physiological in younger people. S4: Late-diastolic sound involving atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle; typically absent in atrial fibrillation. Murmurs: Audible flow-induced vibrations; assess timing, direction and affected valve. Semilunar stenosis / AV regurgitation: Usually systolic murmurs. AV stenosis / semilunar regurgitation: Usually diastolic murmurs. Auscultation: Aortic—second right; pulmonary—second left; tricuspid—lower left sternal border; mitral—apex. Auscultation areas are listening points , not the exact anatomical valve locations. Bruits and nonvalvular murmurs: Can accompany abnormal vascular connections, turbulent flow or high-flow states. Continuous PDA murmur: Flow can persist because the aorta-to-pulmonary artery pressure difference remains present through much of the cardiac cycle."},{"slug":"ecg","title":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms","group":"Heart","source":"Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf","pages":"1-14; extra diagrams Circulation 3 35-38","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record","title":"1. What does an ECG actually record?"},{"id":"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards","title":"2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?"},{"id":"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads","title":"3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?"},{"id":"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference","title":"4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?"},{"id":"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events","title":"5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events"},{"id":"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage","title":"6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?"},{"id":"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing","title":"7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing"},{"id":"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary","title":"8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"electrocardiography-and-basic-rhythms\">Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf, slides 1–14. <strong>Supplementary foundations:</strong> vector reasoning, accurate lead positions, basic rhythm assessment and AV block recognition.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-does-an-ecg-actually-record\">1. What does an ECG actually record?</h2>\n<p>Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac <strong>electrical activation</strong>. As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The <strong><span data-term=\"ecg\">electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG)</span></strong> is a recording of this electrical activity over time.</p>\n<p>An ECG <strong>does not directly measure</strong> contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies <em>ventricular depolarisation</em>, not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse.</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-concept-line\" aria-label=\"Electrical and mechanical sequence\"><span>Electrical activation</span><b>→</b><span>Myocardial contraction</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Blood movement</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why does this distinction matter?</strong> An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-the-tracing-move-upwards-or-downwards\">2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards?</h2>\n<p>An <strong>electrode</strong> is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A <strong>lead</strong> is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads.</p>\n<p>As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical <strong><span data-term=\"ecg-vector\">vector</span></strong>. The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>A depolarisation wave travels…</th><th>Recorded tendency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Towards</strong> a lead's positive direction</td><td>Positive (upward) deflection</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Away from</strong> its positive direction</td><td>Negative (downward) deflection</td></tr><tr><td>Roughly <strong>perpendicular</strong></td><td>Small or biphasic deflection</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> These rules describe <strong>depolarisation</strong>. Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation.</p>\n<p>An upward deflection is <strong>not</strong> a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-can-ten-electrodes-produce-twelve-ecg-leads\">3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</h2>\n<p>The standard <strong>12-lead ECG</strong> uses <strong>10 physical electrodes</strong>: four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lead group</th><th>Views</th><th>Names</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bipolar limb</td><td>3</td><td>I, II, III</td></tr><tr><td>Augmented limb</td><td>3</td><td>aVR, aVL, aVF</td></tr><tr><td>Precordial (chest)</td><td>6</td><td>V1–V6</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The <strong>right-leg (RL)</strong> electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the <strong>frontal plane</strong>, while the six chest leads add views in the <strong>horizontal plane</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-bipolar-limb-leads\">The three bipolar limb leads</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Lead</th><th>Negative electrode</th><th>Positive electrode</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>I</strong></td><td>Right arm (RA)</td><td>Left arm (LA)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>II</strong></td><td>Right arm (RA)</td><td>Left leg (LL)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>III</strong></td><td>Left arm (LA)</td><td>Left leg (LL)</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These directions form <strong>Einthoven's triangle</strong> (lecturer slide 3). Their simultaneous voltages obey <strong>Einthoven's law</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Einthoven's law</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>II</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>III</mi></msub></math></div>\n<p>For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure <strong>+0.8 mV</strong> (ignoring recording error).</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-einthoven\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" alt=\"Diagram of Einthoven triangle connecting arm and leg lead positions with bipolar Leads I, II and III.\" width=\"992\" height=\"744\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions.</strong> Relate the positive and negative recording directions to the three limb leads. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Kychot · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECG-Einthoven-triangle.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"the-augmented-limb-leads\">The augmented limb leads</h3>\n<p><strong>aVR</strong> views towards the right arm, <strong>aVL</strong> towards the left arm and <strong>aVF</strong> towards the left leg/foot (lecturer slide 4). Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-six-chest-leads\">The six chest leads</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Electrode</th><th>Standard position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>V1</strong></td><td>Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V2</strong></td><td>Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V3</strong></td><td>Midway between V2 and V4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V4</strong></td><td>Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V5</strong></td><td>Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4</td></tr><tr><td><strong>V6</strong></td><td>Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>For accurate placement, locate <strong>V4 before V3</strong>, then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-small\" id=\"ecg-precordial\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c4/Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" alt=\"Anterior torso and rib cage with six labelled ECG chest electrodes V1 to V6 in their correct anatomical positions.\" width=\"287\" height=\"252\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions.</strong> The diagram shows where electrodes go; the table adds intercostal-space landmarks. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Jmarchn · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Precordial_Leads_3.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY-SA 3.0</a> · unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2 id=\"4-waves-segments-and-intervals-what-is-the-difference\">4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference?</h2>\n<p>The ECG tracing is made up of <strong>waves</strong>, <strong>segments</strong> and <strong>intervals</strong>. These words are not interchangeable:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Wave:</strong> A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T).</li><li><strong>Segment:</strong> The part <strong>between</strong> specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment).</li><li><strong>Interval:</strong> A measured span including <strong>one or more waves</strong> and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval).</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration ecg-figure-signal\" id=\"ecg-normal-wave\">\n<a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9e/SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" alt=\"Labeled normal ECG with P wave, QRS complex, T wave, PR segment and interval, ST segment and QT interval.\" width=\"512\" height=\"512\"></a>\n<figcaption><strong>Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals.</strong> Compare the <em>PR segment</em> (end of P to start of QRS) with the <em>PR interval</em> (start of P to start of QRS). Open to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SinusRhythmLabels.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikimedia Commons</a> · public domain.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h3 id=\"p-wave-atrial-depolarisation\">P wave — Atrial depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The <strong>P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation</strong>, not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. The lecturer gives an illustrative normal P-wave duration of <strong>0.08–0.10 seconds</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"pr-interval-time-from-atrial-to-ventricular-depolarisation\">PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>PR interval</strong> starts at the <strong>beginning of the P wave</strong> and ends at the <strong>beginning of QRS</strong>. It is normally about <strong>0.12–0.20 seconds</strong>. It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. <strong>The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay.</strong></p>\n<p>In contrast, the <strong>PR segment</strong> runs from the <strong>end</strong> of P to the <strong>start</strong> of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"qrs-complex-ventricular-depolarisation\">QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QRS complex</strong> represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately <strong>0.06–0.10 seconds</strong>; a duration <strong>under 0.12 seconds</strong> is a common broad normal clinical criterion.</p>\n<p><strong>Q</strong> is the first negative deflection before a positive R; <strong>R</strong> is a positive deflection; <strong>S</strong> is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters.</p>\n<p><strong>Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately?</strong> It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent.</p>\n<h3 id=\"st-segment-a-nearly-flat-line-during-continued-ventricular-electrical-activity\">ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity</h3>\n<p>The <strong>ST segment</strong> extends from the <strong>end of QRS (J point)</strong> to the <strong>beginning of the T wave</strong>. During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Flat does not mean inactive.</strong> The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context.</p>\n<h3 id=\"t-wave-ventricular-repolarisation\">T wave — Ventricular repolarisation</h3>\n<p>The <strong>T wave</strong> reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"u-wave-sometimes-present-origin-uncertain\">U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain</h3>\n<p>A small <strong>U wave</strong> may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. <strong>Its precise source is still debated</strong>, so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact.</p>\n<h3 id=\"qt-interval-ventricular-activation-through-recovery\">QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery</h3>\n<p>The <strong>QT interval</strong> extends from the <strong>start of QRS to the end of the T wave</strong>. It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. The lecturer gives an illustrative range of <strong>0.2–0.4 seconds</strong>, noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary: heart-rate correction.</strong> Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Bazett's QT correction</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>QT</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>QT</mi><msqrt><mi>RR</mi></msqrt></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context.</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"5-connect-the-ecg-to-mechanical-events\">5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>ECG feature</th><th>Electrical event</th><th>Mechanical connection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P wave</td><td>Atrial depolarisation</td><td>Atrial contraction follows</td></tr><tr><td>PR interval</td><td>Atrial-to-ventricular activation time</td><td>Includes the AV delay that supports final filling</td></tr><tr><td>QRS complex</td><td>Ventricular depolarisation</td><td>Ventricular contraction follows</td></tr><tr><td>ST segment</td><td>Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised</td><td>Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection</td></tr><tr><td>T wave</td><td>Ventricular repolarisation</td><td>Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: <strong>the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events</strong>. The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-do-ecg-paper-squares-represent-time-and-voltage\">6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's standard settings are <strong>25 mm/s</strong> (paper speed) and <strong>10 mm/mV</strong> (voltage gain).</p>\n<div class=\"ecg-calibration-mini\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"ECG paper calibration\"><div class=\"ecg-paper-sample\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><div><strong>At 25 mm/s</strong><p>1 small 1-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.04 s</strong><br>1 large 5-mm horizontal square = <strong>0.20 s</strong></p><p><strong>At 10 mm/mV:</strong> 1 small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>; 1 large vertical square = <strong>0.5 mV</strong>.</p></div></div>\n<p><strong>Why is this true?</strong> At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans <strong>1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s</strong>; five such squares span <strong>0.20 s</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse axes:</strong> Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-calculate-heart-rate-from-the-tracing\">7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing</h2>\n<p>For a <strong>regular rhythm</strong>, measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">General relationship</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HR</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mrow><mi>RR</mi><mo> </mo><mtext>in seconds</mtext></mrow></mfrac></math></div>\n<p>At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">25 mm/s · regular rhythm</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HR</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1500</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>small squares</mtext></msub></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves.</span></div>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-the-lecturers-22-small-squares\">Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares</h3>\n<p>If an R–R interval spans <strong>22 small squares</strong>:</p>\n<p><strong>RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min</strong>, equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68.</p>\n<p><strong>Important observation about slide 14:</strong> Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation <strong>1500 ÷ 22</strong> gives the rate represented by <em>that particular interval</em>. It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-changes-if-the-rhythm-is-irregular\">What changes if the rhythm is irregular?</h3>\n<p>When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a <strong>6-second strip</strong> and multiply by <strong>10</strong> (supplementary).</p>\n<p><em>Example:</em> 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average <strong>80 beats/min</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-if-paper-speed-is-50-mms\">What if paper speed is 50 mm/s?</h3>\n<p>Each small horizontal square then represents <strong>0.02 s</strong>, so the shortcuts become <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> or <strong>600 ÷ large squares</strong>. Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-basic-rhythm-assessment-supplementary\">8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary)</h2>\n<p>The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose.</p>\n<p>Ask four questions: <strong>Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate?</strong></p>\n<p>A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, <strong>60–100 beats/min</strong> is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances.</p>\n<h3 id=\"recognition-of-av-conduction-disturbance\">Recognition of AV conduction disturbance</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PR >0.20 s</strong>, every P followed by QRS</td><td>First-degree AV block</td></tr><tr><td>Some P waves <strong>not followed by QRS</strong></td><td>Second-degree AV block</td></tr><tr><td>P waves and QRS complexes follow <strong>independent rhythms</strong></td><td>Complete (third-degree) AV block</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice.</p>\n<p>A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: <strong>electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views.</strong> Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Predominantly negative (downward).</strong> A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Lead II = +0.8 mV</strong>, because V<sub>II</sub> = V<sub>I</sub> + V<sub>III</sub>. This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation.</strong> The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>The P wave itself.</strong> The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the <em>beginning</em> of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does <strong>not</strong> indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = <strong>0.88 s</strong>. Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ <strong>68 beats/min</strong> (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is <strong>3000 ÷ small squares</strong> (or 600 ÷ large squares).</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Approximately 80 beats/min.</strong> Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>First-degree AV block.</strong> PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>ECG:</strong> Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction.</li><li><strong>Twelve leads, ten electrodes:</strong> Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads.</li><li><strong>Vector rule:</strong> Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection.</li><li><strong>Lead placement:</strong> I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks.</li><li><strong>P wave:</strong> Atrial myocardial depolarisation; <strong>QRS:</strong> ventricular depolarisation; <strong>T wave:</strong> ventricular repolarisation.</li><li><strong>PR interval:</strong> Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay.</li><li><strong>QRS duration:</strong> About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically.</li><li><strong>ST segment:</strong> Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity.</li><li><strong>QT interval:</strong> Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled.</li><li><strong>Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV:</strong> Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s.</li><li><strong>Heart rate:</strong> Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms.</li><li><strong>Basic rhythm assessment:</strong> Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output.</li></ol>","plain":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms Primary lecture: Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf, slides 1–14. Supplementary foundations: vector reasoning, accurate lead positions, basic rhythm assessment and AV block recognition. 1. What does an ECG actually record? Every heartbeat is preceded by cardiac electrical activation . As myocardial cells depolarise and repolarise, electrical potential differences are produced and conducted through body tissues. Electrodes on the skin detect these differences. The electrocardiogram (ECG, or EKG) is a recording of this electrical activity over time. An ECG does not directly measure contraction strength, pressure, stroke volume or cardiac output. For example, a QRS complex signifies ventricular depolarisation , not the actual volume of blood being ejected. Electrical activity can be present without an effective pulse. Electrical activation → Myocardial contraction → Pressure changes → Blood movement Why does this distinction matter? An ECG can show organised electrical complexes even if ventricular contraction produces little or no effective circulation. 2. Why can the tracing move upwards or downwards? An electrode is a physical contact on the patient's skin. A lead is a particular electrical view calculated from electrode potentials. Different leads view the same heart from different directions, so the same heartbeat can produce different shapes across leads. As activation spreads through the myocardium, we can represent its combined direction and magnitude by a changing electrical vector . The ECG lead detects the part of that vector directed along its positive recording direction. A depolarisation wave travels… Recorded tendency Towards a lead's positive direction Positive (upward) deflection Away from its positive direction Negative (downward) deflection Roughly perpendicular Small or biphasic deflection For example, much of normal ventricular depolarisation is directed inferiorly and towards the patient's left. Since Lead II views the heart in that general direction, its QRS complex is often predominantly upright. Important: These rules describe depolarisation . Repolarisation has a different electrical polarity: a repolarisation wave travelling towards a positive electrode tends to produce a negative deflection. The normal ventricular T wave can therefore be upright even though it represents repolarisation, because the spread and polarity of repolarisation differ from depolarisation. An upward deflection is not a drawing of the heart moving upwards—it is the electrical signal's projection onto that lead. 3. Why can ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? The standard 12-lead ECG uses 10 physical electrodes : four on the limbs and six on the chest. Their potentials are combined to produce twelve electrical views. Lead group Views Names Bipolar limb 3 I, II, III Augmented limb 3 aVR, aVL, aVF Precordial (chest) 6 V1–V6 The right-leg (RL) electrode mainly helps suppress electrical interference; it is not a thirteenth lead. The six limb leads observe the heart in the frontal plane , while the six chest leads add views in the horizontal plane . The three bipolar limb leads Lead Negative electrode Positive electrode I Right arm (RA) Left arm (LA) II Right arm (RA) Left leg (LL) III Left arm (LA) Left leg (LL) These directions form Einthoven's triangle (lecturer slide 3). Their simultaneous voltages obey Einthoven's law : Einthoven's law V II = V I + V III For instance, if Lead I measures +0.5 mV and Lead III measures +0.3 mV at the same instant, Lead II should measure +0.8 mV (ignoring recording error). Figure 01. Bipolar limb-lead directions. Relate the positive and negative recording directions to the three limb leads. Kychot · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. The augmented limb leads aVR views towards the right arm, aVL towards the left arm and aVF towards the left leg/foot (lecturer slide 4). Each uses one positive limb electrode and a reference derived from the other limb electrodes. The normal mean ventricular depolarisation vector usually moves away from aVR, so its QRS complex is commonly predominantly negative. The six chest leads Electrode Standard position V1 Fourth right intercostal space, right sternal border V2 Fourth left intercostal space, left sternal border V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 Fifth left intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 Anterior axillary line, horizontal with V4 V6 Midaxillary line, horizontal with V4 For accurate placement, locate V4 before V3 , then put V3 midway between V2 and V4. Incorrect placement can alter the tracing and create misleading findings. Figure 02. V1–V6 chest electrode positions. The diagram shows where electrodes go; the table adds intercostal-space landmarks. Jmarchn · Wikimedia Commons · CC BY-SA 3.0 · unmodified. 4. Waves, segments and intervals: what is the difference? The ECG tracing is made up of waves , segments and intervals . These words are not interchangeable: Wave: A deflection associated with a change in the heart's summed electrical activity (e.g. P or T). Segment: The part between specified waves (e.g. PR segment or ST segment). Interval: A measured span including one or more waves and often a segment (e.g. PR or QT interval). Figure 03. Normal ECG waves and intervals. Compare the PR segment (end of P to start of QRS) with the PR interval (start of P to start of QRS). Open to enlarge. Agateller (Anthony Atkielski), SVG conversion by atom · Wikimedia Commons · public domain. P wave — Atrial depolarisation The SA node initiates an impulse, which spreads through atrial myocardium. The P wave represents atrial myocardial depolarisation , not merely the firing of the tiny SA node. Atrial contraction follows. The lecturer gives an illustrative normal P-wave duration of 0.08–0.10 seconds . PR interval — Time from atrial to ventricular depolarisation The PR interval starts at the beginning of the P wave and ends at the beginning of QRS . It is normally about 0.12–0.20 seconds . It includes atrial depolarisation and conduction through the AV node and His–Purkinje system up to the start of ventricular activation. The whole PR interval is not simply the AV nodal delay. In contrast, the PR segment runs from the end of P to the start of QRS. Keep the starting points distinct when answering exam questions. QRS complex — Ventricular depolarisation The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarisation. Ventricular muscle mass is much greater than atrial mass, so its summed signal is typically larger than the P wave. The lecturer quotes a QRS duration of approximately 0.06–0.10 seconds ; a duration under 0.12 seconds is a common broad normal clinical criterion. Q is the first negative deflection before a positive R; R is a positive deflection; S is a negative deflection after an R. A particular lead need not show all three letters. Why don't we see atrial repolarisation separately? It usually overlaps with ventricular depolarisation, and its smaller signal is masked by QRS—not absent. ST segment — A nearly flat line during continued ventricular electrical activity The ST segment extends from the end of QRS (J point) to the beginning of the T wave . During much of this interval, ventricular myocardial cells are in relatively similar depolarised states, so the lead often detects little net voltage difference. Flat does not mean inactive. The ST segment roughly corresponds to the ventricular action-potential plateau, not to a period when the cells have returned to rest. ST elevation and depression can have clinical importance, including myocardial ischaemia, but require appropriate context. T wave — Ventricular repolarisation The T wave reflects the spread of ventricular repolarisation. Repolarisation is not instantaneous and may give a broader waveform than QRS. The normal T-wave direction reflects both the spatial sequence of recovery and the polarity of repolarisation. U wave — Sometimes present, origin uncertain A small U wave may follow the T wave. The lecture calls it the final remnants of ventricular repolarisation; this is a traditional teaching description. Its precise source is still debated , so we should not present a specific proposed mechanism as settled fact. QT interval — Ventricular activation through recovery The QT interval extends from the start of QRS to the end of the T wave . It includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. The lecturer gives an illustrative range of 0.2–0.4 seconds , noting that QT varies with heart rate. Its duration shortens or lengthens with heart rate, so a single fixed normal QT value is misleading. Supplementary: heart-rate correction. Bazett's formula is a commonly taught approximation, although it is less reliable at unusually low or high heart rates. Bazett's QT correction QT c = QT RR Use QT and RR measurements in seconds; interpret the result in clinical context. 5. Connect the ECG to mechanical events ECG feature Electrical event Mechanical connection P wave Atrial depolarisation Atrial contraction follows PR interval Atrial-to-ventricular activation time Includes the AV delay that supports final filling QRS complex Ventricular depolarisation Ventricular contraction follows ST segment Much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised Sustained ventricular contraction/ejection T wave Ventricular repolarisation Mechanical relaxation develops, but not instantaneously This connects directly with Chapter 04's Wiggers diagram: the ECG measures the electrical changes; the Wiggers diagram also shows pressure, volume and valve events . The electrical and mechanical curves are connected but are not interchangeable. 6. How do ECG paper squares represent time and voltage? The lecturer's standard settings are 25 mm/s (paper speed) and 10 mm/mV (voltage gain). At 25 mm/s 1 small 1-mm horizontal square = 0.04 s 1 large 5-mm horizontal square = 0.20 s At 10 mm/mV: 1 small vertical square = 0.1 mV ; 1 large vertical square = 0.5 mV . Why is this true? At 25 mm/s, the paper moves 25 mm in one second. Each 1-mm horizontal square therefore spans 1 ÷ 25 = 0.04 s ; five such squares span 0.20 s . Do not confuse axes: Horizontal spacing measures time; vertical height measures voltage. A taller QRS does not mean a longer QRS duration. 7. Calculate heart rate from the tracing For a regular rhythm , measure the R–R interval. Since 60 seconds make one minute: General relationship HR = 60 RR in seconds At 25 mm/s, the common shortcuts follow from 1500 small or 300 large squares passing per minute: 25 mm/s · regular rhythm HR = 1500 N small squares Or 300 ÷ number of large squares between consecutive R waves. Worked example: the lecturer's 22 small squares If an R–R interval spans 22 small squares : RR duration = 22 × 0.04 s = 0.88 s Heart rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min , equivalently 1500 ÷ 22 ≈ 68. Important observation about slide 14: Not all its R–R intervals appear equal. The calculation 1500 ÷ 22 gives the rate represented by that particular interval . It should not automatically be called the average rate for the whole irregular tracing. What changes if the rhythm is irregular? When R–R intervals vary, a single pair of R waves is insufficient to estimate the average. One simple method is to count ventricular complexes in a 6-second strip and multiply by 10 (supplementary). Example: 8 QRS complexes in 6 seconds → approximate average 80 beats/min . What if paper speed is 50 mm/s? Each small horizontal square then represents 0.02 s , so the shortcuts become 3000 ÷ small squares or 600 ÷ large squares . Always check the paper speed before using a shortcut. 8. Basic rhythm assessment (supplementary) The lecture's central focus is ECG fundamentals, not a full arrhythmia course. Still, an initial rhythm assessment connects the tracing to its purpose. Ask four questions: Is the ventricular rate appropriate? Are the R–R intervals regular? Are consistent P waves present before QRS? Is conduction through PR and QRS appropriate? A normal sinus rhythm generally shows P waves of consistent shape, with an appropriate relationship between each P and the following QRS and a reasonably regular rhythm. For healthy adults at rest, 60–100 beats/min is a common reference range, though slower rates can be normal in particular circumstances. Recognition of AV conduction disturbance Pattern Interpretation PR >0.20 s , every P followed by QRS First-degree AV block Some P waves not followed by QRS Second-degree AV block P waves and QRS complexes follow independent rhythms Complete (third-degree) AV block Mechanism: These patterns concern how the atrial impulse is delayed or fails to reach ventricular myocardium. Their detailed subtypes are supplementary and require additional ECG practice. A normal-looking ECG cannot guarantee effective cardiac output: electrical activity does not prove adequate mechanical pumping . Understanding Check 1. How can skin electrodes record activity generated inside the heart? Reveal explanation Cardiac depolarisation and repolarisation produce electrical potential differences that are conducted through body tissues. Electrodes detect differences between skin locations, so the ECG can record the combined electrical activity without touching the myocardium. It records electricity, not the strength of contraction. 2. Why do ten electrodes produce twelve ECG leads? Reveal explanation Electrodes are recording contacts; leads are calculated electrical views. Four limb electrodes and six precordial electrodes provide three bipolar limb views, three augmented limb views and six chest views. The right-leg electrode helps reduce interference rather than producing another lead. 3. If a depolarisation vector moves away from the positive direction of Lead II, what deflection is expected? Reveal explanation Predominantly negative (downward). A lead displays the projection of the heart's electrical vector onto its positive recording direction. Depolarisation moving away from that direction generates a negative deflection; moving towards it produces a positive one. 4. Explain what Einthoven's law means if Leads I and III measure +0.5 and +0.3 mV at the same instant. Reveal explanation Lead II = +0.8 mV , because V II = V I + V III . This relationship follows from how the three bipolar limb leads use the right-arm, left-arm and left-leg electrode potentials. The values must be compared at the same instant. 5. The QRS complex is larger than the P wave. Which electrical event does each represent, and why is the difference expected? Reveal explanation QRS is ventricular depolarisation; P is atrial depolarisation. The ventricles have greater muscle mass, so the summed surface electrical signal during ventricular activation is usually much larger. The small atrial repolarisation signal is normally hidden within QRS. 6. A student measures the PR segment and calls it the PR interval. What is missing? Reveal explanation The P wave itself. The PR segment starts at the end of P and ends at the beginning of QRS; the PR interval starts at the beginning of P. Therefore the PR interval includes atrial depolarisation plus subsequent conduction time, not just AV nodal delay. 7. Why can the ST segment lie near the baseline while most ventricular cells remain depolarised? Reveal explanation During much of the ST segment, ventricular regions are in relatively similar depolarised states. The lead therefore detects only a small net electrical potential difference. A flat segment does not indicate that the ventricle has become electrically inactive. 8. At 25 mm/s, two R waves are separated by 22 small squares. Calculate the rate. Reveal explanation Each small horizontal square lasts 0.04 s. The RR interval is 22 × 0.04 = 0.88 s . Rate = 60 ÷ 0.88 ≈ 68 beats/min (also 1500 ÷ 22). If other R–R intervals differ, this is only the beat-to-beat rate for the chosen interval. 9. Why does the 1500 rule need adjustment at a paper speed of 50 mm/s? Reveal explanation The paper travels twice as far per second, so each small square lasts 0.02 s rather than 0.04 s. There are now 3000 small squares per minute; the corresponding regular-rhythm shortcut is 3000 ÷ small squares (or 600 ÷ large squares). 10. An irregular ECG has eight QRS complexes in six seconds. Estimate its average ventricular rate. Reveal explanation Approximately 80 beats/min. Eight beats in six seconds correspond to 8 × (60 ÷ 6) = 80 beats/min. For an irregular rhythm, counting complexes over a longer interval is more representative than applying the 1500 rule to one R–R interval. 11. Each P wave is followed by QRS, but the PR interval is 0.24 seconds. What pattern does this suggest? Reveal explanation First-degree AV block. PR is prolonged beyond 0.20 s, yet each atrial impulse still conducts to the ventricles. The finding indicates delayed AV transmission, not dropped beats or complete AV dissociation. 12. Can a regular ECG tracing prove that the ventricles are pumping enough blood? Reveal explanation No. An ECG measures electrical activity. Electrical impulses may occur without effective myocardial contraction or sufficient stroke volume, so perfusion requires assessment of the pulse, blood pressure and clinical state. Chapter Essentials ECG: Records electrical potential differences detected at the body surface, not blood pressure or contraction. Twelve leads, ten electrodes: Three bipolar limb, three augmented limb and six chest leads. Vector rule: Depolarisation towards a positive lead produces a positive deflection; away produces a negative deflection. Lead placement: I, II and III use limb-electrode differences; V1–V6 have specific chest landmarks. P wave: Atrial myocardial depolarisation; QRS: ventricular depolarisation; T wave: ventricular repolarisation. PR interval: Start of P to start of QRS, normally about 0.12–0.20 s; do not confuse with PR segment or pure AV nodal delay. QRS duration: About 0.06–0.10 s in the lecturer's examples; often less than 0.12 s clinically. ST segment: Ventricular myocardium remains largely depolarised; a flat tracing does not mean inactivity. QT interval: Start of QRS to end of T; varies with heart rate. U-wave origin remains unsettled. Calibration at 25 mm/s and 10 mm/mV: Small square 0.04 s horizontally and 0.1 mV vertically; large square 0.20 s. Heart rate: Regular rhythm HR = 1500 ÷ small squares or 300 ÷ large squares at 25 mm/s; use longer strips to estimate irregular rhythms. Basic rhythm assessment: Rate, regularity, P waves, PR and QRS; ECG electrical activity does not guarantee cardiac output."},{"slug":"cardiac-output","title":"Cardiac Output and Frank-Starling Law","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"35-51","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter","title":"1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?"},{"id":"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?"},{"id":"3-what-determines-stroke-volume","title":"3. What determines stroke volume?"},{"id":"4-frankstarling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection","title":"4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?"},{"id":"5-how-do-we-read-frankstarling-curves","title":"5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?"},{"id":"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume","title":"6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?"},{"id":"7-what-changes-contractility","title":"7. What changes contractility?"},{"id":"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations","title":"8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?"},{"id":"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states","title":"9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"cardiac-output-and-the-frankstarling-law\">Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 35–51. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> intrinsic sarcomere mechanisms, precise definitions of loading, interpretation of cardiac-function curves, and clinical examples.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\">1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is the <strong>volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time</strong>, usually expressed in <strong>litres per minute (L/min)</strong>. It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat.</p>\n<p>Two quantities determine cardiac output:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Heart rate (HR):</strong> number of beats per minute.</li><li><strong>Stroke volume (SV):</strong> blood ejected from one ventricle <em>per beat</em>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac output</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>HR</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>SV</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = <strong>5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min</strong></span></div>\n<p><strong>Why multiply?</strong> Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL.</p>\n<p>The lecturer illustrates a resting output around <strong>5.5 L/min</strong>. This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-should-right-and-left-cardiac-outputs-be-approximately-equal\">Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal?</h3>\n<p>The pulmonary and systemic circulations are <strong>connected in series</strong>. Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal <strong>over time</strong>, although small moment-to-moment differences can occur.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cardiac-index-allowing-for-body-size\">Cardiac index: allowing for body size</h3>\n<p>A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. <strong>Cardiac index (CI)</strong> expresses cardiac output relative to <strong>body surface area (BSA)</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac index</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CI</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>CO</mi><mi>BSA</mi></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: <strong>CI = 3.0 L/min/m²</strong>.</span></div>\n<p>Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as <strong>L min⁻¹ m⁻²</strong>. It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\">2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?</h2>\n<p>From <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, increasing either HR or SV can raise output <strong>if the other factor remains unchanged</strong>. In a living heart, however, they are connected.</p>\n<p>Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive <strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">chronotropy</span></strong>) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). These effects can raise both HR and SV.</p>\n<p>But <strong>a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute</strong>. At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute.</p>\n<div class=\"co-impact-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Two determinants of cardiac output\"><span><strong>HR ↑</strong><small>More beats per minute</small></span><span><strong>SV ↓ markedly</strong><small>Less blood each beat</small></span><span><strong>CO may ↓</strong><small>Depends on their product</small></span></div>\n<p><strong>Reasoning rule:</strong> Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-determines-stroke-volume\">3. What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<p>From Chapter 04, <strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>: the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction.</p>\n<p>Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>The question it answers</th><th>Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"preload\">Preload</span></strong></td><td>How stretched is the ventricle before contraction?</td><td>More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits</td></tr><tr><td><strong><span data-term=\"afterload\">Afterload</span></strong></td><td>How difficult is it to eject blood?</td><td>More afterload → usually lower SV acutely</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Contractility (inotropy)</strong></td><td>How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading?</td><td>Greater contractility → higher SV</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"preload-the-starting-stretch\">Preload — the starting stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) <strong>at the end of diastole</strong>, just before contraction.</p>\n<p>More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but <strong>volume and pressure are not interchangeable</strong>, particularly in a stiff ventricle.</p>\n<p><strong>Analogy:</strong> Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping.</p>\n<h3 id=\"afterload-the-resistance-or-load-against-ejection\">Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection</h3>\n<p><strong>Afterload</strong> is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to <strong>aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress</strong>. Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload.</p>\n<p>If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject <strong>less blood in that beat</strong>, leaving a higher ESV.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contractility-how-capable-the-muscle-is-at-a-given-load\">Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load</h3>\n<p><strong>Contractility</strong> is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force <strong>when preload and afterload are held comparable</strong>. It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery.</p>\n<p><strong>Important distinction:</strong> A ventricle that contracts more forcefully <em>because it filled more</em> is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does <strong>not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-frankstarling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\">4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?</h2>\n<p>Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat.</p>\n<p>Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects <strong>length-dependent activation</strong>, including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Frank Starling mechanism\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV and fibre stretch ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Force generated ↑</span><b>→</b><span>SV ↑</span></div>\n<p>Thus, the <strong>Frank–Starling law</strong> states that <strong>within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received.</p>\n<h3 id=\"does-greater-preload-always-increase-stroke-volume\">Does greater preload always increase stroke volume?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion.</p>\n<p>Lecture slide 39 includes a <strong>dashed descending limb</strong> at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do <strong>not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-we-read-frankstarling-curves\">5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's slides 39–41 show <strong>ventricular performance</strong> plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be <strong>stroke volume</strong> or <strong>cardiac output</strong>, depending on the particular diagram.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure cardiac-output-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 295\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic stroke-volume versus end-diastolic-volume graph showing increased, normal and reduced cardiac contractility curves.\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99 229 C168 100 285 57 580 55\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 232 C188 181 275 148 580 148\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 235 C205 222 320 208 580 207\" stroke=\"#c39431\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"273\">End-diastolic volume (preload) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"130\" transform=\"rotate(-90,22,130)\">Stroke volume</text><text x=\"387\" y=\"40\">Greater contractility</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"138\">Reference</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"196\">Lower contractility</text></g></svg><p><strong>Read across one curve:</strong> changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. <strong>Compare curves vertically:</strong> at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements.</p></figure>\n<h3 id=\"a-moving-along-the-same-curve\">A. Moving <strong>along</strong> the same curve</h3>\n<p>Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves <strong>rightwards and upwards along a curve</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is primarily a change in <strong>preload</strong>, without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change.</p>\n<h3 id=\"b-shifting-between-curves\">B. Shifting <strong>between</strong> curves</h3>\n<p>Now imagine two beats with the <strong>same EDV</strong>, but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV <strong>without additional stretch</strong>. This is a change in <strong>contractile state</strong>, represented by an <strong>upward function-curve shift</strong>.</p>\n<p>A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a <strong>lower curve</strong>. Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so <strong>a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecturers-unusual-terms-heterometric-and-homometric\">The lecturer's unusual terms: heterometric and homometric</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Heterometric regulation</strong></td><td>Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload)</td><td>Frank–Starling response to increased venous return</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Homometric regulation</strong></td><td>Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length</td><td>β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Exam insight:</strong> <em>Heterometric</em> changes the starting length; <em>homometric</em> changes pumping performance <strong>without needing a length change</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume\">6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?</h2>\n<p><strong>EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood.</strong> More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Often increases EDV</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Increased blood volume</td><td>More blood available to return to the heart</td></tr><tr><td>Increased venous tone</td><td>Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart</td></tr><tr><td>Skeletal-muscle pump</td><td>Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves</td></tr><tr><td>Respiratory pump</td><td>Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return</td></tr><tr><td>Effective atrial contraction</td><td>Completes late ventricular filling</td></tr><tr><td>Longer diastolic filling time</td><td>More time is available for blood to enter</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia</td><td>Less circulating blood and lower venous return</td></tr><tr><td>Prolonged standing</td><td>Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs</td></tr><tr><td>Pericardial effusion / tamponade</td><td>External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion</td></tr><tr><td>Reduced ventricular compliance</td><td>A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Severe tachycardia</td><td>Shortened diastole limits filling time</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The lecturer also mentions <strong>myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease</strong>, including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is <strong>not universally a simple decrease</strong>. The precise direction depends on the disease and stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-contractility\">7. What changes contractility?</h2>\n<p>Sympathetic cardiac nerves release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>, while the adrenal medulla releases circulating <strong>adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate <strong>β₁-adrenergic receptors</strong> in the heart.</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Beta adrenergic inotropic pathway\"><span>β₁ receptor</span><b>→</b><span>Gs / adenylyl cyclase</span><b>→</b><span>cAMP / PKA ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Ca²⁺ cycling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stronger contraction</span></div>\n<p>PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces <strong>positive inotropy</strong>. The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing <strong>positive <span data-term=\"lusitropy\">lusitropy</span></strong> (faster relaxation).</p>\n<p>The lecture cites <strong>caffeine and theophylline</strong> (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-about-the-vagus-nerve\">What about the vagus nerve?</h3>\n<p>The lecturer lists vagal stimulation as negative inotropic. <strong>Clarification:</strong> vagal effects are strong for <strong>heart rate</strong> and AV nodal conduction, but the <strong>direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small</strong>. Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-can-depress-inotropy\">What can depress inotropy?</h3>\n<p>Lecture slide 48 lists <strong>hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure</strong>. Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations\">8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?</h2>\n<div class=\"co-compare-grid\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Three ways stroke volume changes\"><div><strong>More venous return</strong><span>Preload ↑</span><small>Often moves <em>along</em> the curve → SV ↑</small></div><div><strong>Higher aortic pressure</strong><span>Afterload ↑</span><small>Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓</small></div><div><strong>β₁ stimulation</strong><span>Contractility ↑</span><small>More force at same EDV → SV ↑</small></div></div>\n<p><strong>Why are these comparisons useful?</strong> In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat.</p>\n<p>These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states\">9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?</h2>\n<p>During <strong>dynamic exercise</strong>, several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase <strong>venous return</strong>; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in <strong>both</strong> HR and SV.</p>\n<p><strong>Lecture clarification:</strong> Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally <strong>increased</strong>, not decreased, venous return. The lecturer's diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps.</p>\n<p>The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: <strong>eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis</strong>. These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical connection — shock:</strong> During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min.</strong> Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>: what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. Thus CO <strong>decreases</strong> despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a <strong>higher ventricular function curve</strong> rather than merely movement along the original curve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through <strong>length-dependent activation</strong> and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement <strong>along</strong> a Frank–Starling curve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Along one curve:</strong> EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. <strong>Higher curve:</strong> at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>SV usually decreases.</strong> Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively <strong>flat</strong> at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction (<strong>positive inotropy</strong>), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation (<strong>positive lusitropy</strong>). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing <strong>lower preload and EDV</strong>. The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Cardiac output:</strong> CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time.</li><li><strong>Cardiac index:</strong> CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m².</li><li><strong>Stroke volume:</strong> SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO.</li><li><strong>Preload:</strong> End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates.</li><li><strong>Afterload:</strong> Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy.</li><li><strong>Contractility:</strong> Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺.</li><li><strong>Frank–Starling law:</strong> Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV.</li><li><strong>Curve interpretation:</strong> Changing preload moves <em>along</em> a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves.</li><li><strong>Heterometric regulation:</strong> Change in fibre starting length; <strong>homometric regulation:</strong> change in force without change in starting length.</li><li><strong>Venous return and EDV:</strong> Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles.</li><li><strong>Vagal influence:</strong> Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small.</li><li><strong>Severe dysfunction:</strong> A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV.</li><li><strong>Exercise and haemorrhage:</strong> CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone.</li></ol>","plain":"Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 35–51. Supplementary clarification: intrinsic sarcomere mechanisms, precise definitions of loading, interpretation of cardiac-function curves, and clinical examples. 1. What is cardiac output, and why does it matter? Cardiac output (CO) is the volume of blood ejected by one ventricle per unit time , usually expressed in litres per minute (L/min) . It tells us how much blood the heart delivers to a circulation—not how much blood is ejected in a single beat. Two quantities determine cardiac output: Heart rate (HR): number of beats per minute. Stroke volume (SV): blood ejected from one ventricle per beat . Cardiac output CO = HR × SV Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = 5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min Why multiply? Each beat sends out one stroke volume. If the ventricle pumps 70 mL each time and beats 75 times in one minute, the total volume ejected in that minute is 75 × 70 mL. The lecturer illustrates a resting output around 5.5 L/min . This is a representative value, not a universal constant: cardiac output varies with body size, activity and physiological conditions. Why should right and left cardiac outputs be approximately equal? The pulmonary and systemic circulations are connected in series . Blood ejected by the right ventricle enters the lungs and ultimately returns to the left ventricle. If one side consistently pumped a greater volume per minute than the other, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. The two outputs therefore remain approximately equal over time , although small moment-to-moment differences can occur. Cardiac index: allowing for body size A person with a larger body generally needs more total blood flow than a smaller person. Cardiac index (CI) expresses cardiac output relative to body surface area (BSA) . Cardiac index CI = CO BSA For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: CI = 3.0 L/min/m² . Unlike CO, cardiac index is expressed as L min⁻¹ m⁻² . It is a size-adjusted measure, not a different kind of blood flow. 2. Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output? From CO = HR × SV , increasing either HR or SV can raise output if the other factor remains unchanged . In a living heart, however, they are connected. Sympathetic stimulation generally increases SA nodal firing (positive chronotropy ) and makes ventricular contraction stronger (positive inotropy ). These effects can raise both HR and SV. But a faster heartbeat does not always mean more blood pumped per minute . At very high rates, diastole becomes so short that ventricular filling decreases. Reduced filling can lower SV enough to offset the rise in HR. Example: At 75 beats/min and 70 mL/beat, CO is 5.25 L/min . At 130 beats/min but only 35 mL/beat, CO is 4.55 L/min . Even though the heart beats faster, it pumps less blood per minute. HR ↑ More beats per minute SV ↓ markedly Less blood each beat CO may ↓ Depends on their product Reasoning rule: Never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone unless you know what happens to stroke volume. 3. What determines stroke volume? From Chapter 04, SV = EDV − ESV : the amount ejected is the amount present at the end of filling minus the amount remaining after contraction. Three closely related factors determine how much is ejected: Factor The question it answers Typical effect on SV, if other conditions are fixed Preload How stretched is the ventricle before contraction? More preload → usually higher SV within physiological limits Afterload How difficult is it to eject blood? More afterload → usually lower SV acutely Contractility (inotropy) How strongly can the myocardium contract at the same loading? Greater contractility → higher SV Preload — the starting stretch Preload refers to ventricular myocardial fibre stretch (and associated passive wall stress) at the end of diastole , just before contraction. More blood returning to a compliant ventricle usually increases end-diastolic volume (EDV) and stretches its fibres. EDV is therefore a useful teaching representation of preload. End-diastolic pressure is another clinical surrogate, but volume and pressure are not interchangeable , particularly in a stiff ventricle. Analogy: Gently stretching an elastic band before releasing it can help it recoil more forcefully. It is only an analogy: cardiac muscle is active tissue, and excessive stretch does not produce unlimited pumping. Afterload — the resistance or load against ejection Afterload is the mechanical load the ventricle must overcome during ejection. On the left side it is related to aortic pressure, arterial properties and ventricular wall stress . Peripheral vascular resistance contributes to the load but is not identical to afterload. If aortic pressure rises suddenly while preload and contractility remain unchanged, the ventricle must generate more force before and during ejection; it will generally eject less blood in that beat , leaving a higher ESV. Contractility — how capable the muscle is at a given load Contractility is the intrinsic ability of the myocardium to develop force when preload and afterload are held comparable . It depends strongly on intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling and the contractile machinery. Important distinction: A ventricle that contracts more forcefully because it filled more is using the Frank–Starling mechanism; that does not automatically mean its intrinsic contractility increased . 4. Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection? Imagine more blood returning through the veins. The extra blood enters a ventricle during diastole and stretches its muscle fibres before the next beat. Within the normal operating range, increased initial sarcomere length improves force development during the next contraction. This reflects length-dependent activation , including improved myofilament sensitivity to Ca²⁺ and more favourable interaction between contractile proteins. Venous return ↑ → EDV and fibre stretch ↑ → Force generated ↑ → SV ↑ Thus, the Frank–Starling law states that within physiological limits, the heart tends to eject more blood when its fibres are stretched more during filling . Why is this useful? It helps match the amount of blood ejected to the amount returning to the heart. If venous return increases, the ventricle can automatically increase SV without needing a new sympathetic command for every extra millilitre received. Does greater preload always increase stroke volume? No. The relationship is not unlimited. At high filling levels the curve becomes flatter, so additional volume yields progressively less extra output. A severely failing ventricle may be particularly unresponsive to extra filling while pressure rises enough to cause congestion. Lecture slide 39 includes a dashed descending limb at extreme stretch. That portion is a conceptual teaching extension: ordinary intact human hearts do not normally operate on a descending Frank–Starling limb . 5. How do we read Frank–Starling curves? The lecturer's slides 39–41 show ventricular performance plotted against filling. The usual horizontal axis is EDV or a surrogate of preload; the vertical axis may be stroke volume or cardiac output , depending on the particular diagram. Figure 01. Frank–Starling curves — preload versus pumping performance End-diastolic volume (preload) → Stroke volume Greater contractility Reference Lower contractility Read across one curve: changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. Compare curves vertically: at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Original DOMiNANCE schematic; not patient-specific measurements. A. Moving along the same curve Suppose a person lies down after standing. Venous pooling in the legs decreases, so more blood returns to the heart, EDV increases and the ventricle moves rightwards and upwards along a curve . This is primarily a change in preload , without requiring the intrinsic contractility of the myocardium to change. B. Shifting between curves Now imagine two beats with the same EDV , but sympathetic stimulation has increased intracellular Ca²⁺ availability during the second beat. The second beat can eject a greater SV without additional stretch . This is a change in contractile state , represented by an upward function-curve shift . A fall in contractility, as in significant myocardial dysfunction, tends to produce a lower curve . Higher afterload can also lower ventricular performance at a given EDV, so a shifted curve is not proof of altered contractility unless afterload is accounted for . The lecturer's unusual terms: heterometric and homometric Term Meaning Typical example Heterometric regulation Adjusting output through changes in the initial fibre length (preload) Frank–Starling response to increased venous return Homometric regulation Adjusting force independently of the initial fibre length β₁-mediated increase in contractility at the same EDV Exam insight: Heterometric changes the starting length; homometric changes pumping performance without needing a length change . 6. What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume? EDV depends on how much blood returns, the time available to fill, and whether the ventricle can accommodate that blood. More venous return does not always translate into the same EDV change if ventricular compliance is impaired. Often increases EDV Why Increased blood volume More blood available to return to the heart Increased venous tone Shifts blood from compliant venous reservoirs towards the heart Skeletal-muscle pump Contracting muscles compress veins and promote flow through one-way valves Respiratory pump Breathing-related pressure changes can favour venous return Effective atrial contraction Completes late ventricular filling Longer diastolic filling time More time is available for blood to enter Often reduces EDV or limits effective filling Why Haemorrhage / hypovolaemia Less circulating blood and lower venous return Prolonged standing Gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs Pericardial effusion / tamponade External pericardial pressure limits ventricular expansion Reduced ventricular compliance A stiff ventricle fills less readily for a given pressure Severe tachycardia Shortened diastole limits filling time The lecturer also mentions myocardial infarction and infiltrative disease , including endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF). These can impair relaxation, compliance and/or pumping; their effect on EDV is not universally a simple decrease . The precise direction depends on the disease and stage. 7. What changes contractility? Sympathetic cardiac nerves release noradrenaline (norepinephrine) , while the adrenal medulla releases circulating adrenaline (epinephrine) and some noradrenaline. These catecholamines stimulate β₁-adrenergic receptors in the heart. β₁ receptor → Gs / adenylyl cyclase → cAMP / PKA ↑ → Ca²⁺ cycling ↑ → Stronger contraction PKA signalling increases L-type Ca²⁺ entry and enhances Ca²⁺ release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, allowing greater actin–myosin interaction. This produces positive inotropy . The same signalling can speed Ca²⁺ reuptake, producing positive lusitropy (faster relaxation). The lecture cites caffeine and theophylline (xanthines). By inhibiting phosphodiesterase activity at sufficiently high concentrations, such agents can reduce cAMP breakdown, but their physiological and clinical effects are dose- and context-dependent. Do not assume everyday caffeine intake produces a predictable large positive inotropic response. What about the vagus nerve? The lecturer lists vagal stimulation as negative inotropic. Clarification: vagal effects are strong for heart rate and AV nodal conduction, but the direct parasympathetic effect on ventricular contractility is relatively small . Any fall in cardiac output may occur largely through changes in HR and filling rather than a large direct weakening of ventricular myocardium. What can depress inotropy? Lecture slide 48 lists hypercapnia, hypoxia, acidosis, selected drugs and cardiac failure . Severe myocardial hypoxia and acidosis can interfere with Ca²⁺ handling and cellular energy availability, reducing force generation; myocardial injury or failure may also reduce contractility. Responses to individual drugs depend on their specific mechanism. 8. How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations? More venous return Preload ↑ Often moves along the curve → SV ↑ Higher aortic pressure Afterload ↑ Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓ β₁ stimulation Contractility ↑ More force at same EDV → SV ↑ Why are these comparisons useful? In a patient with blood loss, HR may rise through the sympathetic reflex, but low preload can still reduce SV and CO. In a patient with aortic stenosis, even a strong contraction faces a substantial outflow load. In a failing heart, giving more filling volume may only modestly increase SV if the ventricular function curve is flat. These examples show why no single determinant should be interpreted in isolation. 9. What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states? During dynamic exercise , several processes usually occur together: sympathetic stimulation increases HR and inotropy; the skeletal-muscle and respiratory pumps increase venous return ; and arterioles supplying active muscle dilate. CO can therefore increase substantially by changes in both HR and SV. Lecture clarification: Slide 49 contains an ambiguous downward-arrow description of venous return during exercise. For ordinary dynamic exercise, the expected effect of muscle and respiratory pumping is generally increased , not decreased, venous return. The lecturer's diagrams on slides 50–51 also show increased venous return associated with the pumps. The lecture additionally mentions conditions associated with increased CO or cardiac demands: eating, high environmental temperature, pregnancy, anxiety/excitement, catecholamines and thyrotoxicosis . These conditions differ in mechanisms and magnitude; they are not interchangeable. Clinical connection — shock: During major haemorrhage, venous return and preload decrease. Sympathetic tachycardia and vasoconstriction can help maintain arterial pressure temporarily, but this does not guarantee normal CO or adequate tissue perfusion. Understanding Check 1. A person has a heart rate of 75 beats/min and stroke volume of 70 mL. Calculate CO and state the correct units. Reveal explanation CO = HR × SV = 75 × 70 = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min. Heart rate counts beats per minute, while stroke volume gives the blood ejected per beat; multiplying cancels 'beats' and leaves volume per minute. 2. Why must right and left ventricular outputs be approximately equal over time, even though they pump against different pressures? Reveal explanation The systemic and pulmonary circuits are arranged in series : what leaves the right heart eventually returns to the left heart. A persistent unequal flow would cause blood to accumulate in one circuit and deplete the other. The left ventricle produces higher pressure, but not a greater long-term flow rate. 3. Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, but SV falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to CO? Reveal explanation Initially, CO = 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min . Afterwards CO = 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min . Thus CO decreases despite tachycardia. Severe shortening of filling time can reduce SV enough to outweigh the increase in HR. 4. Two ventricular beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects a larger SV following sympathetic stimulation. Has preload necessarily increased? Reveal explanation No. Same EDV implies comparable filling volume, while β₁ stimulation can increase Ca²⁺ entry and SR release, raising intrinsic contractility. At similar preload and afterload, this produces greater force and a larger SV, reflecting a higher ventricular function curve rather than merely movement along the original curve. 5. A patient lies down after standing for a long time. Explain the expected Frank–Starling response. Reveal explanation Standing promotes venous pooling. Lying down reduces the gravitational gradient, usually improving venous return and EDV. Extra ventricular filling stretches fibres, increasing force through length-dependent activation and generally increasing SV. This is primarily movement along a Frank–Starling curve. 6. Why is EDV not an exact measure of preload in a very stiff ventricle? Reveal explanation True preload concerns end-diastolic myocardial stretch and wall stress; EDV is only a surrogate. A stiff ventricle may develop high filling pressure with relatively little increase in volume or fibre stretch. Therefore identical EDVs or pressures need not imply identical myocardial preload across different ventricular properties. 7. On a stroke-volume-versus-EDV graph, what distinguishes moving along one curve from shifting to a higher curve? Reveal explanation Along one curve: EDV changes, and the Frank–Starling length-dependent mechanism changes SV at the same functional state. Higher curve: at the same EDV, the ventricle ejects more blood, often because intrinsic contractility increased; reduced afterload can also improve performance, so loading conditions must be considered. 8. An acute increase in aortic pressure occurs without an immediate change in preload or contractility. Predict the first-beat effect on SV. Reveal explanation SV usually decreases. Higher aortic pressure increases the load opposing ventricular ejection. With initially unchanged preload and inotropy, the ventricle ejects less blood, leaving more residual ESV. Later compensatory changes can alter the result, but they are not part of the immediate first-beat prediction. 9. Why might a large intravenous fluid load fail to appreciably raise SV in a severely failing ventricle? Reveal explanation In marked systolic dysfunction the Frank–Starling curve may be relatively flat at high filling levels. Additional filling can markedly increase pressures and congestion while producing only a small increase in force and SV. More preload therefore does not guarantee proportionately greater output. 10. How can β₁ stimulation increase contraction strength and accelerate myocardial relaxation? Reveal explanation β₁ receptors activate Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA. Greater L-type Ca²⁺ influx and SR Ca²⁺ release strengthen contraction ( positive inotropy ), while enhanced SERCA-mediated Ca²⁺ reuptake accelerates relaxation ( positive lusitropy ). The two effects arise from different parts of the same signalling pathway. 11. Why does dynamic exercise usually increase venous return rather than reduce it? Reveal explanation Contracting skeletal muscles compress veins, and one-way venous valves favour flow towards the heart. Respiratory pressure changes also support return. Combined with sympathetic venoconstriction, these mechanisms tend to move more blood back to the heart, increasing preload and helping sustain the higher CO required during exercise. 12. A patient loses a large volume of blood, becomes tachycardic, yet develops poor tissue perfusion. Explain using CO, preload and compensation. Reveal explanation Haemorrhage reduces blood volume and venous return, causing lower preload and EDV . The reduction in SV can markedly reduce CO. Reflex sympathetic stimulation increases HR and vascular resistance, which may support arterial pressure temporarily; however, CO = HR × SV means a sufficiently large SV fall can overwhelm tachycardic compensation, leaving tissue perfusion inadequate. Chapter Essentials Cardiac output: CO = HR × SV, measured in L/min; right and left outputs are approximately equal over time. Cardiac index: CI = CO/BSA, measured in L/min/m². Stroke volume: SV = EDV − ESV; altered HR alone does not predict CO. Preload: End-diastolic myocardial fibre stretch; EDV and filling pressure are imperfect surrogates. Afterload: Mechanical load opposing ejection; acute increase generally lowers SV at unchanged preload/inotropy. Contractility: Ability to develop force at comparable loading; β₁ stimulation increases inotropy through cAMP/PKA/Ca²⁺. Frank–Starling law: Within physiological limits, increased filling and fibre length increase force and SV. Curve interpretation: Changing preload moves along a curve; changing contractile state shifts performance between curves. Heterometric regulation: Change in fibre starting length; homometric regulation: change in force without change in starting length. Venous return and EDV: Supported by blood volume, venous tone, muscle pump and respiratory pump; restricted by pooling, tamponade and stiff ventricles. Vagal influence: Dominant direct effects on HR and AV conduction; direct ventricular negative inotropy is small. Severe dysfunction: A flat Frank–Starling curve means more filling may raise congestion more than SV. Exercise and haemorrhage: CO must be reasoned from changes in both HR and SV, not heart rate alone."},{"slug":"co-measurement","title":"Cardiac Output Measurement and Work","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"52-54","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone","title":"1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?"},{"id":"2-ficks-principle-build-the-formula-from-the-physical-idea","title":"2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea"},{"id":"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from","title":"3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?"},{"id":"4-how-does-the-indicator-dilution-idea-measure-blood-flow-supplementary","title":"4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary)"},{"id":"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work","title":"5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?"},{"id":"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressurevolume-loop","title":"6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop"},{"id":"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-hearts-oxygen-demand","title":"7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"measuring-cardiac-output-and-the-work-of-the-heart\">Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 52–54. <strong>Supplementary explanations:</strong> sampling for Fick cardiac output, indicator/thermodilution methods and pressure–volume work diagrams. The lecturer's three slides introduce the principles rather than fully describing all measurement methods.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-measure-cardiac-output-instead-of-calculating-it-from-heart-rate-alone\">1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone?</h2>\n<p>From Chapter 07, <strong>cardiac output (CO) = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>. If we know both factors, we can calculate output. But in clinical situations, <strong>stroke volume often cannot be measured simply by counting a pulse</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, we need an independent way to estimate the volume of blood passing through a circulation each minute. The lecturer introduces <strong>Fick's principle</strong>, which uses oxygen exchange as a way to infer blood flow.</p>\n<p>The central idea is <strong>conservation of matter</strong>: oxygen used by a tissue must have been supplied by the blood entering it, minus the oxygen still present in blood leaving it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-ficks-principle-build-the-formula-from-the-physical-idea\">2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea</h2>\n<p>Imagine one litre of arterial blood enters a group of tissues with a certain amount of oxygen. The venous blood leaves with less oxygen because cells have extracted some.</p>\n<p>Two observations determine total oxygen use:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>How much oxygen is removed from each litre of blood?</strong> This is the arterial–venous oxygen-content difference.</li><li><strong>How many litres of blood flow through the tissues each minute?</strong> This is the blood flow rate, which equals systemic cardiac output in steady state.</li></ol>\n<p>The tissue oxygen-consumption rate must equal the product of these quantities.</p>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fick oxygen extraction concept\"><span><strong>Arterial O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ carried into tissues</small></span><b>−</b><span><strong>Mixed venous O₂ content</strong><small>O₂ remaining after tissues</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>O₂ extracted</strong><small>Amount removed from each unit of blood</small></span></div>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick's oxygen principle</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>CO</mi><mo>×</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>)</mo></math></div>\n<p>The relationship is easier to read in plain notation: <strong>O₂ consumption/min = blood flow/min × (arterial O₂ content − mixed venous O₂ content)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Solving for flow gives the equation used to measure cardiac output:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Fick cardiac output</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>CO</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mover><mi>V</mi><mo>˙</mo></mover><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></math></div>\n<p>Here <strong>V̇O₂</strong> = systemic oxygen consumed per minute; <strong>CaO₂</strong> = arterial oxygen content per unit blood volume; and <strong>CvO₂</strong> = <strong>mixed venous</strong> oxygen content. Be careful: <strong>oxygen content</strong>, <strong>oxygen saturation</strong> and <strong>partial pressure of oxygen</strong> are different quantities.</p>\n<h3 id=\"worked-example-the-lecturers-diagram\">Worked example — the lecturer's diagram</h3>\n<p>Suppose the body consumes <strong>250 mL O₂/min</strong>. Arterial blood contains <strong>0.20 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong> and mixed venous blood contains <strong>0.15 mL O₂ per mL of blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>Arterial–venous difference:</p>\n<p><strong>0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood</strong>.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<p><strong>CO = 250 mL O₂/min ÷ (0.05 mL O₂/mL blood) = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min.</strong></p>\n<p>The units matter. <em>Millilitres of O₂</em> cancel, leaving <strong>millilitres of blood per minute</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-doesnt-the-answer-use-venous-oxygen-saturation-alone\">Why doesn't the answer use venous oxygen saturation alone?</h3>\n<p>Because Fick's equation needs the <strong>quantity of oxygen present per volume of blood</strong>, not merely the percentage of haemoglobin-binding sites occupied. Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin concentration, saturation and a smaller dissolved oxygen component. This is particularly important in anaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-where-do-the-oxygen-measurements-come-from\">3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer notes two sampling sites:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Arterial oxygen content:</strong> A sample of systemic arterial blood provides an estimate of oxygen-rich blood entering tissues.</li><li><strong>Mixed venous oxygen content:</strong> A blood sample from the <strong>pulmonary artery</strong>, before the blood passes through the lungs, reflects blood returning from multiple systemic tissues.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Why the pulmonary artery for venous blood?</strong> The right ventricle receives systemic venous return from different organs. By the time it reaches the pulmonary artery, this blood is relatively mixed, so it better represents the whole body's oxygen extraction than a sample from one arm vein.</p>\n<p><strong>Why not simply measure a finger oxygen saturation?</strong> Pulse oximetry estimates saturation; it does not measure mixed venous oxygen content or directly determine how many litres of blood the heart pumps each minute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"direct-versus-estimated-fick\">Direct versus estimated Fick</h3>\n<p>A <strong>direct Fick measurement</strong> involves measuring actual oxygen consumption alongside arterial and mixed venous oxygen contents. Some clinical estimates use a predicted oxygen-consumption rate instead; this is a further assumption and can introduce error.</p>\n<p>The Fick method is most reliable when measurements reflect a reasonably <strong>steady physiological state</strong>. Rapidly changing oxygen demand, significant shunts, sampling errors or inaccurate oxygen-consumption estimates can impair results.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-does-the-indicator-dilution-idea-measure-blood-flow-supplementary\">4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary)</h2>\n<p>Lecture slide 53 includes a <strong>conservation-of-mass diagram</strong>, which is the underlying logic of several flow-measurement techniques.</p>\n<p>If a known quantity of a detectable indicator is introduced into a moving stream of blood, the downstream concentration changes as it becomes dispersed. When appropriately sampled, the amount injected and the time–concentration pattern can be used to infer the flow rate.</p>\n<p><strong>Why?</strong> In faster flow, a given indicator dose generally becomes more diluted and passes the sampling site more rapidly than in slower flow.</p>\n<div class=\"fick-process\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Indicator dilution sequence\"><span><strong>Known indicator dose</strong><small>Introduced upstream</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Mixed into blood</strong><small>Carried downstream</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Concentration over time</strong><small>Flow inferred from first pass</small></span></div>\n<p>For a simple first-pass indicator dilution model, <strong>flow is inversely related to the area under the indicator concentration–time curve</strong> after a known bolus. Actual measurements require appropriate calibration and handling of recirculation.</p>\n<h3 id=\"thermodilution-using-temperature-as-the-indicator-supplementary\">Thermodilution — using temperature as the indicator (Supplementary)</h3>\n<p>A clinical variation introduces a known bolus of saline at a known temperature via a pulmonary artery catheter's proximal port. A downstream <strong>thermistor</strong> detects how blood temperature changes over time.</p>\n<p>The recorded temperature–time response can be used to estimate flow. It uses a related indicator-dilution principle, but the indicator is a <strong>thermal change</strong>, not a dye.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What is measured?</th><th>Important limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fick</strong></td><td>O₂ consumption and arterial–mixed venous O₂-content difference</td><td>Requires valid sampling and steady-state assumptions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dye indicator dilution</strong></td><td>Concentration of injected tracer over time</td><td>Injection/sampling and recirculation affect the curve</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Thermodilution</strong></td><td>Temperature change over time after injected saline</td><td>Catheter position, valve regurgitation and shunts may distort results</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These methods are <strong>supplementary</strong> to the three primary slides. Their purpose here is to show how the lecture's conservation principle extends to other cardiac-output measurements.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-it-mean-for-the-heart-to-perform-work\">5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work?</h2>\n<p>The heart does more than move blood: it must move that blood <strong>against pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>In mechanics, work can be expressed as <strong>force × distance</strong>. Because pressure is force per area, moving a volume of fluid against pressure also requires work.</p>\n<p>The lecture gives a simplified relationship for one ventricular beat:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate external stroke work</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>SW</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>SV</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>P</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">For LV: P ≈ mean aortic pressure; for RV: P ≈ mean pulmonary arterial pressure.</span></div>\n<p>In ordinary teaching examples, <strong>stroke volume × mean arterial pressure</strong> provides a practical approximation. More accurately, <strong>external stroke work is the area within the ventricular pressure–volume loop</strong>, accounting for the pressure throughout filling and ejection. The simple product is not an exact substitute for every loop shape.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-the-left-ventricle-perform-more-pressure-work-than-the-right\">Why does the left ventricle perform more pressure work than the right?</h3>\n<p>The ventricles normally eject <strong>approximately equal volumes per minute</strong>, but the left ventricle ejects against much higher systemic arterial pressure. Therefore, its external pressure work is greater.</p>\n<p><strong>Example (illustrative):</strong> If SV = 70 mL and mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg:</p>\n<p><strong>SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat ≈ 0.93 J per beat.</strong></p>\n<p>The value is an approximate measure of <em>external stroke work</em>, not the total chemical energy consumed by myocardium.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-visualising-stroke-work-on-a-pressurevolume-loop\">6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop</h2>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure work-pv-figure\" id=\"cvs08-pv\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 580 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic left ventricular pressure-volume loop with end-systolic and end-diastolic volumes marked, filling at low pressure, isovolumetric contraction, ejection and relaxation, and interior stroke-work area shaded.\"><defs><marker id=\"workloop-arrow\" markerWidth=\"7\" markerHeight=\"7\" refX=\"5\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0 0 L6 3 L0 6z\" fill=\"#2863dd\"/></marker></defs><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"17\" x2=\"64\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"240\" x2=\"557\" y2=\"240\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".5\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C270 226 374 228 441 216 L441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86 Z\" fill=\"#93c5fd\" opacity=\".28\"/><path d=\"M208 216 C290 231 362 229 441 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M441 216 L441 77\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M441 77 C389 35 314 43 208 86\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><path d=\"M208 86 L208 216\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"3.5\" marker-end=\"url(#workloop-arrow)\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"310\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"800\">Stroke work</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"159\" text-anchor=\"middle\">≈ loop area</text><text x=\"184\" y=\"255\">ESV</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"270\">EDV</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"273\">Ventricular volume →</text><text x=\"21\" y=\"148\" transform=\"rotate(-90,21,148)\">Pressure</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"135\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"458\" y=\"150\">contraction</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"121\">Isovolumetric</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"136\">relaxation</text><text x=\"307\" y=\"37\">Ejection</text><text x=\"306\" y=\"235\">Filling</text></g></svg><p>Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume.</p></figure>\n<p>During <strong>isovolumetric contraction</strong>, pressure rises but ventricular volume does not change. During <strong>ejection</strong>, ventricular volume falls and the heart generates pressure to move blood into the artery. During <strong>isovolumetric relaxation</strong>, pressure falls while volume remains approximately at ESV.</p>\n<p>The area inside this loop represents <strong>external stroke work</strong>. The myocardium also spends energy maintaining electrical activity, cycling Ca²⁺ and sustaining internal tension, so <strong>stroke work is not identical to total myocardial oxygen consumption</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-pressure-loading-increase-the-hearts-oxygen-demand\">7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists three major determinants of myocardial work and oxygen consumption:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Intramyocardial tension (wall stress).</strong></li><li><strong>Contractile state</strong> of the myocardium.</li><li><strong>Heart rate.</strong></li></ul>\n<p>When pressure loading rises, the ventricular wall must develop greater tension to eject blood. Greater force development and more frequent contractions usually increase myocardial metabolic demand and oxygen consumption.</p>\n<p>The lecturer states that <strong>pressure work is more oxygen-demanding than volume work</strong> and uses <strong>aortic stenosis</strong> (pressure loading) compared with <strong>aortic incompetence / regurgitation</strong> (volume loading) as examples.</p>\n<p><strong>How to interpret this safely:</strong> Pressure overload often increases wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand substantially, but it would be incorrect to assume that <em>all</em> patients with aortic stenosis have more angina than <em>all</em> patients with aortic regurgitation. Severity, coronary blood supply and other diseases matter.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-the-heart-need-increased-coronary-flow-during-exercise\">Why does the heart need increased coronary flow during exercise?</h3>\n<p>The myocardium extracts a large fraction of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. When the heart works harder, it cannot increase oxygen supply sufficiently by extraction alone. <strong>Coronary blood flow must increase</strong> to deliver more oxygen.</p>\n<p>This explains the connection between exertion, increased myocardial work and angina when the oxygen supply cannot match demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> The Fick calculation uses oxygen consumption by the <strong>whole body</strong> to measure systemic CO. Myocardial oxygen consumption is the heart's <strong>own</strong> metabolic demand; these are different quantities, even though both use oxygen-extraction principles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can the Fick method calculate CO without directly measuring stroke volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fick's principle uses <strong>conservation of oxygen</strong>. Systemic oxygen consumed each minute equals blood flow each minute multiplied by the oxygen removed from each unit of blood. Therefore cardiac output can be found by dividing oxygen consumption by the arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference, without measuring SV beat by beat.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min, arterial content 0.20 mL O₂/mL blood and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂/mL blood. Calculate CO.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The content difference is <strong>0.05 mL O₂/mL blood</strong>. CO = 250 ÷ 0.05 = <strong>5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min</strong>. The oxygen units cancel, leaving volume of blood per minute.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why is mixed venous blood ideally sampled from the pulmonary artery rather than a peripheral vein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The pulmonary artery contains venous return from many systemic organs after mixing in the right heart. A peripheral vein reflects extraction in a particular region and may not represent whole-body oxygen content. The Fick equation for total systemic flow needs a representative <strong>mixed venous</strong> sample.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. A person has normal arterial oxygen saturation but very low haemoglobin. Can saturation alone be substituted for arterial oxygen content in the Fick formula?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Saturation is a fraction of haemoglobin sites occupied by oxygen, whereas oxygen content is the <em>actual amount of oxygen per unit blood</em>. With low haemoglobin, oxygen content can be reduced despite high saturation, so substituting saturation alone makes the calculation invalid.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. A dye bolus is introduced into a blood stream. Why does the downstream concentration–time pattern contain information about flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The injected dye represents a known amount of indicator. Its concentration is diluted and transported by moving blood. With suitable sampling and a first-pass analysis, the indicator's total time–concentration exposure is inversely related to flow. Recirculation must be recognised rather than included as if it were the original pass.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. What is the principal measurable signal in thermodilution?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Temperature change over time.</strong> A known bolus of saline mixes with blood, and a downstream thermistor measures the resulting thermal signal. The measured pattern is used to estimate cardiac output using indicator-dilution principles. It does not directly count ventricular contractions or measure SV.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Two ventricles eject equal stroke volumes, but one ejects against much higher mean arterial pressure. Which performs more external pressure work?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The ventricle pumping against <strong>higher pressure</strong> performs more approximate external stroke work: SW ≈ SV × mean ejection pressure. This is why the left ventricle normally performs more pressure work than the right despite similar long-term flow.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. A ventricle ejects 70 mL against an approximate mean arterial pressure of 100 mmHg. Estimate external stroke work.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>SW ≈ 70 × 100 = <strong>7,000 mmHg·mL per beat</strong>, which is approximately <strong>0.93 J per beat</strong>. This is an external-work approximation; the myocardium consumes additional metabolic energy for processes not captured by this product.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why does the pressure–volume loop have an area even though both isovolumetric phases involve no change in volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The isovolumetric phases change pressure at almost constant volume. Between them, <strong>ejection and filling</strong> occur at different pressures. The enclosed area between those pressure–volume paths represents the external mechanical work associated with one cardiac cycle.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Why must coronary blood flow increase when myocardial work rises substantially?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Cardiac muscle already extracts a high proportion of the oxygen delivered by coronary blood at rest. When heart rate, force or wall tension increases, myocardial oxygen demand rises. Further extraction alone cannot meet the increase, so <strong>coronary flow must rise</strong> to sustain oxygen delivery.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Does a greater external stroke work always imply the same proportional increase in myocardial oxygen consumption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> External work is only part of total myocardial energetic demand. Intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, cross-bridge activation, basal metabolism, wall stress and contractile state also consume energy. The relation between external work and oxygen consumption therefore depends on cardiac conditions.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Fick's principle:</strong> Whole-body oxygen consumption equals blood flow × arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference.</li><li><strong>Fick cardiac output:</strong> CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂), with consistent units.</li><li><strong>Sampling:</strong> Systemic arterial blood supplies arterial content; pulmonary artery blood supplies mixed venous content.</li><li><strong>Content versus saturation:</strong> Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin and saturation; they are not interchangeable.</li><li><strong>Steady state:</strong> Accuracy is limited when oxygen use, mixing or sampling assumptions are invalid.</li><li><strong>Indicator dilution (supplementary):</strong> Known injected marker and concentration–time trace can estimate flow.</li><li><strong>Thermodilution (supplementary):</strong> Uses a temperature–time response after injected saline.</li><li><strong>External stroke work:</strong> Approximately SV × mean arterial pressure; more accurately the enclosed pressure–volume loop area.</li><li><strong>Left versus right ventricular work:</strong> Similar blood volume ejected, but left side faces much higher pressure.</li><li><strong>Myocardial O₂ demand:</strong> Strongly influenced by wall tension, contractility and heart rate.</li><li><strong>Pressure loading:</strong> Often increases myocardial demand markedly; clinical comparisons need context.</li><li><strong>Coronary flow:</strong> Increased myocardial oxygen demand usually requires increased coronary blood flow.</li></ol>","plain":"Measuring Cardiac Output and the Work of the Heart Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 52–54. Supplementary explanations: sampling for Fick cardiac output, indicator/thermodilution methods and pressure–volume work diagrams. The lecturer's three slides introduce the principles rather than fully describing all measurement methods. 1. Why measure cardiac output instead of calculating it from heart rate alone? From Chapter 07, cardiac output (CO) = heart rate × stroke volume . If we know both factors, we can calculate output. But in clinical situations, stroke volume often cannot be measured simply by counting a pulse . Therefore, we need an independent way to estimate the volume of blood passing through a circulation each minute. The lecturer introduces Fick's principle , which uses oxygen exchange as a way to infer blood flow. The central idea is conservation of matter : oxygen used by a tissue must have been supplied by the blood entering it, minus the oxygen still present in blood leaving it. 2. Fick's principle — build the formula from the physical idea Imagine one litre of arterial blood enters a group of tissues with a certain amount of oxygen. The venous blood leaves with less oxygen because cells have extracted some. Two observations determine total oxygen use: How much oxygen is removed from each litre of blood? This is the arterial–venous oxygen-content difference. How many litres of blood flow through the tissues each minute? This is the blood flow rate, which equals systemic cardiac output in steady state. The tissue oxygen-consumption rate must equal the product of these quantities. Arterial O₂ content O₂ carried into tissues − Mixed venous O₂ content O₂ remaining after tissues → O₂ extracted Amount removed from each unit of blood Fick's oxygen principle V ˙ O 2 = CO × ( C a O 2 − C v O 2 ) The relationship is easier to read in plain notation: O₂ consumption/min = blood flow/min × (arterial O₂ content − mixed venous O₂ content) . Solving for flow gives the equation used to measure cardiac output: Fick cardiac output CO = V ˙ O 2 C a O 2 − C v O 2 Here V̇O₂ = systemic oxygen consumed per minute; CaO₂ = arterial oxygen content per unit blood volume; and CvO₂ = mixed venous oxygen content. Be careful: oxygen content , oxygen saturation and partial pressure of oxygen are different quantities. Worked example — the lecturer's diagram Suppose the body consumes 250 mL O₂/min . Arterial blood contains 0.20 mL O₂ per mL of blood and mixed venous blood contains 0.15 mL O₂ per mL of blood . Arterial–venous difference: 0.20 − 0.15 = 0.05 mL O₂ per mL blood . Then: CO = 250 mL O₂/min ÷ (0.05 mL O₂/mL blood) = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min. The units matter. Millilitres of O₂ cancel, leaving millilitres of blood per minute . Why doesn't the answer use venous oxygen saturation alone? Because Fick's equation needs the quantity of oxygen present per volume of blood , not merely the percentage of haemoglobin-binding sites occupied. Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin concentration, saturation and a smaller dissolved oxygen component. This is particularly important in anaemia. 3. Where do the oxygen measurements come from? The lecturer notes two sampling sites: Arterial oxygen content: A sample of systemic arterial blood provides an estimate of oxygen-rich blood entering tissues. Mixed venous oxygen content: A blood sample from the pulmonary artery , before the blood passes through the lungs, reflects blood returning from multiple systemic tissues. Why the pulmonary artery for venous blood? The right ventricle receives systemic venous return from different organs. By the time it reaches the pulmonary artery, this blood is relatively mixed, so it better represents the whole body's oxygen extraction than a sample from one arm vein. Why not simply measure a finger oxygen saturation? Pulse oximetry estimates saturation; it does not measure mixed venous oxygen content or directly determine how many litres of blood the heart pumps each minute. Direct versus estimated Fick A direct Fick measurement involves measuring actual oxygen consumption alongside arterial and mixed venous oxygen contents. Some clinical estimates use a predicted oxygen-consumption rate instead; this is a further assumption and can introduce error. The Fick method is most reliable when measurements reflect a reasonably steady physiological state . Rapidly changing oxygen demand, significant shunts, sampling errors or inaccurate oxygen-consumption estimates can impair results. 4. How does the indicator-dilution idea measure blood flow? (Supplementary) Lecture slide 53 includes a conservation-of-mass diagram , which is the underlying logic of several flow-measurement techniques. If a known quantity of a detectable indicator is introduced into a moving stream of blood, the downstream concentration changes as it becomes dispersed. When appropriately sampled, the amount injected and the time–concentration pattern can be used to infer the flow rate. Why? In faster flow, a given indicator dose generally becomes more diluted and passes the sampling site more rapidly than in slower flow. Known indicator dose Introduced upstream → Mixed into blood Carried downstream → Concentration over time Flow inferred from first pass For a simple first-pass indicator dilution model, flow is inversely related to the area under the indicator concentration–time curve after a known bolus. Actual measurements require appropriate calibration and handling of recirculation. Thermodilution — using temperature as the indicator (Supplementary) A clinical variation introduces a known bolus of saline at a known temperature via a pulmonary artery catheter's proximal port. A downstream thermistor detects how blood temperature changes over time. The recorded temperature–time response can be used to estimate flow. It uses a related indicator-dilution principle, but the indicator is a thermal change , not a dye. Method What is measured? Important limitation Fick O₂ consumption and arterial–mixed venous O₂-content difference Requires valid sampling and steady-state assumptions Dye indicator dilution Concentration of injected tracer over time Injection/sampling and recirculation affect the curve Thermodilution Temperature change over time after injected saline Catheter position, valve regurgitation and shunts may distort results These methods are supplementary to the three primary slides. Their purpose here is to show how the lecture's conservation principle extends to other cardiac-output measurements. 5. What does it mean for the heart to perform work? The heart does more than move blood: it must move that blood against pressure . In mechanics, work can be expressed as force × distance . Because pressure is force per area, moving a volume of fluid against pressure also requires work. The lecture gives a simplified relationship for one ventricular beat: Approximate external stroke work SW ≈ SV × P For LV: P ≈ mean aortic pressure; for RV: P ≈ mean pulmonary arterial pressure. In ordinary teaching examples, stroke volume × mean arterial pressure provides a practical approximation. More accurately, external stroke work is the area within the ventricular pressure–volume loop , accounting for the pressure throughout filling and ejection. The simple product is not an exact substitute for every loop shape. Why does the left ventricle perform more pressure work than the right? The ventricles normally eject approximately equal volumes per minute , but the left ventricle ejects against much higher systemic arterial pressure. Therefore, its external pressure work is greater. Example (illustrative): If SV = 70 mL and mean arterial pressure = 100 mmHg: SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat ≈ 0.93 J per beat. The value is an approximate measure of external stroke work , not the total chemical energy consumed by myocardium. 6. Visualising stroke work on a pressure–volume loop Figure 01. Ventricular pressure–volume loop — enclosed area represents external stroke work Stroke work ≈ loop area ESV EDV Ventricular volume → Pressure Isovolumetric contraction Isovolumetric relaxation Ejection Filling Original teaching schematic, not measured patient data. The ventricle fills at low pressure, generates pressure with constant volume, ejects blood as volume falls, and then relaxes at approximately constant end-systolic volume. During isovolumetric contraction , pressure rises but ventricular volume does not change. During ejection , ventricular volume falls and the heart generates pressure to move blood into the artery. During isovolumetric relaxation , pressure falls while volume remains approximately at ESV. The area inside this loop represents external stroke work . The myocardium also spends energy maintaining electrical activity, cycling Ca²⁺ and sustaining internal tension, so stroke work is not identical to total myocardial oxygen consumption . 7. Why does pressure loading increase the heart's oxygen demand? The lecturer lists three major determinants of myocardial work and oxygen consumption: Intramyocardial tension (wall stress). Contractile state of the myocardium. Heart rate. When pressure loading rises, the ventricular wall must develop greater tension to eject blood. Greater force development and more frequent contractions usually increase myocardial metabolic demand and oxygen consumption. The lecturer states that pressure work is more oxygen-demanding than volume work and uses aortic stenosis (pressure loading) compared with aortic incompetence / regurgitation (volume loading) as examples. How to interpret this safely: Pressure overload often increases wall stress and myocardial oxygen demand substantially, but it would be incorrect to assume that all patients with aortic stenosis have more angina than all patients with aortic regurgitation. Severity, coronary blood supply and other diseases matter. Why does the heart need increased coronary flow during exercise? The myocardium extracts a large fraction of oxygen from coronary blood even at rest. When the heart works harder, it cannot increase oxygen supply sufficiently by extraction alone. Coronary blood flow must increase to deliver more oxygen. This explains the connection between exertion, increased myocardial work and angina when the oxygen supply cannot match demand. Important: The Fick calculation uses oxygen consumption by the whole body to measure systemic CO. Myocardial oxygen consumption is the heart's own metabolic demand; these are different quantities, even though both use oxygen-extraction principles. Understanding Check 1. Why can the Fick method calculate CO without directly measuring stroke volume? Reveal explanation Fick's principle uses conservation of oxygen . Systemic oxygen consumed each minute equals blood flow each minute multiplied by the oxygen removed from each unit of blood. Therefore cardiac output can be found by dividing oxygen consumption by the arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference, without measuring SV beat by beat. 2. Oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min, arterial content 0.20 mL O₂/mL blood and mixed venous content 0.15 mL O₂/mL blood. Calculate CO. Reveal explanation The content difference is 0.05 mL O₂/mL blood . CO = 250 ÷ 0.05 = 5,000 mL blood/min = 5.0 L/min . The oxygen units cancel, leaving volume of blood per minute. 3. Why is mixed venous blood ideally sampled from the pulmonary artery rather than a peripheral vein? Reveal explanation The pulmonary artery contains venous return from many systemic organs after mixing in the right heart. A peripheral vein reflects extraction in a particular region and may not represent whole-body oxygen content. The Fick equation for total systemic flow needs a representative mixed venous sample. 4. A person has normal arterial oxygen saturation but very low haemoglobin. Can saturation alone be substituted for arterial oxygen content in the Fick formula? Reveal explanation No. Saturation is a fraction of haemoglobin sites occupied by oxygen, whereas oxygen content is the actual amount of oxygen per unit blood . With low haemoglobin, oxygen content can be reduced despite high saturation, so substituting saturation alone makes the calculation invalid. 5. A dye bolus is introduced into a blood stream. Why does the downstream concentration–time pattern contain information about flow? Reveal explanation The injected dye represents a known amount of indicator. Its concentration is diluted and transported by moving blood. With suitable sampling and a first-pass analysis, the indicator's total time–concentration exposure is inversely related to flow. Recirculation must be recognised rather than included as if it were the original pass. 6. What is the principal measurable signal in thermodilution? Reveal explanation Temperature change over time. A known bolus of saline mixes with blood, and a downstream thermistor measures the resulting thermal signal. The measured pattern is used to estimate cardiac output using indicator-dilution principles. It does not directly count ventricular contractions or measure SV. 7. Two ventricles eject equal stroke volumes, but one ejects against much higher mean arterial pressure. Which performs more external pressure work? Reveal explanation The ventricle pumping against higher pressure performs more approximate external stroke work: SW ≈ SV × mean ejection pressure. This is why the left ventricle normally performs more pressure work than the right despite similar long-term flow. 8. A ventricle ejects 70 mL against an approximate mean arterial pressure of 100 mmHg. Estimate external stroke work. Reveal explanation SW ≈ 70 × 100 = 7,000 mmHg·mL per beat , which is approximately 0.93 J per beat . This is an external-work approximation; the myocardium consumes additional metabolic energy for processes not captured by this product. 9. Why does the pressure–volume loop have an area even though both isovolumetric phases involve no change in volume? Reveal explanation The isovolumetric phases change pressure at almost constant volume. Between them, ejection and filling occur at different pressures. The enclosed area between those pressure–volume paths represents the external mechanical work associated with one cardiac cycle. 10. Why must coronary blood flow increase when myocardial work rises substantially? Reveal explanation Cardiac muscle already extracts a high proportion of the oxygen delivered by coronary blood at rest. When heart rate, force or wall tension increases, myocardial oxygen demand rises. Further extraction alone cannot meet the increase, so coronary flow must rise to sustain oxygen delivery. 11. Does a greater external stroke work always imply the same proportional increase in myocardial oxygen consumption? Reveal explanation No. External work is only part of total myocardial energetic demand. Intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, cross-bridge activation, basal metabolism, wall stress and contractile state also consume energy. The relation between external work and oxygen consumption therefore depends on cardiac conditions. Chapter Essentials Fick's principle: Whole-body oxygen consumption equals blood flow × arterial–mixed venous oxygen-content difference. Fick cardiac output: CO = V̇O₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂), with consistent units. Sampling: Systemic arterial blood supplies arterial content; pulmonary artery blood supplies mixed venous content. Content versus saturation: Oxygen content depends on haemoglobin and saturation; they are not interchangeable. Steady state: Accuracy is limited when oxygen use, mixing or sampling assumptions are invalid. Indicator dilution (supplementary): Known injected marker and concentration–time trace can estimate flow. Thermodilution (supplementary): Uses a temperature–time response after injected saline. External stroke work: Approximately SV × mean arterial pressure; more accurately the enclosed pressure–volume loop area. Left versus right ventricular work: Similar blood volume ejected, but left side faces much higher pressure. Myocardial O₂ demand: Strongly influenced by wall tension, contractility and heart rate. Pressure loading: Often increases myocardial demand markedly; clinical comparisons need context. Coronary flow: Increased myocardial oxygen demand usually requires increased coronary blood flow."},{"slug":"hemodynamics","title":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"55-74","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation","title":"1. What propels blood around the circulation?"},{"id":"2-what-actually-determines-flow","title":"2. What actually determines flow?"},{"id":"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles","title":"3. Why do different vessels have different roles?"},{"id":"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries","title":"4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?"},{"id":"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number","title":"5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number"},{"id":"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow","title":"6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?"},{"id":"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells","title":"7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells"},{"id":"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels","title":"8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?"},{"id":"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured","title":"9. How can blood flow be measured?"},{"id":"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries","title":"10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"blood-flow-and-hemodynamics\">Blood Flow and Hemodynamics</h1>\n<p><strong>Lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 55–75. <strong>Supplementary clarifications:</strong> realistic blood rheology, network resistance, and correct microscopic dimensions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-propels-blood-around-the-circulation\">1. What propels blood around the circulation?</h2>\n<p>The heart supplies the energy that establishes a pressure gradient. Blood moves through <strong>arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins → heart</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecturer also names <strong>elastic recoil</strong> of large arteries, the <strong>skeletal-muscle pump</strong> compressing veins and the <strong>respiratory pump</strong> using changing intrathoracic pressures. These assist blood flow at different points; they do not replace the heart as its main pressure source.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does flow continue during ventricular diastole?</strong> Large elastic arteries stretch during systole, storing energy; their recoil helps sustain pressure and forward flow between ejections. One-way valves in many veins help prevent backward flow during muscle compression.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-actually-determines-flow\">2. What actually determines flow?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>effective perfusion pressure</strong> is the difference between upstream and downstream vascular pressure. For the same resistance, a larger pressure difference produces greater flow.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pressure, flow and resistance</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mi>Q</mi></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance.</span></div>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> A pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a system whose resistance is 20 mmHg·min/L produces flow of <strong>4 L/min</strong>. This is an arithmetic illustration, not a specific organ's reference flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Important:</strong> High pressure at one point does not, by itself, guarantee high flow. Blood flow depends on the <strong>pressure drop across the pathway</strong> and its resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-different-vessels-have-different-roles\">3. Why do different vessels have different roles?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Segment</th><th>Special structure</th><th>Primary role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Large arteries</strong></td><td>Elastic walls</td><td>Store pressure energy in systole and recoil in diastole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Arterioles</strong></td><td>Relatively abundant smooth muscle</td><td>Regulate resistance and organ perfusion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Capillaries</strong></td><td>Very thin endothelial walls</td><td>Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Veins</strong></td><td>Distensible thin walls, often valves</td><td>Store blood and support its return</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why are arterioles called resistance vessels?</strong> Small muscular arterioles can change their radius rapidly. Even slight constriction can strongly increase resistance.</p>\n<p><strong>Why are veins called capacitance vessels?</strong> Their high compliance lets them accommodate substantial extra blood with a comparatively modest rise in pressure. Noradrenergic venoconstriction can shift blood from venous reservoirs towards the heart.</p>\n<p>The lecturer briefly describes capillary sphincters, endothelial gaps and fenestrations. <strong>Careful correction:</strong> microscopic gaps and fenestrations are not <em>millimetres</em> wide as the slide's unit suggests. Not every capillary has its own sphincter; local control varies with tissue. These details receive fuller treatment in the microcirculation chapter.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymphatic overview:</strong> Lymph vessels collect excess interstitial fluid and proteins, have valves and often traverse lymph nodes before large lymphatic ducts return fluid near the subclavian–internal jugular venous junctions. Detailed lymphatic physiology follows in its dedicated chapter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-blood-velocity-lowest-in-capillaries\">4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Volume flow (Q)</strong> is the quantity of blood per unit time; <strong>velocity (v)</strong> is the distance travelled per unit time. They are different measurements.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Continuity relation</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>A</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>Q</mi><mi>A</mi></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered.</span></div>\n<p><strong>The crucial word is TOTAL.</strong> Each capillary is narrow, but there are enormously many in parallel. Their combined cross-sectional area is far larger than that of the aorta.</p>\n<p>Therefore, at comparable overall volume flow, mean velocity decreases as blood travels from the aorta towards capillary beds, then increases again towards larger veins as the total area falls. Slow capillary flow also permits more time for exchange.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-laminar-flow-turbulence-and-reynolds-number\">5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number</h2>\n<p>In ideal <strong>laminar flow</strong>, fluid travels in organised layers. The layer touching the vessel wall is slowest because of friction at the wall, while the centre moves faster. This gives the familiar smooth, approximately parabolic velocity profile in a straight tube.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure hemo-flow-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 165\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"An idealised vessel with multiple flow arrows, the longest arrows at the centre and shortest nearest the vessel wall.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"34\" width=\"465\" height=\"99\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#e9f1ff\" stroke=\"#8caccb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M89 49H179m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 72H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 96H446m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M89 118H365m-10 -7 10 7 -10 7\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"41\" y=\"22\">Near the wall: slow</text><text x=\"299\" y=\"22\">Near centre: fast</text><text x=\"41\" y=\"153\">Original schematic — not a measured flow profile</text></g></svg><p>The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic.</p></figure>\n<p><strong>Turbulent or disturbed flow</strong> has changing local velocity directions, often generating audible sounds such as bruits or murmurs. It is made more likely by high velocity, sudden changes in vessel geometry and lower blood viscosity.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces the <strong>Reynolds number (Re)</strong> to describe this tendency:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Reynolds number</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Re</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mi>η</mi></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity.</span></div>\n<p>Higher Reynolds number means a <strong>greater likelihood</strong> of turbulent flow, not a guarantee. The vessel's curvature, pulsatility, branching and stenoses also matter.</p>\n<p><strong>Two different symbols:</strong> <strong>R</strong> denotes vascular resistance in the flow law; <strong>Re</strong> denotes <em>Reynolds number</em>, which is dimensionless. The lecturer uses similar abbreviated labels for both, but they are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Anaemia:</strong> Lower viscosity and a compensatory increase in flow can promote a systolic flow murmur without structural valve disease. <strong>Local narrowing:</strong> Velocity may accelerate through a stenosis, creating disturbed downstream flow and a vascular bruit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-is-vessel-radius-such-a-powerful-determinant-of-flow\">6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer derives the <strong>Hagen–Poiseuille formula</strong> for ideal steady laminar flow through a straight, rigid circular tube:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hagen–Poiseuille equation</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>η</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity.</span></div>\n<p>Combined with <strong>R = ΔP/Q</strong>, this gives:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Ideal tube resistance</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>η</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></math></div>\n<p>The key relationship is <strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴</strong>. When radius decreases, resistance increases dramatically.</p>\n<div class=\"hemo-radius-impact\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Effect of radius on ideal vessel resistance\"><div><strong>Radius halved</strong><span>Resistance ×16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16</small></div><div><strong>Radius doubled</strong><span>Resistance ÷16</span><small>At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold</small></div></div>\n<p><strong>Worked mechanism:</strong> If the radius changes from r to r/2, the new resistance is <strong>2⁴ = 16</strong> times larger, <em>provided length and viscosity remain unchanged</em>. With a fixed pressure difference, Q = ΔP/R means flow falls to one-sixteenth.</p>\n<p>These relationships are <strong>mathematical aids</strong>, not exact universal predictions: blood is non-Newtonian, vessels are compliant rather than rigid, and vascular tone and pressures change in living people.</p>\n<h3 id=\"does-a-faster-jet-at-a-stenosis-mean-total-blood-flow-increases\">Does a faster jet at a stenosis mean total blood flow increases?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> At the narrowed throat, cross-sectional area decreases and velocity can rise locally, even while the stenosis increases resistance and reduces overall blood flow. A high <em>local speed</em> must not be confused with a high <em>volume flow rate</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-viscosity-haematocrit-and-blood-cells\">7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells</h2>\n<p><strong>Viscosity</strong> describes internal resistance to flow. Blood viscosity depends on <strong>haematocrit, plasma proteins, temperature, and red-cell deformation or aggregation</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecturer highlights:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Polycythaemia:</strong> More red cells generally increase viscosity, raising viscous resistance and cardiac work.</li><li><strong>Anaemia:</strong> Viscosity is often lower, but reduced oxygen-carrying capacity can increase cardiac-output demand and produce a flow murmur.</li><li><strong>Abnormal red-cell rigidity:</strong> Conditions such as sickle-cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis can alter rheology.</li></ul>\n<p>In the very smallest vessels, the apparent viscosity differs from predictions for blood in large rigid tubes because cells change their distribution and deformation. Therefore, slide 71's suggestion that viscosity has little effect in all small vessels should be treated as a <strong>context-dependent approximation</strong>, not a universal statement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-arterioles-the-main-controllable-resistance-vessels\">8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels?</h2>\n<p>The vascular network contains vessels <strong>in series</strong> (one after another) and <strong>in parallel</strong> (multiple paths serving different tissues).</p>\n<p>With vessels in series, pressure drops add. With many open pathways in parallel, the overall resistance is lower than that of a single pathway alone.</p>\n<p>This explains an apparent puzzle: each capillary has a tiny radius, but there are very many capillaries <strong>in parallel</strong>. Arterioles, with their active muscular control of diameter, account for a large part of the important pressure drop and resistance regulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Physiological application:</strong> Constricting arterioles reduces flow into a tissue at the same perfusion pressure. Dilation lowers local resistance and can increase flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-how-can-blood-flow-be-measured\">9. How can blood flow be measured?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists three techniques on slide 62. Their underlying physical signals differ:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it measures or detects</th><th>How it relates to flow</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Electromagnetic flowmeter</strong></td><td>Voltage induced by conducting blood in a magnetic field</td><td>Used to estimate blood movement through the field</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Doppler ultrasound flowmeter</strong></td><td>Frequency shift from moving blood cells</td><td>Estimates velocity along the ultrasound beam</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Plethysmography</strong></td><td>Changes in tissue or limb volume</td><td>Can estimate blood inflow with appropriate experimental conditions</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Doppler primarily estimates <strong>velocity</strong>, not volume flow on its own. To estimate total flow, an estimate of the vessel's area and the velocity profile is also required.</p>\n<p>The lecture mentions a historical <strong>arm-to-tongue circulation time around 15 seconds</strong>. This is an illustrative transit-time estimate, not a fixed physiological constant for every person.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-why-can-veins-hold-more-blood-than-arteries\">10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries?</h2>\n<p><strong>Vascular compliance</strong> describes how much volume a vessel can accommodate for a given pressure change.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Compliance</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></div>\n<p>Large veins are more compliant than arteries. Their volume can rise considerably before venous pressure rises by the same amount. This is why veins are called <strong>capacitance vessels</strong>, whereas arterioles are commonly described as <strong>resistance vessels</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecturer gives an approximate distribution of blood volume on slide 75, with a large share in the venous system. The listed percentages are <strong>illustrative, not an exact complete partition</strong>; their values do not add to 100% and vary with physiological conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does venoconstriction help cardiac output?</strong> When the venous system becomes less distensible, stored blood can be redirected towards the heart. This can increase venous return, end-diastolic volume and stroke volume through the Frank–Starling mechanism.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does blood keep moving through the arteries during ventricular diastole?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Large elastic arteries stretch during ventricular systole and store pressure energy. Their recoil during diastole sustains arterial pressure and the pressure gradient towards smaller vessels, so blood can keep moving between ventricular ejections.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. An organ has an upstream-to-downstream pressure difference of 80 mmHg and a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L. What is its flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Q = ΔP/R = 80/20 = 4 L/min.</strong> The pressure difference must be measured across the pathway; pressure at one end by itself does not establish the flow rate. The numbers are for arithmetic practice, not a named organ.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why is mean capillary blood velocity low despite each capillary having a narrow lumen?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The capillary system has an enormous number of vessels in <strong>parallel</strong>. Their <strong>combined</strong> cross-sectional area is much greater than that of the aorta. Because v = Q/A, the same overall volume flow moving through a much larger combined area has a lower average velocity.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why does ideal laminar flow have a greater velocity in the centre than beside the wall?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fluid in immediate contact with the vessel wall moves slowly because of viscous boundary effects. Adjacent layers drag on one another, forming a velocity gradient. Flow towards the centre is less constrained and therefore faster. Real blood can depart from the ideal profile because it contains cells.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. A vessel's radius falls to half its original value at unchanged ΔP, viscosity and length. Predict resistance and flow.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Hagen–Poiseuille gives <strong>R ∝ 1/r⁴</strong>. Halving the radius therefore increases the predicted resistance by <strong>16 times</strong>. With unchanged perfusion pressure and Q = ΔP/R, volume flow decreases to <strong>1/16</strong> of its previous value, under ideal-tube assumptions.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A stenosis produces a very high velocity at its narrowest point. Must total blood flow through the artery have increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Local velocity is volume flow divided by local area, so speed can rise as the cross-sectional area narrows even if volume flow remains the same or decreases. The narrowing may also raise resistance and reduce total flow, depending on the pressure gradient.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why may anaemia produce a systolic flow murmur even with normal valves?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Anaemia often reduces blood viscosity and increases cardiac output to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Higher flow velocity and reduced viscosity can increase the tendency towards disturbed flow, producing audible vibrations without a structurally abnormal valve.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Distinguish vascular resistance R from the Reynolds number Re.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Resistance R</strong> relates the pressure difference across a pathway to the resulting volume flow. <strong>Reynolds number Re</strong> is dimensionless and compares effects of density, velocity, diameter and viscosity to assess the tendency towards turbulent flow. The two quantities are not equivalent.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why are arterioles the main adjustable resistance vessels instead of capillaries?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Arterioles have smooth muscle capable of rapidly changing their radius and therefore flow resistance. Although individual capillaries are narrow, many operate in parallel and their overall equivalent resistance is reduced. Changing arteriolar tone strongly influences organ perfusion and systemic resistance.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. What does Doppler ultrasound directly estimate, and what else would be needed to estimate volume flow?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Doppler detects frequency shifts from moving blood cells and estimates a component of <strong>velocity</strong>. Volume flow also depends on the cross-sectional area and the velocity profile across the vessel, so estimating Q requires more information than a single Doppler velocity reading.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why do veins store more additional blood than arteries for a similar rise in pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Veins have a greater <strong>compliance (C = ΔV/ΔP)</strong>. They accommodate a larger volume increase for a given pressure rise, acting as blood reservoirs. Arterioles instead use their muscular walls to regulate resistance.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Polycythaemia and anaemia may both increase cardiac workload. How do their mechanisms differ?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>In <strong>polycythaemia</strong>, greater haematocrit usually increases viscosity and can raise flow resistance. In <strong>anaemia</strong>, reduced oxygen-carrying capacity often demands greater cardiac output to deliver enough oxygen, producing increased volume flow and workload even when viscosity is lower.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Flow:</strong> Q = ΔP/R; a pressure <em>difference</em>, not isolated vessel pressure, drives net flow.</li><li><strong>Diastolic arterial flow:</strong> Elastic recoil helps maintain forward flow between ventricular ejections.</li><li><strong>Vessel functions:</strong> Elastic arteries, resistance arterioles, exchange capillaries and capacitance veins have distinct roles.</li><li><strong>Velocity versus flow:</strong> Q = vA; the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is enormous.</li><li><strong>Laminar flow:</strong> Fastest near the centre and slowest by the wall in the ideal model.</li><li><strong>Reynolds number:</strong> Re = ρvD/η, a dimensionless measure of turbulence tendency, not vascular resistance.</li><li><strong>Hagen–Poiseuille:</strong> Q = πΔPr⁴/(8ηL) for ideal-tube flow; R = 8ηL/(πr⁴).</li><li><strong>Radius effect:</strong> Halving radius increases ideal resistance sixteenfold at unchanged other factors.</li><li><strong>Arteriolar control:</strong> Small changes in muscular-vessel radius substantially regulate blood flow.</li><li><strong>Viscosity:</strong> Depends on haematocrit, plasma components and red-cell properties; blood is not an ideal fluid.</li><li><strong>Measurement:</strong> Electromagnetic, Doppler and plethysmographic techniques detect different flow-related signals.</li><li><strong>Compliance:</strong> C = ΔV/ΔP; veins accommodate large volume changes at comparatively low pressure.</li><li><strong>Small-vessel and lymphatic physiology:</strong> Introduced here, developed in subsequent chapters.</li></ol>","plain":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics Lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 55–75. Supplementary clarifications: realistic blood rheology, network resistance, and correct microscopic dimensions. 1. What propels blood around the circulation? The heart supplies the energy that establishes a pressure gradient. Blood moves through arteries → arterioles → capillaries → venules → veins → heart . The lecturer also names elastic recoil of large arteries, the skeletal-muscle pump compressing veins and the respiratory pump using changing intrathoracic pressures. These assist blood flow at different points; they do not replace the heart as its main pressure source. Why does flow continue during ventricular diastole? Large elastic arteries stretch during systole, storing energy; their recoil helps sustain pressure and forward flow between ejections. One-way valves in many veins help prevent backward flow during muscle compression. 2. What actually determines flow? The effective perfusion pressure is the difference between upstream and downstream vascular pressure. For the same resistance, a larger pressure difference produces greater flow. Pressure, flow and resistance Q = Δ P R R = Δ P Q Q = blood volume per time; ΔP = upstream − downstream pressure; R = vascular resistance. Example: A pressure drop of 80 mmHg across a system whose resistance is 20 mmHg·min/L produces flow of 4 L/min . This is an arithmetic illustration, not a specific organ's reference flow. Important: High pressure at one point does not, by itself, guarantee high flow. Blood flow depends on the pressure drop across the pathway and its resistance. 3. Why do different vessels have different roles? Segment Special structure Primary role Large arteries Elastic walls Store pressure energy in systole and recoil in diastole Arterioles Relatively abundant smooth muscle Regulate resistance and organ perfusion Capillaries Very thin endothelial walls Exchange gases, nutrients and fluid Veins Distensible thin walls, often valves Store blood and support its return Why are arterioles called resistance vessels? Small muscular arterioles can change their radius rapidly. Even slight constriction can strongly increase resistance. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Their high compliance lets them accommodate substantial extra blood with a comparatively modest rise in pressure. Noradrenergic venoconstriction can shift blood from venous reservoirs towards the heart. The lecturer briefly describes capillary sphincters, endothelial gaps and fenestrations. Careful correction: microscopic gaps and fenestrations are not millimetres wide as the slide's unit suggests. Not every capillary has its own sphincter; local control varies with tissue. These details receive fuller treatment in the microcirculation chapter. Lymphatic overview: Lymph vessels collect excess interstitial fluid and proteins, have valves and often traverse lymph nodes before large lymphatic ducts return fluid near the subclavian–internal jugular venous junctions. Detailed lymphatic physiology follows in its dedicated chapter. 4. Why is blood velocity lowest in capillaries? Volume flow (Q) is the quantity of blood per unit time; velocity (v) is the distance travelled per unit time. They are different measurements. Continuity relation Q = v A v = Q A A = total cross-sectional area of the vessels considered. The crucial word is TOTAL. Each capillary is narrow, but there are enormously many in parallel. Their combined cross-sectional area is far larger than that of the aorta. Therefore, at comparable overall volume flow, mean velocity decreases as blood travels from the aorta towards capillary beds, then increases again towards larger veins as the total area falls. Slow capillary flow also permits more time for exchange. 5. Laminar flow, turbulence and Reynolds number In ideal laminar flow , fluid travels in organised layers. The layer touching the vessel wall is slowest because of friction at the wall, while the centre moves faster. This gives the familiar smooth, approximately parabolic velocity profile in a straight tube. Figure 01. Idealised laminar blood-flow profile Near the wall: slow Near centre: fast Original schematic — not a measured flow profile The ideal no-slip profile explains the lecturer's arrows. Actual blood contains cells and flowing vessels branch, pulse and deform, so the profile is not always exactly parabolic. Turbulent or disturbed flow has changing local velocity directions, often generating audible sounds such as bruits or murmurs. It is made more likely by high velocity, sudden changes in vessel geometry and lower blood viscosity. The lecture introduces the Reynolds number (Re) to describe this tendency: Reynolds number Re = ρ v D η ρ = density; v = velocity; D = diameter; η = dynamic viscosity. Higher Reynolds number means a greater likelihood of turbulent flow, not a guarantee. The vessel's curvature, pulsatility, branching and stenoses also matter. Two different symbols: R denotes vascular resistance in the flow law; Re denotes Reynolds number , which is dimensionless. The lecturer uses similar abbreviated labels for both, but they are not interchangeable. Anaemia: Lower viscosity and a compensatory increase in flow can promote a systolic flow murmur without structural valve disease. Local narrowing: Velocity may accelerate through a stenosis, creating disturbed downstream flow and a vascular bruit. 6. Why is vessel radius such a powerful determinant of flow? The lecturer derives the Hagen–Poiseuille formula for ideal steady laminar flow through a straight, rigid circular tube: Hagen–Poiseuille equation Q = π Δ P r 4 8 η L r = vessel radius; L = vessel length; η = viscosity. Combined with R = ΔP/Q , this gives: Ideal tube resistance R = 8 η L π r 4 The key relationship is R ∝ 1/r⁴ . When radius decreases, resistance increases dramatically. Radius halved Resistance ×16 At unchanged ΔP: flow falls to 1/16 Radius doubled Resistance ÷16 At unchanged ΔP: flow increases sixteenfold Worked mechanism: If the radius changes from r to r/2, the new resistance is 2⁴ = 16 times larger, provided length and viscosity remain unchanged . With a fixed pressure difference, Q = ΔP/R means flow falls to one-sixteenth. These relationships are mathematical aids , not exact universal predictions: blood is non-Newtonian, vessels are compliant rather than rigid, and vascular tone and pressures change in living people. Does a faster jet at a stenosis mean total blood flow increases? No. At the narrowed throat, cross-sectional area decreases and velocity can rise locally, even while the stenosis increases resistance and reduces overall blood flow. A high local speed must not be confused with a high volume flow rate . 7. Viscosity, haematocrit and blood cells Viscosity describes internal resistance to flow. Blood viscosity depends on haematocrit, plasma proteins, temperature, and red-cell deformation or aggregation . The lecturer highlights: Polycythaemia: More red cells generally increase viscosity, raising viscous resistance and cardiac work. Anaemia: Viscosity is often lower, but reduced oxygen-carrying capacity can increase cardiac-output demand and produce a flow murmur. Abnormal red-cell rigidity: Conditions such as sickle-cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis can alter rheology. In the very smallest vessels, the apparent viscosity differs from predictions for blood in large rigid tubes because cells change their distribution and deformation. Therefore, slide 71's suggestion that viscosity has little effect in all small vessels should be treated as a context-dependent approximation , not a universal statement. 8. Why are arterioles the main controllable resistance vessels? The vascular network contains vessels in series (one after another) and in parallel (multiple paths serving different tissues). With vessels in series, pressure drops add. With many open pathways in parallel, the overall resistance is lower than that of a single pathway alone. This explains an apparent puzzle: each capillary has a tiny radius, but there are very many capillaries in parallel . Arterioles, with their active muscular control of diameter, account for a large part of the important pressure drop and resistance regulation. Physiological application: Constricting arterioles reduces flow into a tissue at the same perfusion pressure. Dilation lowers local resistance and can increase flow. 9. How can blood flow be measured? The lecturer lists three techniques on slide 62. Their underlying physical signals differ: Method What it measures or detects How it relates to flow Electromagnetic flowmeter Voltage induced by conducting blood in a magnetic field Used to estimate blood movement through the field Doppler ultrasound flowmeter Frequency shift from moving blood cells Estimates velocity along the ultrasound beam Plethysmography Changes in tissue or limb volume Can estimate blood inflow with appropriate experimental conditions Key distinction: Doppler primarily estimates velocity , not volume flow on its own. To estimate total flow, an estimate of the vessel's area and the velocity profile is also required. The lecture mentions a historical arm-to-tongue circulation time around 15 seconds . This is an illustrative transit-time estimate, not a fixed physiological constant for every person. 10. Why can veins hold more blood than arteries? Vascular compliance describes how much volume a vessel can accommodate for a given pressure change. Compliance C = Δ V Δ P Large veins are more compliant than arteries. Their volume can rise considerably before venous pressure rises by the same amount. This is why veins are called capacitance vessels , whereas arterioles are commonly described as resistance vessels . The lecturer gives an approximate distribution of blood volume on slide 75, with a large share in the venous system. The listed percentages are illustrative, not an exact complete partition ; their values do not add to 100% and vary with physiological conditions. Why does venoconstriction help cardiac output? When the venous system becomes less distensible, stored blood can be redirected towards the heart. This can increase venous return, end-diastolic volume and stroke volume through the Frank–Starling mechanism. Understanding Check 1. Why does blood keep moving through the arteries during ventricular diastole? Reveal explanation Large elastic arteries stretch during ventricular systole and store pressure energy. Their recoil during diastole sustains arterial pressure and the pressure gradient towards smaller vessels, so blood can keep moving between ventricular ejections. 2. An organ has an upstream-to-downstream pressure difference of 80 mmHg and a resistance of 20 mmHg·min/L. What is its flow? Reveal explanation Q = ΔP/R = 80/20 = 4 L/min. The pressure difference must be measured across the pathway; pressure at one end by itself does not establish the flow rate. The numbers are for arithmetic practice, not a named organ. 3. Why is mean capillary blood velocity low despite each capillary having a narrow lumen? Reveal explanation The capillary system has an enormous number of vessels in parallel . Their combined cross-sectional area is much greater than that of the aorta. Because v = Q/A, the same overall volume flow moving through a much larger combined area has a lower average velocity. 4. Why does ideal laminar flow have a greater velocity in the centre than beside the wall? Reveal explanation Fluid in immediate contact with the vessel wall moves slowly because of viscous boundary effects. Adjacent layers drag on one another, forming a velocity gradient. Flow towards the centre is less constrained and therefore faster. Real blood can depart from the ideal profile because it contains cells. 5. A vessel's radius falls to half its original value at unchanged ΔP, viscosity and length. Predict resistance and flow. Reveal explanation Hagen–Poiseuille gives R ∝ 1/r⁴ . Halving the radius therefore increases the predicted resistance by 16 times . With unchanged perfusion pressure and Q = ΔP/R, volume flow decreases to 1/16 of its previous value, under ideal-tube assumptions. 6. A stenosis produces a very high velocity at its narrowest point. Must total blood flow through the artery have increased? Reveal explanation No. Local velocity is volume flow divided by local area, so speed can rise as the cross-sectional area narrows even if volume flow remains the same or decreases. The narrowing may also raise resistance and reduce total flow, depending on the pressure gradient. 7. Why may anaemia produce a systolic flow murmur even with normal valves? Reveal explanation Anaemia often reduces blood viscosity and increases cardiac output to compensate for reduced oxygen-carrying capacity. Higher flow velocity and reduced viscosity can increase the tendency towards disturbed flow, producing audible vibrations without a structurally abnormal valve. 8. Distinguish vascular resistance R from the Reynolds number Re. Reveal explanation Resistance R relates the pressure difference across a pathway to the resulting volume flow. Reynolds number Re is dimensionless and compares effects of density, velocity, diameter and viscosity to assess the tendency towards turbulent flow. The two quantities are not equivalent. 9. Why are arterioles the main adjustable resistance vessels instead of capillaries? Reveal explanation Arterioles have smooth muscle capable of rapidly changing their radius and therefore flow resistance. Although individual capillaries are narrow, many operate in parallel and their overall equivalent resistance is reduced. Changing arteriolar tone strongly influences organ perfusion and systemic resistance. 10. What does Doppler ultrasound directly estimate, and what else would be needed to estimate volume flow? Reveal explanation Doppler detects frequency shifts from moving blood cells and estimates a component of velocity . Volume flow also depends on the cross-sectional area and the velocity profile across the vessel, so estimating Q requires more information than a single Doppler velocity reading. 11. Why do veins store more additional blood than arteries for a similar rise in pressure? Reveal explanation Veins have a greater compliance (C = ΔV/ΔP) . They accommodate a larger volume increase for a given pressure rise, acting as blood reservoirs. Arterioles instead use their muscular walls to regulate resistance. 12. Polycythaemia and anaemia may both increase cardiac workload. How do their mechanisms differ? Reveal explanation In polycythaemia , greater haematocrit usually increases viscosity and can raise flow resistance. In anaemia , reduced oxygen-carrying capacity often demands greater cardiac output to deliver enough oxygen, producing increased volume flow and workload even when viscosity is lower. Chapter Essentials Flow: Q = ΔP/R; a pressure difference , not isolated vessel pressure, drives net flow. Diastolic arterial flow: Elastic recoil helps maintain forward flow between ventricular ejections. Vessel functions: Elastic arteries, resistance arterioles, exchange capillaries and capacitance veins have distinct roles. Velocity versus flow: Q = vA; the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is enormous. Laminar flow: Fastest near the centre and slowest by the wall in the ideal model. Reynolds number: Re = ρvD/η, a dimensionless measure of turbulence tendency, not vascular resistance. Hagen–Poiseuille: Q = πΔPr⁴/(8ηL) for ideal-tube flow; R = 8ηL/(πr⁴). Radius effect: Halving radius increases ideal resistance sixteenfold at unchanged other factors. Arteriolar control: Small changes in muscular-vessel radius substantially regulate blood flow. Viscosity: Depends on haematocrit, plasma components and red-cell properties; blood is not an ideal fluid. Measurement: Electromagnetic, Doppler and plethysmographic techniques detect different flow-related signals. Compliance: C = ΔV/ΔP; veins accommodate large volume changes at comparatively low pressure. Small-vessel and lymphatic physiology: Introduced here, developed in subsequent chapters."},{"slug":"blood-pressure","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"76-83","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure","title":"1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?"},{"id":"2-why-doesnt-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole","title":"2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?"},{"id":"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate","title":"3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?"},{"id":"4-what-is-pulse-pressure","title":"4. What is pulse pressure?"},{"id":"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isnt-it-simply-the-midpoint","title":"5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?"},{"id":"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart","title":"6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?"},{"id":"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure","title":"7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?"},{"id":"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately","title":"8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?"},{"id":"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation","title":"9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"arterial-blood-pressure\">Arterial Blood Pressure</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 76–83. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> pressure–flow relationships, posture-related hydrostatic calculations, measurement technique and cuff-related errors.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-arterial-blood-pressure\">1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Arterial blood pressure</strong> is the lateral pressure exerted by circulating blood against the arterial walls. It changes throughout each heartbeat because the left ventricle ejects blood intermittently.</p>\n<p>Two characteristic values are normally reported:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Systolic blood pressure (SBP)</strong></td><td>Highest arterial pressure reached during ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)</strong></td><td>Lowest arterial pressure before the next ventricular ejection</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>A reading such as <strong>120/70 mmHg</strong> means systolic pressure is 120 mmHg and diastolic pressure is 70 mmHg. These are <strong>illustrative numbers from the lecture</strong>, not an instruction to diagnose everyone's BP against one fixed value.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does pressure oscillate?</strong> Ventricular ejection causes a rise in aortic blood volume and pressure. Between ejections, arterial pressure gradually falls because blood continues escaping into the smaller vessels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-doesnt-blood-stop-moving-during-diastole\">2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole?</h2>\n<p>At first glance, this seems puzzling. During diastole the aortic valve is closed, so no new blood is being ejected into the aorta. Yet blood still moves through the systemic arteries.</p>\n<p>The explanation is the <strong>Windkessel effect</strong> (the lecturer's term):</p>\n<ol><li>In <strong>systole</strong>, the ventricle ejects blood into the aorta. Elastic arterial walls <strong>stretch</strong> and temporarily store part of the pressure energy.</li><li>In <strong>diastole</strong>, those walls <strong>recoil</strong>, releasing stored energy as blood continues flowing downstream, even with the aortic valve closed.</li></ol>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure bp-windkessel-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two original arterial diagrams: during systole the large artery widens as blood enters and the wall expands; during diastole the artery recoils and sustains forward flow with the aortic valve closed.\"><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\" font-weight=\"800\"><text x=\"15\" y=\"21\">Systole</text><text x=\"15\" y=\"105\">Diastole</text></g><g stroke=\"#bd515a\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"#fce1e0\"><path d=\"M124 42 C200 23 290 23 357 42 L475 42 L475 81 L357 81 C290 100 200 100 124 81 Z\"/><path d=\"M124 118 C219 112 290 112 357 118 L475 118 L475 143 L357 143 C290 149 219 149 124 143 Z\"/></g><g stroke=\"#2162be\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M147 60H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M147 131H447m-13 -8 13 8 -13 8\" fill=\"none\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11.5\"><text x=\"488\" y=\"64\">Flow →</text><text x=\"488\" y=\"137\">Flow →</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"34\">Artery expands</text><text x=\"213\" y=\"160\">Artery recoils</text></g></svg><p>Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale.</p></figure>\n<p>This does <strong>not</strong> mean arterial pressure remains constant. It still declines during diastole; recoil merely slows the decline and preserves forward flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical connection:</strong> A stiffer aorta stretches less for a given stroke volume, so systolic pressure and pulse pressure may rise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-do-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure-depend-on-more-than-heart-rate\">3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate?</h2>\n<p>Blood pressure reflects the interaction between <strong>cardiac output, vascular resistance, circulating volume and arterial compliance</strong>.</p>\n<p>A useful steady-state systemic relationship is:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Mean pressure and flow</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MAP</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>CVP</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>CO</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>SVR</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance.</span></div>\n<p>If CVP is small relative to MAP, a common approximation is <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong>. Therefore an increased heart rate is not sufficient by itself to establish what BP will do. Stroke volume, resistance and compensation also matter.</p>\n<p>For example, during haemorrhage, sympathetic vasoconstriction may help preserve BP even while circulating blood volume and cardiac output are falling. <strong>Maintained BP does not guarantee adequate tissue perfusion.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-is-pulse-pressure\">4. What is pulse pressure?</h2>\n<p><strong>Pulse pressure (PP)</strong> is the difference between systolic and diastolic arterial pressure.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Pulse pressure</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PP</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>SBP</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>DBP</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">For 120/70 mmHg: <strong>PP = 50 mmHg</strong>.</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why does the pulse occur?</strong> Each ventricular ejection raises arterial pressure and expands the arterial wall. A pressure wave travels through the arteries; it is not the same thing as a particular bolus of blood travelling at the speed of the palpable pulse.</p>\n<p>Pulse pressure is influenced by <strong>stroke volume and arterial compliance</strong>, among other factors. A greater SV tends to expand the aorta more; a stiffer artery develops a larger pressure rise for the same added blood volume. Thus <strong>a wide pulse pressure is not automatically proof of increased stroke volume</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-is-mean-arterial-pressure-and-why-isnt-it-simply-the-midpoint\">5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint?</h2>\n<p><strong>Mean arterial pressure (MAP)</strong> is the <strong>average arterial pressure over an entire cardiac cycle</strong>. At usual resting heart rates, the heart typically spends more time in diastole than systole. The mean pressure therefore lies closer to the diastolic value than to the arithmetic midpoint.</p>\n<p>The lecturer's approximation is:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Approximate mean arterial pressure</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>MAP</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>DBP</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>(</mo><mi>SBP</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>DBP</mi><mo>)</mo></math><span class=\"equation-example\">At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure.</span></div>\n<p>For <strong>120/70 mmHg</strong>, pulse pressure is 50 mmHg, so <strong>MAP ≈ 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this an estimate?</strong> The exact average depends on the <em>shape and duration</em> of the arterial pressure waveform. When heart rate or waveform shape changes markedly, the one-third approximation becomes less reliable. True mean arterial pressure is the time average across the cycle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-why-does-measured-bp-depend-on-whether-the-arm-is-above-or-below-the-heart\">6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer highlights the importance of <strong>gravity</strong> and heart-level reference.</p>\n<p>Fluid pressure in a vertical column changes according to the height difference:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Hydrostatic pressure change</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></math><span class=\"equation-example\">ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference.</span></div>\n<p>For whole blood, this corresponds to <strong>about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre</strong> of vertical displacement.</p>\n<div class=\"bp-height-rules\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Arm height measurement bias\"><div><strong>Arm 20 cm below heart</strong><span>About +15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher</small></div><div><strong>Arm 20 cm above heart</strong><span>About −15–16 mmHg</span><small>Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower</small></div></div>\n<p>These are approximate physics examples for the height component, not predictions of a person's entire measured BP response to changing posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Why support the arm?</strong> If the person holds the arm up using their own muscles, isometric exertion can itself alter BP. So the arm should rest on a support <strong>at heart level</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-how-does-a-cuff-and-stethoscope-reveal-systolic-and-diastolic-pressure\">7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure?</h2>\n<p>The traditional <strong>auscultatory method</strong> uses an upper-arm cuff, pressure gauge and stethoscope over the <strong>brachial artery</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phase-a-cuff-pressure-higher-than-systolic-pressure\">Phase A — Cuff pressure higher than systolic pressure</h3>\n<p>The cuff compresses the brachial artery sufficiently to stop flow beneath it. <strong>No Korotkoff sounds are heard</strong>, because blood is not passing through the compressed segment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phase-b-cuff-pressure-falls-just-below-systolic-pressure\">Phase B — Cuff pressure falls just below systolic pressure</h3>\n<p>During each systolic peak, arterial pressure is now high enough to force some blood through the partially compressed artery. Flow becomes rapid and disturbed, generating the first characteristic tapping sounds.</p>\n<p><strong>The first consistent Korotkoff sound (Phase I) marks systolic pressure.</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"phase-c-cuff-pressure-lies-between-systolic-and-diastolic-pressures\">Phase C — Cuff pressure lies between systolic and diastolic pressures</h3>\n<p>The artery opens during part of the cardiac cycle but is still compressed during another part. Sounds continue and may change in pitch, loudness or character as cuff pressure decreases.</p>\n<h3 id=\"phase-d-cuff-pressure-falls-below-diastolic-pressure\">Phase D — Cuff pressure falls below diastolic pressure</h3>\n<p>The artery can remain open throughout the cycle, so the sounds ultimately disappear as the obstruction is relieved.</p>\n<p><strong>Disappearance of Korotkoff sounds (Phase V) generally marks diastolic pressure in adults.</strong> In some circumstances, muffling (Phase IV) may be used rather than disappearance.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Cuff pressure</th><th>Arterial state</th><th>What is heard?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Above SBP</strong></td><td>Artery occluded</td><td>Silence</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Just below SBP</strong></td><td>Intermittent systolic flow begins</td><td><strong>First tapping sound = SBP</strong></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Between SBP and DBP</strong></td><td>Partially compressed artery</td><td>Korotkoff sounds</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Below DBP</strong></td><td>Flow no longer significantly obstructed</td><td><strong>Sounds disappear = DBP</strong></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why are these sounds not ordinary S1 and S2?</strong> They are generated locally in the <em>partly compressed brachial artery</em> during cuff deflation, rather than by closing heart valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-how-should-bp-actually-be-measured-accurately\">8. How should BP actually be measured accurately?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer discusses <strong>auscultation, palpation, direct arterial cannulation and manometry</strong>. For a conventional manual cuff measurement:</p>\n<ol><li>Let the patient <strong>rest quietly</strong>, ideally for at least five minutes, with back supported and feet flat when seated; avoid talking during measurement.</li><li>Use a <strong>correctly sized cuff</strong> on the bare upper arm, supported at <strong>heart level</strong>.</li><li>Palpate the brachial or radial pulse to estimate systolic pressure and help avoid missing an auscultatory gap.</li><li>Inflate sufficiently above the palpated systolic level, then <strong>deflate gradually</strong> (about 2 mmHg per second) while listening over the brachial artery.</li><li>Record the <strong>first consistent Korotkoff sound</strong> as systolic and the <strong>disappearance</strong> as diastolic for most adult measurements.</li></ol>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-palpation-alone-tell-you\">What does palpation alone tell you?</h3>\n<p>A palpated return of the radial pulse during cuff deflation estimates <strong>systolic pressure</strong>. It <strong>cannot reliably identify diastolic pressure</strong>, because palpation does not provide the necessary information about the Korotkoff sound phases.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-do-cuff-sizes-matter\">Why do cuff sizes matter?</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Technique error</th><th>Typical direction of error</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Cuff too small</strong> for the arm</td><td>Falsely <strong>high</strong> reading</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Cuff too large</strong> for the arm</td><td>Falsely <strong>low</strong> reading</td></tr><tr><td>Arm hangs <strong>below</strong> heart level</td><td>Falsely <strong>high</strong> reading</td></tr><tr><td>Arm held <strong>above</strong> heart level</td><td>Falsely <strong>low</strong> reading</td></tr><tr><td>Talking, unsupported arm or recent exertion</td><td>Can alter the reading, often upward</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The original lecture explains the importance of larger cuff sizes for larger arms and appropriately sized cuffs for children. The error is about the <strong>cuff dimensions relative to arm circumference</strong>, not merely body weight.</p>\n<p><strong>Supplementary:</strong> Automated oscillometric monitors estimate BP using cuff-pressure oscillations and validated algorithms. Their displayed values are not derived by a human listening for Korotkoff sounds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-why-does-pressure-decrease-as-blood-travels-through-the-circulation\">9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation?</h2>\n<p>The mean pressure does not remain constant from the aorta to the venae cavae. Some pressure energy is lost as blood flows against resistance.</p>\n<p>The lecturer's final diagram (slide 83) shows the biggest mean pressure fall across the <strong>arterioles</strong>, consistent with their key role as resistance vessels.</p>\n<div class=\"bp-drop-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Main locations of systemic pressure decrease\"><span><strong>Aorta / arteries</strong><small>High pressure · strong pulses</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Arterioles</strong><small>Large pressure drop</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Capillaries / veins</strong><small>Lower pressure · damped pulses</small></span></div>\n<p><strong>Why do pulses diminish in the microcirculation?</strong> Pulsatile energy is progressively damped as blood passes through elastic vessels and resistance pathways. Capillary pressure and velocity also differ from aortic values.</p>\n<p>The fall in mean pressure is <strong>not evenly distributed</strong> across all segments. Arteriolar tone can therefore affect both the amount of blood reaching tissues and the systemic pressure upstream.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why does blood keep flowing through arteries during diastole, even though the aortic valve is closed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During ventricular systole, the aorta and large elastic arteries stretch and store energy. During diastole, their <strong>elastic recoil</strong> maintains an arterial-to-peripheral pressure gradient, permitting continuing forward flow. Pressure declines but does not immediately become zero when ejection stops.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. A student's BP is 120/70 mmHg. Calculate pulse pressure and approximate MAP.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>PP = 120 − 70 = 50 mmHg.</strong> At an ordinary resting heart rate, <strong>MAP ≈ DBP + PP/3 = 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg</strong>. MAP is the average over time, so this is an approximation rather than the simple midpoint of SBP and DBP.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why is the mean arterial pressure closer to diastolic than systolic pressure at usual resting heart rates?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>At typical resting rates, the cardiac cycle spends more time in diastole than at the highest systolic pressure. MAP is a <strong>time-weighted average</strong> across the waveform, so the longer diastolic interval gives the lower pressure more weight. The exact result varies with waveform and heart rate.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. A person's upper-arm cuff is positioned 20 cm below heart level. What approximate hydrostatic error would you expect?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The hydrostatic component is about <strong>0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre</strong> for whole blood. At 20 cm below heart level, the observed pressure may be approximately <strong>15–16 mmHg higher</strong> because of that height difference. The arm should be supported at heart level for a comparable reading.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why can a very small BP cuff produce a falsely high blood pressure reading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A cuff too small for the upper-arm circumference does not transmit its indicated pressure uniformly or effectively enough to compress the artery. The observer may need to inflate to a <strong>higher recorded cuff pressure</strong> to occlude the vessel, so the displayed BP can be falsely elevated.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure. Why are Korotkoff sounds initially absent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The cuff has compressed the brachial artery so that blood cannot pass beneath it. Without intermittent blood flow through the partly compressed segment, there is no local sound-generating vibration. The first tapping sounds emerge when cuff pressure falls just below the systolic peak.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. What physiological event causes the first audible Korotkoff sound during deflation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>When cuff pressure falls just below the arterial <strong>systolic pressure</strong>, blood begins to pass through the partly compressed brachial artery during each pressure peak. The resulting disturbed flow and vessel vibration produce the first tapping sounds (Phase I), which estimate SBP.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Why does the disappearance of Korotkoff sounds generally indicate diastolic pressure in adults?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Once cuff pressure falls sufficiently below diastolic arterial pressure, the artery can remain open throughout the cycle rather than becoming intermittently compressed. The flow-related sounds disappear (Phase V), which is commonly used as the adult DBP endpoint.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why can't palpation alone determine both systolic and diastolic pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Palpation can detect when the distal pulse returns as cuff pressure falls, providing a useful estimate of <strong>systolic pressure</strong>. It does not identify the pattern or disappearance of Korotkoff sounds, so it cannot reliably determine <strong>diastolic pressure</strong> by this method.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Why can a stiff aorta have a wider pulse pressure than a more compliant aorta at the same stroke volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A compliant aorta expands more when the same blood volume enters it, limiting the pressure increase. A stiffer aorta expands less, so the same stroke volume can produce a larger systolic pressure rise and often a <strong>wider pulse pressure</strong>. Stroke volume and compliance both influence the result.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. A patient with severe blood loss develops tachycardia and vasoconstriction, yet tissue perfusion is poor. Why might BP be misleading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage lowers venous return, EDV and stroke volume. Reflex tachycardia and increased systemic vascular resistance can temporarily support mean arterial pressure, but <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong> may still be too low to meet tissue oxygen needs. A near-normal pressure does not ensure adequate blood flow.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Why is the largest fall in systemic mean pressure often across arterioles rather than uniformly across all vessels?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Arterioles have relatively narrow lumens and active smooth muscle that regulates resistance. They are a major resistance segment, so a substantial fraction of the perfusion-pressure difference is expended across them. This allows arteriolar tone to govern local flow and strongly influence upstream arterial pressure.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Arterial pressure:</strong> SBP is the systolic peak and DBP the minimum pressure before the next ejection.</li><li><strong>Windkessel effect:</strong> Elastic artery expansion during systole and recoil in diastole maintain forward flow.</li><li><strong>Mean pressure and resistance:</strong> MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR; heart rate alone does not determine BP.</li><li><strong>Pulse pressure:</strong> PP = SBP − DBP; affected by stroke volume and arterial compliance.</li><li><strong>Mean arterial pressure:</strong> MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP at usual resting heart rates; it is a time average, not an arithmetic midpoint.</li><li><strong>Gravity:</strong> Hydrostatic pressure changes by approximately 0.77–0.78 mmHg for each centimetre of vertical displacement of whole blood.</li><li><strong>Cuff at heart level:</strong> Below heart → falsely high; above heart → falsely low hydrostatic contribution.</li><li><strong>Korotkoff Phase I:</strong> First tapping sound during cuff deflation estimates SBP.</li><li><strong>Korotkoff Phase V:</strong> Disappearance of sounds generally estimates adult DBP.</li><li><strong>Palpation:</strong> Can estimate systolic but not reliably diastolic pressure.</li><li><strong>Cuff sizing:</strong> Too small commonly reads falsely high; too large commonly reads falsely low.</li><li><strong>Measurement quality:</strong> Quiet rest, supported arm, bare upper arm, correct cuff and gradual deflation matter.</li><li><strong>Pressure through vessels:</strong> Largest mean pressure drop occurs across resistance arterioles; pulsations progressively dampen.</li><li><strong>Clinical reasoning:</strong> Maintained pressure does not guarantee normal cardiac output or tissue oxygen delivery.</li></ol>","plain":"Arterial Blood Pressure Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 76–83. Supplementary clarification: pressure–flow relationships, posture-related hydrostatic calculations, measurement technique and cuff-related errors. 1. What exactly is arterial blood pressure? Arterial blood pressure is the lateral pressure exerted by circulating blood against the arterial walls. It changes throughout each heartbeat because the left ventricle ejects blood intermittently. Two characteristic values are normally reported: Measurement What it means Systolic blood pressure (SBP) Highest arterial pressure reached during ventricular systole Diastolic blood pressure (DBP) Lowest arterial pressure before the next ventricular ejection A reading such as 120/70 mmHg means systolic pressure is 120 mmHg and diastolic pressure is 70 mmHg. These are illustrative numbers from the lecture , not an instruction to diagnose everyone's BP against one fixed value. Why does pressure oscillate? Ventricular ejection causes a rise in aortic blood volume and pressure. Between ejections, arterial pressure gradually falls because blood continues escaping into the smaller vessels. 2. Why doesn't blood stop moving during diastole? At first glance, this seems puzzling. During diastole the aortic valve is closed, so no new blood is being ejected into the aorta. Yet blood still moves through the systemic arteries. The explanation is the Windkessel effect (the lecturer's term): In systole , the ventricle ejects blood into the aorta. Elastic arterial walls stretch and temporarily store part of the pressure energy. In diastole , those walls recoil , releasing stored energy as blood continues flowing downstream, even with the aortic valve closed. Figure 01. Windkessel mechanism — elastic arterial energy storage and recoil Systole Diastole Flow → Flow → Artery expands Artery recoils Original schematic showing that arterial elasticity converts intermittent ventricular ejection into a more continuous downstream flow. Not drawn to scale. This does not mean arterial pressure remains constant. It still declines during diastole; recoil merely slows the decline and preserves forward flow. Clinical connection: A stiffer aorta stretches less for a given stroke volume, so systolic pressure and pulse pressure may rise. 3. Why do systolic and diastolic pressure depend on more than heart rate? Blood pressure reflects the interaction between cardiac output, vascular resistance, circulating volume and arterial compliance . A useful steady-state systemic relationship is: Mean pressure and flow MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR MAP = mean arterial pressure; CVP = central venous pressure; SVR = systemic vascular resistance. If CVP is small relative to MAP, a common approximation is MAP ≈ CO × SVR . Therefore an increased heart rate is not sufficient by itself to establish what BP will do. Stroke volume, resistance and compensation also matter. For example, during haemorrhage, sympathetic vasoconstriction may help preserve BP even while circulating blood volume and cardiac output are falling. Maintained BP does not guarantee adequate tissue perfusion. 4. What is pulse pressure? Pulse pressure (PP) is the difference between systolic and diastolic arterial pressure. Pulse pressure PP = SBP − DBP For 120/70 mmHg: PP = 50 mmHg . Why does the pulse occur? Each ventricular ejection raises arterial pressure and expands the arterial wall. A pressure wave travels through the arteries; it is not the same thing as a particular bolus of blood travelling at the speed of the palpable pulse. Pulse pressure is influenced by stroke volume and arterial compliance , among other factors. A greater SV tends to expand the aorta more; a stiffer artery develops a larger pressure rise for the same added blood volume. Thus a wide pulse pressure is not automatically proof of increased stroke volume . 5. What is mean arterial pressure, and why isn't it simply the midpoint? Mean arterial pressure (MAP) is the average arterial pressure over an entire cardiac cycle . At usual resting heart rates, the heart typically spends more time in diastole than systole. The mean pressure therefore lies closer to the diastolic value than to the arithmetic midpoint. The lecturer's approximation is: Approximate mean arterial pressure MAP ≈ DBP + 1 3 ( SBP − DBP ) At ordinary resting heart rates: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure. For 120/70 mmHg , pulse pressure is 50 mmHg, so MAP ≈ 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg . Why is this an estimate? The exact average depends on the shape and duration of the arterial pressure waveform. When heart rate or waveform shape changes markedly, the one-third approximation becomes less reliable. True mean arterial pressure is the time average across the cycle. 6. Why does measured BP depend on whether the arm is above or below the heart? The lecturer highlights the importance of gravity and heart-level reference. Fluid pressure in a vertical column changes according to the height difference: Hydrostatic pressure change Δ P = ρ g h ρ = fluid density; g = gravitational acceleration; h = vertical height difference. For whole blood, this corresponds to about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre of vertical displacement. Arm 20 cm below heart About +15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading higher Arm 20 cm above heart About −15–16 mmHg Hydrostatic contribution makes the reading lower These are approximate physics examples for the height component, not predictions of a person's entire measured BP response to changing posture. Why support the arm? If the person holds the arm up using their own muscles, isometric exertion can itself alter BP. So the arm should rest on a support at heart level . 7. How does a cuff and stethoscope reveal systolic and diastolic pressure? The traditional auscultatory method uses an upper-arm cuff, pressure gauge and stethoscope over the brachial artery . Phase A — Cuff pressure higher than systolic pressure The cuff compresses the brachial artery sufficiently to stop flow beneath it. No Korotkoff sounds are heard , because blood is not passing through the compressed segment. Phase B — Cuff pressure falls just below systolic pressure During each systolic peak, arterial pressure is now high enough to force some blood through the partially compressed artery. Flow becomes rapid and disturbed, generating the first characteristic tapping sounds. The first consistent Korotkoff sound (Phase I) marks systolic pressure. Phase C — Cuff pressure lies between systolic and diastolic pressures The artery opens during part of the cardiac cycle but is still compressed during another part. Sounds continue and may change in pitch, loudness or character as cuff pressure decreases. Phase D — Cuff pressure falls below diastolic pressure The artery can remain open throughout the cycle, so the sounds ultimately disappear as the obstruction is relieved. Disappearance of Korotkoff sounds (Phase V) generally marks diastolic pressure in adults. In some circumstances, muffling (Phase IV) may be used rather than disappearance. Cuff pressure Arterial state What is heard? Above SBP Artery occluded Silence Just below SBP Intermittent systolic flow begins First tapping sound = SBP Between SBP and DBP Partially compressed artery Korotkoff sounds Below DBP Flow no longer significantly obstructed Sounds disappear = DBP Why are these sounds not ordinary S1 and S2? They are generated locally in the partly compressed brachial artery during cuff deflation, rather than by closing heart valves. 8. How should BP actually be measured accurately? The lecturer discusses auscultation, palpation, direct arterial cannulation and manometry . For a conventional manual cuff measurement: Let the patient rest quietly , ideally for at least five minutes, with back supported and feet flat when seated; avoid talking during measurement. Use a correctly sized cuff on the bare upper arm, supported at heart level . Palpate the brachial or radial pulse to estimate systolic pressure and help avoid missing an auscultatory gap. Inflate sufficiently above the palpated systolic level, then deflate gradually (about 2 mmHg per second) while listening over the brachial artery. Record the first consistent Korotkoff sound as systolic and the disappearance as diastolic for most adult measurements. What does palpation alone tell you? A palpated return of the radial pulse during cuff deflation estimates systolic pressure . It cannot reliably identify diastolic pressure , because palpation does not provide the necessary information about the Korotkoff sound phases. Why do cuff sizes matter? Technique error Typical direction of error Cuff too small for the arm Falsely high reading Cuff too large for the arm Falsely low reading Arm hangs below heart level Falsely high reading Arm held above heart level Falsely low reading Talking, unsupported arm or recent exertion Can alter the reading, often upward The original lecture explains the importance of larger cuff sizes for larger arms and appropriately sized cuffs for children. The error is about the cuff dimensions relative to arm circumference , not merely body weight. Supplementary: Automated oscillometric monitors estimate BP using cuff-pressure oscillations and validated algorithms. Their displayed values are not derived by a human listening for Korotkoff sounds. 9. Why does pressure decrease as blood travels through the circulation? The mean pressure does not remain constant from the aorta to the venae cavae. Some pressure energy is lost as blood flows against resistance. The lecturer's final diagram (slide 83) shows the biggest mean pressure fall across the arterioles , consistent with their key role as resistance vessels. Aorta / arteries High pressure · strong pulses → Arterioles Large pressure drop → Capillaries / veins Lower pressure · damped pulses Why do pulses diminish in the microcirculation? Pulsatile energy is progressively damped as blood passes through elastic vessels and resistance pathways. Capillary pressure and velocity also differ from aortic values. The fall in mean pressure is not evenly distributed across all segments. Arteriolar tone can therefore affect both the amount of blood reaching tissues and the systemic pressure upstream. Understanding Check 1. Why does blood keep flowing through arteries during diastole, even though the aortic valve is closed? Reveal explanation During ventricular systole, the aorta and large elastic arteries stretch and store energy. During diastole, their elastic recoil maintains an arterial-to-peripheral pressure gradient, permitting continuing forward flow. Pressure declines but does not immediately become zero when ejection stops. 2. A student's BP is 120/70 mmHg. Calculate pulse pressure and approximate MAP. Reveal explanation PP = 120 − 70 = 50 mmHg. At an ordinary resting heart rate, MAP ≈ DBP + PP/3 = 70 + 50/3 ≈ 86.7 mmHg . MAP is the average over time, so this is an approximation rather than the simple midpoint of SBP and DBP. 3. Why is the mean arterial pressure closer to diastolic than systolic pressure at usual resting heart rates? Reveal explanation At typical resting rates, the cardiac cycle spends more time in diastole than at the highest systolic pressure. MAP is a time-weighted average across the waveform, so the longer diastolic interval gives the lower pressure more weight. The exact result varies with waveform and heart rate. 4. A person's upper-arm cuff is positioned 20 cm below heart level. What approximate hydrostatic error would you expect? Reveal explanation The hydrostatic component is about 0.77–0.78 mmHg per centimetre for whole blood. At 20 cm below heart level, the observed pressure may be approximately 15–16 mmHg higher because of that height difference. The arm should be supported at heart level for a comparable reading. 5. Why can a very small BP cuff produce a falsely high blood pressure reading? Reveal explanation A cuff too small for the upper-arm circumference does not transmit its indicated pressure uniformly or effectively enough to compress the artery. The observer may need to inflate to a higher recorded cuff pressure to occlude the vessel, so the displayed BP can be falsely elevated. 6. A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure. Why are Korotkoff sounds initially absent? Reveal explanation The cuff has compressed the brachial artery so that blood cannot pass beneath it. Without intermittent blood flow through the partly compressed segment, there is no local sound-generating vibration. The first tapping sounds emerge when cuff pressure falls just below the systolic peak. 7. What physiological event causes the first audible Korotkoff sound during deflation? Reveal explanation When cuff pressure falls just below the arterial systolic pressure , blood begins to pass through the partly compressed brachial artery during each pressure peak. The resulting disturbed flow and vessel vibration produce the first tapping sounds (Phase I), which estimate SBP. 8. Why does the disappearance of Korotkoff sounds generally indicate diastolic pressure in adults? Reveal explanation Once cuff pressure falls sufficiently below diastolic arterial pressure, the artery can remain open throughout the cycle rather than becoming intermittently compressed. The flow-related sounds disappear (Phase V), which is commonly used as the adult DBP endpoint. 9. Why can't palpation alone determine both systolic and diastolic pressure? Reveal explanation Palpation can detect when the distal pulse returns as cuff pressure falls, providing a useful estimate of systolic pressure . It does not identify the pattern or disappearance of Korotkoff sounds, so it cannot reliably determine diastolic pressure by this method. 10. Why can a stiff aorta have a wider pulse pressure than a more compliant aorta at the same stroke volume? Reveal explanation A compliant aorta expands more when the same blood volume enters it, limiting the pressure increase. A stiffer aorta expands less, so the same stroke volume can produce a larger systolic pressure rise and often a wider pulse pressure . Stroke volume and compliance both influence the result. 11. A patient with severe blood loss develops tachycardia and vasoconstriction, yet tissue perfusion is poor. Why might BP be misleading? Reveal explanation Haemorrhage lowers venous return, EDV and stroke volume. Reflex tachycardia and increased systemic vascular resistance can temporarily support mean arterial pressure, but CO = HR × SV may still be too low to meet tissue oxygen needs. A near-normal pressure does not ensure adequate blood flow. 12. Why is the largest fall in systemic mean pressure often across arterioles rather than uniformly across all vessels? Reveal explanation Arterioles have relatively narrow lumens and active smooth muscle that regulates resistance. They are a major resistance segment, so a substantial fraction of the perfusion-pressure difference is expended across them. This allows arteriolar tone to govern local flow and strongly influence upstream arterial pressure. Chapter Essentials Arterial pressure: SBP is the systolic peak and DBP the minimum pressure before the next ejection. Windkessel effect: Elastic artery expansion during systole and recoil in diastole maintain forward flow. Mean pressure and resistance: MAP − CVP ≈ CO × SVR; heart rate alone does not determine BP. Pulse pressure: PP = SBP − DBP; affected by stroke volume and arterial compliance. Mean arterial pressure: MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓PP at usual resting heart rates; it is a time average, not an arithmetic midpoint. Gravity: Hydrostatic pressure changes by approximately 0.77–0.78 mmHg for each centimetre of vertical displacement of whole blood. Cuff at heart level: Below heart → falsely high; above heart → falsely low hydrostatic contribution. Korotkoff Phase I: First tapping sound during cuff deflation estimates SBP. Korotkoff Phase V: Disappearance of sounds generally estimates adult DBP. Palpation: Can estimate systolic but not reliably diastolic pressure. Cuff sizing: Too small commonly reads falsely high; too large commonly reads falsely low. Measurement quality: Quiet rest, supported arm, bare upper arm, correct cuff and gradual deflation matter. Pressure through vessels: Largest mean pressure drop occurs across resistance arterioles; pulsations progressively dampen. Clinical reasoning: Maintained pressure does not guarantee normal cardiac output or tissue oxygen delivery."},{"slug":"pulses","title":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"22-26","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse","title":"1. What is an arterial pulse?"},{"id":"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse","title":"2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?"},{"id":"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck","title":"3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?"},{"id":"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents","title":"4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?"},{"id":"5-how-do-you-distinguish-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse","title":"5. How do you distinguish JVP from the carotid pulse?"},{"id":"6-how-is-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside","title":"6. How is JVP estimated at the bedside?"},{"id":"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important","title":"7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"arterial-pulse-and-jugular-venous-pulse\">Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 22–26. <strong>Supplementary teaching:</strong> arterial pulse contour, careful bedside JVP assessment and interpretation of abnormal waves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-an-arterial-pulse\">1. What is an arterial pulse?</h2>\n<p>Each left ventricular ejection raises aortic pressure and stretches its elastic wall. This expansion generates a <strong>pressure and distension wave</strong> that travels along the arteries, including to the radial artery at the wrist.</p>\n<p><strong>An arterial pulse is not the same as the speed of blood flow.</strong> Individual red cells move along the vessel, but the pressure wave travels through the blood–arterial wall system much faster. Thus you can feel a wrist pulse without imagining a packet of blood travelling from the heart to the wrist at that same speed.</p>\n<div class=\"pulse-mechanism\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"How an arterial pulse arises\"><span>LV ejection</span><b>→</b><span>Aortic pressure ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Arterial wall expands</span><b>→</b><span>Pressure wave propagates</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why does pulse-wave speed increase with age?</strong> The lecturer notes that arteries become more rigid. In a stiffer artery, wall deformation is less for a given pressure disturbance, so the pressure wave generally propagates faster. This does <strong>not</strong> imply that blood velocity itself rises as the arterial wall stiffens.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-do-we-assess-when-palpating-a-pulse\">2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists <strong>rate, volume, strength, character and location</strong>. Rhythm, symmetry and timing are useful supplementary clinical descriptors.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What is assessed</th><th>Why it can be useful</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Rate</strong></td><td>Palpable beats per minute</td><td>Reflects the number of effective arterial pulsations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rhythm</strong></td><td>Regularity of intervals</td><td>Irregularity may reflect rhythm disturbances</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Volume/amplitude</strong></td><td>Size of palpable distension</td><td>Influenced by pulse pressure and arterial properties</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Strength</strong></td><td>How prominent the pulse feels</td><td>Influenced by pressure wave and examination conditions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Character/contour</strong></td><td>Speed of upstroke and subsequent contour</td><td>May reflect ventricular ejection or arterial/valve disease</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Location/symmetry</strong></td><td>Which artery and whether both sides match</td><td>Can identify unequal or delayed peripheral pulsations</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why are pulse volume and pulse pressure related?</strong> A larger stroke volume or stiffer artery may generate a larger pressure change during systole. But peripheral pulse character also depends on reflected waves, arterial tone and the arterial path.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied example:</strong> If a person has a very fast heart rhythm but poor ventricular filling, the heart may generate weak peripheral pulsations; electrical heart rate and palpable pulse rate need not always match exactly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-why-is-the-jugular-venous-pulse-visible-in-the-neck\">3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>jugular venous pulse (JVP)</strong> reflects changes in <strong>right atrial pressure</strong> transmitted through the connected central venous column to the internal jugular veins.</p>\n<p>It does not require blood to flow <em>backwards</em> up the entire vein. <strong>Pressure changes can propagate through a fluid column even while the average flow remains towards the heart.</strong></p>\n<p>The internal jugular vein is useful because of its relatively direct connection to the right atrium. The visible waveform is a sequence of pressure rises and falls during the cardiac cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Arterial pulsation is mainly a transmitted ejection-pressure wave; jugular venous pulsation reflects <strong>right atrial pressure events</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-are-the-a-c-and-v-waves-separated-by-x-and-y-descents\">4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer describes <strong>three positive waves: a, c and v</strong>, and two descents, <strong>x and y</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure jvp-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. A normal jugular venous pulse — sequence and meaning</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 245\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic jugular venous pressure curve showing an a wave from atrial contraction, c wave during early ventricular systole, x descent from atrial relaxation, v wave during atrial filling, and y descent during tricuspid opening.\"><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"203\" x2=\"611\" y2=\"203\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\".5\"/><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"36\" y2=\"203\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" opacity=\".5\"/><path d=\"M45 168 C75 166 93 88 120 76 C144 72 152 142 178 132 C195 129 199 104 214 111 C233 120 244 179 287 181 C340 185 355 101 407 91 C451 83 467 179 509 179 C543 181 558 167 595 166\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"800\"><text x=\"115\" y=\"58\">a</text><text x=\"207\" y=\"97\">c</text><text x=\"283\" y=\"195\">x</text><text x=\"405\" y=\"75\">v</text><text x=\"512\" y=\"195\">y</text></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"42\" y=\"18\">Right atrial pressure ↑</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"230\">Time →</text></g></svg><p>Original teaching schematic (not a patient tracing). The <strong>a, c and v</strong> are pressure rises; <strong>x and y</strong> are falls. Timing and actual prominence vary between patients.</p></figure>\n<h3 id=\"a-wave-atrial-contraction\">a wave — atrial contraction</h3>\n<p>The right atrium contracts near the end of ventricular diastole, increasing right atrial pressure. This pressure rise produces the <strong>a wave</strong>. It is <strong>not simply regurgitation</strong> through the great veins; the normal atrial pressure rise is transmitted into the venous column even though systemic venous return continues.</p>\n<h3 id=\"c-wave-early-ventricular-systole\">c wave — early ventricular systole</h3>\n<p>When the right ventricle begins contracting, the closed tricuspid valve can bulge towards the atrium, contributing to the <strong>c wave</strong>. A transmitted nearby carotid impulse can also affect the observed jugular contour. The c wave is often difficult to distinguish visibly at the bedside.</p>\n<h3 id=\"x-descent-atrial-relaxation-and-downward-valve-motion\">x descent — atrial relaxation and downward valve motion</h3>\n<p>After atrial contraction, the atrium relaxes. During right ventricular systole, downward displacement of the tricuspid annular region can also help lower right atrial pressure. Together these produce the <strong>x descent</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"v-wave-atrial-filling-while-the-tricuspid-valve-is-closed\">v wave — atrial filling while the tricuspid valve is closed</h3>\n<p>Systemic venous blood continues entering the right atrium <strong>during ventricular systole</strong>, when the tricuspid valve is closed. The rising atrial volume and pressure produce the <strong>v wave</strong>, even though the atrium is not contracting.</p>\n<h3 id=\"y-descent-tricuspid-opening-and-early-ventricular-filling\">y descent — tricuspid opening and early ventricular filling</h3>\n<p>During early ventricular diastole, the tricuspid valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure. Blood moves quickly into the right ventricle, right atrial pressure decreases, and the <strong>y descent</strong> appears.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>JVP feature</th><th>Main cause</th><th>Closest mechanical event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>a wave</strong></td><td>Atrial contraction</td><td>Late diastole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>c wave</strong></td><td>Tricuspid bulging / transmitted arterial impulse</td><td>Early ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>x descent</strong></td><td>Atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion</td><td>Ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>v wave</strong></td><td>Atrial filling against closed tricuspid valve</td><td>Late ventricular systole</td></tr><tr><td><strong>y descent</strong></td><td>Right atrial emptying after tricuspid opening</td><td>Early ventricular diastole</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-you-distinguish-jvp-from-the-carotid-pulse\">5. How do you distinguish JVP from the carotid pulse?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Jugular venous pulsation</th><th>Carotid arterial pulse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Usually <strong>not palpable</strong></td><td>Clearly <strong>palpable</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Often multiple components per cycle</td><td>One principal systolic outward impulse</td></tr><tr><td>Changes significantly with reclining angle</td><td>Less affected by the patient's position</td></tr><tr><td>Normally falls on inspiration</td><td>No equivalent regular inspiratory JVP fall</td></tr><tr><td>Reflects <strong>right atrial pressure</strong></td><td>Reflects transmitted systemic arterial pressure</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>This distinction matters because mistaking the carotid pulse for JVP can lead to an incorrect conclusion about right atrial pressure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-is-jvp-estimated-at-the-bedside\">6. How is JVP estimated at the bedside?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer recommends reclining the patient at approximately <strong>45°</strong>, but the angle is <strong>adjusted</strong> to display the top of the venous column. If neck veins are very engorged, the patient may need to sit more upright; if the column is low, a flatter position may be necessary.</p>\n<p>The examiner observes the internal jugular venous pulsation (commonly on the right), identifies its highest sustained point and measures the <strong>vertical height above the sternal angle</strong>, keeping the ruler vertical. This is a clinical <em>estimate</em> of central venous/right atrial pressure, not a direct catheter measurement. Some conventional descriptions add an estimated distance from sternal angle to mid-right atrium; that anatomical offset varies.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does the column commonly fall during inspiration?</strong> More negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return into the thorax, usually lowering observable JVP. An unusual inspiratory rise can be clinically important but requires detailed evaluation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-which-abnormal-jvp-waves-are-most-important\">7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer gives three clinical connections:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Tricuspid incompetence/regurgitation:</strong> Blood leaks from RV into RA during systole, potentially producing prominent <strong>systolic v or fused c–v waves</strong>. The lecture describes a “big c wave”; clinically a large <strong>v/cv wave</strong> better reflects systolic regurgitant filling.</li><li><strong>Heart block or AV dissociation:</strong> Right atrial contraction may occur when the tricuspid valve is closed. Atrial pressure can rise abruptly, producing a <strong>cannon a wave</strong>.</li><li><strong>Right-sided heart failure/venous congestion:</strong> Elevated right atrial pressure can make JVP abnormally high. Waveform and height should be interpreted together.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Why aren't prominent v and cannon a waves interchangeable?</strong> A large v/cv wave reflects atrial filling or systolic regurgitation, whereas a cannon a wave reflects <strong>active atrial contraction against a closed valve</strong>. They are produced by different phases and pressure events.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why is a radial artery pulse not a packet of blood travelling from the aorta to the wrist?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The left ventricle raises aortic pressure and generates a wave of arterial-wall distension. That pressure wave propagates along connected arteries much faster than the average blood cells themselves travel. What the examiner palpates is the <strong>pressure/distension wave</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why does arterial stiffening with age tend to increase pulse-wave velocity?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Stiffer arteries deform less for a given pressure disturbance. A pressure wave therefore propagates more quickly through the blood–vessel system. This relates to <strong>wave transmission</strong>, not necessarily a rise in mean blood flow velocity.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Name the five arterial-pulse characteristics explicitly listed in the lecture.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Rate, volume, strength, character and location.</strong> Rate is beats per minute; volume and strength describe palpable size; character describes the shape of the impulse; and location identifies the artery examined. Rhythm and symmetry are useful added clinical descriptors.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why can the neck show JVP waves even though venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The right atrium changes pressure during contraction, relaxation and filling. Those <strong>pressure changes propagate through the connected venous column</strong> towards the neck. The direction of a pressure signal is not the same as the net direction of blood flow.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Which JVP wave is produced by right atrial contraction, and when does it occur?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>a wave</strong> is produced when the right atrium contracts near the end of ventricular diastole. It raises right atrial pressure before ventricular systole. Do not confuse it with the v wave, which develops while the atrium fills.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why does the v wave occur while the tricuspid valve is closed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Venous blood continues to enter the right atrium during ventricular systole, but the closed tricuspid valve prevents normal passage into the ventricle. The atrium therefore fills and its pressure rises, producing the <strong>v wave</strong> without atrial contraction.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. What produces the x and y descents, respectively?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>x descent</strong> is caused mainly by right atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid annular movement during ventricular systole. The <strong>y descent</strong> follows tricuspid opening, when accumulated right atrial blood flows into the right ventricle in early diastole.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. A visible neck pulsation is easily palpable and does not vary markedly with breathing or reclining angle. Is it more likely jugular or carotid?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>It is more consistent with the <strong>carotid arterial pulse</strong>, which is palpable and has a dominant systolic impulse. JVP is typically non-palpable, more complex and influenced by respiration and patient position.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might JVP decrease on inspiration?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Inspiration normally makes intrathoracic pressure more negative, facilitating systemic venous return into the thorax and right atrium. The venous pressure column observed in the neck therefore commonly falls. The response may be altered in specific cardiac diseases.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. In tricuspid regurgitation, why can the jugular v or c–v wave become unusually large?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>During ventricular systole, blood leaks backward through the incompetent tricuspid valve into the right atrium. This adds to ordinary venous filling and creates a larger <strong>systolic rise in right atrial pressure</strong>, appearing as a prominent v or fused c–v wave.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. How is a cannon a wave different from the large v wave of tricuspid regurgitation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>A <strong>cannon a wave</strong> occurs when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can happen with AV dissociation. A large <strong>v/cv wave</strong> is predominantly associated with atrial filling and/or regurgitant flow during ventricular systole. They represent different mechanical causes.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Why should the examiner change the reclining angle instead of insisting that every JVP be measured at 45°?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The aim is to bring the top of the jugular venous column into view. A very high column may require the person to sit more upright; a low column may require a flatter position. Once visible, its <strong>vertical height relative to the sternal angle</strong> provides a clinical estimate of central venous pressure.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Arterial pulse:</strong> A propagating pressure/distension wave created by ventricular ejection, not blood moving at wave speed.</li><li><strong>Arterial stiffness:</strong> Greater stiffness generally increases pulse-wave velocity.</li><li><strong>Lecture characteristics:</strong> Rate, volume, strength, character and location.</li><li><strong>JVP:</strong> Reflects transmitted right atrial pressure changes through the internal jugular venous column.</li><li><strong>Three positive JVP waves:</strong> a = atrial contraction; c = early ventricular systole; v = atrial filling while tricuspid closed.</li><li><strong>Two descents:</strong> x = atrial relaxation/downward tricuspid motion; y = atrial emptying following tricuspid opening.</li><li><strong>Jugular versus carotid:</strong> JVP is usually not palpable and changes with breathing and posture.</li><li><strong>JVP assessment:</strong> Observe the venous column and measure vertical height from the sternal angle; adjust patient angle to see it.</li><li><strong>Tricuspid regurgitation:</strong> May produce large systolic v/cv waves.</li><li><strong>Cannon a wave:</strong> Atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve, often with AV dissociation.</li><li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> High JVP can reflect elevated right atrial pressure and systemic venous congestion.</li><li><strong>The wave names reflect mechanisms:</strong> Understand what blood and valves are doing instead of simply memorising the letters.</li></ol>","plain":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 22–26. Supplementary teaching: arterial pulse contour, careful bedside JVP assessment and interpretation of abnormal waves. 1. What is an arterial pulse? Each left ventricular ejection raises aortic pressure and stretches its elastic wall. This expansion generates a pressure and distension wave that travels along the arteries, including to the radial artery at the wrist. An arterial pulse is not the same as the speed of blood flow. Individual red cells move along the vessel, but the pressure wave travels through the blood–arterial wall system much faster. Thus you can feel a wrist pulse without imagining a packet of blood travelling from the heart to the wrist at that same speed. LV ejection → Aortic pressure ↑ → Arterial wall expands → Pressure wave propagates Why does pulse-wave speed increase with age? The lecturer notes that arteries become more rigid. In a stiffer artery, wall deformation is less for a given pressure disturbance, so the pressure wave generally propagates faster. This does not imply that blood velocity itself rises as the arterial wall stiffens. 2. What do we assess when palpating a pulse? The lecturer lists rate, volume, strength, character and location . Rhythm, symmetry and timing are useful supplementary clinical descriptors. Feature What is assessed Why it can be useful Rate Palpable beats per minute Reflects the number of effective arterial pulsations Rhythm Regularity of intervals Irregularity may reflect rhythm disturbances Volume/amplitude Size of palpable distension Influenced by pulse pressure and arterial properties Strength How prominent the pulse feels Influenced by pressure wave and examination conditions Character/contour Speed of upstroke and subsequent contour May reflect ventricular ejection or arterial/valve disease Location/symmetry Which artery and whether both sides match Can identify unequal or delayed peripheral pulsations Why are pulse volume and pulse pressure related? A larger stroke volume or stiffer artery may generate a larger pressure change during systole. But peripheral pulse character also depends on reflected waves, arterial tone and the arterial path. Applied example: If a person has a very fast heart rhythm but poor ventricular filling, the heart may generate weak peripheral pulsations; electrical heart rate and palpable pulse rate need not always match exactly. 3. Why is the jugular venous pulse visible in the neck? The jugular venous pulse (JVP) reflects changes in right atrial pressure transmitted through the connected central venous column to the internal jugular veins. It does not require blood to flow backwards up the entire vein. Pressure changes can propagate through a fluid column even while the average flow remains towards the heart. The internal jugular vein is useful because of its relatively direct connection to the right atrium. The visible waveform is a sequence of pressure rises and falls during the cardiac cycle. Key distinction: Arterial pulsation is mainly a transmitted ejection-pressure wave; jugular venous pulsation reflects right atrial pressure events . 4. Why are the a, c and v waves separated by x and y descents? The lecturer describes three positive waves: a, c and v , and two descents, x and y . Figure 01. A normal jugular venous pulse — sequence and meaning a c x v y Right atrial pressure ↑ Time → Original teaching schematic (not a patient tracing). The a, c and v are pressure rises; x and y are falls. Timing and actual prominence vary between patients. a wave — atrial contraction The right atrium contracts near the end of ventricular diastole, increasing right atrial pressure. This pressure rise produces the a wave . It is not simply regurgitation through the great veins; the normal atrial pressure rise is transmitted into the venous column even though systemic venous return continues. c wave — early ventricular systole When the right ventricle begins contracting, the closed tricuspid valve can bulge towards the atrium, contributing to the c wave . A transmitted nearby carotid impulse can also affect the observed jugular contour. The c wave is often difficult to distinguish visibly at the bedside. x descent — atrial relaxation and downward valve motion After atrial contraction, the atrium relaxes. During right ventricular systole, downward displacement of the tricuspid annular region can also help lower right atrial pressure. Together these produce the x descent . v wave — atrial filling while the tricuspid valve is closed Systemic venous blood continues entering the right atrium during ventricular systole , when the tricuspid valve is closed. The rising atrial volume and pressure produce the v wave , even though the atrium is not contracting. y descent — tricuspid opening and early ventricular filling During early ventricular diastole, the tricuspid valve opens as ventricular pressure falls below right atrial pressure. Blood moves quickly into the right ventricle, right atrial pressure decreases, and the y descent appears. JVP feature Main cause Closest mechanical event a wave Atrial contraction Late diastole c wave Tricuspid bulging / transmitted arterial impulse Early ventricular systole x descent Atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion Ventricular systole v wave Atrial filling against closed tricuspid valve Late ventricular systole y descent Right atrial emptying after tricuspid opening Early ventricular diastole 5. How do you distinguish JVP from the carotid pulse? Jugular venous pulsation Carotid arterial pulse Usually not palpable Clearly palpable Often multiple components per cycle One principal systolic outward impulse Changes significantly with reclining angle Less affected by the patient's position Normally falls on inspiration No equivalent regular inspiratory JVP fall Reflects right atrial pressure Reflects transmitted systemic arterial pressure This distinction matters because mistaking the carotid pulse for JVP can lead to an incorrect conclusion about right atrial pressure. 6. How is JVP estimated at the bedside? The lecturer recommends reclining the patient at approximately 45° , but the angle is adjusted to display the top of the venous column. If neck veins are very engorged, the patient may need to sit more upright; if the column is low, a flatter position may be necessary. The examiner observes the internal jugular venous pulsation (commonly on the right), identifies its highest sustained point and measures the vertical height above the sternal angle , keeping the ruler vertical. This is a clinical estimate of central venous/right atrial pressure, not a direct catheter measurement. Some conventional descriptions add an estimated distance from sternal angle to mid-right atrium; that anatomical offset varies. Why does the column commonly fall during inspiration? More negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return into the thorax, usually lowering observable JVP. An unusual inspiratory rise can be clinically important but requires detailed evaluation. 7. Which abnormal JVP waves are most important? The lecturer gives three clinical connections: Tricuspid incompetence/regurgitation: Blood leaks from RV into RA during systole, potentially producing prominent systolic v or fused c–v waves . The lecture describes a “big c wave”; clinically a large v/cv wave better reflects systolic regurgitant filling. Heart block or AV dissociation: Right atrial contraction may occur when the tricuspid valve is closed. Atrial pressure can rise abruptly, producing a cannon a wave . Right-sided heart failure/venous congestion: Elevated right atrial pressure can make JVP abnormally high. Waveform and height should be interpreted together. Why aren't prominent v and cannon a waves interchangeable? A large v/cv wave reflects atrial filling or systolic regurgitation, whereas a cannon a wave reflects active atrial contraction against a closed valve . They are produced by different phases and pressure events. Understanding Check 1. Why is a radial artery pulse not a packet of blood travelling from the aorta to the wrist? Reveal explanation The left ventricle raises aortic pressure and generates a wave of arterial-wall distension. That pressure wave propagates along connected arteries much faster than the average blood cells themselves travel. What the examiner palpates is the pressure/distension wave . 2. Why does arterial stiffening with age tend to increase pulse-wave velocity? Reveal explanation Stiffer arteries deform less for a given pressure disturbance. A pressure wave therefore propagates more quickly through the blood–vessel system. This relates to wave transmission , not necessarily a rise in mean blood flow velocity. 3. Name the five arterial-pulse characteristics explicitly listed in the lecture. Reveal explanation Rate, volume, strength, character and location. Rate is beats per minute; volume and strength describe palpable size; character describes the shape of the impulse; and location identifies the artery examined. Rhythm and symmetry are useful added clinical descriptors. 4. Why can the neck show JVP waves even though venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium? Reveal explanation The right atrium changes pressure during contraction, relaxation and filling. Those pressure changes propagate through the connected venous column towards the neck. The direction of a pressure signal is not the same as the net direction of blood flow. 5. Which JVP wave is produced by right atrial contraction, and when does it occur? Reveal explanation The a wave is produced when the right atrium contracts near the end of ventricular diastole. It raises right atrial pressure before ventricular systole. Do not confuse it with the v wave, which develops while the atrium fills. 6. Why does the v wave occur while the tricuspid valve is closed? Reveal explanation Venous blood continues to enter the right atrium during ventricular systole, but the closed tricuspid valve prevents normal passage into the ventricle. The atrium therefore fills and its pressure rises, producing the v wave without atrial contraction. 7. What produces the x and y descents, respectively? Reveal explanation The x descent is caused mainly by right atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid annular movement during ventricular systole. The y descent follows tricuspid opening, when accumulated right atrial blood flows into the right ventricle in early diastole. 8. A visible neck pulsation is easily palpable and does not vary markedly with breathing or reclining angle. Is it more likely jugular or carotid? Reveal explanation It is more consistent with the carotid arterial pulse , which is palpable and has a dominant systolic impulse. JVP is typically non-palpable, more complex and influenced by respiration and patient position. 9. Why might JVP decrease on inspiration? Reveal explanation Inspiration normally makes intrathoracic pressure more negative, facilitating systemic venous return into the thorax and right atrium. The venous pressure column observed in the neck therefore commonly falls. The response may be altered in specific cardiac diseases. 10. In tricuspid regurgitation, why can the jugular v or c–v wave become unusually large? Reveal explanation During ventricular systole, blood leaks backward through the incompetent tricuspid valve into the right atrium. This adds to ordinary venous filling and creates a larger systolic rise in right atrial pressure , appearing as a prominent v or fused c–v wave. 11. How is a cannon a wave different from the large v wave of tricuspid regurgitation? Reveal explanation A cannon a wave occurs when the right atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can happen with AV dissociation. A large v/cv wave is predominantly associated with atrial filling and/or regurgitant flow during ventricular systole. They represent different mechanical causes. 12. Why should the examiner change the reclining angle instead of insisting that every JVP be measured at 45°? Reveal explanation The aim is to bring the top of the jugular venous column into view. A very high column may require the person to sit more upright; a low column may require a flatter position. Once visible, its vertical height relative to the sternal angle provides a clinical estimate of central venous pressure. Chapter Essentials Arterial pulse: A propagating pressure/distension wave created by ventricular ejection, not blood moving at wave speed. Arterial stiffness: Greater stiffness generally increases pulse-wave velocity. Lecture characteristics: Rate, volume, strength, character and location. JVP: Reflects transmitted right atrial pressure changes through the internal jugular venous column. Three positive JVP waves: a = atrial contraction; c = early ventricular systole; v = atrial filling while tricuspid closed. Two descents: x = atrial relaxation/downward tricuspid motion; y = atrial emptying following tricuspid opening. Jugular versus carotid: JVP is usually not palpable and changes with breathing and posture. JVP assessment: Observe the venous column and measure vertical height from the sternal angle; adjust patient angle to see it. Tricuspid regurgitation: May produce large systolic v/cv waves. Cannon a wave: Atrial contraction against a closed tricuspid valve, often with AV dissociation. Heart failure: High JVP can reflect elevated right atrial pressure and systemic venous congestion. The wave names reflect mechanisms: Understand what blood and valves are doing instead of simply memorising the letters."},{"slug":"venous-return","title":"Veins and Venous Return","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"43-44 and 73-75 and 97-98","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential","title":"1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?"},{"id":"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels","title":"2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?"},{"id":"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return","title":"3. What pressure actually drives venous return?"},{"id":"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work","title":"4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?"},{"id":"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return","title":"5. How does breathing help venous return?"},{"id":"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us","title":"6. What does central venous pressure tell us?"},{"id":"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing","title":"7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"veins-and-venous-return\">Veins and Venous Return</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 43–44, 73–75 and 97–98. <strong>Supplementary mechanism:</strong> pressure driving venous return, central venous pressure, and physiological effects of standing and respiration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-venous-return-and-why-is-it-essential\">1. What is venous return, and why is it essential?</h2>\n<p><strong>Venous return (VR)</strong> is the <strong>volume of blood returning to the right atrium per unit time</strong>. In a closed circulation at steady state, venous return must equal <strong>cardiac output (CO)</strong>: the heart cannot pump more blood out indefinitely than the circulation brings back.</p>\n<p>A stronger heart cannot maintain normal stroke volume when it has too little blood to fill it. This is the link to the Frank–Starling law in Chapter 07:</p>\n<div class=\"vr-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Venous return to stroke volume\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Right-heart filling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stroke volume ↑</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why doesn't increased return immediately increase the left ventricular output?</strong> Blood first passes through the right heart and lungs; effects on left ventricular filling follow after this transit. The two outputs become approximately equal over time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-are-veins-called-capacitance-vessels\">2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels?</h2>\n<p>Compared with thick-walled arteries, large veins generally have <strong>thin, compliant, distensible walls</strong>. They can hold a substantial amount of blood while pressure remains relatively low.</p>\n<p>The lecture contrasts a filling artery with a filling vein: the artery's pressure rises more sharply for a given added volume, while the vein can accommodate far more volume with a small initial pressure rise.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Vascular compliance</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise.</span></div>\n<p>The lecturer's slide 75 places roughly <strong>half of circulating blood in the systemic venous compartment</strong> in an illustrative resting distribution. The listed percentages are approximations and do not form an exact complete partition.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-does-venoconstriction-increase-available-central-blood\">Why does venoconstriction increase available central blood?</h3>\n<p>Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation contracts venous smooth muscle. Veins become less distensible and hold less blood at a given pressure. Blood may shift out of peripheral venous reservoirs towards the heart, increasing the pressure driving venous return.</p>\n<p><strong>This is not identical to arteriolar constriction.</strong> Arterioles chiefly adjust resistance to organ perfusion; venoconstriction chiefly influences <strong>venous capacitance and filling</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-pressure-actually-drives-venous-return\">3. What pressure actually drives venous return?</h2>\n<p>Venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium because its upstream filling pressure exceeds downstream right atrial pressure.</p>\n<p>A supplementary conceptual model expresses this relation as:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Venous-return relation</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>VR</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>msf</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>RA</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>VR</mi></msub></mfrac></math><span class=\"equation-example\">Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return.</span></div>\n<p><strong>Mean systemic filling pressure</strong> is a conceptual pressure determined by the amount of stressed blood volume and vascular properties; it is not simply the measured pressure in an ordinary peripheral vein while the heart is pumping.</p>\n<p>At unchanged upstream filling pressure and resistance, <strong>a higher right atrial pressure narrows the gradient and can reduce venous return</strong>. Conversely, increasing stressed blood volume or venous tone can increase upstream filling pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> This model is simplified; the great veins may partially collapse when intrathoracic pressure becomes sufficiently negative, so lowering right atrial pressure does not increase venous return without limit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-how-do-the-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-venous-valves-work\">4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work?</h2>\n<p>Veins in the limbs commonly contain <strong>one-way valves</strong>. When skeletal muscles contract around deep veins, the veins are compressed and blood is pushed onward, usually towards the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure vr-pump-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Skeletal-muscle pump and one-way venous valves</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 540 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a vertical leg vein, one-way valves and surrounding muscle compression moving venous blood upwards towards the heart.\"><rect x=\"230\" y=\"16\" width=\"82\" height=\"128\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#d7eafe\" stroke=\"#4381c1\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M230 55 Q271 65 273 79 Q273 65 312 55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M230 107 Q271 117 273 132 Q273 117 312 107\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#174f92\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M273 114V23 M264 35 L273 23 L282 35\" stroke=\"#1463bd\" fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><g stroke=\"#e38c38\" stroke-width=\"4\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"><path d=\"M172 80H222 M213 71 L222 80 L213 89\"/><path d=\"M371 80H318 M327 71 L318 80 L327 89\"/></g><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"163\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"163\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"64\">Muscle</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"104\">squeezes</text><text x=\"276\" y=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\">To heart ↑</text><text x=\"12\" y=\"149\">Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression</text></g></svg><p>Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released.</p></figure>\n<h3 id=\"why-do-soldiers-subtly-exercise-their-legs-while-standing\">Why do soldiers subtly exercise their legs while standing?</h3>\n<p>When a person stands motionless, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in leg veins and promotes <strong>venous pooling</strong>. Less blood is immediately available to return to the heart. The resulting fall in preload can reduce stroke volume and may produce light-headedness or fainting.</p>\n<p>Repeated <strong>calf contractions, toe raises or marching in place</strong> compress veins and improve return. This is the physiological reason that standing at attention can be more difficult than walking or subtly contracting the leg muscles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-how-does-breathing-help-venous-return\">5. How does breathing help venous return?</h2>\n<p>During normal spontaneous <strong>inspiration</strong>, intrathoracic pressure becomes more negative. The thoracic venous/atrial pressure may decrease, promoting return from extrathoracic veins to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<p>The <strong>diaphragm</strong> also changes abdominal pressure, helping generate a pressure gradient along the venae cavae. The effect varies with posture, volume status and the breathing pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Important qualification:</strong> Positive-pressure ventilation can change these dynamics; “inspiration always increases venous return” is too absolute. Also, right-heart filling may increase before a similar effect is transmitted to the left heart.</p>\n<p>The lecture also mentions arterial pulsation compressing adjacent veins, which can assist movement locally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-does-central-venous-pressure-tell-us\">6. What does central venous pressure tell us?</h2>\n<p><strong>Central venous pressure (CVP)</strong> usually refers to pressure near the right atrium or great thoracic veins. It varies through the cardiac cycle and with respiration, reflecting right-sided filling pressures.</p>\n<p>The lecturer gives low pressures near the right atrium, including approximately <strong>4.6 mmHg</strong> in its example, but a single absolute value is <strong>not a universal normal</strong>. Actual readings depend on the reference level, respiratory phase, volume status, venous return and right ventricular function.</p>\n<p><strong>Why can CVP rise when cardiac output falls?</strong> If the right ventricle cannot empty effectively, blood may accumulate upstream and right atrial pressure may increase even while forward flow decreases. CVP is therefore not a direct measurement of circulating blood volume or cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-happens-to-veins-in-the-head-when-standing\">7. What happens to veins in the head when standing?</h2>\n<p>Gravity changes venous pressure depending on height relative to the heart. Thin-walled extracranial veins may partially collapse when the surrounding pressure exceeds the pressure inside.</p>\n<p>The lecturer contrasts these with <strong>dural venous sinuses</strong>, whose walls are held open by dural attachments. If an open sinus is exposed while the internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, <strong>air may enter the venous circulation</strong>, potentially causing a venous air embolism.</p>\n<p>This is a specific clinical risk of exposed low-pressure noncollapsible veins, not evidence that blood normally flows backwards from the brain to the heart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output in steady state?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic and pulmonary circulations form a closed series circuit. Blood returning to the right heart must eventually be ejected through the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Persistent differences would cause progressive accumulation or depletion of blood in a compartment, so long-term <strong>VR = CO</strong>.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Why can't a very strong heart maintain normal output during major haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Haemorrhage reduces circulating volume and the pressure driving venous return. The ventricle receives less blood, so <strong>EDV and stroke volume fall</strong>. Increased intrinsic contractility cannot fully compensate for the absence of adequate filling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Explain the difference between venous compliance and arteriolar resistance.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Compliance</strong> is volume change per pressure change; veins can store large volumes at low pressure. <strong>Resistance</strong> determines the pressure drop required for a particular blood flow; arterioles change their radius to control organ perfusion. The two vascular properties serve different functions.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why can sympathetic venoconstriction increase venous return even if total blood volume has not changed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Venoconstriction reduces venous capacitance and mobilises blood stored in peripheral veins. This increases the stressed volume and may increase mean systemic filling pressure, strengthening the gradient towards the right atrium without adding new blood.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. What does the difference between mean systemic filling pressure and right atrial pressure represent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>It represents a conceptual <strong>pressure gradient driving systemic venous return</strong>. Venous return is greater at the same resistance when that gradient increases. Mean systemic filling pressure is not simply the pressure measured in a peripheral vein during ongoing circulation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Why may a rise in right atrial pressure reduce venous return if upstream filling pressure remains fixed?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The pressure difference moving blood towards the right atrium becomes smaller. From the simplified relation VR = (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR, a higher downstream right atrial pressure reduces the gradient. Severe rises may reflect right-heart pumping failure as well.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Why does subtle calf contraction help someone who is standing still for a long time?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Standing promotes gravitational pooling in leg veins, which can reduce venous return and EDV. Calf contractions compress the veins while one-way valves favour upward flow. This mobilises pooled blood towards the heart, improving preload and helping prevent faintness.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. How does inspiration ordinarily support right-heart filling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Spontaneous inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, while abdominal and thoracic pressure changes favour movement of blood towards the chest. This can increase systemic venous return to the right heart. Positive-pressure ventilation or unusual pressures may change the effect.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Does an elevated CVP always mean the patient has excess circulating blood volume?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> CVP measures pressure near the right atrium and also depends on ventricular function, intrathoracic pressure and venous tone. Right-heart dysfunction can elevate CVP through upstream congestion despite inadequate forward flow, so CVP is not simply a blood-volume gauge.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Why can the left ventricular stroke volume change after, rather than exactly when, venous return to the right atrium increases?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>New venous return first enters the right atrium and ventricle, then passes through the pulmonary circulation before reaching the left heart. The right-heart filling response therefore precedes the corresponding change in left ventricular EDV and ejection.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why can a dural venous sinus admit air when opened above heart level?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Pressure may become subatmospheric in veins above the heart. Ordinary thin-walled veins can collapse, but dural sinuses are held open by surrounding tissue. If such a sinus is cut while internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may be drawn into it, causing a dangerous venous air embolism.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Why might a person feel faint after suddenly standing, even with a normal heart?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Gravity temporarily shifts blood into dependent veins. Reduced immediate venous return lowers preload, stroke volume and sometimes arterial pressure until autonomic reflexes and muscle pumping compensate. This is a filling and circulation problem, not proof that the heart muscle is intrinsically weak.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Venous return:</strong> Blood reaching the right atrium per minute; it equals cardiac output at steady state.</li><li><strong>Veins:</strong> High-compliance capacitance reservoirs, unlike resistance-regulating arterioles.</li><li><strong>Venous tone:</strong> Sympathetic venoconstriction can mobilise stored blood and increase central filling.</li><li><strong>Pressure gradient:</strong> VR ≈ (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR in a simplified model.</li><li><strong>Mean systemic filling pressure:</strong> Conceptual upstream filling pressure, not an ordinary peripheral venous reading.</li><li><strong>Skeletal-muscle pump:</strong> Contracting leg muscles compress veins and valves limit backward flow.</li><li><strong>Standing:</strong> Gravity promotes venous pooling, reducing venous return and potentially stroke volume.</li><li><strong>Respiratory pump:</strong> Spontaneous inspiration usually helps right-heart venous return.</li><li><strong>CVP:</strong> Pressure close to the right atrium; influenced by right-heart function and intrathoracic pressure.</li><li><strong>CVP is not blood volume:</strong> High right atrial pressure can accompany poor forward cardiac output.</li><li><strong>Dural venous sinuses:</strong> Remain open despite subatmospheric pressure, creating an air-embolism risk if exposed.</li><li><strong>Filling and output:</strong> Strong contraction cannot fully compensate for insufficient ventricular filling.</li></ol>","plain":"Veins and Venous Return Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 43–44, 73–75 and 97–98. Supplementary mechanism: pressure driving venous return, central venous pressure, and physiological effects of standing and respiration. 1. What is venous return, and why is it essential? Venous return (VR) is the volume of blood returning to the right atrium per unit time . In a closed circulation at steady state, venous return must equal cardiac output (CO) : the heart cannot pump more blood out indefinitely than the circulation brings back. A stronger heart cannot maintain normal stroke volume when it has too little blood to fill it. This is the link to the Frank–Starling law in Chapter 07: Venous return ↑ → Right-heart filling ↑ → EDV ↑ → Stroke volume ↑ Why doesn't increased return immediately increase the left ventricular output? Blood first passes through the right heart and lungs; effects on left ventricular filling follow after this transit. The two outputs become approximately equal over time. 2. Why are veins called capacitance vessels? Compared with thick-walled arteries, large veins generally have thin, compliant, distensible walls . They can hold a substantial amount of blood while pressure remains relatively low. The lecture contrasts a filling artery with a filling vein: the artery's pressure rises more sharply for a given added volume, while the vein can accommodate far more volume with a small initial pressure rise. Vascular compliance C = Δ V Δ P The higher C of veins permits greater volume storage for a given pressure rise. The lecturer's slide 75 places roughly half of circulating blood in the systemic venous compartment in an illustrative resting distribution. The listed percentages are approximations and do not form an exact complete partition. Why does venoconstriction increase available central blood? Noradrenergic sympathetic stimulation contracts venous smooth muscle. Veins become less distensible and hold less blood at a given pressure. Blood may shift out of peripheral venous reservoirs towards the heart, increasing the pressure driving venous return. This is not identical to arteriolar constriction. Arterioles chiefly adjust resistance to organ perfusion; venoconstriction chiefly influences venous capacitance and filling . 3. What pressure actually drives venous return? Venous blood normally returns towards the right atrium because its upstream filling pressure exceeds downstream right atrial pressure. A supplementary conceptual model expresses this relation as: Venous-return relation VR = P msf − P RA R VR Pmsf = mean systemic filling pressure; PRA = right atrial pressure; RVR = resistance to venous return. Mean systemic filling pressure is a conceptual pressure determined by the amount of stressed blood volume and vascular properties; it is not simply the measured pressure in an ordinary peripheral vein while the heart is pumping. At unchanged upstream filling pressure and resistance, a higher right atrial pressure narrows the gradient and can reduce venous return . Conversely, increasing stressed blood volume or venous tone can increase upstream filling pressure. Limitation: This model is simplified; the great veins may partially collapse when intrathoracic pressure becomes sufficiently negative, so lowering right atrial pressure does not increase venous return without limit. 4. How do the skeletal-muscle pump and venous valves work? Veins in the limbs commonly contain one-way valves . When skeletal muscles contract around deep veins, the veins are compressed and blood is pushed onward, usually towards the heart. Figure 01. Skeletal-muscle pump and one-way venous valves Muscle squeezes Muscle squeezes To heart ↑ Valves favour one-way upward movement during compression Illustration of the principle, not a scale drawing. Valves limit backward flow when muscle pressure is released. Why do soldiers subtly exercise their legs while standing? When a person stands motionless, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in leg veins and promotes venous pooling . Less blood is immediately available to return to the heart. The resulting fall in preload can reduce stroke volume and may produce light-headedness or fainting. Repeated calf contractions, toe raises or marching in place compress veins and improve return. This is the physiological reason that standing at attention can be more difficult than walking or subtly contracting the leg muscles. 5. How does breathing help venous return? During normal spontaneous inspiration , intrathoracic pressure becomes more negative. The thoracic venous/atrial pressure may decrease, promoting return from extrathoracic veins to the right side of the heart. The diaphragm also changes abdominal pressure, helping generate a pressure gradient along the venae cavae. The effect varies with posture, volume status and the breathing pattern. Important qualification: Positive-pressure ventilation can change these dynamics; “inspiration always increases venous return” is too absolute. Also, right-heart filling may increase before a similar effect is transmitted to the left heart. The lecture also mentions arterial pulsation compressing adjacent veins, which can assist movement locally. 6. What does central venous pressure tell us? Central venous pressure (CVP) usually refers to pressure near the right atrium or great thoracic veins. It varies through the cardiac cycle and with respiration, reflecting right-sided filling pressures. The lecturer gives low pressures near the right atrium, including approximately 4.6 mmHg in its example, but a single absolute value is not a universal normal . Actual readings depend on the reference level, respiratory phase, volume status, venous return and right ventricular function. Why can CVP rise when cardiac output falls? If the right ventricle cannot empty effectively, blood may accumulate upstream and right atrial pressure may increase even while forward flow decreases. CVP is therefore not a direct measurement of circulating blood volume or cardiac output. 7. What happens to veins in the head when standing? Gravity changes venous pressure depending on height relative to the heart. Thin-walled extracranial veins may partially collapse when the surrounding pressure exceeds the pressure inside. The lecturer contrasts these with dural venous sinuses , whose walls are held open by dural attachments. If an open sinus is exposed while the internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may enter the venous circulation , potentially causing a venous air embolism. This is a specific clinical risk of exposed low-pressure noncollapsible veins, not evidence that blood normally flows backwards from the brain to the heart. Understanding Check 1. Why must venous return equal cardiac output in steady state? Reveal explanation The systemic and pulmonary circulations form a closed series circuit. Blood returning to the right heart must eventually be ejected through the pulmonary and systemic circuits. Persistent differences would cause progressive accumulation or depletion of blood in a compartment, so long-term VR = CO . 2. Why can't a very strong heart maintain normal output during major haemorrhage? Reveal explanation Haemorrhage reduces circulating volume and the pressure driving venous return. The ventricle receives less blood, so EDV and stroke volume fall . Increased intrinsic contractility cannot fully compensate for the absence of adequate filling. 3. Explain the difference between venous compliance and arteriolar resistance. Reveal explanation Compliance is volume change per pressure change; veins can store large volumes at low pressure. Resistance determines the pressure drop required for a particular blood flow; arterioles change their radius to control organ perfusion. The two vascular properties serve different functions. 4. Why can sympathetic venoconstriction increase venous return even if total blood volume has not changed? Reveal explanation Venoconstriction reduces venous capacitance and mobilises blood stored in peripheral veins. This increases the stressed volume and may increase mean systemic filling pressure, strengthening the gradient towards the right atrium without adding new blood. 5. What does the difference between mean systemic filling pressure and right atrial pressure represent? Reveal explanation It represents a conceptual pressure gradient driving systemic venous return . Venous return is greater at the same resistance when that gradient increases. Mean systemic filling pressure is not simply the pressure measured in a peripheral vein during ongoing circulation. 6. Why may a rise in right atrial pressure reduce venous return if upstream filling pressure remains fixed? Reveal explanation The pressure difference moving blood towards the right atrium becomes smaller. From the simplified relation VR = (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR, a higher downstream right atrial pressure reduces the gradient. Severe rises may reflect right-heart pumping failure as well. 7. Why does subtle calf contraction help someone who is standing still for a long time? Reveal explanation Standing promotes gravitational pooling in leg veins, which can reduce venous return and EDV. Calf contractions compress the veins while one-way valves favour upward flow. This mobilises pooled blood towards the heart, improving preload and helping prevent faintness. 8. How does inspiration ordinarily support right-heart filling? Reveal explanation Spontaneous inspiration lowers intrathoracic pressure, while abdominal and thoracic pressure changes favour movement of blood towards the chest. This can increase systemic venous return to the right heart. Positive-pressure ventilation or unusual pressures may change the effect. 9. Does an elevated CVP always mean the patient has excess circulating blood volume? Reveal explanation No. CVP measures pressure near the right atrium and also depends on ventricular function, intrathoracic pressure and venous tone. Right-heart dysfunction can elevate CVP through upstream congestion despite inadequate forward flow, so CVP is not simply a blood-volume gauge. 10. Why can the left ventricular stroke volume change after, rather than exactly when, venous return to the right atrium increases? Reveal explanation New venous return first enters the right atrium and ventricle, then passes through the pulmonary circulation before reaching the left heart. The right-heart filling response therefore precedes the corresponding change in left ventricular EDV and ejection. 11. Why can a dural venous sinus admit air when opened above heart level? Reveal explanation Pressure may become subatmospheric in veins above the heart. Ordinary thin-walled veins can collapse, but dural sinuses are held open by surrounding tissue. If such a sinus is cut while internal pressure is below atmospheric pressure, air may be drawn into it, causing a dangerous venous air embolism. 12. Why might a person feel faint after suddenly standing, even with a normal heart? Reveal explanation Gravity temporarily shifts blood into dependent veins. Reduced immediate venous return lowers preload, stroke volume and sometimes arterial pressure until autonomic reflexes and muscle pumping compensate. This is a filling and circulation problem, not proof that the heart muscle is intrinsically weak. Chapter Essentials Venous return: Blood reaching the right atrium per minute; it equals cardiac output at steady state. Veins: High-compliance capacitance reservoirs, unlike resistance-regulating arterioles. Venous tone: Sympathetic venoconstriction can mobilise stored blood and increase central filling. Pressure gradient: VR ≈ (Pmsf − PRA)/RVR in a simplified model. Mean systemic filling pressure: Conceptual upstream filling pressure, not an ordinary peripheral venous reading. Skeletal-muscle pump: Contracting leg muscles compress veins and valves limit backward flow. Standing: Gravity promotes venous pooling, reducing venous return and potentially stroke volume. Respiratory pump: Spontaneous inspiration usually helps right-heart venous return. CVP: Pressure close to the right atrium; influenced by right-heart function and intrathoracic pressure. CVP is not blood volume: High right atrial pressure can accompany poor forward cardiac output. Dural venous sinuses: Remain open despite subatmospheric pressure, creating an air-embolism risk if exposed. Filling and output: Strong contraction cannot fully compensate for insufficient ventricular filling."},{"slug":"microcirculation","title":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"57-60 and 84-89","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary","title":"1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?"},{"id":"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of","title":"2. What is a capillary wall made of?"},{"id":"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall","title":"3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?"},{"id":"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood","title":"4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?"},{"id":"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood","title":"5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?"},{"id":"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding","title":"6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding"},{"id":"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability","title":"7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"microcirculation-and-capillary-exchange\">Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 57–60 and 84–89. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> capillary structural types and the revised glycocalyx-based Starling principle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-is-the-microcirculation-necessary\">1. Why is the microcirculation necessary?</h2>\n<p>The heart delivers blood to organs, but <strong>cells do not receive oxygen directly from the aorta</strong>. Oxygen and nutrients must cross the tiny exchange vessels close to each tissue cell.</p>\n<p>The <strong>microcirculation</strong> includes small arterioles, metarterioles or transitional vessels where present, capillaries and venules. Its tasks are to distribute blood locally, exchange substances and regulate interstitial fluid.</p>\n<div class=\"micro-path\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Microcirculatory sequence\"><span>Small arteriole</span><b>→</b><span>Capillary exchange</span><b>→</b><span>Venule</span><b>→</b><span>Venous return</span></div>\n<p><strong>Why are capillaries suitable?</strong> Their thin walls and the very large combined capillary surface area bring flowing blood close to cells. Blood moves relatively slowly because the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is large.</p>\n<p>The lecturer notes that capillaries contain only a <strong>small fraction of total blood volume, around 5% in its example</strong>, despite their importance for tissue exchange.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-is-a-capillary-wall-made-of\">2. What is a capillary wall made of?</h2>\n<p>A typical capillary is lined by <strong>one layer of endothelial cells</strong> supported by specialised extracellular matrix called the <strong>basal lamina (part of the basement membrane)</strong>. Pericytes may lie externally and help support microvascular stability.</p>\n<p><strong>Important histology distinction:</strong> A capillary does <strong>not</strong> possess a typical three-coat wall with a substantial smooth-muscle tunica media, unlike an artery. A capillary also does <strong>not</strong> need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane.</p>\n<h3 id=\"three-principal-capillary-patterns\">Three principal capillary patterns</h3>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Structure</th><th>Common examples</th><th>Exchange implication</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Continuous</strong></td><td>Relatively uninterrupted endothelial lining</td><td>Muscle, skin, CNS</td><td>Controlled passage; CNS barrier especially restrictive</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fenestrated</strong></td><td>Endothelial pores/fenestrae</td><td>Intestinal villi, endocrine organs, renal glomeruli</td><td>Rapid exchange of water and small solutes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Discontinuous / sinusoidal</strong></td><td>Wider gaps and discontinuities</td><td>Liver, spleen, bone marrow</td><td>Exchange of larger materials, specialised by tissue</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>A note on the lecturer's units:</strong> Slide 58 prints measurements for gaps/fenestrations in <strong>“mm”</strong>. These openings are microscopic, often measured in <strong>nanometres</strong>, not millimetres. Treat the printed units as erroneous, while keeping the structural distinction the slide intended.</p>\n<p><strong>Why do fenestrations help?</strong> A porous endothelium provides lower resistance to selected water-soluble materials crossing the vessel. But renal filtration still depends on additional specialised layers, including the glomerular basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits; pores alone do not determine the whole barrier.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-do-substances-actually-cross-the-capillary-wall\">3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall?</h2>\n<p>The lecture names <strong>diffusion, filtration, fenestrations and vesicular transport</strong>. These are not all the same kind of process.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What moves?</th><th>Main driving factor</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Diffusion</strong></td><td>O₂, CO₂ and many dissolved solutes</td><td>Concentration or partial-pressure differences</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Intercellular routes/fenestrae</strong></td><td>Water and selected small hydrophilic substances</td><td>Barrier structure plus relevant gradients</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Vesicular transport</strong></td><td>Selected macromolecular cargo</td><td>Active endothelial transport mechanisms</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bulk filtration</strong></td><td>Water and associated dissolved materials</td><td>Hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"why-can-oxygen-cross-efficiently\">Why can oxygen cross efficiently?</h3>\n<p>Oxygen diffuses down its <strong>partial-pressure gradient</strong> from capillary blood into metabolically active tissue. Carbon dioxide tends to diffuse in the opposite direction because cells produce it.</p>\n<p>The short diffusion distance and extensive capillary surface area enable exchange. <strong>Diffusion is not the same as fluid being forcibly filtered across the wall.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"4-what-decides-which-capillaries-receive-more-blood\">4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood?</h2>\n<p>Tissues require more delivery when they become active. Local metabolic changes can dilate resistance vessels and increase the number of well-perfused microvascular pathways.</p>\n<p>The lecturer describes <strong>metarterioles and precapillary sphincters</strong>. Such structures are useful classical teaching concepts but vary across vascular beds; not every capillary has an identical anatomical sphincter.</p>\n<p><strong>Applied example:</strong> During exercise, active skeletal muscle produces metabolites that promote local dilation. More blood is delivered into exchange networks where oxygen demand is high.</p>\n<p>The lecturer notes that capillary pressure may be estimated from the external force required to occlude a capillary. It is a <strong>method-dependent estimate</strong>, not a direct measure of all capillary pressures everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-does-fluid-leave-capillaries-even-when-protein-stays-in-blood\">5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood?</h2>\n<p>Two families of pressure forces influence movement of water across a microvascular wall:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Hydrostatic pressure:</strong> Fluid pressure; inside a capillary it usually tends to push water outward.</li><li><strong>Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure:</strong> Due mainly to proteins; plasma proteins generally oppose excessive outward water movement by attracting or retaining water.</li></ul>\n<p>The classic balance uses four named quantities:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Location</th><th>Typical direction in simple model</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pc</strong></td><td>Capillary hydrostatic pressure</td><td>Promotes outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pi</strong></td><td>Interstitial hydrostatic pressure</td><td>May oppose or favour filtration, depending on sign</td></tr><tr><td><strong>πc</strong></td><td>Plasma capillary oncotic pressure</td><td>Opposes outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>πi</strong></td><td>Interstitial oncotic pressure</td><td>Favours outward filtration</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure micro-force-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 570 168\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original schematic capillary containing plasma proteins, with an outward hydrostatic arrow from blood to tissue and an opposing inward plasma oncotic arrow.\"><rect x=\"40\" y=\"51\" width=\"490\" height=\"76\" rx=\"30\" fill=\"#f5d8df\" stroke=\"#b66b80\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ae536c\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"176\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"327\" cy=\"88\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"458\" cy=\"89\" r=\"9\"/></g><path d=\"M239 77 V14 M230 27 L239 14 L248 27\" stroke=\"#d27c26\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M389 14 V75 M380 62 L389 75 L398 62\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\"><text x=\"166\" y=\"13\">Pc: outward</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"13\">Plasma oncotic: inward</text><text x=\"167\" y=\"96\">Capillary blood</text><text x=\"181\" y=\"151\">Interstitial tissue space</text></g></svg><p>A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx.</p></figure>\n<p><strong>Why does plasma albumin matter?</strong> Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. Low albumin weakens the oncotic opposition to filtration, increasing oedema risk if lymphatic drainage cannot keep up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-classic-starling-model-versus-revised-understanding\">6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's slides 85–88 teach hydrostatic and oncotic gradients and give an example of <strong>24 litres filtered per day, 85% reabsorbed into capillaries and 15% returned by lymph</strong>. This is the <strong>classic textbook model</strong> and explains the intended arithmetic.</p>\n<p><strong>Modern physiological clarification:</strong> The <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> is a protein-rich surface layer that affects effective filtration and protein gradients immediately beneath it. In many continuous capillary beds, a low level of <strong>net filtration persists during steady state</strong>, including towards the venous end. Much of the filtered fluid is returned through <strong>lymphatics</strong>, rather than assuming a universal large sustained venous-end reabsorption.</p>\n<p><strong>What should a student learn?</strong> Understand the lecturer's four pressure forces and numerical illustration, but also know that the 85%/15% split <strong>is not a universal modern physiological rule</strong>. Transient absorption after sudden pressure changes and some specialised tissues are exceptions.</p>\n<p>This distinction prevents a major misconception: the lymphatic system is not merely an optional back-up after routine large venous reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-microvascular-filtration-or-permeability\">7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability?</h2>\n<p>Local vessel conditions alter exchange:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Arteriolar dilation:</strong> May increase blood delivery and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging filtration under some conditions.</li><li><strong>Venous congestion:</strong> Increases downstream and often capillary hydrostatic pressure, promoting filtration.</li><li><strong>Increased endothelial permeability:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and substance P are examples named by the lecturer; inflammatory conditions can increase fluid/protein escape.</li><li><strong>Increased surface area:</strong> Perfusion of more capillaries can increase total exchange capacity.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Clinical link:</strong> Inflammation causes microvascular endothelial changes, enabling more protein and fluid to escape into tissues. Venous obstruction may raise fluid filtration without requiring an initially leaky capillary barrier. These are different causes of swelling.</p>\n<p>The next two chapters distinguish <strong>excessive tissue-fluid accumulation (oedema)</strong> from <strong>inadequate lymphatic clearance</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can capillaries exchange substances efficiently even though their walls are so thin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Their thin endothelial lining and short diffusion distance bring blood close to tissue fluid. Numerous capillaries provide a large total surface area, while relatively low mean velocity creates time for exchange. Thinness is therefore an <strong>advantage for transport</strong>, not evidence that capillaries are ineffective vessels.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. Do capillaries need a thick connective-tissue subendothelial layer to have a basement membrane?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. It is not the same thing as a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer, and a typical capillary lacks the muscular middle coat of an artery.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why are fenestrated capillaries useful in endocrine organs and the intestinal villi?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Endothelial fenestrations permit relatively rapid water and small-solute exchange, suited to tissues with high exchange requirements. Their barrier properties still depend on other structural features and the tissue involved.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why is the lecture's “mm” unit for endothelial fenestrations unsuitable?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Fenestrations are tiny pores in microscopic endothelial cells, commonly described on the <strong>nanometre scale</strong>. A pore several millimetres wide would dwarf the cell and vessel. The lecture intends to describe small permeable openings, not literal millimetre-sized gaps.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. What drives oxygen diffusion from capillaries into active tissues?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Tissue cells consume oxygen, lowering local tissue oxygen partial pressure. Oxygen then diffuses from the relatively higher partial pressure in capillary blood towards lower tissue partial pressure. This is <strong>diffusion down a partial-pressure gradient</strong>, not bulk filtration of the entire blood volume.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. Distinguish diffusion from filtration in one physiological example each.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Diffusion</strong> transfers individual molecules such as O₂ down a concentration/partial-pressure difference. <strong>Filtration</strong> moves bulk water across the microvascular barrier because of hydrostatic and oncotic forces. Solutes may accompany filtered fluid, but the driving principles differ.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. If capillary hydrostatic pressure increases with other factors unchanged, what happens to filtration?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Outward filtration generally increases.</strong> Hydrostatic pressure within the capillary pushes fluid towards the interstitium. Extra filtered fluid must be removed by lymphatic drainage or it may accumulate as oedema.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Why does a severe fall in plasma albumin promote oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Albumin contributes importantly to plasma colloid osmotic pressure. When it falls, the oncotic opposition to outward capillary filtration weakens. If net filtration exceeds lymphatic return, interstitial fluid accumulates.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Does every capillary have an identical precapillary sphincter controlling whether it opens?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Precapillary sphincters appear in classic microvascular models and some anatomical beds, but the degree and arrangement of local smooth-muscle control vary between tissues. Arteriolar dilation and local endothelial/metabolic mechanisms can change perfusion without a universal sphincter at every capillary.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Why does the revised Starling model place greater importance on lymphatic drainage than the classic 85% reabsorption diagram?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The endothelial glycocalyx modifies the effective oncotic gradient. In many tissues, slight net filtration persists even near venular ends at steady state, rather than sustained reabsorption of most filtered fluid. The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> returns this filtered fluid and proteins to blood.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. How may histamine increase tissue swelling without an initial increase in systemic arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Histamine can increase microvascular permeability, allowing more fluid and plasma proteins to cross into tissue. These local barrier changes can increase filtration and interstitial protein effects even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Why can capillary recruitment improve exchange in active muscle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>When resistance vessels dilate and more exchange paths are adequately perfused, effective capillary surface area and blood delivery increase. This brings oxygenated blood close to more active fibres and facilitates diffusion in response to tissue demand.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Microcirculation:</strong> Small arterioles, capillaries and venules coordinate local flow and tissue exchange.</li><li><strong>Capillary structure:</strong> Thin endothelium, basal lamina and possible pericytes; no typical muscular tunica media.</li><li><strong>Types:</strong> Continuous, fenestrated and sinusoidal/discontinuous vessels differ in permeability.</li><li><strong>Transport:</strong> Diffusion, fenestrae/paracellular routes, vesicular movement and bulk filtration are distinct.</li><li><strong>Diffusion:</strong> O₂ and CO₂ move mainly along partial-pressure gradients.</li><li><strong>Hydrostatic Pc:</strong> Usually promotes outward filtration; <strong>plasma oncotic πc</strong> usually opposes it.</li><li><strong>Other forces:</strong> Interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures influence net fluid movement.</li><li><strong>Classic lecture model:</strong> 24 L/day filtered, 85% reabsorbed, 15% by lymph — a historical illustrative model, not a universal modern rule.</li><li><strong>Revised Starling concept:</strong> Endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients modify filtration, often leaving steady-state net filtration.</li><li><strong>Lymphatic removal:</strong> Returns excess interstitial fluid and proteins to circulating blood.</li><li><strong>Capillary perfusion:</strong> Arteriolar tone, local activity and vascular geometry govern exchange-path access.</li><li><strong>Permeability mediators:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and substance P can increase endothelial leakage and swelling.</li></ol>","plain":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 57–60 and 84–89. Supplementary clarification: capillary structural types and the revised glycocalyx-based Starling principle. 1. Why is the microcirculation necessary? The heart delivers blood to organs, but cells do not receive oxygen directly from the aorta . Oxygen and nutrients must cross the tiny exchange vessels close to each tissue cell. The microcirculation includes small arterioles, metarterioles or transitional vessels where present, capillaries and venules. Its tasks are to distribute blood locally, exchange substances and regulate interstitial fluid. Small arteriole → Capillary exchange → Venule → Venous return Why are capillaries suitable? Their thin walls and the very large combined capillary surface area bring flowing blood close to cells. Blood moves relatively slowly because the total cross-sectional area of parallel capillaries is large. The lecturer notes that capillaries contain only a small fraction of total blood volume, around 5% in its example , despite their importance for tissue exchange. 2. What is a capillary wall made of? A typical capillary is lined by one layer of endothelial cells supported by specialised extracellular matrix called the basal lamina (part of the basement membrane) . Pericytes may lie externally and help support microvascular stability. Important histology distinction: A capillary does not possess a typical three-coat wall with a substantial smooth-muscle tunica media, unlike an artery. A capillary also does not need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane. Three principal capillary patterns Type Structure Common examples Exchange implication Continuous Relatively uninterrupted endothelial lining Muscle, skin, CNS Controlled passage; CNS barrier especially restrictive Fenestrated Endothelial pores/fenestrae Intestinal villi, endocrine organs, renal glomeruli Rapid exchange of water and small solutes Discontinuous / sinusoidal Wider gaps and discontinuities Liver, spleen, bone marrow Exchange of larger materials, specialised by tissue A note on the lecturer's units: Slide 58 prints measurements for gaps/fenestrations in “mm” . These openings are microscopic, often measured in nanometres , not millimetres. Treat the printed units as erroneous, while keeping the structural distinction the slide intended. Why do fenestrations help? A porous endothelium provides lower resistance to selected water-soluble materials crossing the vessel. But renal filtration still depends on additional specialised layers, including the glomerular basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits; pores alone do not determine the whole barrier. 3. How do substances actually cross the capillary wall? The lecture names diffusion, filtration, fenestrations and vesicular transport . These are not all the same kind of process. Mechanism What moves? Main driving factor Diffusion O₂, CO₂ and many dissolved solutes Concentration or partial-pressure differences Intercellular routes/fenestrae Water and selected small hydrophilic substances Barrier structure plus relevant gradients Vesicular transport Selected macromolecular cargo Active endothelial transport mechanisms Bulk filtration Water and associated dissolved materials Hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences Why can oxygen cross efficiently? Oxygen diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from capillary blood into metabolically active tissue. Carbon dioxide tends to diffuse in the opposite direction because cells produce it. The short diffusion distance and extensive capillary surface area enable exchange. Diffusion is not the same as fluid being forcibly filtered across the wall. 4. What decides which capillaries receive more blood? Tissues require more delivery when they become active. Local metabolic changes can dilate resistance vessels and increase the number of well-perfused microvascular pathways. The lecturer describes metarterioles and precapillary sphincters . Such structures are useful classical teaching concepts but vary across vascular beds; not every capillary has an identical anatomical sphincter. Applied example: During exercise, active skeletal muscle produces metabolites that promote local dilation. More blood is delivered into exchange networks where oxygen demand is high. The lecturer notes that capillary pressure may be estimated from the external force required to occlude a capillary. It is a method-dependent estimate , not a direct measure of all capillary pressures everywhere. 5. Why does fluid leave capillaries even when protein stays in blood? Two families of pressure forces influence movement of water across a microvascular wall: Hydrostatic pressure: Fluid pressure; inside a capillary it usually tends to push water outward. Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure: Due mainly to proteins; plasma proteins generally oppose excessive outward water movement by attracting or retaining water. The classic balance uses four named quantities: Quantity Location Typical direction in simple model Pc Capillary hydrostatic pressure Promotes outward filtration Pi Interstitial hydrostatic pressure May oppose or favour filtration, depending on sign πc Plasma capillary oncotic pressure Opposes outward filtration πi Interstitial oncotic pressure Favours outward filtration Figure 01. Hydrostatic and oncotic influences on capillary filtration Pc: outward Plasma oncotic: inward Capillary blood Interstitial tissue space A conceptual comparison of two important forces. Actual net filtration also depends on interstitial forces, surface area, permeability and the endothelial glycocalyx. Why does plasma albumin matter? Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. Low albumin weakens the oncotic opposition to filtration, increasing oedema risk if lymphatic drainage cannot keep up. 6. Classic Starling model versus revised understanding The lecturer's slides 85–88 teach hydrostatic and oncotic gradients and give an example of 24 litres filtered per day, 85% reabsorbed into capillaries and 15% returned by lymph . This is the classic textbook model and explains the intended arithmetic. Modern physiological clarification: The endothelial glycocalyx is a protein-rich surface layer that affects effective filtration and protein gradients immediately beneath it. In many continuous capillary beds, a low level of net filtration persists during steady state , including towards the venous end. Much of the filtered fluid is returned through lymphatics , rather than assuming a universal large sustained venous-end reabsorption. What should a student learn? Understand the lecturer's four pressure forces and numerical illustration, but also know that the 85%/15% split is not a universal modern physiological rule . Transient absorption after sudden pressure changes and some specialised tissues are exceptions. This distinction prevents a major misconception: the lymphatic system is not merely an optional back-up after routine large venous reabsorption. 7. What changes microvascular filtration or permeability? Local vessel conditions alter exchange: Arteriolar dilation: May increase blood delivery and capillary hydrostatic pressure, encouraging filtration under some conditions. Venous congestion: Increases downstream and often capillary hydrostatic pressure, promoting filtration. Increased endothelial permeability: Histamine, bradykinin and substance P are examples named by the lecturer; inflammatory conditions can increase fluid/protein escape. Increased surface area: Perfusion of more capillaries can increase total exchange capacity. Clinical link: Inflammation causes microvascular endothelial changes, enabling more protein and fluid to escape into tissues. Venous obstruction may raise fluid filtration without requiring an initially leaky capillary barrier. These are different causes of swelling. The next two chapters distinguish excessive tissue-fluid accumulation (oedema) from inadequate lymphatic clearance . Understanding Check 1. Why can capillaries exchange substances efficiently even though their walls are so thin? Reveal explanation Their thin endothelial lining and short diffusion distance bring blood close to tissue fluid. Numerous capillaries provide a large total surface area, while relatively low mean velocity creates time for exchange. Thinness is therefore an advantage for transport , not evidence that capillaries are ineffective vessels. 2. Do capillaries need a thick connective-tissue subendothelial layer to have a basement membrane? Reveal explanation No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. It is not the same thing as a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer, and a typical capillary lacks the muscular middle coat of an artery. 3. Why are fenestrated capillaries useful in endocrine organs and the intestinal villi? Reveal explanation Endothelial fenestrations permit relatively rapid water and small-solute exchange, suited to tissues with high exchange requirements. Their barrier properties still depend on other structural features and the tissue involved. 4. Why is the lecture's “mm” unit for endothelial fenestrations unsuitable? Reveal explanation Fenestrations are tiny pores in microscopic endothelial cells, commonly described on the nanometre scale . A pore several millimetres wide would dwarf the cell and vessel. The lecture intends to describe small permeable openings, not literal millimetre-sized gaps. 5. What drives oxygen diffusion from capillaries into active tissues? Reveal explanation Tissue cells consume oxygen, lowering local tissue oxygen partial pressure. Oxygen then diffuses from the relatively higher partial pressure in capillary blood towards lower tissue partial pressure. This is diffusion down a partial-pressure gradient , not bulk filtration of the entire blood volume. 6. Distinguish diffusion from filtration in one physiological example each. Reveal explanation Diffusion transfers individual molecules such as O₂ down a concentration/partial-pressure difference. Filtration moves bulk water across the microvascular barrier because of hydrostatic and oncotic forces. Solutes may accompany filtered fluid, but the driving principles differ. 7. If capillary hydrostatic pressure increases with other factors unchanged, what happens to filtration? Reveal explanation Outward filtration generally increases. Hydrostatic pressure within the capillary pushes fluid towards the interstitium. Extra filtered fluid must be removed by lymphatic drainage or it may accumulate as oedema. 8. Why does a severe fall in plasma albumin promote oedema? Reveal explanation Albumin contributes importantly to plasma colloid osmotic pressure. When it falls, the oncotic opposition to outward capillary filtration weakens. If net filtration exceeds lymphatic return, interstitial fluid accumulates. 9. Does every capillary have an identical precapillary sphincter controlling whether it opens? Reveal explanation No. Precapillary sphincters appear in classic microvascular models and some anatomical beds, but the degree and arrangement of local smooth-muscle control vary between tissues. Arteriolar dilation and local endothelial/metabolic mechanisms can change perfusion without a universal sphincter at every capillary. 10. Why does the revised Starling model place greater importance on lymphatic drainage than the classic 85% reabsorption diagram? Reveal explanation The endothelial glycocalyx modifies the effective oncotic gradient. In many tissues, slight net filtration persists even near venular ends at steady state, rather than sustained reabsorption of most filtered fluid. The lymphatic system returns this filtered fluid and proteins to blood. 11. How may histamine increase tissue swelling without an initial increase in systemic arterial pressure? Reveal explanation Histamine can increase microvascular permeability, allowing more fluid and plasma proteins to cross into tissue. These local barrier changes can increase filtration and interstitial protein effects even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen. 12. Why can capillary recruitment improve exchange in active muscle? Reveal explanation When resistance vessels dilate and more exchange paths are adequately perfused, effective capillary surface area and blood delivery increase. This brings oxygenated blood close to more active fibres and facilitates diffusion in response to tissue demand. Chapter Essentials Microcirculation: Small arterioles, capillaries and venules coordinate local flow and tissue exchange. Capillary structure: Thin endothelium, basal lamina and possible pericytes; no typical muscular tunica media. Types: Continuous, fenestrated and sinusoidal/discontinuous vessels differ in permeability. Transport: Diffusion, fenestrae/paracellular routes, vesicular movement and bulk filtration are distinct. Diffusion: O₂ and CO₂ move mainly along partial-pressure gradients. Hydrostatic Pc: Usually promotes outward filtration; plasma oncotic πc usually opposes it. Other forces: Interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures influence net fluid movement. Classic lecture model: 24 L/day filtered, 85% reabsorbed, 15% by lymph — a historical illustrative model, not a universal modern rule. Revised Starling concept: Endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients modify filtration, often leaving steady-state net filtration. Lymphatic removal: Returns excess interstitial fluid and proteins to circulating blood. Capillary perfusion: Arteriolar tone, local activity and vascular geometry govern exchange-path access. Permeability mediators: Histamine, bradykinin and substance P can increase endothelial leakage and swelling."},{"slug":"edema","title":"Formation of Oedema","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"89-95","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-exactly-is-oedema","title":"1. What exactly is oedema?"},{"id":"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration","title":"2. What physical forces govern filtration?"},{"id":"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid","title":"3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid"},{"id":"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema","title":"4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?"},{"id":"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema","title":"5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?"},{"id":"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema","title":"6. How does inflammation increase oedema?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?"},{"id":"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different","title":"8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?"},{"id":"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism","title":"9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"formation-of-oedema\">Formation of Oedema</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 89–95. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> revised Starling physiology, endothelial glycocalyx, mechanisms and clinical interpretation of pitting versus non-pitting swelling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-exactly-is-oedema\">1. What exactly is oedema?</h2>\n<p><strong>Oedema</strong> is the <strong>abnormal accumulation of excess fluid in tissue interstitial spaces</strong>. It is not simply the presence of fluid outside blood vessels: cells normally live surrounded by a regulated volume of interstitial fluid.</p>\n<p>The important balance is between:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Microvascular filtration:</strong> Fluid leaving blood vessels into tissue.</li><li><strong>Lymphatic drainage:</strong> Fluid and proteins being returned from tissues to the venous circulation.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"edema-balance\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Fluid balance causes oedema\"><span><strong>Filtration ↑</strong><small>More fluid entering tissue</small></span><b>+</b><span><strong>Drainage insufficient</strong><small>Less fluid leaving tissue</small></span><b>→</b><span><strong>Oedema</strong><small>Fluid accumulates</small></span></div>\n<p>When filtration exceeds the removal capacity of lymphatic vessels, interstitial fluid accumulates. Oedema may therefore occur even if the heart is still beating and blood is continuing to circulate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-physical-forces-govern-filtration\">2. What physical forces govern filtration?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer identifies <strong>capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, filtration coefficient, pre-/postcapillary resistance and capillary surface area</strong>.</p>\n<p>A conventional form of the Starling equation summarises these:</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Traditional Starling filtration model</span><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>[</mo><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mo>]</mo></math></div>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Jv</strong></td><td>Net fluid flux</td><td>Positive value represents net outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Kf</strong></td><td>Filtration coefficient</td><td>Reflects permeability and filtering surface area</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pc</strong></td><td>Capillary hydrostatic pressure</td><td>Drives fluid out of capillaries</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pi</strong></td><td>Interstitial hydrostatic pressure</td><td>Can oppose outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>πc</strong></td><td>Capillary plasma oncotic pressure</td><td>Opposes outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>πi</strong></td><td>Interstitial oncotic pressure</td><td>Can promote outward filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>σ</strong></td><td>Protein reflection coefficient</td><td>How effectively the barrier restricts protein passage</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Why is albumin important?</strong> Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. If plasma protein concentration falls, its normal opposition to fluid loss becomes weaker.</p>\n<h3 id=\"important-refinement-endothelial-glycocalyx\">Important refinement: endothelial glycocalyx</h3>\n<p>The lecturer's classic model assumes a simple balance between capillary and bulk interstitial proteins. Modern research shows the <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> affects the relevant oncotic gradient immediately beneath the endothelial surface.</p>\n<p>In many tissues, steady-state filtration persists along much of the capillary bed; a large universal reabsorption at the venous end is <strong>not</strong> expected. Lymphatics provide the main ongoing return path for filtered fluid.</p>\n<p>The lecture's <strong>24 L/day, 85% capillary reabsorption and 15% lymphatic return</strong> is a <strong>historical illustrative model</strong>, not an exact modern description for all tissues. Some transient reabsorption and specialised tissue exceptions remain possible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-five-routes-to-the-same-outcome-excess-interstitial-fluid\">3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Physiological change</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Higher Pc</strong></td><td>More hydrostatic pressure pushes water outward</td><td>Venous obstruction, heart failure</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Lower πc</strong></td><td>Weaker plasma oncotic opposition to water loss</td><td>Liver disease, renal protein loss, malnutrition</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Higher permeability/Kf</strong></td><td>Vessel barrier allows more fluid and protein escape</td><td>Inflammation, burns, allergy</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Impaired lymph drainage</strong></td><td>Filtered fluid and proteins cannot return adequately</td><td>Filariasis, lymph node removal</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Salt and water retention</strong></td><td>More ECF volume and often higher microvascular pressure</td><td>Renal dysfunction</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>Key reasoning:</strong> Different diseases can all create swelling, but through <strong>different failed parts of the fluid-balance system</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-does-raised-venous-pressure-cause-oedema\">4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer lists increased venous pressure, venous obstruction, venous valve incompetence, prolonged standing and heart failure.</p>\n<p>If pressure at the venous end of a capillary bed rises, the pressure gradient across its resistance vessels changes, often raising <strong>capillary hydrostatic pressure Pc</strong>. More fluid is pushed into the interstitium.</p>\n<p><strong>Example — standing still:</strong> Gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent leg veins. Without the calf-muscle pump, venous pooling persists and capillary filtration rises. Ankles can swell even though the heart's intrinsic muscle is normal.</p>\n<p><strong>Example — cardiac failure:</strong> Elevated systemic venous pressure may produce dependent peripheral oedema; elevated pulmonary venous pressure may produce pulmonary oedema, impairing lung gas exchange.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-can-arteriolar-dilation-or-venular-constriction-affect-filtration\">Why can arteriolar dilation or venular constriction affect filtration?</h3>\n<p>A simplified microvascular pressure system has resistance <strong>before</strong> and <strong>after</strong> the capillary bed. Reduced precapillary resistance or increased postcapillary resistance can raise capillary pressure relative to surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p>The lecturer explicitly lists <strong>arteriolar dilation and venular constriction</strong> as factors that can increase filtration. Their effect depends on the wider microvascular context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-can-low-plasma-proteins-cause-oedema\">5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema?</h2>\n<p>Plasma proteins help maintain <strong>plasma oncotic pressure</strong>. If albumin falls markedly, the oncotic pressure opposing capillary filtration decreases.</p>\n<p>The lecturer gives three important examples:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Liver disease:</strong> Reduced albumin synthesis in significant hepatic dysfunction.</li><li><strong>Kidney disease:</strong> Excess urinary protein loss in conditions such as nephrotic syndrome.</li><li><strong>Severe malnutrition:</strong> Inadequate protein availability and related metabolic effects.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Albumin ↓ → plasma oncotic pressure ↓ → filtration easier relative to retention → interstitial fluid may accumulate if lymphatic return is exceeded.</p>\n<p>The lecture also mentions <strong>osmotically active substances accumulating in tissues</strong>. Increased effective interstitial oncotic forces can draw or retain more water in the tissue environment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-inflammation-increase-oedema\">6. How does inflammation increase oedema?</h2>\n<p>The lecture names <strong>histamine, bradykinin and substance P</strong> among agents that increase permeability.</p>\n<p>Inflammatory signals change endothelial barrier properties, allowing greater fluid movement and, in some vascular beds, more protein escape. This can increase <strong>Kf</strong> and alter the effective oncotic balance, promoting local tissue swelling.</p>\n<p>This is not identical to swelling caused purely by raised venous pressure: the <strong>barrier itself</strong> has changed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-persistent-swelling\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling?</h2>\n<p>Even when capillary pressure is fairly normal, some fluid and proteins continually reach the interstitium. Lymphatic vessels normally return these materials to the venous circulation.</p>\n<p>If lymphatic channels are damaged or obstructed, drainage cannot match filtration. Protein-rich fluid accumulates and chronic inflammation and tissue remodelling can follow.</p>\n<p>The lecturer identifies <strong>filarial infection (elephantiasis)</strong> and <strong>radical mastectomy</strong> affecting lymph drainage as examples. Details are developed in Chapter 15.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-why-are-pitting-and-non-pitting-oedema-different\">8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different?</h2>\n<p><strong>Pitting oedema</strong> means sustained finger pressure produces a temporary indentation. Mobile interstitial fluid is displaced by the pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-pitting oedema</strong> may involve tissue changes such as fibrosis, protein deposition or altered connective-tissue properties, so an indentation is less easily produced.</p>\n<p><strong>Important qualification:</strong> Early lymphoedema can <strong>pit</strong>. Longstanding lymphoedema may become firm and non-pitting as fibrosis develops. Therefore, the lecturer's short “lymphoedema — not pitting” statement describes a common <strong>late pattern</strong>, not every stage.</p>\n<p>Clinical signs alone should not be used to determine a diagnosis without considering the full presentation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-which-location-of-oedema-suggests-which-mechanism\">9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism?</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>A physiological possibility</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Dependent ankle swelling after standing</strong></td><td>Gravity-related venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure rise</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pulmonary oedema</strong></td><td>Raised pulmonary microvascular pressure and/or permeability, impairing gas exchange</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Generalised swelling with low albumin</strong></td><td>Reduced plasma oncotic opposition to filtration</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Chronic swelling after lymph-node removal</strong></td><td>Impaired regional lymphatic drainage</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These are <strong>mechanistic associations</strong>, not exclusive diagnoses. For example, more than one oedema mechanism can coexist in heart or kidney disease.</p>\n<p><strong>Central takeaway:</strong> The visible swelling is the outcome. The important question is <strong>which fluid-balance mechanism failed?</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why doesn't a healthy person normally develop continually increasing tissue swelling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Microvascular filtration supplies some interstitial fluid, but lymphatic vessels return the excess fluid and escaped proteins to the venous circulation. Normally drainage matches ongoing net filtration sufficiently to prevent progressive interstitial accumulation.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. A patient has venous obstruction in one leg. Which Starling force is most likely increased?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc).</strong> Obstruction increases pressure upstream in venules and capillaries, pushing more fluid into tissue. Swelling develops when the increase in filtration exceeds local lymphatic capacity.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why might prolonged motionless standing produce ankle oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Gravity raises venous pressure in dependent legs. This elevates capillary hydrostatic pressure and filtration. Lack of calf-muscle pumping also reduces venous emptying, favouring interstitial fluid accumulation at the ankles.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Why can advanced liver disease cause oedema through a different mechanism from venous obstruction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Severe liver disease may reduce albumin synthesis, lowering <strong>plasma oncotic pressure</strong>. Fluid is then less strongly retained within capillaries. Venous obstruction, by contrast, chiefly increases hydrostatic pressure; either may cause excess filtration and oedema.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. How can nephrotic protein loss produce swelling despite apparently normal arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Heavy urinary protein loss can reduce circulating albumin, decreasing plasma colloid osmotic pressure. This favours greater net fluid escape from microvessels; additional renal sodium and water retention may worsen the swelling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. A patient develops red, warm swelling around an inflamed area. What barrier mechanism can produce this?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Inflammatory mediators such as histamine and bradykinin can increase endothelial permeability. More water and some proteins enter local tissues, raising filtration and changing the effective oncotic conditions. This is <strong>increased permeability</strong>, not merely pressure loading.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. What does the capillary filtration coefficient Kf represent?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Kf</strong> represents the combined effect of exchange surface area and hydraulic permeability of the microvascular barrier. A larger Kf permits more volume movement for a given effective pressure difference, potentially increasing filtration and swelling.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. Why is the classic statement that 85% of filtered fluid is always reabsorbed into venous capillaries misleading?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The revised Starling principle accounts for the endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients. In many continuous capillary beds, low net filtration persists at steady state instead of large sustained venous-end reabsorption. <strong>Lymphatic return</strong> is central to fluid balance. The percentage is a historical illustrative model, not universal physiology.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. Why might early lymphoedema be pitting while longstanding lymphoedema is often non-pitting?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Early obstruction produces relatively mobile accumulated tissue fluid that may be displaced by finger pressure. Chronic lymphatic failure can cause tissue inflammation, fibrosis and skin thickening, making the swollen tissue firmer and less readily indented.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Compare oedema caused by heart failure with oedema caused by lymph-node removal.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Heart failure may elevate systemic or pulmonary venous pressure, increasing capillary hydrostatic filtration. Lymph-node removal can damage lymphatic return pathways, reducing clearance even if filtration initially remains near normal. Both ultimately produce excess interstitial fluid through different primary mechanisms.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why can left-sided cardiac failure interfere with breathing by producing oedema?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Impaired left-heart forward function may elevate pulmonary venous and capillary pressures, raising fluid filtration into the lung interstitium and potentially alveoli. This increases diffusion distance and disrupts gas exchange, contributing to breathlessness.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. Why is renal sodium/water retention able to worsen oedema in more than one disease?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Retention expands extracellular fluid volume and can increase vascular filling and capillary hydrostatic pressure. If plasma oncotic pressure is low, venous pressure is high or lymph drainage is impaired, the additional retained fluid can further exceed tissue-fluid clearance capacity.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Oedema:</strong> Abnormal excess interstitial fluid caused by filtration exceeding clearance or reduced lymph drainage.</li><li><strong>Traditional Starling model:</strong> Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)].</li><li><strong>Pc:</strong> Capillary hydrostatic pressure favours outward fluid movement.</li><li><strong>Plasma πc:</strong> Mainly albumin-derived oncotic pressure opposes outward filtration.</li><li><strong>Kf:</strong> Hydraulic permeability × filtering surface; inflammatory changes can raise fluid leakage.</li><li><strong>Raised venous pressure:</strong> From obstruction, heart failure or standing increases capillary filtration.</li><li><strong>Reduced plasma albumin:</strong> May follow liver disease, kidney protein loss or malnutrition.</li><li><strong>Inflammation:</strong> Histamine, bradykinin and other mediators can increase endothelial permeability.</li><li><strong>Lymphatic dysfunction:</strong> Impaired fluid/protein return causes local or regional swelling.</li><li><strong>Revised Starling model:</strong> Glycocalyx-mediated gradients and lymphatic return revise universal venous-end reabsorption assumptions.</li><li><strong>Pitting:</strong> Early fluid-rich swelling can pit; advanced lymphoedema may become non-pitting with fibrosis.</li><li><strong>Distribution:</strong> Dependent, pulmonary, generalised and post-surgical patterns suggest different mechanisms.</li><li><strong>Excess salt/water:</strong> Can amplify swelling through expanded ECF and increased microvascular pressures.</li></ol>","plain":"Formation of Oedema Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 89–95. Supplementary clarification: revised Starling physiology, endothelial glycocalyx, mechanisms and clinical interpretation of pitting versus non-pitting swelling. 1. What exactly is oedema? Oedema is the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid in tissue interstitial spaces . It is not simply the presence of fluid outside blood vessels: cells normally live surrounded by a regulated volume of interstitial fluid. The important balance is between: Microvascular filtration: Fluid leaving blood vessels into tissue. Lymphatic drainage: Fluid and proteins being returned from tissues to the venous circulation. Filtration ↑ More fluid entering tissue + Drainage insufficient Less fluid leaving tissue → Oedema Fluid accumulates When filtration exceeds the removal capacity of lymphatic vessels, interstitial fluid accumulates. Oedema may therefore occur even if the heart is still beating and blood is continuing to circulate. 2. What physical forces govern filtration? The lecturer identifies capillary hydrostatic pressure, interstitial hydrostatic pressure, plasma oncotic pressure, interstitial oncotic pressure, filtration coefficient, pre-/postcapillary resistance and capillary surface area . A conventional form of the Starling equation summarises these: Traditional Starling filtration model J v = K f [ ( P c − P i ) − σ ( π c − π i ) ] Symbol Meaning Why it matters Jv Net fluid flux Positive value represents net outward filtration Kf Filtration coefficient Reflects permeability and filtering surface area Pc Capillary hydrostatic pressure Drives fluid out of capillaries Pi Interstitial hydrostatic pressure Can oppose outward filtration πc Capillary plasma oncotic pressure Opposes outward filtration πi Interstitial oncotic pressure Can promote outward filtration σ Protein reflection coefficient How effectively the barrier restricts protein passage Why is albumin important? Albumin contributes strongly to plasma oncotic pressure. If plasma protein concentration falls, its normal opposition to fluid loss becomes weaker. Important refinement: endothelial glycocalyx The lecturer's classic model assumes a simple balance between capillary and bulk interstitial proteins. Modern research shows the endothelial glycocalyx affects the relevant oncotic gradient immediately beneath the endothelial surface. In many tissues, steady-state filtration persists along much of the capillary bed; a large universal reabsorption at the venous end is not expected. Lymphatics provide the main ongoing return path for filtered fluid. The lecture's 24 L/day, 85% capillary reabsorption and 15% lymphatic return is a historical illustrative model , not an exact modern description for all tissues. Some transient reabsorption and specialised tissue exceptions remain possible. 3. Five routes to the same outcome: excess interstitial fluid Mechanism Physiological change Example Higher Pc More hydrostatic pressure pushes water outward Venous obstruction, heart failure Lower πc Weaker plasma oncotic opposition to water loss Liver disease, renal protein loss, malnutrition Higher permeability/Kf Vessel barrier allows more fluid and protein escape Inflammation, burns, allergy Impaired lymph drainage Filtered fluid and proteins cannot return adequately Filariasis, lymph node removal Salt and water retention More ECF volume and often higher microvascular pressure Renal dysfunction Key reasoning: Different diseases can all create swelling, but through different failed parts of the fluid-balance system . 4. Why does raised venous pressure cause oedema? The lecturer lists increased venous pressure, venous obstruction, venous valve incompetence, prolonged standing and heart failure. If pressure at the venous end of a capillary bed rises, the pressure gradient across its resistance vessels changes, often raising capillary hydrostatic pressure Pc . More fluid is pushed into the interstitium. Example — standing still: Gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent leg veins. Without the calf-muscle pump, venous pooling persists and capillary filtration rises. Ankles can swell even though the heart's intrinsic muscle is normal. Example — cardiac failure: Elevated systemic venous pressure may produce dependent peripheral oedema; elevated pulmonary venous pressure may produce pulmonary oedema, impairing lung gas exchange. Why can arteriolar dilation or venular constriction affect filtration? A simplified microvascular pressure system has resistance before and after the capillary bed. Reduced precapillary resistance or increased postcapillary resistance can raise capillary pressure relative to surrounding tissue. The lecturer explicitly lists arteriolar dilation and venular constriction as factors that can increase filtration. Their effect depends on the wider microvascular context. 5. Why can low plasma proteins cause oedema? Plasma proteins help maintain plasma oncotic pressure . If albumin falls markedly, the oncotic pressure opposing capillary filtration decreases. The lecturer gives three important examples: Liver disease: Reduced albumin synthesis in significant hepatic dysfunction. Kidney disease: Excess urinary protein loss in conditions such as nephrotic syndrome. Severe malnutrition: Inadequate protein availability and related metabolic effects. Mechanism: Albumin ↓ → plasma oncotic pressure ↓ → filtration easier relative to retention → interstitial fluid may accumulate if lymphatic return is exceeded. The lecture also mentions osmotically active substances accumulating in tissues . Increased effective interstitial oncotic forces can draw or retain more water in the tissue environment. 6. How does inflammation increase oedema? The lecture names histamine, bradykinin and substance P among agents that increase permeability. Inflammatory signals change endothelial barrier properties, allowing greater fluid movement and, in some vascular beds, more protein escape. This can increase Kf and alter the effective oncotic balance, promoting local tissue swelling. This is not identical to swelling caused purely by raised venous pressure: the barrier itself has changed. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause persistent swelling? Even when capillary pressure is fairly normal, some fluid and proteins continually reach the interstitium. Lymphatic vessels normally return these materials to the venous circulation. If lymphatic channels are damaged or obstructed, drainage cannot match filtration. Protein-rich fluid accumulates and chronic inflammation and tissue remodelling can follow. The lecturer identifies filarial infection (elephantiasis) and radical mastectomy affecting lymph drainage as examples. Details are developed in Chapter 15. 8. Why are pitting and non-pitting oedema different? Pitting oedema means sustained finger pressure produces a temporary indentation. Mobile interstitial fluid is displaced by the pressure. Non-pitting oedema may involve tissue changes such as fibrosis, protein deposition or altered connective-tissue properties, so an indentation is less easily produced. Important qualification: Early lymphoedema can pit . Longstanding lymphoedema may become firm and non-pitting as fibrosis develops. Therefore, the lecturer's short “lymphoedema — not pitting” statement describes a common late pattern , not every stage. Clinical signs alone should not be used to determine a diagnosis without considering the full presentation. 9. Which location of oedema suggests which mechanism? Pattern A physiological possibility Dependent ankle swelling after standing Gravity-related venous and capillary hydrostatic pressure rise Pulmonary oedema Raised pulmonary microvascular pressure and/or permeability, impairing gas exchange Generalised swelling with low albumin Reduced plasma oncotic opposition to filtration Chronic swelling after lymph-node removal Impaired regional lymphatic drainage These are mechanistic associations , not exclusive diagnoses. For example, more than one oedema mechanism can coexist in heart or kidney disease. Central takeaway: The visible swelling is the outcome. The important question is which fluid-balance mechanism failed? Understanding Check 1. Why doesn't a healthy person normally develop continually increasing tissue swelling? Reveal explanation Microvascular filtration supplies some interstitial fluid, but lymphatic vessels return the excess fluid and escaped proteins to the venous circulation. Normally drainage matches ongoing net filtration sufficiently to prevent progressive interstitial accumulation. 2. A patient has venous obstruction in one leg. Which Starling force is most likely increased? Reveal explanation Capillary hydrostatic pressure (Pc). Obstruction increases pressure upstream in venules and capillaries, pushing more fluid into tissue. Swelling develops when the increase in filtration exceeds local lymphatic capacity. 3. Why might prolonged motionless standing produce ankle oedema? Reveal explanation Gravity raises venous pressure in dependent legs. This elevates capillary hydrostatic pressure and filtration. Lack of calf-muscle pumping also reduces venous emptying, favouring interstitial fluid accumulation at the ankles. 4. Why can advanced liver disease cause oedema through a different mechanism from venous obstruction? Reveal explanation Severe liver disease may reduce albumin synthesis, lowering plasma oncotic pressure . Fluid is then less strongly retained within capillaries. Venous obstruction, by contrast, chiefly increases hydrostatic pressure; either may cause excess filtration and oedema. 5. How can nephrotic protein loss produce swelling despite apparently normal arterial pressure? Reveal explanation Heavy urinary protein loss can reduce circulating albumin, decreasing plasma colloid osmotic pressure. This favours greater net fluid escape from microvessels; additional renal sodium and water retention may worsen the swelling. 6. A patient develops red, warm swelling around an inflamed area. What barrier mechanism can produce this? Reveal explanation Inflammatory mediators such as histamine and bradykinin can increase endothelial permeability. More water and some proteins enter local tissues, raising filtration and changing the effective oncotic conditions. This is increased permeability , not merely pressure loading. 7. What does the capillary filtration coefficient Kf represent? Reveal explanation Kf represents the combined effect of exchange surface area and hydraulic permeability of the microvascular barrier. A larger Kf permits more volume movement for a given effective pressure difference, potentially increasing filtration and swelling. 8. Why is the classic statement that 85% of filtered fluid is always reabsorbed into venous capillaries misleading? Reveal explanation The revised Starling principle accounts for the endothelial glycocalyx and local protein gradients. In many continuous capillary beds, low net filtration persists at steady state instead of large sustained venous-end reabsorption. Lymphatic return is central to fluid balance. The percentage is a historical illustrative model, not universal physiology. 9. Why might early lymphoedema be pitting while longstanding lymphoedema is often non-pitting? Reveal explanation Early obstruction produces relatively mobile accumulated tissue fluid that may be displaced by finger pressure. Chronic lymphatic failure can cause tissue inflammation, fibrosis and skin thickening, making the swollen tissue firmer and less readily indented. 10. Compare oedema caused by heart failure with oedema caused by lymph-node removal. Reveal explanation Heart failure may elevate systemic or pulmonary venous pressure, increasing capillary hydrostatic filtration. Lymph-node removal can damage lymphatic return pathways, reducing clearance even if filtration initially remains near normal. Both ultimately produce excess interstitial fluid through different primary mechanisms. 11. Why can left-sided cardiac failure interfere with breathing by producing oedema? Reveal explanation Impaired left-heart forward function may elevate pulmonary venous and capillary pressures, raising fluid filtration into the lung interstitium and potentially alveoli. This increases diffusion distance and disrupts gas exchange, contributing to breathlessness. 12. Why is renal sodium/water retention able to worsen oedema in more than one disease? Reveal explanation Retention expands extracellular fluid volume and can increase vascular filling and capillary hydrostatic pressure. If plasma oncotic pressure is low, venous pressure is high or lymph drainage is impaired, the additional retained fluid can further exceed tissue-fluid clearance capacity. Chapter Essentials Oedema: Abnormal excess interstitial fluid caused by filtration exceeding clearance or reduced lymph drainage. Traditional Starling model: Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]. Pc: Capillary hydrostatic pressure favours outward fluid movement. Plasma πc: Mainly albumin-derived oncotic pressure opposes outward filtration. Kf: Hydraulic permeability × filtering surface; inflammatory changes can raise fluid leakage. Raised venous pressure: From obstruction, heart failure or standing increases capillary filtration. Reduced plasma albumin: May follow liver disease, kidney protein loss or malnutrition. Inflammation: Histamine, bradykinin and other mediators can increase endothelial permeability. Lymphatic dysfunction: Impaired fluid/protein return causes local or regional swelling. Revised Starling model: Glycocalyx-mediated gradients and lymphatic return revise universal venous-end reabsorption assumptions. Pitting: Early fluid-rich swelling can pit; advanced lymphoedema may become non-pitting with fibrosis. Distribution: Dependent, pulmonary, generalised and post-surgical patterns suggest different mechanisms. Excess salt/water: Can amplify swelling through expanded ECF and increased microvascular pressures."},{"slug":"lymphatics","title":"Lymphatic Circulation","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"60 and 94-95; Regional Circulation 47-48","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins","title":"1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?"},{"id":"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary","title":"2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?"},{"id":"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart","title":"3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?"},{"id":"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood","title":"4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?"},{"id":"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption","title":"5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?"},{"id":"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance","title":"6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?"},{"id":"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema","title":"7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?"},{"id":"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost","title":"8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"}],"html":"<h1 id=\"lymphatic-circulation\">Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 60 and 94–95, with diagrammatic support from Regional Circulation Proper, slides 47–48. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> lymphatic vessel structure, intestinal chylomicron transport, and stages of lymphoedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-lymphatic-vessels-if-we-already-have-veins\">1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins?</h2>\n<p>Blood capillaries continuously exchange substances and filter some water into surrounding tissues. If every bit of fluid and escaped protein stayed there, tissues would swell progressively.</p>\n<p>The <strong>lymphatic system</strong> collects excess <strong>interstitial fluid, proteins and selected particles</strong> and returns them to the venous circulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Lymph</strong> is fluid inside lymphatic vessels. It develops from interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries; its composition varies with the tissue drained.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Lymphatic return route\"><span>Capillary filtration</span><b>→</b><span>Interstitial fluid</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph capillaries</span><b>→</b><span>Lymph vessels and ducts</span><b>→</b><span>Venous blood</span></div>\n<p>The lecturer stresses that lymphatic drainage handles fluid left after microvascular exchange. Under the <strong>revised Starling principle</strong>, lymphatic return is a major normal steady-state route for filtered fluid, not merely a back-up for a universal 85% venous-end reabsorption process.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-how-does-lymph-enter-a-lymphatic-capillary\">2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary?</h2>\n<p><strong>Initial lymphatic capillaries</strong> begin as <strong>blind-ended, thin-walled vessels</strong> in tissues. Their endothelial cells overlap and are connected with specialised anchoring structures to surrounding tissue.</p>\n<p>When local interstitial pressure exceeds pressure inside the initial lymphatic, overlapping endothelial margins can separate to admit <strong>fluid, proteins and some particles</strong>. The flap-like arrangement restricts reverse leakage when pressure changes.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure lymph-entry-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 590 185\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Original diagram of a blood capillary above an interstitial space, with arrows showing fluid filtration into a blind-ended green lymphatic capillary and one-way lymph movement towards a collecting vessel.\"><rect x=\"38\" y=\"28\" width=\"370\" height=\"40\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"#efc9cc\" stroke=\"#bb626e\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><g fill=\"#ac4857\"><circle cx=\"108\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"196\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"326\" cy=\"48\" r=\"8\"/></g><path d=\"M220 73 V113m-7 -10 7 10 7 -10\" stroke=\"#d38428\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M36 145 Q37 121 70 121 L407 121 L407 166 L70 166 Q37 166 36 145 Z\" fill=\"#d9f3e6\" stroke=\"#3b9474\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><path d=\"M104 145H360m-10 -8 10 8 -10 8\" stroke=\"#16805e\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><g font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"43\" y=\"17\">Blood capillary</text><text x=\"237\" y=\"94\">Filtered fluid ↓</text><text x=\"65\" y=\"112\">Interstitial space</text><text x=\"115\" y=\"174\">Initial lymphatic capillary →</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"144\">To larger</text><text x=\"431\" y=\"159\">lymphatics</text></g></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy.</p></figure>\n<p><strong>Why is this structure useful?</strong> It can admit material that cannot simply diffuse rapidly back into a blood capillary, including interstitial proteins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-can-lymph-move-when-there-is-no-central-lymphatic-heart\">3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart?</h2>\n<p>Lymph flows along a low-pressure system. Several mechanisms promote its movement:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>One-way valves:</strong> Limit backward flow when external compression changes.</li><li><strong>Contracting collecting lymphatics:</strong> Smooth-muscle segments rhythmically compress lymph.</li><li><strong>Skeletal-muscle movement:</strong> Surrounding muscle compression moves lymph along valved channels.</li><li><strong>Respiratory pump:</strong> Changing thoracic and abdominal pressures help direct flow.</li><li><strong>Adjacent tissue/arterial movement:</strong> Can also contribute to mechanical propulsion.</li></ul>\n<p>The lecturer lists all these components, including pumping by the lymphatic walls and the pressure relationships where the major ducts empty into veins.</p>\n<p><strong>A useful comparison:</strong> Venous return and lymphatic flow both rely partly on valves and muscle/respiratory pumping, but lymphatics begin <strong>blindly in the interstitium</strong> rather than as continuations of blood capillaries within a closed blood vessel circuit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-where-does-lymph-finally-return-to-blood\">4. Where does lymph finally return to blood?</h2>\n<p>The lymphatic system has major collecting pathways:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Pathway</th><th>Main drainage territory</th><th>Typical return location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Thoracic duct</strong></td><td>Most of body, including both lower limbs, abdomen and left upper body</td><td>Left venous angle near left internal jugular–subclavian junction</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Right lymphatic duct</strong></td><td>Right side of head/neck, right upper limb, right part of thorax</td><td>Right venous angle near right internal jugular–subclavian junction</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>These are standard drainage territories; <strong>normal anatomical variants</strong> occur.</p>\n<p>The lecturer describes lymph entering the right and left subclavian venous regions. More precise anatomy places the usual openings at or near the <strong>venous angles</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-role-do-lymph-nodes-play\">What role do lymph nodes play?</h3>\n<p>Lymph travelling through regional collecting vessels often passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>. These structures expose lymph-borne materials to immune cells and contribute to immune surveillance.</p>\n<p>Lymph nodes are <strong>not</strong> the structures that create the main pumping force. Fluid movement primarily depends on lymphatic vessel contractions, pressure gradients, movement and valves.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-why-is-the-intestinal-lymphatic-system-important-in-fat-absorption\">5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer names fatty acids and cholesterol absorption as lymphatic functions. A major intestinal lymphatic capillary inside each villus is a <strong>lacteal</strong>.</p>\n<p>After digestion, many long-chain dietary fatty acids and monoacylglycerols are taken up by enterocytes, reassembled into triglycerides and packaged, together with cholesterol and other lipids, into <strong>chylomicrons</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Intestinal lipid absorption through lymphatics\"><span>Long-chain dietary lipids</span><b>→</b><span>Enterocyte chylomicrons</span><b>→</b><span>Villus lacteal</span><b>→</b><span>Mesenteric lymph</span><b>→</b><span>Thoracic duct → Blood</span></div>\n<p>Why take the lymphatic route? Chylomicrons are relatively large lipid-rich particles and preferentially enter the lacteal system rather than directly entering ordinary intestinal blood capillaries.</p>\n<p><strong>Important exception:</strong> Not every absorbed fat follows this path. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the <strong>portal circulation</strong> directly, while long-chain lipids are especially associated with chylomicron transport.</p>\n<p>The lipid-rich lymph leaving the intestine is called <strong>chyle</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-how-does-lymphatic-drainage-protect-tissue-fluid-balance\">6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance?</h2>\n<p>In many tissues there is continuing <strong>net capillary filtration</strong>. The lymphatics collect fluid and proteins that escape into the interstitium and return them to blood.</p>\n<p>If filtration suddenly increases, lymphatic return can often rise to compensate for a while. <strong>Oedema develops when the total demand exceeds the available drainage capacity, or when lymphatic outflow is obstructed.</strong></p>\n<p>This gives two routes to swelling:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>What fails?</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Excess filtration</strong></td><td>Too much fluid enters tissue for the drainage capacity</td><td>Venous congestion</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reduced lymphatic clearance</strong></td><td>Normal or increased filtration cannot be removed</td><td>Filarial damage or surgical disruption</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>The relationship is especially important when interpreting the lecture's classic <strong>24 L/day and 85%/15%</strong> example: those percentages are a historical illustration, whereas modern understanding gives lymphatics a central role in ongoing normal tissue-fluid balance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-does-lymphatic-obstruction-cause-lymphoedema\">7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema?</h2>\n<p>The lecturer gives two examples:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Filarial worm infection:</strong> Parasites and associated inflammation can damage lymphatic vessels, producing chronic swelling and sometimes elephantiasis.</li><li><strong>Radical mastectomy/axillary lymph-node surgery:</strong> Removing or damaging lymphatic channels may impair drainage from the affected upper limb or chest wall.</li></ul>\n<p>Other conditions, including malignancy and congenital lymphatic abnormalities, can also interfere with lymph flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Mechanism:</strong> Impaired drainage → interstitial fluid and protein accumulation → persistent swelling → chronic inflammatory changes and fibrosis if longstanding.</p>\n<h3 id=\"is-lymphoedema-always-non-pitting\">Is lymphoedema always non-pitting?</h3>\n<p><strong>No.</strong> The lecture says “not pitting,” describing a common advanced appearance. Early lymphoedema can be <strong>soft and pitting</strong>, because fluid is still mobile. With chronicity, tissue thickening and fibrosis can produce <strong>firm, less-pitting or non-pitting</strong> swelling.</p>\n<p>Pitting alone does not distinguish all lymphatic from all venous causes. The duration, distribution and clinical context matter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-what-happens-if-lymphatic-function-is-lost\">8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost?</h2>\n<p>Three broad consequences follow:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fluid balance:</strong> Less interstitial fluid and protein are returned, causing swelling.</li><li><strong>Immune function:</strong> Lymph transport through nodes and immune surveillance are disrupted.</li><li><strong>Nutrient transport:</strong> Damaged intestinal lacteals can impair delivery of long-chain dietary lipids via chylomicrons.</li></ul>\n<p>The lecturer briefly describes lymphatics as helping bodily fluid balance and intestinal absorption. The more precise mechanism is <strong>return of interstitial fluid and transport of fat-rich chylomicrons</strong>, rather than lymphatic vessels directly performing the kidney's renal water reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why can a person develop oedema even if their blood vessels are structurally intact?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Some fluid is normally filtered from capillaries into tissue. If the lymphatic drainage pathway is obstructed or damaged, the fluid and escaped proteins cannot return adequately to venous blood. They accumulate as <strong>oedema</strong> even when the blood-vessel walls are not newly damaged.</p></details>\n<p><strong>2. How do initial lymphatic capillaries admit interstitial fluid without simply leaking it back out?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Initial lymphatics have overlapping endothelial flaps that can open when interstitial pressure favours entry. Their specialised arrangement helps restrict reverse leakage when internal pressure rises. Collecting lymphatics then have additional valves favouring one-way flow.</p></details>\n<p><strong>3. Why can lymph move upwards from the legs without a dedicated central lymph pump?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>One-way valves combine with <strong>intrinsic lymphatic smooth-muscle contractions</strong> and external skeletal-muscle and respiratory movements. Each compression episode helps drive lymph onward while valves limit backflow. A separate lymphatic heart is unnecessary.</p></details>\n<p><strong>4. Name the usual territories drained by the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The <strong>thoracic duct</strong> drains most of the body, including the lower limbs, abdomen and left upper regions. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> commonly drains the right upper quadrant: right head/neck, upper limb and thorax. Both usually empty near their corresponding internal jugular–subclavian venous angles.</p></details>\n<p><strong>5. Why are lymph nodes important even though they do not serve as the main lymphatic pumps?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Lymph nodes expose material transported in lymph to immune cells and help organise immune surveillance. Fluid movement mainly comes from pressure gradients, intrinsic lymphatic contractions and external pumps rather than active pumping by the nodes themselves.</p></details>\n<p><strong>6. How are most long-chain dietary lipids delivered to systemic blood after absorption?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Enterocytes absorb digestion products, resynthesise triglycerides and package many long-chain lipids into <strong>chylomicrons</strong>. These enter villous <strong>lacteals</strong>, travel through intestinal lymphatic vessels and the thoracic duct, and ultimately enter systemic venous blood.</p></details>\n<p><strong>7. Must all absorbed fatty acids pass through the lymphatic lacteals?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>No.</strong> Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly. The lymphatic chylomicron route is especially important for long-chain dietary lipids and other fat-soluble materials packaged into these particles.</p></details>\n<p><strong>8. How is chyle different from ordinary tissue lymph?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p><strong>Chyle</strong> is intestinal lymph enriched in dietary lipid-rich chylomicrons after a meal. Lymph from other tissues varies in composition but usually does not contain the same high concentration of absorbed intestinal chylomicrons.</p></details>\n<p><strong>9. How can filarial infection cause the severe chronic swelling associated with elephantiasis?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Filarial infection and the associated inflammatory response can damage or obstruct lymphatic pathways. Protein-rich interstitial fluid accumulates and prolonged inflammation stimulates tissue remodelling and fibrosis. Severe chronic disease may produce thickened, enlarged limbs and skin changes.</p></details>\n<p><strong>10. Why might an arm swell after axillary lymph-node dissection?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Dissection can remove or damage lymphatic channels draining that arm. Normal tissue filtration continues but clearance becomes inadequate, causing regional lymphoedema. The swelling is due primarily to <strong>impaired lymph drainage</strong>, not necessarily reduced cardiac output.</p></details>\n<p><strong>11. Why is it incorrect to say that all lymphoedema is non-pitting?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Early lymphoedema may consist largely of mobile interstitial fluid, leaving an indentation after pressure. With chronic disease, inflammation and fibrosis make the tissue firmer and often non-pitting. The lecturer's non-pitting description mainly fits advanced stages.</p></details>\n<p><strong>12. What is the important modern correction to the classical claim that only 15% of filtered capillary fluid is removed by lymphatics?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The revised Starling principle recognises the endothelial glycocalyx and persistent modest net filtration in many capillary beds at steady state. <strong>Lymphatic drainage is the principal continuous return route for much of that filtered fluid</strong>; the lecturer's 85%/15% calculation is historical illustrative teaching, not a universal proportion.</p></details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Lymphatics:</strong> Return interstitial fluid and proteins to venous circulation; also transport immune cells/material.</li><li><strong>Initial capillaries:</strong> Blind-ended and thin-walled, with specialised endothelial flaps admitting tissue fluid.</li><li><strong>Collecting vessels:</strong> Valves and contractile smooth-muscle segments favour one-way lymph flow.</li><li><strong>External pumps:</strong> Skeletal-muscle movement and respiration assist lymph propulsion.</li><li><strong>Thoracic duct:</strong> Usually drains most of the body into the left venous angle.</li><li><strong>Right lymphatic duct:</strong> Usually drains the right upper quadrant into the right venous angle.</li><li><strong>Lymph nodes:</strong> Sites of filtration and immune surveillance, not the main lymph-propelling pumps.</li><li><strong>Lacteals:</strong> Intestinal lymphatics receiving chylomicrons formed from long-chain dietary lipids.</li><li><strong>Chyle:</strong> Lipid-enriched intestinal lymph.</li><li><strong>Steady-state fluid balance:</strong> Lymphatics continually return filtered fluid and proteins; excess filtration or reduced drainage causes oedema.</li><li><strong>Lymphoedema causes:</strong> Filarial injury, lymphatic surgery, malignancy and developmental disorders.</li><li><strong>Pitting:</strong> Early lymphoedema may pit; chronic fibrotic lymphoedema may become non-pitting.</li><li><strong>Transport consequences:</strong> Lymph dysfunction affects tissue fluid balance, immune surveillance and intestinal dietary-lipid transport.</li></ol>","plain":"Lymphatic Circulation Primary lecture: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 60 and 94–95, with diagrammatic support from Regional Circulation Proper, slides 47–48. Supplementary clarification: lymphatic vessel structure, intestinal chylomicron transport, and stages of lymphoedema. 1. Why do we need lymphatic vessels if we already have veins? Blood capillaries continuously exchange substances and filter some water into surrounding tissues. If every bit of fluid and escaped protein stayed there, tissues would swell progressively. The lymphatic system collects excess interstitial fluid, proteins and selected particles and returns them to the venous circulation. Lymph is fluid inside lymphatic vessels. It develops from interstitial fluid that has entered lymphatic capillaries; its composition varies with the tissue drained. Capillary filtration → Interstitial fluid → Lymph capillaries → Lymph vessels and ducts → Venous blood The lecturer stresses that lymphatic drainage handles fluid left after microvascular exchange. Under the revised Starling principle , lymphatic return is a major normal steady-state route for filtered fluid, not merely a back-up for a universal 85% venous-end reabsorption process. 2. How does lymph enter a lymphatic capillary? Initial lymphatic capillaries begin as blind-ended, thin-walled vessels in tissues. Their endothelial cells overlap and are connected with specialised anchoring structures to surrounding tissue. When local interstitial pressure exceeds pressure inside the initial lymphatic, overlapping endothelial margins can separate to admit fluid, proteins and some particles . The flap-like arrangement restricts reverse leakage when pressure changes. Figure 01. From capillary filtration to a one-way lymphatic entry route Blood capillary Filtered fluid ↓ Interstitial space Initial lymphatic capillary → To larger lymphatics Original conceptual schematic based on the lecturer's lymphatic motion/pressure-gradient diagram. It illustrates fluid entry and one-way transport, not exact vessel anatomy. Why is this structure useful? It can admit material that cannot simply diffuse rapidly back into a blood capillary, including interstitial proteins. 3. How can lymph move when there is no central lymphatic heart? Lymph flows along a low-pressure system. Several mechanisms promote its movement: One-way valves: Limit backward flow when external compression changes. Contracting collecting lymphatics: Smooth-muscle segments rhythmically compress lymph. Skeletal-muscle movement: Surrounding muscle compression moves lymph along valved channels. Respiratory pump: Changing thoracic and abdominal pressures help direct flow. Adjacent tissue/arterial movement: Can also contribute to mechanical propulsion. The lecturer lists all these components, including pumping by the lymphatic walls and the pressure relationships where the major ducts empty into veins. A useful comparison: Venous return and lymphatic flow both rely partly on valves and muscle/respiratory pumping, but lymphatics begin blindly in the interstitium rather than as continuations of blood capillaries within a closed blood vessel circuit. 4. Where does lymph finally return to blood? The lymphatic system has major collecting pathways: Pathway Main drainage territory Typical return location Thoracic duct Most of body, including both lower limbs, abdomen and left upper body Left venous angle near left internal jugular–subclavian junction Right lymphatic duct Right side of head/neck, right upper limb, right part of thorax Right venous angle near right internal jugular–subclavian junction These are standard drainage territories; normal anatomical variants occur. The lecturer describes lymph entering the right and left subclavian venous regions. More precise anatomy places the usual openings at or near the venous angles . What role do lymph nodes play? Lymph travelling through regional collecting vessels often passes through lymph nodes . These structures expose lymph-borne materials to immune cells and contribute to immune surveillance. Lymph nodes are not the structures that create the main pumping force. Fluid movement primarily depends on lymphatic vessel contractions, pressure gradients, movement and valves. 5. Why is the intestinal lymphatic system important in fat absorption? The lecturer names fatty acids and cholesterol absorption as lymphatic functions. A major intestinal lymphatic capillary inside each villus is a lacteal . After digestion, many long-chain dietary fatty acids and monoacylglycerols are taken up by enterocytes, reassembled into triglycerides and packaged, together with cholesterol and other lipids, into chylomicrons . Long-chain dietary lipids → Enterocyte chylomicrons → Villus lacteal → Mesenteric lymph → Thoracic duct → Blood Why take the lymphatic route? Chylomicrons are relatively large lipid-rich particles and preferentially enter the lacteal system rather than directly entering ordinary intestinal blood capillaries. Important exception: Not every absorbed fat follows this path. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly, while long-chain lipids are especially associated with chylomicron transport. The lipid-rich lymph leaving the intestine is called chyle . 6. How does lymphatic drainage protect tissue fluid balance? In many tissues there is continuing net capillary filtration . The lymphatics collect fluid and proteins that escape into the interstitium and return them to blood. If filtration suddenly increases, lymphatic return can often rise to compensate for a while. Oedema develops when the total demand exceeds the available drainage capacity, or when lymphatic outflow is obstructed. This gives two routes to swelling: Problem What fails? Example Excess filtration Too much fluid enters tissue for the drainage capacity Venous congestion Reduced lymphatic clearance Normal or increased filtration cannot be removed Filarial damage or surgical disruption The relationship is especially important when interpreting the lecture's classic 24 L/day and 85%/15% example: those percentages are a historical illustration, whereas modern understanding gives lymphatics a central role in ongoing normal tissue-fluid balance. 7. Why does lymphatic obstruction cause lymphoedema? The lecturer gives two examples: Filarial worm infection: Parasites and associated inflammation can damage lymphatic vessels, producing chronic swelling and sometimes elephantiasis. Radical mastectomy/axillary lymph-node surgery: Removing or damaging lymphatic channels may impair drainage from the affected upper limb or chest wall. Other conditions, including malignancy and congenital lymphatic abnormalities, can also interfere with lymph flow. Mechanism: Impaired drainage → interstitial fluid and protein accumulation → persistent swelling → chronic inflammatory changes and fibrosis if longstanding. Is lymphoedema always non-pitting? No. The lecture says “not pitting,” describing a common advanced appearance. Early lymphoedema can be soft and pitting , because fluid is still mobile. With chronicity, tissue thickening and fibrosis can produce firm, less-pitting or non-pitting swelling. Pitting alone does not distinguish all lymphatic from all venous causes. The duration, distribution and clinical context matter. 8. What happens if lymphatic function is lost? Three broad consequences follow: Fluid balance: Less interstitial fluid and protein are returned, causing swelling. Immune function: Lymph transport through nodes and immune surveillance are disrupted. Nutrient transport: Damaged intestinal lacteals can impair delivery of long-chain dietary lipids via chylomicrons. The lecturer briefly describes lymphatics as helping bodily fluid balance and intestinal absorption. The more precise mechanism is return of interstitial fluid and transport of fat-rich chylomicrons , rather than lymphatic vessels directly performing the kidney's renal water reabsorption. Understanding Check 1. Why can a person develop oedema even if their blood vessels are structurally intact? Reveal explanation Some fluid is normally filtered from capillaries into tissue. If the lymphatic drainage pathway is obstructed or damaged, the fluid and escaped proteins cannot return adequately to venous blood. They accumulate as oedema even when the blood-vessel walls are not newly damaged. 2. How do initial lymphatic capillaries admit interstitial fluid without simply leaking it back out? Reveal explanation Initial lymphatics have overlapping endothelial flaps that can open when interstitial pressure favours entry. Their specialised arrangement helps restrict reverse leakage when internal pressure rises. Collecting lymphatics then have additional valves favouring one-way flow. 3. Why can lymph move upwards from the legs without a dedicated central lymph pump? Reveal explanation One-way valves combine with intrinsic lymphatic smooth-muscle contractions and external skeletal-muscle and respiratory movements. Each compression episode helps drive lymph onward while valves limit backflow. A separate lymphatic heart is unnecessary. 4. Name the usual territories drained by the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct. Reveal explanation The thoracic duct drains most of the body, including the lower limbs, abdomen and left upper regions. The right lymphatic duct commonly drains the right upper quadrant: right head/neck, upper limb and thorax. Both usually empty near their corresponding internal jugular–subclavian venous angles. 5. Why are lymph nodes important even though they do not serve as the main lymphatic pumps? Reveal explanation Lymph nodes expose material transported in lymph to immune cells and help organise immune surveillance. Fluid movement mainly comes from pressure gradients, intrinsic lymphatic contractions and external pumps rather than active pumping by the nodes themselves. 6. How are most long-chain dietary lipids delivered to systemic blood after absorption? Reveal explanation Enterocytes absorb digestion products, resynthesise triglycerides and package many long-chain lipids into chylomicrons . These enter villous lacteals , travel through intestinal lymphatic vessels and the thoracic duct, and ultimately enter systemic venous blood. 7. Must all absorbed fatty acids pass through the lymphatic lacteals? Reveal explanation No. Many short- and medium-chain fatty acids can enter the portal circulation directly. The lymphatic chylomicron route is especially important for long-chain dietary lipids and other fat-soluble materials packaged into these particles. 8. How is chyle different from ordinary tissue lymph? Reveal explanation Chyle is intestinal lymph enriched in dietary lipid-rich chylomicrons after a meal. Lymph from other tissues varies in composition but usually does not contain the same high concentration of absorbed intestinal chylomicrons. 9. How can filarial infection cause the severe chronic swelling associated with elephantiasis? Reveal explanation Filarial infection and the associated inflammatory response can damage or obstruct lymphatic pathways. Protein-rich interstitial fluid accumulates and prolonged inflammation stimulates tissue remodelling and fibrosis. Severe chronic disease may produce thickened, enlarged limbs and skin changes. 10. Why might an arm swell after axillary lymph-node dissection? Reveal explanation Dissection can remove or damage lymphatic channels draining that arm. Normal tissue filtration continues but clearance becomes inadequate, causing regional lymphoedema. The swelling is due primarily to impaired lymph drainage , not necessarily reduced cardiac output. 11. Why is it incorrect to say that all lymphoedema is non-pitting? Reveal explanation Early lymphoedema may consist largely of mobile interstitial fluid, leaving an indentation after pressure. With chronic disease, inflammation and fibrosis make the tissue firmer and often non-pitting. The lecturer's non-pitting description mainly fits advanced stages. 12. What is the important modern correction to the classical claim that only 15% of filtered capillary fluid is removed by lymphatics? Reveal explanation The revised Starling principle recognises the endothelial glycocalyx and persistent modest net filtration in many capillary beds at steady state. Lymphatic drainage is the principal continuous return route for much of that filtered fluid ; the lecturer's 85%/15% calculation is historical illustrative teaching, not a universal proportion. Chapter Essentials Lymphatics: Return interstitial fluid and proteins to venous circulation; also transport immune cells/material. Initial capillaries: Blind-ended and thin-walled, with specialised endothelial flaps admitting tissue fluid. Collecting vessels: Valves and contractile smooth-muscle segments favour one-way lymph flow. External pumps: Skeletal-muscle movement and respiration assist lymph propulsion. Thoracic duct: Usually drains most of the body into the left venous angle. Right lymphatic duct: Usually drains the right upper quadrant into the right venous angle. Lymph nodes: Sites of filtration and immune surveillance, not the main lymph-propelling pumps. Lacteals: Intestinal lymphatics receiving chylomicrons formed from long-chain dietary lipids. Chyle: Lipid-enriched intestinal lymph. Steady-state fluid balance: Lymphatics continually return filtered fluid and proteins; excess filtration or reduced drainage causes oedema. Lymphoedema causes: Filarial injury, lymphatic surgery, malignancy and developmental disorders. Pitting: Early lymphoedema may pit; chronic fibrotic lymphoedema may become non-pitting. Transport consequences: Lymph dysfunction affects tissue fluid balance, immune surveillance and intestinal dietary-lipid transport."},{"slug":"local-regulation","title":"Local Control of Blood Flow","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"1-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control","title":"1. Why does a tissue need local control?"},{"id":"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady","title":"2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady"},{"id":"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic","title":"3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic"},{"id":"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia","title":"4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia"},{"id":"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute","title":"5. What does the endothelium contribute?"},{"id":"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress","title":"6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress"},{"id":"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together","title":"7. Bringing local and whole-body control together"},{"id":"8-understanding-checks","title":"8. Understanding Checks"},{"id":"9-chapter-essentials","title":"9. Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"10-references-and-scope-notes","title":"10. References and scope notes"}],"html":"<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 1–11. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> tissue-specific mediators, the distinction between autoregulation and hyperaemia, endothelial signalling and mathematically consistent Laplace relationships.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-a-tissue-need-local-control\">1. Why does a tissue need local control?</h2>\n<p>The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations. <strong>Local blood-flow control</strong> means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles.</p>\n<p>The starting relationship is <strong>Q = ΔP / R</strong>, where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed.</p>\n<p>In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow, <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong> (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance <strong>16-fold</strong> when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation.</p>\n<div class=\"lymph-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Local flow adjustment\"><span>Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes</span><b>→</b><span>Local sensing</span><b>→</b><span>Arteriolar tone changes</span><b>→</b><span>Resistance and flow adjust</span></div>\n<h2 id=\"2-autoregulation-keeping-blood-flow-relatively-steady\">2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a <em>range</em> of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand.</p>\n<p>If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then <strong>constrict</strong>, increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to <strong>dilate</strong> and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output.</p>\n<p>| Disturbance | Initial passive tendency | Compensatory change | Intended result | |---|---|---|---| | Increased perfusion pressure | Flow rises | Arteriolar constriction increases R | Flow moves back toward baseline | | Decreased perfusion pressure | Flow falls | Arteriolar dilation decreases R | Flow moves back toward baseline |</p>\n<p><strong>Limitation:</strong> If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-two-complementary-mechanisms-myogenic-and-metabolic\">3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic</h2>\n<h3>Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch</h3>\n<p>An arteriole experiences <strong>transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure</strong>. Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is this useful?</strong> Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds.</p>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure\"><figcaption>Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 620 140\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Higher transmural pressure stretches an arteriole, triggering smooth-muscle contraction, raising resistance and opposing a large rise in flow\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e9f3fd\" stroke=\"#4d84aa\"/><rect x=\"224\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#e4f4ea\" stroke=\"#4e9b74\"/><rect x=\"443\" y=\"32\" width=\"172\" height=\"62\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fff2d6\" stroke=\"#b88933\"/><g fill=\"#20324b\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"91\" y=\"57\">Pressure ↑</text><text x=\"91\" y=\"77\">Wall stretch ↑</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"57\">Smooth muscle</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"77\">contracts</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"57\">Radius ↓; R ↑</text><text x=\"529\" y=\"77\">Flow rise opposed</text></g><path d=\"M181 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7M400 63h36m-9 -7 9 7 -9 7\" stroke=\"#527895\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/></svg><p>Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response.</p></figure>\n<h3>Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs</h3>\n<p>When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include <strong>adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension</strong>. The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals.</p>\n<p>Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases <strong>even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Distinguish the triggers:</strong> Myogenic control principally senses <strong>vessel stretch/pressure</strong>, whereas metabolic control senses <strong>local supply relative to demand</strong>. Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-active-versus-reactive-hyperaemia\">4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia</h2>\n<p><strong>Hyperaemia</strong> means increased blood flow.</p>\n<p>| Active hyperaemia | Reactive hyperaemia | |---|---| | Occurs <strong>during increased tissue activity</strong> | Occurs <strong>after a period of reduced or interrupted flow</strong> | | Example: working skeletal muscle | Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion | | Increased metabolism promotes dilation | Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure | | Flow rises to meet ongoing demand | A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences |</p>\n<p>A useful sequence for <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong> is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue.</p>\n<p><strong>Do not confuse these with autoregulation.</strong> Autoregulation describes relative <strong>stability</strong> of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an <strong>increase</strong> in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-what-does-the-endothelium-contribute\">5. What does the endothelium contribute?</h2>\n<p>The <strong>endothelium</strong> is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Nitric oxide (NO):</strong> endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates <strong>soluble guanylyl cyclase</strong>, raises <strong>cGMP</strong>, and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production.</li><li><strong>Endothelin-1:</strong> a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects.</li><li><strong>Prostacyclin (PGI₂):</strong> an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation.</li><li><strong>Histamine and bradykinin:</strong> can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Application:</strong> Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-laplace-s-principle-pressure-radius-tension-and-stress\">6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress</h2>\n<p>The lecturer introduces Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. We must specify <strong>which geometry</strong> and <strong>which quantity</strong> a formula describes.</p>\n<p>For an ideal <strong>thin-walled cylindrical vessel</strong>, circumferential <em>wall tension per unit axial length</em> is approximately <strong>T = Ptm × r</strong>, where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential <strong>wall stress</strong>, <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h</strong>, with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled <em>spherical</em> membrane, <strong>T = Ptm × r / 2</strong>. These idealizations are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p>At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However, <strong>Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling</strong>: living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls.</p>\n<p><strong>Numerical reasoning:</strong> Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-bringing-local-and-whole-body-control-together\">7. Bringing local and whole-body control together</h2>\n<p>Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise, <strong>active skeletal muscle</strong> can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ.</p>\n<p>A fall in systemic pressure can trigger <strong>baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction</strong>, while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction.</p>\n<p><strong>Clinical application:</strong> If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-understanding-checks\">8. Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01.</strong> Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range.</details>\n<p><strong>02.</strong> Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance.</details>\n<p><strong>03.</strong> When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil.</details>\n<p><strong>04.</strong> A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed.</details>\n<p><strong>05.</strong> During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia.</details>\n<p><strong>06.</strong> Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion.</details>\n<p><strong>07.</strong> Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation.</details>\n<p><strong>08.</strong> Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow.</details>\n<p><strong>09.</strong> Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed.</details>\n<p><strong>10.</strong> Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress.</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model.</details>\n<p><strong>11.</strong> Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation.</details>\n<p><strong>12.</strong> A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction?</p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted.</details>\n<h2 id=\"9-chapter-essentials\">9. Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li>Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance: <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>.</li><li>Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance.</li><li><strong>Autoregulation</strong> defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range.</li><li>Increased vessel stretch often produces <strong>myogenic constriction</strong>.</li><li>A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop <strong>metabolic vasodilation</strong>.</li><li><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong> accompanies increased tissue activity.</li><li><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong> follows temporary underperfusion.</li><li>Endothelial <strong>NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP</strong> promotes smooth-muscle relaxation.</li><li><strong>Endothelin-1</strong> commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent.</li><li>Cylindrical wall tension <strong>T ≈ Ptm × r</strong> and wall stress <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h</strong> require their assumptions.</li><li>Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed.</li><li>Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"10-references-and-scope-notes\">10. References and scope notes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Lecturer scope:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging.</li><li><strong>Reference physiology:</strong> Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>, chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>, vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control.</li><li><strong>Supplementary precision:</strong> Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels.</li></ul>","plain":" Local Control of Blood Flow \n  Primary lecture:  CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 1–11.  Supplementary clarification:  tissue-specific mediators, the distinction between autoregulation and hyperaemia, endothelial signalling and mathematically consistent Laplace relationships. \n 1. Why does a tissue need local control? \n The heart generates flow, but every organ must receive a supply matched to its moment-to-moment needs. During exercise, contracting muscles require more oxygen and nutrient delivery; meanwhile the brain needs fairly stable perfusion despite ordinary blood-pressure fluctuations.  Local blood-flow control  means a vascular bed adjusts the calibre of its own resistance vessels, especially small arteries and arterioles. \n The starting relationship is  Q = ΔP / R , where Q is volume flow (for example mL/min), ΔP is the pressure difference across a vascular bed (mmHg), and R is vascular resistance (mmHg·min/mL). If pressure rises at unchanged resistance, flow rises. If local arterioles constrict enough to raise resistance, that rise in flow can be opposed. \n In an ideal rigid tube with laminar flow,  R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)  (Poiseuille relationship). η is dynamic viscosity, L is length and r is radius. Living vessels are not rigid tubes, but the fourth-power dependence explains why even a modest arteriolar radius change can strongly affect resistance. Halving radius would increase idealized resistance  16-fold  when the other variables are unchanged; do not treat this as an exact prediction for an intact circulation. \n  Tissue activity or perfusion pressure changes  →  Local sensing  →  Arteriolar tone changes  →  Resistance and flow adjust  \n 2. Autoregulation: keeping blood flow relatively steady \n  Autoregulation  is the ability of an organ to maintain relatively constant blood flow across a  range  of perfusion pressures by changing local vascular resistance. It does not mean perfect flow constancy, and it fails outside the organ's effective autoregulatory range. Cerebral and renal circulations are especially important examples; coronary regulation also strongly matches flow to metabolic demand. \n If perfusion pressure suddenly rises, flow initially tends to increase. Local arterioles then  constrict , increasing resistance to counter the rise. If pressure falls moderately, they tend to  dilate  and lower resistance to defend flow. This is a local response and need not wait for a change in the heart's output. \n | Disturbance | Initial passive tendency | Compensatory change | Intended result | |---|---|---|---| | Increased perfusion pressure | Flow rises | Arteriolar constriction increases R | Flow moves back toward baseline | | Decreased perfusion pressure | Flow falls | Arteriolar dilation decreases R | Flow moves back toward baseline | \n  Limitation:  If pressure becomes too low for further dilation, blood flow falls despite maximal compensation. Thus autoregulation is not a guarantee against shock or severe hypotension. \n 3. Two complementary mechanisms: myogenic and metabolic \n Myogenic mechanism: the vessel responds to stretch \n An arteriole experiences  transmural pressure = intravascular pressure − surrounding tissue pressure . Higher transmural pressure distends its wall, activating vascular smooth-muscle signalling that increases contraction. Constriction reduces radius and raises resistance. Lower pressure reduces wall stretch, permitting relaxation and dilation. \n  Why is this useful?  Without the active response, increased pressure would push extra blood through vulnerable small vessels. Myogenic autoregulation helps moderate flow variation and microvascular pressure, notably in cerebral and renal beds. \n  Figure 01. Myogenic opposition to a rise in pressure       Pressure ↑  Wall stretch ↑  Smooth muscle  contracts  Radius ↓; R ↑  Flow rise opposed     Original conceptual schematic. The first pressure-driven increase in flow occurs before the slower corrective vascular response.  \n Metabolic mechanism: the tissue signals its needs \n When metabolism increases or perfusion is inadequate, local vasodilator influences rise. Depending on the tissue, these may include  adenosine, CO₂, H⁺, extracellular K⁺ and reduced tissue oxygen tension . The relative importance of each signal differs among organs. Dilation lowers local resistance and improves delivery; increasing flow can also wash out accumulated signals. \n Example: A vigorously contracting skeletal muscle consumes oxygen faster and produces metabolites. Its small resistance vessels dilate; muscle flow increases  even if systemic arterial pressure has not risen . \n  Distinguish the triggers:  Myogenic control principally senses  vessel stretch/pressure , whereas metabolic control senses  local supply relative to demand . Both can act simultaneously; this is not an either/or choice. \n 4. Active versus reactive hyperaemia \n  Hyperaemia  means increased blood flow. \n | Active hyperaemia | Reactive hyperaemia | |---|---| | Occurs  during increased tissue activity  | Occurs  after a period of reduced or interrupted flow  | | Example: working skeletal muscle | Example: increased hand flow after releasing a temporary occlusion | | Increased metabolism promotes dilation | Ischaemia-associated metabolites, myogenic changes and other signals contribute; release restores driving pressure | | Flow rises to meet ongoing demand | A temporary overshoot helps repay the supply deficit and dissipate vasodilator influences | \n A useful sequence for  reactive hyperaemia  is: brief arterial occlusion → oxygen delivery falls and vasodilator influences develop → obstruction is released → previously dilated vascular bed receives a transient flow surge → flow gradually returns toward baseline. The magnitude and duration depend on the degree and length of occlusion and on the tissue. \n  Do not confuse these with autoregulation.  Autoregulation describes relative  stability  of flow when perfusion pressure changes; hyperaemia describes an  increase  in flow in response to activity or prior underperfusion. Some underlying mechanisms overlap. \n 5. What does the endothelium contribute? \n The  endothelium  is more than a passive lining. It senses shear stress and chemical signals and releases substances that modify underlying smooth-muscle tone. \n   Nitric oxide (NO):  endothelial NO diffuses into vascular smooth muscle, activates  soluble guanylyl cyclase , raises  cGMP , and promotes relaxation. Shear stress from flowing blood can stimulate endothelial NO production.   Endothelin-1:  a powerful vasoconstrictor peptide acting principally via smooth-muscle ETᴀ receptors; endothelial ETʙ signalling can have different effects.   Prostacyclin (PGI₂):  an endothelial vasodilator that also inhibits platelet aggregation.   Histamine and bradykinin:  can promote endothelial-dependent vasodilation, and in suitable vascular beds increase permeability; their effects vary with receptor and tissue.  \n  Application:  Endothelial dysfunction can reduce NO bioavailability, contributing to impaired vasodilation. It should not be equated with the complete disappearance of myogenic or metabolic control. \n 6. Laplace's principle: pressure, radius, tension and stress \n The lecturer introduces Laplace's law near myogenic regulation. We must specify  which geometry  and  which quantity  a formula describes. \n For an ideal  thin-walled cylindrical vessel , circumferential  wall tension per unit axial length  is approximately  T = Ptm × r , where Ptm is transmural pressure and r is internal radius. For average circumferential  wall stress ,  σθ ≈ Ptm × r / h , with wall thickness h. For an ideal thin-walled  spherical  membrane,  T = Ptm × r / 2 . These idealizations are not interchangeable. \n At a fixed pressure and thickness, larger vessel radius implies greater average circumferential stress. However,  Laplace's relationship does not by itself explain myogenic signalling : living vascular smooth muscle detects deformation and activates ion-channel and contractile pathways. Real arteries have finite wall thickness and active, nonlinear walls. \n  Numerical reasoning:  Two thin-walled cylindrical vessels have equal Ptm and h. If one has twice the radius, its estimated wall tension and stress are each twice as large. It would be incorrect to say resistance also doubles: resistance responds oppositely and much more strongly to radius in the simplified Poiseuille model. \n 7. Bringing local and whole-body control together \n Local regulation operates alongside the autonomic nervous system and circulating hormones. In exercise,  active skeletal muscle  can show powerful metabolic vasodilation even while sympathetic activity increases elsewhere. This is one reason blood supply can be redistributed to working tissue rather than rising equally in every organ. \n A fall in systemic pressure can trigger  baroreflex sympathetic vasoconstriction , while critically underperfused tissues simultaneously accumulate local dilator signals. Which effect dominates depends on organ, severity, receptors, and physiological state. The next chapters examine the systemic side of this interaction. \n  Clinical application:  If systemic shock drives perfusion pressure below the lower autoregulatory limit, local dilation cannot maintain normal cerebral or renal flow. This connects local autoregulation directly to shock and organ dysfunction. \n 8. Understanding Checks \n  01.  Perfusion pressure through a vascular bed doubles while its resistance remains unchanged. What happens to flow initially, and what local adjustment can defend its baseline? \n  Show explanation From Q = ΔP/R, flow initially doubles if R is fixed. Autoregulation can increase arteriolar resistance through constriction, opposing the rise. A perfect twofold adjustment is an idealization; real responses vary by tissue and pressure range. \n  02.  Why are arterioles more effective local flow regulators than large veins? \n  Show explanation Arterioles contain actively controlled vascular smooth muscle and supply a large share of systemic vascular resistance. Small radius changes alter resistance substantially. Veins primarily serve as capacitance vessels and modulate venous return rather than controlling each tissue's entry resistance. \n  03.  When a renal resistance arteriole experiences increased transmural pressure, why may its smooth muscle contract rather than simply remain stretched? \n  Show explanation Stretch activates a myogenic smooth-muscle response that increases tone. A smaller radius raises resistance and helps counter an otherwise excessive pressure-driven rise in flow. The response is active, not merely elastic recoil. \n  04.  A student says myogenic and metabolic autoregulation are the same because both change arteriolar radius. What is missing? \n  Show explanation The initiating signals differ. Myogenic regulation responds chiefly to pressure-driven vessel stretch. Metabolic regulation responds to a mismatch between local perfusion and metabolic demand, via signals such as adenosine, H⁺ and K⁺. They can cooperate in the same vascular bed. \n  05.  During a sprint, muscle perfusion increases even though arterial pressure barely changes. Explain the local mechanism. \n  Show explanation Working muscle increases its metabolic demand. Local vasodilator signals lower arteriolar resistance; therefore flow can rise at nearly constant driving pressure according to Q = ΔP/R. This is active hyperaemia. \n  06.  Blood flow surges briefly after a pressure cuff is released. Is this active hyperaemia, reactive hyperaemia or autoregulation? \n  Show explanation Reactive hyperaemia. Temporary occlusion reduced oxygen supply and fostered vasodilator influences; reopening permits a transient overshoot before signals dissipate. The defining feature is increased flow after preceding underperfusion. \n  07.  Does adenosine have identical importance in every vascular bed? Explain. \n  Show explanation No. Local metabolic mediators differ by organ and context. Adenosine is influential in some settings, especially coronary metabolic regulation, while combinations of oxygen, CO₂, H⁺, K⁺, endothelial and myogenic influences contribute elsewhere. Avoid memorising a universal single-metabolite explanation. \n  08.  Trace the intracellular pathway by which endothelial NO relaxes adjacent vascular smooth muscle. \n  Show explanation Endothelial NO diffuses into smooth muscle and activates soluble guanylyl cyclase, increasing cGMP and promoting smooth-muscle relaxation. This increases vessel radius, decreases resistance and can increase flow. \n  09.  Why can endothelin-1 and nitric oxide have opposite effects on local flow? \n  Show explanation They affect contractile tone differently: endothelin-1 typically stimulates smooth-muscle contraction, whereas NO promotes cGMP-dependent relaxation. The final response depends on their relative signalling and the receptors of the vascular bed. \n  10.  Two ideal thin-walled cylindrical vessels face equal transmural pressure. One has double the radius and the same wall thickness. Compare estimated wall stress. \n  Show explanation Average circumferential stress is approximately σθ = Ptm × r/h. With equal pressure and thickness, doubling radius doubles estimated stress. This is distinct from Poiseuille resistance, which decreases sharply as radius increases in the ideal rigid-tube model. \n  11.  Why can autoregulation not fully protect the brain during profound haemorrhagic shock? \n  Show explanation Autoregulation is effective only over a bounded pressure range. Below its lower limit, resistance vessels cannot dilate sufficiently to preserve flow; critically reduced perfusion pressure then leads to inadequate cerebral blood delivery despite local compensation. \n  12.  A patient exercising has sympathetic activation, yet blood flow rises in active muscle. Is there a contradiction? \n  Show explanation No. Sympathetic vasoconstrictor influence and local metabolic vasodilation act together. In active muscle, local demand-related dilation can strongly attenuate sympathetic constriction, so that perfusion increases while flow in less active beds may be restricted. \n 9. Chapter Essentials \n  Tissue flow depends on pressure difference and vascular resistance:  Q = ΔP/R .  Arteriolar calibre is a major adjustable determinant of local resistance.   Autoregulation  defends relatively stable flow only within a limited pressure range.  Increased vessel stretch often produces  myogenic constriction .  A tissue with increased metabolic demand tends to develop  metabolic vasodilation .   Active hyperaemia  accompanies increased tissue activity.   Reactive hyperaemia  follows temporary underperfusion.  Endothelial  NO → soluble guanylyl cyclase → cGMP  promotes smooth-muscle relaxation.   Endothelin-1  commonly promotes vasoconstriction; endothelial responses are context-dependent.  Cylindrical wall tension  T ≈ Ptm × r  and wall stress  σθ ≈ Ptm × r/h  require their assumptions.  Local and systemic controls may compete or cooperate in one vascular bed.  Severe shock can push perfusion below the effective autoregulatory range.  \n 10. References and scope notes \n   Lecturer scope:  CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, pp. 1–11 (local regulation, myogenic/metabolic theories, Laplace and mediators). Slide numbering/source mapping should be rechecked against the exact uploaded lecture PDF before merging.   Reference physiology:  Guyton and Hall,  Textbook of Medical Physiology , chapters on local and humoral control of tissue blood flow; Boron and Boulpaep,  Medical Physiology , vascular smooth-muscle and regional-flow control.   Supplementary precision:  Autoregulatory ranges are tissue-specific; mediator examples are not universal; Laplace equations refer to ideal geometries; Poiseuille's law is an explanatory approximation in biological vessels.  "},{"slug":"neural-humoral","title":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation","title":"1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?"},{"id":"2-the-medullary-control-network","title":"2. The medullary control network"},{"id":"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins","title":"3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins"},{"id":"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint","title":"4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint"},{"id":"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents","title":"5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents"},{"id":"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise","title":"6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?"},{"id":"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits","title":"7. Time scales and reset limits"},{"id":"8-clinical-application","title":"8. Clinical application"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Neural and Hormonal Regulation</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-does-the-whole-body-need-cardiovascular-regulation\">1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</h2>\n<p>Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes. <strong>MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR</strong>, and <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>. These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-medullary-control-network\">2. The medullary control network</h2>\n<p>The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>. The <strong>rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)</strong> provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)</strong> contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The lecturer's <em>pressor</em> and <em>depressor</em> regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-sympathetic-effects-on-the-heart-arterioles-and-veins\">3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</h2>\n<p>Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release <strong>noradrenaline (norepinephrine)</strong>. Cardiac <strong>β₁</strong> signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular <strong>α₁</strong> stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax. <strong>Venoconstriction does not create blood</strong>: its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-parasympathetic-effects-and-tonic-vagal-restraint\">4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</h2>\n<p>Vagal <strong>acetylcholine</strong> binds cardiac <strong>M₂ muscarinic receptors</strong>, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-hormones-and-circulating-vasomotor-agents\">5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</h2>\n<p><strong>Adrenaline (epinephrine)</strong> from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release. <strong>Aldosterone</strong> increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions. <strong>Vasopressin (ADH)</strong> increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels. <strong>ANP/BNP</strong> counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-changes-in-haemorrhage-and-in-exercise\">6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</h2>\n<p><strong>Haemorrhage:</strong> reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery. <strong>Exercise:</strong> sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-time-scales-and-reset-limits\">7. Time scales and reset limits</h2>\n<p>Fast <strong>neural</strong> adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-clinical-application\">8. Clinical application</h2>\n<p>An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired. <strong>Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone</strong>; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone.</details>\n<p><strong>02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate.</details>\n<p><strong>03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy.</details>\n<p><strong>04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload.</details>\n<p><strong>05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit.</details>\n<p><strong>06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow.</details>\n<p><strong>07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds.</details>\n<p><strong>08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse.</details>\n<p><strong>09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels.</details>\n<p><strong>10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed.</details>\n<p><strong>11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery.</details>\n<p><strong>12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation.</details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?</strong> — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes.</li><li><strong>The medullary control network</strong> — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch.</li><li><strong>Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins</strong> — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine).</li><li><strong>Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint</strong> — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction.</li><li><strong>Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents</strong> — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution.</li><li><strong>What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?</strong> — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch.</li><li><strong>Time scales and reset limits</strong> — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days.</li><li><strong>Clinical application</strong> — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).</li><li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li><li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li></ul>","plain":" Neural and Hormonal Regulation \n  Primary lecture:  CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30.  Supplementary clarification:  mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams. \n 1. Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation? \n Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes.  MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × SVR , and  CO = HR × SV . These are interconnected: changing venous tone can alter preload and stroke volume, while arteriolar tone changes afterload. A higher heart rate is therefore not proof of a higher output. \n 2. The medullary control network \n The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch. Sensory information reaches the  nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) . The  rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)  provides a major tonic sympathoexcitatory output; the  caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)  contributes inhibitory relay activity. Cardiac vagal output involves nucleus ambiguus and dorsal motor nucleus of vagus. The lecturer's  pressor  and  depressor  regions describe the net effects of stimulating these networks. Increased sympathetic vasomotor firing narrows many arterioles; reducing its tonic activity permits dilation. \n 3. Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins \n Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release  noradrenaline (norepinephrine) . Cardiac  β₁  signalling increases SA node firing, AV conduction and ventricular contractility. Vascular  α₁  stimulation constricts many arterioles, increasing resistance, and many veins, reducing venous capacitance and mobilising blood toward the thorax.  Venoconstriction does not create blood : its contribution is limited by circulating volume and right-heart function. During haemorrhage, systemic vascular resistance can preserve MAP while tissue flow remains inadequate. \n 4. Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint \n Vagal  acetylcholine  binds cardiac  M₂ muscarinic receptors , reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction. Parasympathetic innervation is relatively modest in most systemic resistance vessels; the vagus therefore regulates the heart much more directly than systemic vascular resistance. At rest, vagal restraint often keeps heart rate below the intrinsic SA nodal rate. Atropine blocks muscarinic signalling and usually increases resting heart rate. \n 5. Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents \n  Adrenaline (epinephrine)  from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution.  Angiotensin II  acts mainly at AT₁ receptors to constrict arterioles, stimulate aldosterone and thirst, and support ADH release.  Aldosterone  increases distal nephron sodium reabsorption over hours to days; water retention follows depending on conditions.  Vasopressin (ADH)  increases renal water reabsorption through V₂ receptors and can cause V₁-mediated vasoconstriction at higher levels.  ANP/BNP  counter volume overload by promoting natriuresis and related effects. Endothelial NO and endothelin modify this systemic control locally. \n 6. What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise? \n  Haemorrhage:  reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch. Baroreflex unloading increases sympathetic activity and reduces vagal tone, raising heart rate, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. RAAS and ADH contribute over a longer time scale. If haemorrhage is severe, increased resistance cannot restore lost oxygen delivery.  Exercise:  sympathetic cardiac stimulation raises output while local metabolic dilation in active muscles can overcome much of the local vasoconstrictor influence. Other vascular beds may constrict, allowing redistribution. The final response is the sum of central and regional signals. \n 7. Time scales and reset limits \n Fast  neural  adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days. The arterial baroreflex can reset during sustained pressure changes and does not by itself determine long-term blood pressure. Kidney-mediated sodium and water balance are central to long-term volume and pressure regulation. \n 8. Clinical application \n An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur. An individual taking an α₁ blocker may develop postural symptoms because peripheral vasoconstriction is impaired.  Do not diagnose shock from blood pressure alone ; follow perfusion, mental state, urine output, capillary refill and the clinical context. \n Understanding Checks \n  01. Sympathetic activity rises but pulse barely changes. How can MAP still rise?  \n  Show explanation α₁-mediated arteriolar constriction increases systemic vascular resistance; venoconstriction can support preload. MAP depends on both output and resistance, not heart rate alone. \n  02. Why does vagal withdrawal increase SA nodal firing?  \n  Show explanation Removing ACh–M₂ signalling reduces inhibitory effects on pacemaker activity, allowing rate to approach the intrinsic SA nodal rate. \n  03. Which receptor most directly increases ventricular contractility during sympathetic activation?  \n  Show explanation Myocardial β₁ adrenergic receptor stimulation increases calcium availability and excitation–contraction coupling, increasing inotropy. \n  04. What is the difference between constricting an arteriole and constricting a vein?  \n  Show explanation Arteriolar constriction mainly increases resistance and alters tissue flow; venoconstriction reduces capacitance and can move stored venous blood centrally to support preload. \n  05. Can a strong sympathetic response restore the blood volume lost in severe haemorrhage?  \n  Show explanation No. It redistributes remaining blood and increases vascular tone but cannot manufacture lost volume; fluid replacement and control of bleeding address the primary deficit. \n  06. How does the NTS participate in rapid BP regulation?  \n  Show explanation It receives baroreceptor afferent signals and engages medullary pathways that adjust sympathetic and vagal outflow. \n  07. Why can adrenaline dilate some vessels yet constrict others?  \n  Show explanation Adrenergic receptor subtype distribution and concentration matter: β₂ can favour skeletal-muscle vasodilation, while α activation causes constriction in other beds. \n  08. Why is aldosterone slower than direct sympathetic vasoconstriction?  \n  Show explanation Aldosterone modifies renal tubular transporter activity and sodium retention, influencing circulating volume over hours or days rather than acting as an immediate nerve impulse. \n  09. How can ADH influence both vascular tone and water retention?  \n  Show explanation V₂ receptor stimulation increases collecting-duct water reabsorption; V₁ receptors mediate vasoconstriction, especially at higher vasopressin levels. \n  10. Why might active muscle receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activity?  \n  Show explanation Local metabolic vasodilator signals in active muscle can attenuate vasoconstriction, while cardiac output rises and blood is redistributed. \n  11. If heart rate doubles but stroke volume halves, what happens to cardiac output?  \n  Show explanation Since CO = HR × SV, the product remains unchanged. Tachycardia alone is not proof of improved blood delivery. \n  12. Why is the baroreflex insufficient as the sole explanation for chronic BP control?  \n  Show explanation It resets and buffers short-term changes; renal pressure natriuresis, fluid balance and longer-term hormonal mechanisms matter critically for persistent pressure regulation. \n Chapter Essentials \n   Why does the whole body need cardiovascular regulation?  — Local control adapts individual organs; central regulation coordinates the circulation to defend arterial pressure and distribute flow when demand or volume changes.   The medullary control network  — The cardiovascular centres are functional networks, not a single on/off switch.   Sympathetic effects on the heart, arterioles and veins  — Most sympathetic postganglionic fibres release noradrenaline (norepinephrine).   Parasympathetic effects and tonic vagal restraint  — Vagal acetylcholine binds cardiac M₂ muscarinic receptors, reducing SA nodal pacing and slowing AV nodal conduction.   Hormones and circulating vasomotor agents  — Adrenaline (epinephrine) from the adrenal medulla can activate β₁ cardiac receptors; β₂ stimulation dilates some skeletal-muscle vascular beds, while α stimulation constricts others, depending on concentration and receptor distribution.   What changes in haemorrhage and in exercise?  — Haemorrhage: reduced central blood volume lowers venous return, stroke volume and arterial stretch.   Time scales and reset limits  — Fast neural adjustments may appear within seconds; circulating catecholamines and angiotensin II can change tone rapidly; renal salt-water balance, aldosterone and structural adaptation shape control over hours to days.   Clinical application  — An individual on a β₁ blocker may have a reduced tachycardic response despite blood loss, while α-mediated vasoconstriction may still occur.  \n Sources and scope \n  Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 12–30 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).  Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall,  Textbook of Medical Physiology ; Boron and Boulpaep,  Medical Physiology .  Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.  "},{"slug":"reflexes","title":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and Cushing Response","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"18-36","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex","title":"1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?"},{"id":"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents","title":"2. Baroreceptors and their afferents"},{"id":"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback","title":"3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback"},{"id":"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response","title":"4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response"},{"id":"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes","title":"5. Chemoreceptor reflexes"},{"id":"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response","title":"6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response"},{"id":"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion","title":"7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion"},{"id":"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses","title":"8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-makes-a-cardiovascular-reflex\">1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</h2>\n<p>A reflex involves a <strong>stimulus</strong>, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-baroreceptors-and-their-afferents\">2. Baroreceptors and their afferents</h2>\n<p>Arterial <strong>stretch-sensitive endings</strong> in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through <strong>Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS</strong>. Aortic arch signals travel primarily through <strong>vagus (CN X) → NTS</strong>. They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-arterial-pressure-rises-trace-negative-feedback\">3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</h2>\n<p>Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action. <strong>Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall</strong>, opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers <em>rapid</em> changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-arterial-pressure-falls-trace-the-reverse-response\">4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</h2>\n<p>Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-chemoreceptor-reflexes\">5. Chemoreceptor reflexes</h2>\n<p>Peripheral chemoreceptors in <strong>carotid and aortic bodies</strong> sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes. <strong>Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-low-pressure-receptors-and-the-bainbridge-response\">6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</h2>\n<p>Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. The lecturer distinguishes <strong>type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction</strong> and <strong>type B discharge around filling</strong>. Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-cushing-response-threatened-cerebral-perfusion\">7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</h2>\n<p><strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke <strong>sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension</strong> to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates <strong>arterial baroreceptors</strong>, contributing to <strong>bradycardia</strong>. Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic <strong>Cushing triad</strong>—an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-compare-apparently-opposite-reflex-responses\">8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</h2>\n<p><strong>Ordinary blood loss</strong> tends to cause baroreflex <strong>tachycardia</strong> and vasoconstriction. <strong>Increased ICP</strong> may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic <strong>hypertension</strong> followed by <strong>baroreflex bradycardia</strong>. <strong>Increased atrial filling</strong> can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension.</details>\n<p><strong>02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Hering's nerve joins the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), transmitting the signal to the medullary NTS.</details>\n<p><strong>03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The vagus nerve (CN X) conveys aortic baroreceptor input to the medulla.</details>\n<p><strong>04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP.</details>\n<p><strong>05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient.</details>\n<p><strong>06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor.</details>\n<p><strong>07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes.</details>\n<p><strong>08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors.</details>\n<p><strong>09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure.</details>\n<p><strong>10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The secondary systemic hypertension stimulates arterial baroreceptors, contributing to reflex bradycardia.</details>\n<p><strong>11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>CPP ≈ MAP − ICP. Higher ICP at unchanged MAP decreases cerebral perfusion pressure.</details>\n<p><strong>12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It is typically a late, ominous pattern and its absence does not rule out intracranial hypertension.</details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>What makes a cardiovascular reflex?</strong> — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response.</li><li><strong>Baroreceptors and their afferents</strong> — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more.</li><li><strong>Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback</strong> — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing.</li><li><strong>Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response</strong> — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint.</li><li><strong>Chemoreceptor reflexes</strong> — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions.</li><li><strong>Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response</strong> — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling.</li><li><strong>Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion</strong> — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure.</li><li><strong>Compare apparently opposite reflex responses</strong> — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li>Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).</li><li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li><li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li></ul>","plain":" Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response \n  Primary lecture:  CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36.  Supplementary clarification:  mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams. \n 1. What makes a cardiovascular reflex? \n A reflex involves a  stimulus , receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response. Reflexes use negative feedback to stabilise arterial pressure or support filling. Understanding the direction of each link avoids memorising isolated arrows. \n 2. Baroreceptors and their afferents \n Arterial  stretch-sensitive endings  in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more. Carotid sinus signals pass through  Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) → medullary NTS . Aortic arch signals travel primarily through  vagus (CN X) → NTS . They respond to mechanical stretch associated with pressure, not directly to blood oxygen concentration. \n 3. Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback \n Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing. The medulla decreases sympathetic cardiac/vascular drive and increases cardiac vagal action.  Heart rate, contractility, arteriolar resistance and often venous tone fall , opposing the rise in MAP. The baroreflex buffers  rapid  changes; prolonged hypertension can reset its operating point. \n 4. Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response \n Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint. HR and contractility tend to rise; arterioles constrict and veins mobilise blood centrally. These responses defend pressure but cannot replace major blood loss. Reflex tachycardia can be absent or blunted because of medications, conduction disease or other competing influences. \n 5. Chemoreceptor reflexes \n Peripheral chemoreceptors in  carotid and aortic bodies  sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions. Carotid body afferents travel via CN IX; aortic body afferents primarily via CN X. Their chief role includes stimulating ventilation, while cardiovascular effects depend on the interaction with respiratory and other reflexes.  Do not confuse carotid body chemoreceptors with carotid sinus baroreceptors.  \n 6. Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response \n Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling. The lecturer distinguishes  type A atrial receptor discharge around atrial contraction  and  type B discharge around filling . Stretch at the atrial–venous junction can promote a  Bainbridge reflex  increase in heart rate, helping manage increased venous return. The outcome depends on concurrent baroreflex input; atrial filling does not invariably produce tachycardia. Atrial stretch can also promote ANP release and alter volume-related hormonal responses. \n 7. Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion \n  Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP  when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure. Markedly rising intracranial pressure reduces perfusion. Severe brainstem ischaemia can provoke  sympathetic vasoconstriction and hypertension  to sustain perfusion. The resulting raised MAP activates  arterial baroreceptors , contributing to  bradycardia . Abnormal respiration can develop from brainstem dysfunction. Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular respiration comprise the classic  Cushing triad —an ominous, often late sign, not a universal early presentation of elevated ICP. \n 8. Compare apparently opposite reflex responses \n  Ordinary blood loss  tends to cause baroreflex  tachycardia  and vasoconstriction.  Increased ICP  may create a Cushing sequence of sympathetic  hypertension  followed by  baroreflex bradycardia .  Increased atrial filling  can stimulate a Bainbridge tendency toward tachycardia. Different primary stimuli can therefore yield different HR directions; always identify the initiating stimulus before predicting output. \n Understanding Checks \n  01. Where are the two major high-pressure arterial baroreceptor sites?  \n  Show explanation At the carotid sinus and aortic arch, where stretch-sensitive endings report pressure-related changes in vessel-wall distension. \n  02. Which cranial nerve carries carotid sinus baroreceptor signals centrally?  \n  Show explanation Hering's nerve joins the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), transmitting the signal to the medullary NTS. \n  03. Which cranial nerve primarily carries aortic arch baroreceptor afferents?  \n  Show explanation The vagus nerve (CN X) conveys aortic baroreceptor input to the medulla. \n  04. A sudden rise in MAP activates the baroreflex. What happens to HR and resistance?  \n  Show explanation Increased baroreceptor firing promotes vagal activity and decreases sympathetic output, lowering HR and arteriolar resistance to oppose high MAP. \n  05. Why is haemorrhagic reflex tachycardia not evidence of restored oxygen delivery?  \n  Show explanation HR may rise while reduced venous return and stroke volume lower cardiac output; tissue delivery can remain insufficient. \n  06. How does a carotid body differ physiologically from a carotid sinus?  \n  Show explanation The carotid body is a peripheral chemoreceptor sensing blood gas and acid-base changes; the sinus is a stretch-sensitive arterial baroreceptor. \n  07. What stimulus is most closely associated with the Bainbridge reflex?  \n  Show explanation Increased venous return and stretch at atrial or venous junctions may increase HR, depending on competing reflexes. \n  08. What are the lecturer's atrial receptor type A and B timing distinctions?  \n  Show explanation Type A impulses occur around atrial contraction; type B impulses are associated with atrial filling. They are low-pressure volume-related receptors. \n  09. Why does the Cushing response initially increase systemic BP?  \n  Show explanation Severe intracranial hypertension compromises brainstem perfusion, provoking intense sympathetic vasoconstriction that raises systemic arterial pressure. \n  10. Why can the pulse slow while BP rises in the Cushing response?  \n  Show explanation The secondary systemic hypertension stimulates arterial baroreceptors, contributing to reflex bradycardia. \n  11. What does CPP approximate when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure?  \n  Show explanation CPP ≈ MAP − ICP. Higher ICP at unchanged MAP decreases cerebral perfusion pressure. \n  12. Is the Cushing triad a reliable early screening test for raised ICP?  \n  Show explanation No. It is typically a late, ominous pattern and its absence does not rule out intracranial hypertension. \n Chapter Essentials \n   What makes a cardiovascular reflex?  — A reflex involves a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, integration centre, autonomic efferent pathway and target response.   Baroreceptors and their afferents  — Arterial stretch-sensitive endings in the carotid sinus and aortic arch increase firing when their walls stretch more.   Arterial pressure rises: trace negative feedback  — Increased pressure stretches baroreceptors and increases afferent firing.   Arterial pressure falls: trace the reverse response  — Less arterial stretch reduces baroreceptor firing, increases sympathetic outflow and withdraws vagal restraint.   Chemoreceptor reflexes  — Peripheral chemoreceptors in carotid and aortic bodies sense decreased arterial oxygen, elevated CO₂ and/or increased H⁺ under appropriate conditions.   Low-pressure receptors and the Bainbridge response  — Receptors in the atria and pulmonary vessels monitor central filling.   Cushing response: threatened cerebral perfusion  — Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP when ICP exceeds the relevant venous outflow pressure.   Compare apparently opposite reflex responses  — Ordinary blood loss tends to cause baroreflex tachycardia and vasoconstriction.  \n Sources and scope \n  Primary: CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf, slides 18–36 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).  Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall,  Textbook of Medical Physiology ; Boron and Boulpaep,  Medical Physiology .  Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.  "},{"slug":"gravity","title":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"2-11; duplicate Lecture 5 pp2-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column","title":"1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column"},{"id":"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output","title":"2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output"},{"id":"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue","title":"3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue"},{"id":"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention","title":"4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention"},{"id":"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition","title":"5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition"},{"id":"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation","title":"6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation"},{"id":"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema","title":"7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema"},{"id":"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences","title":"8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-standing-changes-the-hydrostatic-column\">1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column</h2>\n<p>In the upright body, pressure at points <strong>below</strong> heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure <strong>above</strong> heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference (<strong>ΔP = ρgh</strong>). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-why-venous-pooling-lowers-cardiac-output\">2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</h2>\n<p>Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular <strong>end-diastolic volume → stroke volume</strong> by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-seconds-long-baroreflex-rescue\">3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</h2>\n<p>Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases: <strong>HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict</strong>. This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-skeletal-muscle-pump-and-standing-at-attention\">4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</h2>\n<p>Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle <strong>isometric calf contraction</strong> can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-orthostatic-hypotension-and-its-clinical-definition\">5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</h2>\n<p>Classical <strong>orthostatic hypotension</strong> is a sustained drop of at least <strong>20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing</strong> from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-vasovagal-syncope-is-not-simply-normal-orthostatic-compensation\">6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</h2>\n<p>Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate <strong>vasodilation and/or bradycardia</strong>, leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces <strong>sympathetic activation and tachycardia</strong>. A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-gravity-also-explains-dependent-oedema\">7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema</h2>\n<p>Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-acceleration-aviation-and-clinical-consequences\">8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</h2>\n<p>Under positive <strong>+Gz</strong> (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under <strong>−Gz</strong>, blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The vertical blood column adds hydrostatic pressure below the heart; the difference is approximately ρgh.</details>\n<p><strong>02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology.</details>\n<p><strong>03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint.</details>\n<p><strong>04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP.</details>\n<p><strong>05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling.</details>\n<p><strong>06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>A sustained systolic fall of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic fall of at least 10 mmHg within three minutes of standing.</details>\n<p><strong>07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for.</details>\n<p><strong>08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion.</details>\n<p><strong>09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Walking activates the skeletal-muscle pump, improving venous return and supporting stroke volume.</details>\n<p><strong>10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage.</details>\n<p><strong>11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain.</details>\n<p><strong>12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding.</details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Standing changes the hydrostatic column</strong> — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower.</li><li><strong>Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output</strong> — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins.</li><li><strong>The seconds-long baroreflex rescue</strong> — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing.</li><li><strong>Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention</strong> — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement.</li><li><strong>Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition</strong> — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions.</li><li><strong>Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation</strong> — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting.</li><li><strong>Gravity also explains dependent oedema</strong> — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing.</li><li><strong>Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences</strong> — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).</li><li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li><li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li></ul>","plain":" Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis \n  Primary lecture:  CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11.  Supplementary clarification:  mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams. \n 1. Standing changes the hydrostatic column \n In the upright body, pressure at points  below  heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure  above  heart level is relatively lower. Hydrostatic pressure depends on density, gravitational acceleration and vertical height difference ( ΔP = ρgh ). This is distinct from the pressure generated by the heart itself. \n 2. Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output \n Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins. Central blood volume and right atrial filling decline, lowering ventricular  end-diastolic volume → stroke volume  by the Frank–Starling mechanism. CO can transiently fall, endangering blood pressure at head level and cerebral blood flow. \n 3. The seconds-long baroreflex rescue \n Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing. Vagal restraint decreases and sympathetic drive increases:  HR rises, contractility rises, arterioles constrict and veins constrict . This response raises systemic resistance and supports venous return. A strong heartbeat alone cannot compensate if preload is critically low. \n 4. Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention \n Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement. Walking and subtle  isometric calf contraction  can reduce dependent pooling. Soldiers standing still may perform small muscular contractions to support venous return. The muscle pump complements—not replaces—the baroreflex. \n 5. Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition \n Classical  orthostatic hypotension  is a sustained drop of at least  20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing  from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions. Dehydration, medications, autonomic neuropathy, haemorrhage and prolonged bed rest can contribute. Symptoms include dizziness, visual darkening and syncope from impaired cerebral perfusion. Some forms occur later than three minutes or immediately; formal definitions vary by timing. \n 6. Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation \n Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate  vasodilation and/or bradycardia , leading to transient hypotension and fainting. Contrast this with the usual immediate compensation to standing, which produces  sympathetic activation and tachycardia . A patient can also have mixed mechanisms; clinical history matters. \n 7. Gravity also explains dependent oedema \n Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing. Lymphatic return and muscular movement limit swelling; long immobility can overcome these defences. This connects Chapter 19 directly to capillary exchange and oedema. \n 8. Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences \n Under positive  +Gz  (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness. Anti-G manoeuvres and suits reduce dependent pooling. Under  −Gz , blood shifts toward the head, contributing to congestion and visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, intensity and duration. Supine positioning reduces the head-heart hydrostatic gradient during a simple fainting episode, but persistent or unexplained loss of consciousness needs medical assessment. \n Understanding Checks \n  01. Why is ankle venous pressure higher than heart-level venous pressure when standing?  \n  Show explanation The vertical blood column adds hydrostatic pressure below the heart; the difference is approximately ρgh. \n  02. What is the first cardiac consequence of sudden dependent venous pooling?  \n  Show explanation Reduced central venous return lowers ventricular filling and end-diastolic volume, reducing stroke volume through Frank–Starling physiology. \n  03. What happens to baroreceptor firing after a sudden orthostatic fall in arterial pressure?  \n  Show explanation Firing falls due to reduced arterial stretch, increasing sympathetic output and withdrawing cardiac vagal restraint. \n  04. Why does heart rate usually rise on standing?  \n  Show explanation The baroreflex increases sympathetic cardiac drive and reduces vagal inhibition to support cardiac output and MAP. \n  05. How do brief calf contractions help a person standing still?  \n  Show explanation Muscle compression of deep veins, with one-way valves, propels venous blood toward the heart and reduces pooling. \n  06. What is the standard three-minute definition of classical orthostatic hypotension?  \n  Show explanation A sustained systolic fall of at least 20 mmHg or diastolic fall of at least 10 mmHg within three minutes of standing. \n  07. Why can a dehydrated person develop more severe orthostatic symptoms?  \n  Show explanation Reduced circulating volume limits venous return and preload, making gravitational pooling harder to compensate for. \n  08. How does vasovagal syncope differ from ordinary compensation to standing?  \n  Show explanation Instead of protective vasoconstriction and tachycardia, vasovagal reflexes may cause inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, dropping cerebral perfusion. \n  09. Why can walking relieve dizziness felt during prolonged motionless standing?  \n  Show explanation Walking activates the skeletal-muscle pump, improving venous return and supporting stroke volume. \n  10. Why does ankle oedema worsen after standing motionless for hours?  \n  Show explanation Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure increases net filtration; prolonged excess filtration can exceed lymphatic drainage. \n  11. Why can positive head-to-foot acceleration impair vision?  \n  Show explanation It drives blood toward dependent tissues and reduces pressure and flow to the retina and brain. \n  12. Does lying flat restore lost blood volume after haemorrhage?  \n  Show explanation No. It can improve the gravitational relationship and venous return transiently but does not replace lost volume or treat the source of bleeding. \n Chapter Essentials \n   Standing changes the hydrostatic column  — In the upright body, pressure at points below heart level rises because of the hydrostatic blood column, while pressure above heart level is relatively lower.   Why venous pooling lowers cardiac output  — Standing suddenly shifts blood toward compliant lower-limb and splanchnic veins.   The seconds-long baroreflex rescue  — Reduced arterial stretch causes lower carotid/aortic baroreceptor firing.   Skeletal-muscle pump and standing at attention  — Contracting calf muscles compress deep leg veins; one-way valves direct flow centrally instead of permitting backward movement.   Orthostatic hypotension and its clinical definition  — Classical orthostatic hypotension is a sustained drop of at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing from supine, using standard clinical measurement conditions.   Vasovagal syncope is not simply normal orthostatic compensation  — Prolonged standing, heat, fear or pain can provoke a reflex with inappropriate vasodilation and/or bradycardia, leading to transient hypotension and fainting.   Gravity also explains dependent oedema  — Higher dependent venous and capillary hydrostatic pressures favour filtration into ankle interstitium during prolonged standing.   Acceleration, aviation and clinical consequences  — Under positive +Gz (head-to-foot acceleration), blood shifts toward legs and brain perfusion may fall, causing grey-out, blackout or loss of consciousness.  \n Sources and scope \n  Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 2–11 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).  Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall,  Textbook of Medical Physiology ; Boron and Boulpaep,  Medical Physiology .  Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.  "},{"slug":"shock","title":"Circulatory Shock","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"12-30; duplicate Lecture 5 pp12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold","title":"1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold"},{"id":"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury","title":"2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury"},{"id":"3-four-mechanistic-classes","title":"3. Four mechanistic classes"},{"id":"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion","title":"4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion"},{"id":"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer","title":"5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer"},{"id":"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback","title":"6. Progressive shock and positive feedback"},{"id":"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit","title":"7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit"},{"id":"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs","title":"8. The physiology behind bedside signs"},{"id":"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning","title":"9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning"},{"id":"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits","title":"10. Clinical synthesis and limits"},{"id":"understanding-checks","title":"Understanding Checks"},{"id":"chapter-essentials","title":"Chapter Essentials"},{"id":"sources-and-scope","title":"Sources and scope"}],"html":"<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<p><strong>Primary lecture:</strong> CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30. <strong>Supplementary clarification:</strong> mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-shock-is-failure-of-tissue-perfusion-not-a-bp-threshold\">1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</h2>\n<p><strong>Circulatory shock</strong> is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall. <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-oxygen-delivery-connects-physiology-to-cellular-injury\">2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</h2>\n<p>Systemic oxygen delivery <strong>DO₂ = CO × CaO₂</strong>, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately <strong>CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂</strong> (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-four-mechanistic-classes\">3. Four mechanistic classes</h2>\n<p><strong>Hypovolaemic:</strong> reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns). <strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia). <strong>Distributive:</strong> abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock). <strong>Obstructive:</strong> impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-follow-blood-loss-all-the-way-to-organ-hypoperfusion\">4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</h2>\n<p>Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic <strong>tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction</strong>. Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-compensation-can-preserve-pressure-while-organs-suffer\">5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</h2>\n<p>Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow. <strong>Excessive tachycardia</strong> shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-progressive-shock-and-positive-feedback\">6. Progressive shock and positive feedback</h2>\n<p>Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further. <strong>Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow</strong> is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-why-acute-haemorrhage-may-initially-show-normal-hb-and-haematocrit\">7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</h2>\n<p>A person loses <strong>whole blood</strong>, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their <em>ratio</em>—haematocrit—and Hb <em>concentration</em> may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-the-physiology-behind-bedside-signs\">8. The physiology behind bedside signs</h2>\n<p><strong>Tachycardia</strong> reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes. <strong>Cool skin</strong> often reflects peripheral constriction; <strong>warm skin</strong> can occur early in distributive shock. <strong>Oliguria</strong> suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-hypovolaemic-cardiogenic-distributive-and-obstructive-reasoning\">9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</h2>\n<p>In <strong>hypovolaemia</strong>, filling pressure is often low; in <strong>cardiogenic pump failure</strong>, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in <strong>distributive</strong> forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in <strong>obstructive</strong> forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are <strong>typical patterns</strong>, not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-clinical-synthesis-and-limits\">10. Clinical synthesis and limits</h2>\n<p>The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting. <strong>Bleeding requires haemorrhage control</strong>, pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-checks\">Understanding Checks</h2>\n<p><strong>01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters.</details>\n<p><strong>02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. Lower output can reduce oxygen delivery even before measured Hb concentration changes.</details>\n<p><strong>03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It shortens diastole and may reduce ventricular filling, further lowering stroke volume when preload is already limited.</details>\n<p><strong>04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood.</details>\n<p><strong>05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output.</details>\n<p><strong>06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures.</details>\n<p><strong>07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction.</details>\n<p><strong>08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Red cells and plasma are removed together, so their relative proportion and Hb concentration may initially be preserved.</details>\n<p><strong>09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Interstitial fluid recruitment and administered fluids expand plasma relative to remaining red cells, causing dilution.</details>\n<p><strong>10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate.</details>\n<p><strong>11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion.</details>\n<p><strong>12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Show explanation</summary>Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism.</details>\n<h2 id=\"chapter-essentials\">Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold</strong> — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements.</li><li><strong>Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury</strong> — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content.</li><li><strong>Four mechanistic classes</strong> — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns).</li><li><strong>Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion</strong> — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓.</li><li><strong>Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer</strong> — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow.</li><li><strong>Progressive shock and positive feedback</strong> — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury.</li><li><strong>Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit</strong> — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together.</li><li><strong>The physiology behind bedside signs</strong> — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes.</li><li><strong>Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning</strong> — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded.</li><li><strong>Clinical synthesis and limits</strong> — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"sources-and-scope\">Sources and scope</h2>\n<ul><li>Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).</li><li>Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall, <em>Textbook of Medical Physiology</em>; Boron and Boulpaep, <em>Medical Physiology</em>.</li><li>Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.</li></ul>","plain":" Circulatory Shock \n  Primary lecture:  CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30.  Supplementary clarification:  mechanism-level physiology and clinical distinctions are labelled where they extend beyond introductory lecture diagrams. \n 1. Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold \n  Circulatory shock  is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements. Blood pressure can be near normal early, because systemic resistance rises while cardiac output and regional perfusion fall.  MAP ≈ CO × SVR  describes how a low CO can be masked by compensatory constriction. Evaluate circulation and tissue perfusion, not one cuff reading. \n 2. Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury \n Systemic oxygen delivery  DO₂ = CO × CaO₂ , where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content. Approximately  CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂  (mL O₂/dL, with Hb in g/dL and PaO₂ in mmHg). Thus haemorrhage can reduce delivery by reducing CO even before a measured Hb concentration falls. Failure of oxygen delivery/utilisation impairs ATP production; lactate can rise, although elevated lactate is not specific proof of tissue hypoxia. \n 3. Four mechanistic classes \n  Hypovolaemic:  reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns).  Cardiogenic:  inadequate pumping (large myocardial infarction, critical arrhythmia).  Distributive:  abnormal vascular tone and flow distribution (sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic shock).  Obstructive:  impeded filling or forward flow (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism). Causes can overlap; early septic shock may have warm extremities, so cool skin is not universal. \n 4. Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion \n Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓. The fall in arterial stretch unloads baroreceptors, driving sympathetic  tachycardia, increased contractility, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction . Skin may become pale and cool while renal perfusion and urine output decline. Reduced venous filling limits the benefit of simply making the heart beat faster. \n 5. Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer \n Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow.  Excessive tachycardia  shortens diastolic filling time, potentially making stroke volume smaller. Strong vasoconstriction diverts flow away from skin and some organs; it cannot restore absent circulating volume. Angiotensin II, aldosterone, ADH and thirst help support volume over longer periods but are insufficient without control of major ongoing losses. \n 6. Progressive shock and positive feedback \n Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury. Capillary leak and microvascular dysfunction can reduce effective circulating volume and oxygen extraction further.  Worsening flow → tissue injury → poorer pump/vascular function → still worse flow  is a reinforcing loop. Severe stages may become refractory despite treatment; there is no universal clock at which shock becomes irreversible. \n 7. Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit \n A person loses  whole blood , so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together. Their  ratio —haematocrit—and Hb  concentration  may remain near baseline immediately. Subsequent interstitial fluid recruitment and infused IV fluid dilute the residual red cells; Hb and haematocrit can then fall. Initial laboratory concentrations must not override evidence of acute bleeding and hypoperfusion. Erythropoietin-driven red cell replacement takes much longer than volume adjustments. \n 8. The physiology behind bedside signs \n  Tachycardia  reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes.  Cool skin  often reflects peripheral constriction;  warm skin  can occur early in distributive shock.  Oliguria  suggests reduced renal perfusion or renal dysfunction; altered mentation may signal cerebral hypoperfusion. Lactate, capillary refill and trends can contribute, but none alone establishes or excludes shock. \n 9. Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning \n In  hypovolaemia , filling pressure is often low; in  cardiogenic pump failure , filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in  distributive  forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in  obstructive  forms flow is mechanically impeded. These are  typical patterns , not hard rules. Identify the failing link rather than treating all hypotension as a lack of fluid. \n 10. Clinical synthesis and limits \n The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting.  Bleeding requires haemorrhage control , pump failure requires cause-directed management, obstructive causes need relief, and distributive causes need targeted treatment. This chapter teaches physiology, not a substitute for emergency clinical protocols. \n Understanding Checks \n  01. Can a patient be shocked with a near-normal MAP after major bleeding?  \n  Show explanation Yes. Raised SVR may sustain MAP while reduced venous return and cardiac output impair tissue oxygen delivery. Clinical perfusion matters. \n  02. Write the equation connecting oxygen delivery to cardiac output.  \n  Show explanation DO₂ = CO × CaO₂. Lower output can reduce oxygen delivery even before measured Hb concentration changes. \n  03. Why does severe tachycardia sometimes worsen haemorrhagic shock?  \n  Show explanation It shortens diastole and may reduce ventricular filling, further lowering stroke volume when preload is already limited. \n  04. Why is vasoconstriction initially adaptive in haemorrhage?  \n  Show explanation It increases SVR and supports arterial pressure while diverting limited blood toward vital organs, but cannot replace lost blood. \n  05. Which shock type is most directly caused by cardiac tamponade?  \n  Show explanation Obstructive shock, because pericardial pressure mechanically restricts ventricular filling and forward output. \n  06. Which shock type usually follows a large infarct with severe pump dysfunction?  \n  Show explanation Cardiogenic shock: inadequate myocardial pumping reduces forward output, sometimes despite elevated filling pressures. \n  07. Why can early septic shock have warm skin?  \n  Show explanation Distributive vasodilation can increase cutaneous blood flow despite severe circulatory dysfunction. \n  08. Why may Hb and haematocrit be nearly unchanged immediately after acute whole-blood loss?  \n  Show explanation Red cells and plasma are removed together, so their relative proportion and Hb concentration may initially be preserved. \n  09. Why does measured Hb often fall later after bleeding?  \n  Show explanation Interstitial fluid recruitment and administered fluids expand plasma relative to remaining red cells, causing dilution. \n  10. Why can lactate rise in shock, and why is it not fully specific?  \n  Show explanation Low oxygen delivery may increase anaerobic glycolysis and lactate, but adrenergic stimulation, clearance changes and other causes also affect lactate. \n  11. Explain one positive feedback loop in progressive shock.  \n  Show explanation Low perfusion produces acidosis and myocardial/endothelial injury, worsening cardiac function, microvascular flow and effective volume; this further lowers perfusion. \n  12. Why is the treatment target different in obstructive and hypovolaemic shock?  \n  Show explanation Obstructive shock requires relief of the mechanical block; hypovolaemic shock requires correcting volume loss and its cause. Treating every low BP identically misses the mechanism. \n Chapter Essentials \n   Shock is failure of tissue perfusion, not a BP threshold  — Circulatory shock is life-threatening circulatory dysfunction leading to inadequate oxygen delivery and/or use relative to tissue metabolic requirements.   Oxygen delivery connects physiology to cellular injury  — Systemic oxygen delivery DO₂ = CO × CaO₂, where CaO₂ is arterial oxygen content.   Four mechanistic classes  — Hypovolaemic: reduced effective intravascular volume (haemorrhage, GI losses, burns).   Follow blood loss all the way to organ hypoperfusion  — Haemorrhage → circulating volume ↓ → venous return ↓ → preload ↓ → stroke volume ↓ → cardiac output ↓ → oxygen delivery ↓.   Compensation can preserve pressure while organs suffer  — Early sympathetic responses can restore or partly maintain MAP despite inadequate tissue flow.   Progressive shock and positive feedback  — Persistent hypoperfusion can produce cellular energy failure, acidosis, impaired myocardial performance and endothelial injury.   Why acute haemorrhage may initially show normal Hb and haematocrit  — A person loses whole blood, so both circulating red cells and plasma are removed together.   The physiology behind bedside signs  — Tachycardia reflects sympathetic compensation in many forms, but can be absent with β-blockade, conduction disturbances or neurogenic causes.   Hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, distributive and obstructive reasoning  — In hypovolaemia, filling pressure is often low; in cardiogenic pump failure, filling pressures may rise behind the failing ventricle; in distributive forms systemic resistance is often low and circulation maldistributed, though later mixed myocardial depression occurs; in obstructive forms flow is mechanically impeded.   Clinical synthesis and limits  — The immediate priorities in suspected shock are urgent assessment of airway, breathing and circulation and rapid treatment of the underlying cause in a clinical setting.  \n Sources and scope \n  Primary: CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf, slides 12–30 (verify specific slide alignment against the original lecture deck before release).  Cross-reference: Guyton and Hall,  Textbook of Medical Physiology ; Boron and Boulpaep,  Medical Physiology .  Clinical thresholds and mechanistic clarifications are supplementary and should not be mistaken for verbatim lecture claims.  "},{"slug":"heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure Physiology","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"31-33; duplicate Lecture 5 pp31-33","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"define-the-pump-problem","title":"Define the pump problem"},{"id":"forward-versus-backward-consequences","title":"Forward versus backward consequences"},{"id":"compensation-can-become-harmful","title":"Compensation can become harmful"},{"id":"systolic-and-diastolic-problems","title":"Systolic and diastolic problems"},{"id":"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion","title":"Forward flow and backward congestion"},{"id":"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm","title":"What compensates and why compensation can harm"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"define-the-pump-problem\">Define the pump problem</h2>\n<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-versus-backward-consequences\">Forward versus backward consequences</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward consequences</strong> relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow). <strong>Backward consequences</strong> reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compensation-can-become-harmful\">Compensation can become harmful</h2>\n<p>Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output, <strong>RAAS</strong> and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"systolic-and-diastolic-problems\">Systolic and diastolic problems</h2>\n<p>In <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong>, ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong>, filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion\">Forward flow and backward congestion</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward failure</strong> emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion. <strong>Backward failure</strong> emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites.</p>\n<p>These are explanatory perspectives, <strong>not four mutually exclusive diseases</strong>. Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm\">What compensates and why compensation can harm</h2>\n<p>Reduced forward output activates <strong>sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH</strong>. Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or <strong>preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately.</p>\n","plain":" Heart Failure Physiology \n Define the pump problem \n Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output. \n Forward versus backward consequences \n  Forward consequences  relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow).  Backward consequences  reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema. \n Compensation can become harmful \n Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output,  RAAS  and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency. \n Systolic and diastolic problems \n In  systolic dysfunction , ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In  diastolic dysfunction , filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion. \n  Check:  Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume? \n  Answer Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion. \n\n Forward flow and backward congestion \n  Forward failure  emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion.  Backward failure  emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites. \n These are explanatory perspectives,  not four mutually exclusive diseases . Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem. \n What compensates and why compensation can harm \n Reduced forward output activates  sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH . Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or  preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal . \n  Practice:  Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately. \n"},{"slug":"cerebral","title":"Cerebral Circulation and CSF","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"2-25; extra diagrams Circulation 3 39-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"supply-and-flow-regulation","title":"Supply and flow regulation"},{"id":"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle","title":"Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle"},{"id":"csf-path-and-functions","title":"CSF path and functions"},{"id":"blood-brain-barrier-bbb","title":"Blood–brain barrier (BBB)"},{"id":"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability","title":"Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability"},{"id":"meninges-csf-and-pressure","title":"Meninges, CSF and pressure"},{"id":"cerebral-blood-flow-control","title":"Cerebral blood flow control"}],"html":"<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"supply-and-flow-regulation\">Supply and flow regulation</h2>\n<p>The <strong>internal carotid</strong> and <strong>vertebrobasilar</strong> systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance. <strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure.</p>\n<p>Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle\">Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle</h2>\n<p>Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation <strong>ICP rises sharply</strong>, potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"csf-path-and-functions\">CSF path and functions</h2>\n<p>CSF is produced mainly by <strong>choroid plexus</strong>, flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>. It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrocephalus</strong> may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a <strong>potential</strong>, not normally CSF-filled, space.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-brain-barrier-bbb\">Blood–brain barrier (BBB)</h2>\n<p>Specialised brain capillary <strong>endothelial tight junctions</strong>, transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability\">Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability</h2>\n<p>The <strong>anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries</strong> supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but <strong>not itself part of the arterial circle</strong>. The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes <strong>ischaemic</strong> (thrombotic/embolic) and <strong>haemorrhagic</strong> stroke as broad categories.</p>\n<h2 id=\"meninges-csf-and-pressure\">Meninges, CSF and pressure</h2>\n<p>From outside inward: <strong>dura mater</strong>, <strong>arachnoid mater</strong>, <strong>subarachnoid space containing CSF</strong>, then <strong>pia mater</strong> adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>, with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles → <strong>foramina of Monro</strong> → third ventricle → <strong>aqueduct of Sylvius</strong> → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways. <strong>Communicating hydrocephalus</strong> generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway; <strong>noncommunicating hydrocephalus</strong> has an obstruction within it.</p>\n<p>CSF protects via <strong>buoyancy and cushioning</strong> and supports interstitial solute clearance. <strong>Lumbar puncture</strong> samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cerebral-blood-flow-control\">Cerebral blood flow control</h2>\n<p><strong>CPP ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to <strong>CO₂</strong>: rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as <strong>ketone bodies during prolonged starvation</strong>; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism.</p>\n<p>The <strong>BBB</strong> depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses <strong>GLUT1</strong>, and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is <strong>not</strong> the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged.</p>\n","plain":" Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier \n Supply and flow regulation \n The  internal carotid  and  vertebrobasilar  systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the  Circle of Willis , offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance.  Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP  when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure. \n Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity. \n Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle \n Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation  ICP rises sharply , potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response. \n CSF path and functions \n CSF is produced mainly by  choroid plexus , flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about  150 mL . It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling. \n  Hydrocephalus  may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a  potential , not normally CSF-filled, space. \n Blood–brain barrier (BBB) \n Specialised brain capillary  endothelial tight junctions , transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access. \n  Check:  Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal? \n  Answer CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP. \n\n Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability \n The  anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries  supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the  Circle of Willis , potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but  not itself part of the arterial circle . The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes  ischaemic  (thrombotic/embolic) and  haemorrhagic  stroke as broad categories. \n Meninges, CSF and pressure \n From outside inward:  dura mater ,  arachnoid mater ,  subarachnoid space containing CSF , then  pia mater  adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about  150 mL , with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles →  foramina of Monro  → third ventricle →  aqueduct of Sylvius  → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways.  Communicating hydrocephalus  generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway;  noncommunicating hydrocephalus  has an obstruction within it. \n CSF protects via  buoyancy and cushioning  and supports interstitial solute clearance.  Lumbar puncture  samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected. \n Cerebral blood flow control \n  CPP ≈ MAP − ICP  in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to  CO₂ : rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as  ketone bodies during prolonged starvation ; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism. \n The  BBB  depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses  GLUT1 , and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is  not  the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus. \n  Practice:  A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged. \n"},{"slug":"coronary","title":"Coronary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"26-29; extra diagrams Circulation 3 42-43","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion","title":"The myocardium needs its own perfusion"},{"id":"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole","title":"Why left coronary flow favours diastole"},{"id":"strong-metabolic-regulation","title":"Strong metabolic regulation"},{"id":"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction","title":"Ischaemia, angina and infarction"},{"id":"routes-and-drainage","title":"Routes and drainage"},{"id":"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow","title":"Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow"}],"html":"<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion\">The myocardium needs its own perfusion</h2>\n<p>The <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong> originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong>; additional veins may drain directly into chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole\">Why left coronary flow favours diastole</h2>\n<p>During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during <strong>diastole</strong>, coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"strong-metabolic-regulation\">Strong metabolic regulation</h2>\n<p>Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local dilators include <strong>adenosine, NO</strong> and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction\">Ischaemia, angina and infarction</h2>\n<p>A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing <strong>angina</strong>. Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>, with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"routes-and-drainage\">Routes and drainage</h2>\n<p>The <strong>left coronary artery</strong> typically divides into <strong>left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)</strong> and <strong>circumflex</strong> branches; the <strong>right coronary artery</strong> supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow\">Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow</h2>\n<p>Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local metabolites including <strong>adenosine</strong>, changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial <strong>NO</strong> help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes <strong>secondary metabolic coronary dilation</strong>; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>left ventricle</strong>, intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in <strong>diastole</strong>. Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise (<strong>demand ischaemia/angina</strong>); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes (<strong>infarction</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion.</p>\n","plain":" Coronary Circulation \n The myocardium needs its own perfusion \n The  right and left coronary arteries  originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the  coronary sinus ; additional veins may drain directly into chambers. \n Why left coronary flow favours diastole \n During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during  diastole , coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises. \n Strong metabolic regulation \n Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by  increased coronary blood flow . Local dilators include  adenosine, NO  and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work. \n Ischaemia, angina and infarction \n A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing  angina . Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause  myocardial infarction , with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow. \n  Check:  Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply? \n  Answer More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest. \n\n Routes and drainage \n The  left coronary artery  typically divides into  left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)  and  circumflex  branches; the  right coronary artery  supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the  posterior descending artery , not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the  coronary sinus  to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately. \n Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow \n Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is  increased coronary blood flow . Local metabolites including  adenosine , changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial  NO  help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes  secondary metabolic coronary dilation ; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate. \n In the  left ventricle , intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in  diastole . Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise ( demand ischaemia/angina ); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes ( infarction ). \n  Practice:  Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion. \n"},{"slug":"pulmonary","title":"Pulmonary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"30-32","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"one-flow-different-pressures","title":"One flow, different pressures"},{"id":"gravity-and-distribution","title":"Gravity and distribution"},{"id":"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen","title":"The unusual response to low oxygen"},{"id":"oedema-prevention","title":"Oedema prevention"},{"id":"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits","title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circuits"},{"id":"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment","title":"Gravity and vascular recruitment"},{"id":"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations","title":"Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations"}],"html":"<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"one-flow-different-pressures\">One flow, different pressures</h2>\n<p>The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has <strong>far lower vascular resistance and pressure</strong>. Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The <strong>bronchial circulation</strong> from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-distribution\">Gravity and distribution</h2>\n<p>In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with <strong>West lung zones</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen\">The unusual response to low oxygen</h2>\n<p>Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but <strong>small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂</strong>. This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-prevention\">Oedema prevention</h2>\n<p>Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits\">Pulmonary vs systemic circuits</h2>\n<p>Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations: <strong>pulmonary vessels</strong> for gas exchange and <strong>bronchial arteries</strong> (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment\">Gravity and vascular recruitment</h2>\n<p>In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic <strong>West lung zones</strong> compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations\">Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations</h2>\n<p>When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict (<strong>hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong>) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical <strong>systemic local hypoxic dilation</strong> that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region.</p>\n","plain":" Pulmonary Circulation \n One flow, different pressures \n The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has  far lower vascular resistance and pressure . Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The  bronchial circulation  from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation. \n Gravity and distribution \n In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with  West lung zones . \n The unusual response to low oxygen \n Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but  small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂ . This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension. \n Oedema prevention \n Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema. \n  Check:  Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung? \n  Answer Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure. \n\n Pulmonary vs systemic circuits \n Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations:  pulmonary vessels  for gas exchange and  bronchial arteries  (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture. \n Gravity and vascular recruitment \n In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic  West lung zones  compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail. \n Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations \n When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict ( hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction ) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical  systemic local hypoxic dilation  that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain. \n  Practice:  Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region. \n"},{"slug":"splanchnic","title":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"33-37","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks","title":"An unusual route through two capillary networks"},{"id":"hepatic-microcirculation","title":"Hepatic microcirculation"},{"id":"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock","title":"Blood flow after meals and in shock"},{"id":"ascites","title":"Ascites"},{"id":"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route","title":"Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route"},{"id":"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage","title":"Digestion, shock and venous storage"},{"id":"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story","title":"Explain ascites without one misleading protein story"}],"html":"<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks\">An unusual route through two capillary networks</h2>\n<p>The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the <strong>coeliac trunk</strong>, <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the <strong>portal vein</strong> to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic-microcirculation\">Hepatic microcirculation</h2>\n<p>Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver <strong>sinusoids</strong>, and drains toward <strong>central veins</strong>. Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while <strong>zone 3</strong> hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">Blood flow after meals and in shock</h2>\n<p>Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ascites\">Ascites</h2>\n<p>Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours <strong>ascites</strong>, accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route\">Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route</h2>\n<p>Arteries from the <strong>coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries</strong> supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through <strong>portal vein and hepatic artery</strong> branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to <strong>central veins → hepatic veins → IVC</strong>. The <strong>portal triad</strong> contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage.</p>\n<p>In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow; <strong>centrilobular (zone 3)</strong> hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage\">Digestion, shock and venous storage</h2>\n<p>Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases <strong>splanchnic flow</strong> (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial <strong>blood reservoir</strong>. During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story\">Explain ascites without one misleading protein story</h2>\n<p><strong>Ascites</strong> is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is <strong>not universal</strong> in all ascites.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen.</p>\n","plain":" Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation \n An unusual route through two capillary networks \n The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the  coeliac trunk ,  superior mesenteric artery  and  inferior mesenteric artery . Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the  portal vein  to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution. \n Hepatic microcirculation \n Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver  sinusoids , and drains toward  central veins . Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while  zone 3  hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources. \n Blood flow after meals and in shock \n Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia. \n Ascites \n Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours  ascites , accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed. \n  Check:  Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended? \n  Answer Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure. \n\n Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route \n Arteries from the  coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries  supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through  portal vein and hepatic artery  branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to  central veins → hepatic veins → IVC . The  portal triad  contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage. \n In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow;  centrilobular (zone 3)  hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini. \n Digestion, shock and venous storage \n Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases  splanchnic flow  (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial  blood reservoir . During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel. \n Explain ascites without one misleading protein story \n  Ascites  is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is  not universal  in all ascites. \n  Practice:  Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen. \n"},{"slug":"skin","title":"Cutaneous Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"38-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat","title":"Skin blood flow regulates heat"},{"id":"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin","title":"Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin"},{"id":"shock-and-temperature","title":"Shock and temperature"},{"id":"distinguish-blood-flow-functions","title":"Distinguish blood-flow functions"},{"id":"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin","title":"Lewis triple response to stroking the skin"}],"html":"<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat\">Skin blood flow regulates heat</h2>\n<p>Skin has a rich vascular network, including <strong>arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin\">Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the <strong>white reaction</strong>). Firmer stroking may produce the <strong>triple response of Lewis</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Red line</strong> near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation.</li>\n<li><strong>Flare</strong>, a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators.</li>\n<li><strong>Wheal</strong>, a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"shock-and-temperature\">Shock and temperature</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for <strong>thermoregulation</strong>, not simply nutrient demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-blood-flow-functions\">Distinguish blood-flow functions</h2>\n<p>Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must <strong>lose heat</strong>. Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin\">Lewis triple response to stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A firm, blunt stroke produces a <strong>red line</strong> at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding <strong>flare</strong> (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve <strong>axon reflex</strong>) and a raised <strong>wheal</strong> (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief <strong>white line response</strong> with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The <strong>wheal</strong>; the flare is spreading redness.</p>\n","plain":" Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response \n Skin blood flow regulates heat \n Skin has a rich vascular network, including  arteriovenous anastomoses  in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage. \n Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin \n A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the  white reaction ). Firmer stroking may produce the  triple response of Lewis : \n \n  Red line  near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation. \n  Flare , a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators. \n  Wheal , a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage. \n \n Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary. \n Shock and temperature \n In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for  thermoregulation , not simply nutrient demand. \n  Check:  What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch? \n  Answer A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex. \n\n Distinguish blood-flow functions \n Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must  lose heat . Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness. \n Lewis triple response to stroking the skin \n A firm, blunt stroke produces a  red line  at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding  flare  (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve  axon reflex ) and a raised  wheal  (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief  white line response  with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response. \n  Practice:  Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The  wheal ; the flare is spreading redness. \n"},{"slug":"fetal","title":"Fetal Circulation and Birth Transitions","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"42-45","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts","title":"Why fetal flow takes shortcuts"},{"id":"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths","title":"What changes after the first breaths?"},{"id":"fetal-haemoglobin","title":"Fetal haemoglobin"},{"id":"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream","title":"Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream"},{"id":"what-closes-after-delivery","title":"What closes after delivery?"}],"html":"<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts\">Why fetal flow takes shortcuts</h2>\n<p>Before birth, gas exchange happens in the <strong>placenta</strong>, not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the <strong>umbilical vein</strong>. Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow).</li>\n<li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> pulmonary trunk → descending aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two <strong>umbilical arteries</strong> carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths\">What changes after the first breaths?</h2>\n<p>Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising <strong>left atrial pressure relative to right</strong> functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin</h2>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds <strong>2,3-BPG</strong> less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream\">Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream</h2>\n<p>The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The <strong>umbilical vein</strong> brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs. <strong>Umbilical arteries</strong> return lower-oxygen blood to placenta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-closes-after-delivery\">What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing <strong>pulmonary vascular resistance</strong> and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases <strong>systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote <strong>ductus arteriosus constriction</strong>. Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce <strong>pulmonary</strong> resistance; placental separation independently raises <strong>systemic</strong> resistance.</p>\n","plain":" Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth \n Why fetal flow takes shortcuts \n Before birth, gas exchange happens in the  placenta , not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the  umbilical vein . Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the  ductus venosus  to the inferior vena cava. \n Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways: \n \n  Foramen ovale:  right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow). \n  Ductus arteriosus:  pulmonary trunk → descending aorta. \n \n Two  umbilical arteries  carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers. \n What changes after the first breaths? \n Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising  left atrial pressure relative to right  functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus. \n Fetal haemoglobin \n  HbF  has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds  2,3-BPG  less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months. \n  Check:  Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium? \n  Answer Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium. \n\n Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream \n The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The  umbilical vein  brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the  ductus venosus  to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the  foramen ovale  supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through  ductus arteriosus  into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs.  Umbilical arteries  return lower-oxygen blood to placenta. \n What closes after delivery? \n First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing  pulmonary vascular resistance  and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases  systemic vascular resistance . Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote  ductus arteriosus constriction . Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant. \n  HbF  holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months. \n  Practice:  Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce  pulmonary  resistance; placental separation independently raises  systemic  resistance. \n"}];
